Finder.load({"v":1,"chapters":["electrostatics","current-electricity","magnetism","thermodynamics"],"docs":[[0,1,0,"lesson","Charge & Coulomb’s Law","Meet the property that makes matter push and pull — and the one equation that every other idea in this chapter is built on.",[],""],[0,1,1,"sec","The strongest force you never notice","Gravity gets all the fame, but it is embarrassingly weak. Take the proton and the electron in a hydrogen atom. They attract each other in two ways: gravitationally (they have mass) and electrically (they have charge). Divide one force by the other:",[],""],[0,1,2,"text","The strongest force you never notice","Gravity gets all the fame, but it is embarrassingly weak. Take the proton and the electron in a hydrogen atom. They attract each other in two ways: gravitationally (they have mass) and electrically (they have charge). Divide one force by the other:",[],""],[0,1,3,"formula","The strongest force you never notice","Gravity gets all the fame, but it is embarrassingly weak. Take the proton and the electron in a hydrogen atom. They attract each other in two ways: gravitationally (they have mass) and electrically (they have charge). Divide one force by the other:",["(F_(electric))/(F_(gravity)) = (e^2/4πε_0)/(G m_pm_e) ≈ 2 × 10^39"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>F</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">electric</span></sub></span><span><i>F</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">gravity</span></sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><sup>2</sup>/4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span><span><i>G</i> <i>m</i><sub><i>p</i></sub><i>m</i><sub><i>e</i></sub></span></span> ≈ 2 × 10<sup>39</sup>"],[0,1,4,"text","The strongest force you never notice","That is a 2 followed by thirty-nine zeros. So why does gravity run the solar system while electricity seems to do nothing? Because matter is almost perfectly neutral: the plus and minus cancel so precisely that the giant hides. Disturb that balance by even a billionth of a percent and you get lightning.",[],""],[0,1,5,"text","The strongest force you never notice","You are feeling the electric force right now. The floor holds you up because the electrons in your shoes refuse to overlap with the electrons in the floor. A few atomic layers of repulsion are beating the gravitational pull of the entire Earth.",[],""],[0,1,6,"story","The plan for this chapter","We will build four tools, each one easier to use than the last: force (this lesson) → field → flux and Gauss’s law → potential. JEE Advanced rarely asks “do you know the formula?”. It asks “did you pick the right tool?”. So for every tool we will ask the same question: when does this one make life easy?",[],""],[0,1,7,"sec","Charge: three rules, no exceptions","Charge is simply a property of matter — like mass, except it comes in two flavours.",[],""],[0,1,8,"text","Charge: three rules, no exceptions","Charge is simply a property of matter — like mass, except it comes in two flavours.",[],""],[0,1,9,"text","Charge: three rules, no exceptions","Two kinds. Positive and negative. Likes repel, unlikes attract. It comes in packets (quantisation). Every free charge ever measured is a whole-number multiple of e : q = ±ne, e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C , n = 0, 1, 2, … It is never created or destroyed (conservation). When you rub a balloon on your hair, electrons move from hair to balloon. Nothing was made; the books always balance.",["e","q = ±ne, e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C , n = 0, 1, 2, …"],""],[0,1,9,"formula","Charge: three rules, no exceptions","ver measured is a whole-number multiple of e : q = ±ne, e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C , n = 0, 1, 2, … It is never created or destroyed (conservation). When you rub a balloon on your hair, electrons move from hair to balloon. Nothing was made; the books always balance.",["q = ±ne, e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C , n = 0, 1, 2, …"],"<i>q</i> = ±<i>n</i><i>e</i>, <i>e</i> = 1.6 × 10<sup>−19</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">C</span>, <i>n</i> = 0, 1, 2, …"],[0,1,11,"text","Charge: three rules, no exceptions","One more fact that mass does not share: charge is invariant — a proton moving at 0.99c carries exactly the same charge as one at rest.",["0.99c"],""],[0,1,12,"intuition","One coulomb is absurdly large","Two charges of 1 C placed 1 m apart would push on each other with 9 × 10^9 N — the weight of roughly nine lakh tonnes. That is why every realistic problem uses μ C (10^(−6)), nC (10^(−9)) or pC (10^(−12)). If your answer needs a whole coulomb sitting on a small ball, recheck your powers of ten.",["1 C","1 m","9 × 10^9 N","μ C","10^(−6)","nC","10^(−9)","pC","10^(−12)"],""],[0,1,13,"q","Quantisation · Single correct","Which of these cannot be the charge on an isolated body? 3.2 × 10^(−19) C · 8.0 × 10^(−19) C · 2.4 × 10^(−19) C · 1.6 × 10^(−18) C",["3.2 × 10^(−19) C","8.0 × 10^(−19) C","2.4 × 10^(−19) C","1.6 × 10^(−18) C"],""],[0,1,13,"sol","Quantisation · Single correct","Divide each by e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C : you get 2 , 5 , 1.5 and 10 . A body cannot carry one and a half electrons’ worth of charge, so (C) is impossible.",["e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C","2","5","1.5","10"],""],[0,1,14,"sec","Moving charge around","A conductor (metals, salty water, you) has charges that are free to wander. An insulator (plastic, glass, dry air) keeps its charges pinned where they are. Three ways to charge an object:",[],""],[0,1,15,"text","Moving charge around","A conductor (metals, salty water, you) has charges that are free to wander. An insulator (plastic, glass, dry air) keeps its charges pinned where they are. Three ways to charge an object:",[],""],[0,1,16,"text","Moving charge around","Friction — rubbing transfers electrons from one material to the other. Both end up charged, equal and opposite. Contact — touch a charged conductor to another conductor and the charge is shared. For two identical spheres, symmetry leaves no choice: each walks away with half the total. Induction — bring a charged rod near a conductor (no touching). The free charges inside rearrange: unlike charge crowds to the near side, like charge is pushed to the far side. Earth the far side for a moment and the conductor is left with a net charge opposite to the rod’s — and the rod has lost nothing.",[],""],[0,1,17,"text","Moving charge around","Induction also explains why a charged comb attracts neutral bits of paper: the nearer side of the paper becomes slightly opposite in charge, it is closer, so attraction wins over repulsion.",[],""],[0,1,18,"trap","“They attract, so they must be oppositely charged”","Not necessarily. A charged body attracts a neutral one too (induction). Only repulsion proves that both bodies are charged — and with the same sign.",[],""],[0,1,19,"sec","Coulomb’s law","Two point charges q_1 and q_2 , at rest, a distance r apart. The force between them acts along the line joining them, and its size is",[],""],[0,1,20,"text","Coulomb’s law","Two point charges q_1 and q_2 , at rest, a distance r apart. The force between them acts along the line joining them, and its size is",["q_1","q_2","r"],""],[0,1,21,"formula","Coulomb’s law","Two point charges q_1 and q_2 , at rest, a distance r apart. The force between them acts along the line joining them, and its size is",["F = 1/(4πε_0) (∣q_1q_2∣)/(r^2)"],"<i>F</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∣<i>q</i><sub>1</sub><i>q</i><sub>2</sub>∣</span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>"],[0,1,22,"text","Coulomb’s law","The constant in front is usually just called k , and ε_0 is the permittivity of free space:",["k","ε_0"],""],[0,1,23,"formula","Coulomb’s law","The constant in front is usually just called k , and ε_0 is the permittivity of free space:",["k = 1/(4πε_0) ≈ 9 × 10^9 N m ^2 C ^(−2), ε_0 = 8.85 × 10^(−12) C ^2 N ^(−1) m ^(−2)"],"<i>k</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ≈ 9 × 10<sup>9</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">N m</span><sup>2</sup><span class=\"fx-t\">C</span><sup>−2</sup>, <i>ε</i><sub>0</sub> = 8.85 × 10<sup>−12</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">C</span><sup>2</sup><span class=\"fx-t\">N</span><sup>−1</sup><span class=\"fx-t\">m</span><sup>−2</sup>"],[0,1,24,"text","Coulomb’s law","Double the distance and the force drops to a quarter. Triple it — a ninth.",[],""],[0,1,25,"intuition","Why inverse square?","Imagine the charge spraying its “influence” equally in all directions. At distance r that influence is spread over a sphere of area 4πr^2 . Twice as far, four times the area, a quarter of the intensity. This is also why 4π sits in the constant — and it is the seed of Gauss’s law, two lessons from now.",["r","4πr^2","4π"],""],[0,1,26,"text","Coulomb’s law","The vector form does the direction bookkeeping for you. With position vectors r_1, r_2 , the force on charge 1 due to charge 2 is",["r_1, r_2"],""],[0,1,27,"formula","Coulomb’s law","The vector form does the direction bookkeeping for you. With position vectors r_1, r_2 , the force on charge 1 due to charge 2 is",["F_12 = 1/(4πε_0) (q_1q_2)/(∣r_1 − r_2∣^3) (r_1 − r_2)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span><sub>12</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><sub>1</sub><i>q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span>∣<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span><sub>1</sub> − <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span><sub>2</sub>∣<sup>3</sup></span></span> (<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span><sub>1</sub> − <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span><sub>2</sub>)"],[0,1,28,"text","Coulomb’s law","Put the charges in with their signs: like signs give a force along r_1 − r_2 (pushed away), unlike signs flip it (pulled in). And F_21 = −F_12 always — Newton’s third law, even when the charges are wildly unequal.",["r_1 − r_2","F_21 = −F_12"],""],[0,1,29,"trap","That cube is not an inverse-cube law","The ∣r_1 − r_2∣^3 downstairs is cancelled once by the vector upstairs. The magnitude is still 1/r^2 .",["∣r_1 − r_2∣^3","1/r^2"],""],[0,1,30,"text","Coulomb’s law","In a medium. Sink both charges in an insulating liquid of dielectric constant K and the force between them becomes F/K . Water has K ≈ 80 — which is precisely why salt dissolves in it: the ions simply stop holding on to each other.",["K","F/K","K ≈ 80"],""],[0,1,31,"q","Touch and go · Single correct","Two identical small conducting spheres carry +6 μ C and −2 μ C . Held a distance r apart they attract with a force F . They are touched together and returned to the same separation. The force between them is now F/3 , attractive · F/3 , repulsive · 4F/3 , repulsive · F , repulsive",["+6 μ C","−2 μ C","r","F","F/3","F/3","4F/3","F"],""],[0,1,31,"sol","Touch and go · Single correct","Before: F ∝ ∣(+6)(−2)∣ = 12 . Contact: total charge = +4 μ C , shared equally, so each sphere now carries +2 μ C . After: F^′ ∝ (2)(2) = 4 , hence F^′ = 4/12F = F/3 . Both charges are positive, so the force is repulsive. Charge is conserved, and identical spheres split the total equally.",["F ∝ ∣(+6)(−2)∣ = 12","= +4 μ C","+2 μ C","F^′ ∝ (2)(2) = 4","F^′ = 4/12F = F/3"],""],[0,1,32,"lab","Feel Coulomb’s law","Try these, in order: Drag q_2 so the separation doubles. Watch the force readout — it should fall to one quarter. Set q_1 = +5 μ C and q_2 = −0.5 μ C . Are the two yellow force arrows still the same length? (They must be. Why?) Tap Like charges +4, +1. A green ring marks the spot where the field vanishes. Is it closer to the big charge or the small one? Park the probe P on it and check. Turn on Field lines and tap Dipole ±3. You will be looking at the most important picture in electrostatics — more in Lesson 2. F_12 means “force on q_1 due to q_2 ”. The dashed arrows at P are the separate fields E_1 and E_2 drawn tip-to-tail; the solid green arrow is their sum.",["q_2","q_1 = +5 μ C","q_2 = −0.5 μ C","P","F_12","q_1","q_2","P","E_1","E_2"],""],[0,1,33,"sec","Superposition: forces simply add","With three or more charges, here is the best news in the chapter: the force between any two charges is unaffected by the presence of the others. So the net force on a charge is just the vector sum of the individual Coulomb forces,",[],""],[0,1,34,"text","Superposition: forces simply add","With three or more charges, here is the best news in the chapter: the force between any two charges is unaffected by the presence of the others. So the net force on a charge is just the vector sum of the individual Coulomb forces,",[],""],[0,1,35,"formula","Superposition: forces simply add","With three or more charges, here is the best news in the chapter: the force between any two charges is unaffected by the presence of the others. So the net force on a charge is just the vector sum of the individual Coulomb forces,",["F_(net on)q = F_1 + F_2 + F_3 + ⋯"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net on </span></sub><i>q</i> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span><sub>1</sub> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span><sub>2</sub> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span><sub>3</sub> + ⋯"],[0,1,36,"text","Superposition: forces simply add","A recipe that never fails:",[],""],[0,1,37,"text","Superposition: forces simply add","Draw the charge you care about and one arrow per other charge (attract → towards, repel → away). Find each magnitude from Coulomb’s law. Forget signs here; the arrows already carry them. Look for symmetry — components that cancel. JEE setters love symmetric layouts. Add what is left, component by component.",[],""],[0,1,38,"key","The classic: two hanging charged balls","Two identical balls (mass m , charge q) hang from one point on threads of length L . In equilibrium each thread makes an angle θ with the vertical. Three forces act on each ball — tension, weight, Coulomb repulsion — and they give tan θ = F_e/mg = (kq^2)/(mg x^2), x = 2L sin θ. A favourite twist: dip the whole thing in a liquid of density ρ_ℓ and dielectric constant K . The electric force becomes F_e/K and the effective weight becomes mg (1 − ρ_ℓ/ρ) . The angle is unchanged only if these shrink by the same factor: K = ρ/(ρ − ρ_ℓ) .",["m","q","L","θ","tan θ = F_e/mg = (kq^2)/(mg x^2), x = 2L sin θ.","ρ_ℓ","K","F_e/K","mg (1 − ρ_ℓ/ρ)","K = ρ/(ρ − ρ_ℓ)"],""],[0,1,38,"formula","The classic: two hanging charged balls",". A favourite twist: dip the whole thing in a liquid of density ρ_ℓ and dielectric constant K . The electric force becomes F_e/K and the effective weight becomes mg (1 − ρ_ℓ/ρ) . The angle is unchanged only if these shrink by the same factor: K = ρ/(ρ − ρ_ℓ) .",["tan θ = F_e/mg = (kq^2)/(mg x^2), x = 2L sin θ."],"tan <i>θ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>F</i><sub><i>e</i></sub></span><span><i>m</i><i>g</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>m</i><i>g</i> <i>x</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>, <i>x</i> = 2<i>L</i> sin <i>θ</i>."],[0,1,40,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1987 · Single correct","A charge q is placed at the centre of the line joining two equal charges Q . The system of the three charges will be in equilibrium if q is equal to −Q/2 · −Q/4 · +Q/4 · +Q/2",["q","Q","q","−Q/2","−Q/4","+Q/4","+Q/2"],""],[0,1,40,"sol","IIT-JEE 1987 · Single correct","Let the two Q ’s be a distance 2d apart, with q midway. The middle charge is pulled (or pushed) equally by both ends, so it is in equilibrium for any q . The real condition comes from an end charge. On the right-hand Q : from the other Q(kQ^2)/((2d)^2) + from qkQq/(d^2) = 0 ⟹ Q/4 + q = 0 ⟹ q = −Q/4. The sign makes sense: the two Q ’s repel, so the middle charge must attract each of them, and because it is twice as close it needs only a quarter of the charge. Answer (B). Worth knowing: this equilibrium is unstable. Nudge the middle charge towards one end and it gets pulled further. In fact no arrangement of static charges can hold a charge in stable equilibrium using electrostatic forces alone (Earnshaw’s theorem). The middle charge is automatically balanced. Demand zero net force on one of the end charges.",["Q","2d","q","q","Q","from the other Q(kQ^2)/((2d)^2) + from qkQq/(d^2) = 0 ⟹ Q/4 + q = 0 ⟹ q = −Q/4.","Q"],""],[0,1,41,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1986 · Think, then reveal","Two small balls having equal positive charge Q on each are suspended by two insulating strings of equal length L from a hook fixed to a stand. The whole set-up is taken in a satellite into space where there is no gravity (a state of weightlessness). Find (i) the angle between the two strings and (ii) the tension in each string. Decide on your answers before you reveal.",["Q","L"],""],[0,1,41,"sol","IIT-JEE 1986 · Think, then reveal","On Earth, weight pulls each ball down and keeps the strings at some angle less than 180^∘ . Remove gravity and the only forces left on a ball are the Coulomb repulsion and the string tension. Two forces can balance only if they lie along the same line — so each string must lie along the line joining the balls. (i) The strings point in opposite directions: the angle between them is 180^∘ . (ii) The balls are now 2L apart, and the tension must balance the repulsion: T = 1/(4πε_0) (Q^2)/((2L)^2) = (Q^2)/(16πε_0L^2).",["180^∘","180^∘","2L","T = 1/(4πε_0) (Q^2)/((2L)^2) = (Q^2)/(16πε_0L^2)."],""],[0,1,42,"recap","Recap","Charge is quantised (q = ne), conserved and invariant. Coulomb: F = k ∣q_1q_2∣/r^2 along the joining line; in a medium divide by K . Superposition: add the pairwise forces as vectors. Hunt for symmetry first. For equilibrium of a system, check every charge — the obviously balanced one is never the one that gives the equation.",["q = ne","F = k ∣q_1q_2∣/r^2","K"],""],[0,2,0,"lesson","The Electric Field","How does one charge “know” another is there? It doesn’t. It only feels the field at its own location — and that single idea makes every hard problem simpler.",[],""],[0,2,1,"sec","Space gets a personality","Coulomb’s law says charge A pushes charge B across empty space. But how does B know A exists? If A suddenly moves, does B feel it instantly?",[],""],[0,2,2,"text","Space gets a personality","Coulomb’s law says charge A pushes charge B across empty space. But how does B know A exists? If A suddenly moves, does B feel it instantly?",[],""],[0,2,3,"text","Space gets a personality","The modern answer: A does not act on B directly. A changes the space around itself — it sets up an electric field everywhere. B simply responds to the field at the spot where B happens to be sitting. Move A, and the news travels outward through the field at the speed of light.",[],""],[0,2,4,"text","Space gets a personality","To measure the field at a point, bring a tiny positive test charge q_0 there and see what force it feels:",["q_0"],""],[0,2,5,"formula","Space gets a personality","To measure the field at a point, bring a tiny positive test charge q_0 there and see what force it feels:",["E = F/q_0 ⟺ F = qE"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span></span><span><i>q</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⟺ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = <i>q</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span>"],[0,2,6,"text","Space gets a personality","The unit is N/C (identical to V/m , as we will see). A positive charge is pushed along E ; a negative charge is pushed against it.",["N/C","V/m","E"],""],[0,2,7,"intuition","A weather map for charges","Think of a wind map: an arrow at every point telling you which way — and how hard — the air would push a kite placed there. The field is the same idea. E at a point is the force that one coulomb of positive charge would feel there, whether or not anything is actually sitting at that point.",["E"],""],[0,2,8,"text","Space gets a personality","For a single point charge Q , divide Coulomb’s law by q_0 :",["Q","q_0"],""],[0,2,9,"formula","Space gets a personality","For a single point charge Q , divide Coulomb’s law by q_0 :",["E = 1/(4πε_0) Q/(r^2) r"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>r</i>̂</span>"],[0,2,10,"text","Space gets a personality","Radially outward from a positive charge, radially inward to a negative one.",[],""],[0,2,11,"sec","Superposition and null points","Fields add exactly the way forces do:",[],""],[0,2,12,"text","Superposition and null points","Fields add exactly the way forces do:",[],""],[0,2,13,"formula","Superposition and null points","Fields add exactly the way forces do:",["E_net = E_1 + E_2 + E_3 + ⋯"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span><sub>1</sub> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span><sub>2</sub> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span><sub>3</sub> + ⋯"],[0,2,14,"text","Superposition and null points","A beloved exam question: where is the net field zero? For two charges, the answer always lies on the line through them. You can locate the region without any algebra:",[],""],[0,2,15,"text","Superposition and null points","Like charges → the null point is between them, nearer the smaller charge. (Between them the two fields oppose each other; you need to stand close to the weak one for it to keep up.) Unlike charges → the null point is outside, beyond the smaller charge. (Between them both fields point the same way and can never cancel.) Equal and opposite → no null point at any finite distance.",[],""],[0,2,16,"text","Superposition and null points","If the charges have magnitudes q_1 < q_2 and are a distance d apart, the null point sits at a distance x from the smaller charge, where",["q_1 < q_2","d","x"],""],[0,2,17,"formula","Superposition and null points","If the charges have magnitudes q_1 < q_2 and are a distance d apart, the null point sits at a distance x from the smaller charge, where",["x = d/(√(q_2/q_1) + 1) (like, between) x = d/(√(q_2/q_1) − 1) (unlike, outside)"],"<i>x</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i></span><span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><i>q</i><sub>2</sub>/<i>q</i><sub>1</sub></span> + 1</span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">(like, between)</span> <i>x</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i></span><span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><i>q</i><sub>2</sub>/<i>q</i><sub>1</sub></span> − 1</span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">(unlike, outside)</span>"],[0,2,18,"text","Superposition and null points","Do not memorise these. Just write (kq_1)/(x^2) = (kq_2)/((d ∓ x)^2) and take a square root.",["(kq_1)/(x^2) = (kq_2)/((d ∓ x)^2)"],""],[0,2,19,"q","Find the null point · Single correct","A charge +4q sits at the origin and a charge −q at x = 3 m . The electric field is zero at x = 2 m · x = 1 m · x = 6 m · x = −3 m",["+4q","−q","x = 3 m","x = 2 m","x = 1 m","x = 6 m","x = −3 m"],""],[0,2,19,"sol","Find the null point · Single correct","The charges are unlike, so the null point is outside the pair, beyond the smaller charge — that is, somewhere at x > 3 . Let it be a distance s beyond −q : (k(4q))/((3 + s)^2) = kq/(s^2) ⟹ 2/(3 + s) = 1/s ⟹ s = 3 m . So the field vanishes at x = 6 m : (C). Option (A), x = 2 m , is where the null point would be if both charges were positive — the classic slip. Check both cases in the lab in Lesson 1. Unlike charges: the null point cannot be between them. Which side is the weaker charge on?",["x > 3","s","−q","(k(4q))/((3 + s)^2) = kq/(s^2) ⟹ 2/(3 + s) = 1/s ⟹ s = 3 m .","x = 6 m","x = 2 m"],""],[0,2,20,"sec","Field lines: the picture worth a thousand vectors","Faraday’s trick for seeing a field. The rules:",[],""],[0,2,21,"text","Field lines: the picture worth a thousand vectors","Faraday’s trick for seeing a field. The rules:",[],""],[0,2,22,"text","Field lines: the picture worth a thousand vectors","Lines start on positive charges and end on negative ones (or run off to infinity). The tangent to a line gives the direction of E there. Crowded lines mean a strong field; sparse lines, a weak one. The number of lines leaving or entering a charge is proportional to its magnitude. Two lines never cross — the field cannot point two ways at one place. Electrostatic field lines never form closed loops.",["E"],""],[0,2,23,"trap","A field line is not a trajectory","A charge released from rest starts moving along the field line through it — but as soon as the line curves, the charge’s inertia carries it off the line. Field lines show the direction of the force, not of the velocity.",[],""],[0,2,24,"text","Field lines: the picture worth a thousand vectors","When a problem shows a field-line sketch, read it like this: count the lines on each charge to compare magnitudes; look for a spot the lines avoid to locate a null point; and if some lines escape to infinity, the charges are not equal and opposite.",[],""],[0,2,25,"sec","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","Real objects carry charge smeared along a line, over a surface or through a volume:",[],""],[0,2,26,"text","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","Real objects carry charge smeared along a line, over a surface or through a volume:",[],""],[0,2,27,"formula","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","Real objects carry charge smeared along a line, over a surface or through a volume:",["dq = λ dℓ dq = σ dA dq = ρ dV"],"d<i>q</i> = <i>λ</i> dℓ d<i>q</i> = <i>σ</i> d<i>A</i> d<i>q</i> = <i>ρ</i> d<i>V</i>"],[0,2,28,"text","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","There is only one method, and it always works:",[],""],[0,2,29,"text","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","Chop the object into tiny pieces dq . Each piece is a point charge: dE = k dq/r^2 . Aim. Use symmetry to see which components cancel in pairs. Keep only the survivor. Add the surviving components with an integral.",["dq","dE = k dq/r^2"],""],[0,2,30,"text","Continuous charge: chop, aim, add","Step 2 is where the marks are. Never integrate dE itself — it is a vector whose direction changes from piece to piece.",["dE"],""],[0,2,31,"sec","The ring (on its axis)","A ring of radius R carries charge Q . At a point on the axis, a distance x from the centre:",[],""],[0,2,32,"text","The ring (on its axis)","A ring of radius R carries charge Q . At a point on the axis, a distance x from the centre:",["R","Q","x"],""],[0,2,33,"formula","The ring (on its axis)","A ring of radius R carries charge Q . At a point on the axis, a distance x from the centre:",["E = 1/(4πε_0) Qx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) along the axis"],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><i>x</i></span><span>(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">along the axis</span>"],[0,2,34,"text","The ring (on its axis)","DerivationField on the axis of a ring Every piece dq of the ring is the same distance √(x^2 + R^2) from the point, so each produces dE = kdq/(x^2 + R^2) . For each piece there is a partner diametrically opposite. Their components perpendicular to the axis cancel; their components along the axis add. The axial part of dE is dE cos θ with cos θ = x/(√(x^2 + R^2)) , and neither x nor R changes as we go round the ring, so E = ∫ kdq/(x^2 + R^2) ⋅ x/(√(x^2 + R^2)) = kx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) ∫ dq = kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)). Note that the result did not need the charge to be uniformly spread — but only the axial component is given by this formula if it is not.",["dq","√(x^2 + R^2)","dE = kdq/(x^2 + R^2)","dE","dE cos θ","cos θ = x/(√(x^2 + R^2))","x","R","E = ∫ kdq/(x^2 + R^2) ⋅ x/(√(x^2 + R^2)) = kx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) ∫ dq = kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2))."],""],[0,2,34,"formula","The ring (on its axis)","we go round the ring, so E = ∫ kdq/(x^2 + R^2) ⋅ x/(√(x^2 + R^2)) = kx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) ∫ dq = kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)). Note that the result did not need the charge to be uniformly spread — but only the axial component is given by this formula if it is not.",["E = ∫ kdq/(x^2 + R^2) ⋅ x/(√(x^2 + R^2)) = kx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) ∫ dq = kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2))."],"<i>E</i> = ∫ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i> d<i>q</i></span><span><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>x</i></span><span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>x</i></span><span>(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> ∫ d<i>q</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><i>x</i></span><span>(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>."],[0,2,36,"text","The ring (on its axis)","Read this formula like a physicist:",[],""],[0,2,37,"text","The ring (on its axis)","At the centre (x = 0): E = 0 . Every piece is cancelled by its opposite. Far away (x ≫ R): E → kQ/x^2 . From far enough, everything looks like a point charge. Always run this check on your answers. In between there must be a maximum. Setting dE/dx = 0 gives x = R/(√2) , where E_max = (2kQ)/(3√3 R^2) . Close to the centre (x ≪ R): E ≈ kQ/(R^3) x — proportional to displacement. A charge −q released near the centre feels a restoring force −kQq/(R^3)x and performs SHM with ω = √(kQq/(mR^3)) .",["x = 0","E = 0","x ≫ R","E → kQ/x^2","dE/dx = 0","x = R/(√2)","E_max = (2kQ)/(3√3 R^2)","x ≪ R","E ≈ kQ/(R^3) x","−q","−kQq/(R^3)x","ω = √(kQq/(mR^3))"],""],[0,2,38,"sec","The long straight wire","An infinitely long line with charge λ per unit length. At a perpendicular distance r :",[],""],[0,2,39,"text","The long straight wire","An infinitely long line with charge λ per unit length. At a perpendicular distance r :",["λ","r"],""],[0,2,40,"formula","The long straight wire","An infinitely long line with charge λ per unit length. At a perpendicular distance r :",["E = λ/(2πε_0 r) = (2kλ)/r radially away from the wire"],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>λ</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub> <i>r</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">radially away from the wire</span>"],[0,2,41,"text","The long straight wire","It falls only as 1/r — slower than a point charge — because as you step back, more and more of the wire comes “into view”.",["1/r"],""],[0,2,42,"text","The long straight wire","DerivationField of an infinite line by direct integration Take the point P at distance r from the wire. A piece of wire at height y subtends an angle θ at P , with y = r tan θ , so dy = r sec^2 θ dθ and its distance from P is r sec θ . dE = (kλ dy)/((r sec θ)^2) = kλ/r dθ. Components along the wire cancel between the pieces at +y and −y . The perpendicular component is dE cos θ : E = kλ/r ∫_(−π/2)^(π/2) cos θ dθ = (2kλ)/r. For a finite wire that subtends angles α and β on either side of the perpendicular, the same integral with new limits gives E_⊥ = kλ/r(sin α + sin β), E_∥ = kλ/r ∣cos α − cos β∣ .",["P","r","y","θ","P","y = r tan θ","dy = r sec^2 θ dθ","P","r sec θ","dE = (kλ dy)/((r sec θ)^2) = kλ/r dθ.","+y","−y"],""],[0,2,42,"formula","The long straight wire","erpendicular component is dE cos θ : E = kλ/r ∫_(−π/2)^(π/2) cos θ dθ = (2kλ)/r. For a finite wire that subtends angles α and β on either side of the perpendicular, the same integral with new limits gives E_⊥ = kλ/r(sin α + sin β), E_∥ = kλ/r ∣cos α − cos β∣ .",["dE = (kλ dy)/((r sec θ)^2) = kλ/r dθ."],"d<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>λ</i> d<i>y</i></span><span>(<i>r</i> sec <i>θ</i>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> d<i>θ</i>."],[0,2,42,"formula","The long straight wire","erpendicular component is dE cos θ : E = kλ/r ∫_(−π/2)^(π/2) cos θ dθ = (2kλ)/r. For a finite wire that subtends angles α and β on either side of the perpendicular, the same integral with new limits gives E_⊥ = kλ/r(sin α + sin β), E_∥ = kλ/r ∣cos α − cos β∣ .",["E = kλ/r ∫_(−π/2)^(π/2) cos θ dθ = (2kλ)/r."],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> ∫<sub>−<i>π</i>/2</sub><sup><i>π</i>/2</sup> cos <i>θ</i> d<i>θ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span>."],[0,2,42,"formula","The long straight wire","erpendicular component is dE cos θ : E = kλ/r ∫_(−π/2)^(π/2) cos θ dθ = (2kλ)/r. For a finite wire that subtends angles α and β on either side of the perpendicular, the same integral with new limits gives E_⊥ = kλ/r(sin α + sin β), E_∥ = kλ/r ∣cos α − cos β∣ .",["E_⊥ = kλ/r(sin α + sin β), E_∥ = kλ/r ∣cos α − cos β∣ ."],"<i>E</i><sub> ⊥ </sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span>(sin <i>α</i> + sin <i>β</i>), <i>E</i><sub> ∥ </sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>λ</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> ∣cos <i>α</i> − cos <i>β</i>∣ ."],[0,2,46,"sec","Results worth knowing cold","Let R → ∞ in the disc formula and the infinite-sheet result drops out. The sheet’s field does not weaken with distance at all — a fact we will re-derive in two lines with Gauss’s law.",[],""],[0,2,47,"table","Results worth knowing cold","Charge distribution Field Ring, on axis at distance x kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) Infinite line, at distance r (2kλ)/r Semi-infinite line, at distance r from its end (perpendicular to it) E_⊥ = E_∥ = kλ/r , so (√2 kλ)/r at 45^∘ Arc of radius R subtending angle ϕ , at its centre (2kλ)/R sin ϕ/2 Uniform disc (radius R), on axis at distance x σ/(2ε_0) (1 − x/(√(x^2 + R^2))) Infinite sheet σ/(2ε_0) , independent of distance",["x","kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2))","r","(2kλ)/r","r","E_⊥ = E_∥ = kλ/r","(√2 kλ)/r","45^∘","R","ϕ","(2kλ)/R sin ϕ/2","R"],""],[0,2,48,"text","Results worth knowing cold","Let R → ∞ in the disc formula and the infinite-sheet result drops out. The sheet’s field does not weaken with distance at all — a fact we will re-derive in two lines with Gauss’s law.",["R → ∞"],""],[0,2,49,"q","The ring’s sweet spot · Single correct","A uniformly charged ring has radius R . The electric field on its axis is zero at the centre and fades to zero far away. At what distance from the centre is it greatest? R/2 · R/√2 · R · √2 R",["R","R/2","R/√2","R","√2 R"],""],[0,2,49,"sol","The ring’s sweet spot · Single correct","E(x) ∝ x (x^2 + R^2)^(−3/2) . Differentiate and set to zero: (x^2 + R^2)^(−3/2) − 3x^2(x^2 + R^2)^(−5/2) = 0 ⟹ x^2 + R^2 = 3x^2 ⟹ x = R/(√2).",["E(x) ∝ x (x^2 + R^2)^(−3/2)","(x^2 + R^2)^(−3/2) − 3x^2(x^2 + R^2)^(−5/2) = 0 ⟹ x^2 + R^2 = 3x^2 ⟹ x = R/(√2)."],""],[0,2,50,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","Two infinitely long static line charges of constant positive line charge density λ are kept parallel to each other. In their resulting electric field, a point charge is kept in equilibrium midway between them and is confined to move in the x -direction only (perpendicular to both lines, in their plane). Two situations are considered: in one the point charge is +q , in the other it is −q . If each is given a small displacement about its equilibrium position, then both charges execute simple harmonic motion · both charges continue moving in the direction of their displacement · charge +q executes simple harmonic motion while charge −q continues moving in the direction of its displacement · charge −q executes simple harmonic motion while charge +q continues moving in the direction of its displacement",["λ","x","+q","−q","+q","−q","−q","+q"],""],[0,2,50,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","Put the wires at x = −d and x = +d . At a point x between them, the left wire pushes a positive test charge to the right and the right wire pushes it to the left: E_x = (2kλ)/(d + x) − (2kλ)/(d − x) = −(4kλ x)/(d^2 − x^2) ≈ −(4kλ)/(d^2) x (x ≪ d). So the field always points back towards the midpoint — it gets stronger on the side you move towards, because you are closer to that wire. For +q : F = qE_x = − ((4kλq)/(d^2)) x . A restoring force proportional to displacement — SHM, with ω^2 = (4kλq)/(md^2) . For −q : the force is reversed, F = + ((4kλq)/(d^2)) x . It is pushed further in the direction it was displaced and keeps going. Answer (C). The midpoint is a stable equilibrium for +q only because the charge is confined to the x -axis; let it move along the wires too and Earnshaw’s theorem takes its revenge. Find the net field at a small distance x from the midpoint. Which way does it point?",["x = −d","x = +d","x","E_x = (2kλ)/(d + x) − (2kλ)/(d − x) = −(4kλ x)/(d^2 − x^2) ≈ −(4kλ)/(d^2) x (x ≪ d).","+q","F = qE_x = − ((4kλq)/(d^2)) x","ω^2 = (4kλq)/(md^2)","−q","F = + ((4kλq)/(d^2)) x","+q","x","x"],""],[0,2,51,"sec","A charge in a uniform field","Between two large, oppositely charged plates the field is the same everywhere. A charge q of mass m in a uniform field has a constant acceleration",[],""],[0,2,52,"text","A charge in a uniform field","Between two large, oppositely charged plates the field is the same everywhere. A charge q of mass m in a uniform field has a constant acceleration",["q","m"],""],[0,2,53,"formula","A charge in a uniform field","Between two large, oppositely charged plates the field is the same everywhere. A charge q of mass m in a uniform field has a constant acceleration",["a = qE/m"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>a</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>"],[0,2,54,"text","A charge in a uniform field","and constant acceleration means you already know everything: this is projectile motion with g replaced by qE/m . A particle entering at speed v at right angles to the field follows a parabola, y = qE/(2mv^2) x^2 .",["g","qE/m","v","y = qE/(2mv^2) x^2"],""],[0,2,55,"text","A charge in a uniform field","When gravity matters as well, just add the two constant forces. If a particle released from rest moves along a line at angle θ to the vertical, then tan θ = qE/mg — it moves straight along the net force.",["θ","tan θ = qE/mg"],""],[0,2,56,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","Two large vertical and parallel metal plates having a separation of 1 cm are connected to a DC voltage source of potential difference X . A proton is released at rest midway between the two plates. It is found to move at 45^∘ to the vertical just after release. Then X is nearly 1 × 10^(−5) V · 1 × 10^(−7) V · 1 × 10^(−9) V · 1 × 10^(−10) V",["1 cm","X","45^∘","X","1 × 10^(−5) V","1 × 10^(−7) V","1 × 10^(−9) V","1 × 10^(−10) V"],""],[0,2,56,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","Two constant forces act on the proton: gravity mg (vertical) and the electric force eE (horizontal, since the plates are vertical). Starting from rest, it moves along the resultant. A 45^∘ path means the two forces are equal: eE = mg ⟹ E = mg/e = ((1.67 × 10^(−27))(9.8))/(1.6 × 10^(−19)) ≈ 1.0 × 10^(−7) V/m . For a uniform field between plates, X = Ed = (1.0 × 10^(−7))(10^(−2)) ≈ 1 × 10^(−9) V . Answer (C). Notice how tiny this is: a billionth of a volt is enough to match gravity on a proton. In almost every problem involving electrons or protons in a field, gravity is completely negligible. Released from rest, it moves along the net force. What does 45^∘ tell you about the two forces?",["mg","eE","45^∘","eE = mg ⟹ E = mg/e = ((1.67 × 10^(−27))(9.8))/(1.6 × 10^(−19)) ≈ 1.0 × 10^(−7) V/m .","X = Ed = (1.0 × 10^(−7))(10^(−2)) ≈ 1 × 10^(−9) V","45^∘"],""],[0,2,57,"recap","Recap","E = F/q_0 . Point charge: kQ/r^2 , outward from + , inward to − . Null point: between like charges, beyond the smaller of two unlike charges. Continuous charge: chop → aim (symmetry) → add. Always test your result at x = 0 and x → ∞ . Ring: kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) , maximum at R/√2 . Line: (2kλ)/r . Sheet: σ/(2ε_0) . Uniform field → constant acceleration → projectile methods.",["E = F/q_0","kQ/r^2","+","−","x = 0","x → ∞","kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2))","R/√2","(2kλ)/r","σ/(2ε_0)"],""],[0,3,0,"lesson","Flux & Gauss’s Law","Stop adding up vectors. Start counting field lines. This is the idea JEE Advanced tests more creatively than any other in the chapter.",[],""],[0,3,1,"sec","Flux: how much field pokes through","Hold a window frame out in the rain. How much rain passes through it depends on three things: how hard it is raining, how big the frame is, and how you tilt it. Face-on catches the most; edge-on catches nothing.",[],""],[0,3,2,"text","Flux: how much field pokes through","Hold a window frame out in the rain. How much rain passes through it depends on three things: how hard it is raining, how big the frame is, and how you tilt it. Face-on catches the most; edge-on catches nothing.",[],""],[0,3,3,"text","Flux: how much field pokes through","Electric flux is the same idea with field lines instead of raindrops. For a flat surface in a uniform field,",[],""],[0,3,4,"formula","Flux: how much field pokes through","Electric flux is the same idea with field lines instead of raindrops. For a flat surface in a uniform field,",["Φ = E ⋅ A = EA cos θ"],"Φ = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span> = <i>E</i><i>A</i> cos <i>θ</i>"],[0,3,5,"text","Flux: how much field pokes through","where A is the area vector: its length is the area, its direction is perpendicular to the surface, and θ is the angle between E and that perpendicular. When the field or the tilt changes from place to place, chop the surface into tiny tiles and add:",["A","θ","E"],""],[0,3,6,"formula","Flux: how much field pokes through","where A is the area vector: its length is the area, its direction is perpendicular to the surface, and θ is the angle between E and that perpendicular. When the field or the tilt changes from place to place, chop the surface into tiny tiles and add:",["Φ = ∫ E ⋅ dA"],"Φ = ∫ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> ⋅ d<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span>"],[0,3,7,"text","Flux: how much field pokes through","For a closed surface the area vector always points outward. So field leaving the surface counts as positive flux, field entering counts as negative.",[],""],[0,3,8,"intuition","Flux only sees the shadow","EA cos θ = E × (A cos θ) , and A cos θ is simply the area of the surface’s shadow on a plane perpendicular to the field. A tilted, crumpled or curved surface in a uniform field has exactly the flux of its flat shadow. This one sentence solves the next problem in a single line.",["EA cos θ = E × (A cos θ)","A cos θ"],""],[0,3,9,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","Consider an electric field E = E_0 x , where E_0 is a constant. The flux through the shaded rectangular area, whose corners are (0, 0, 0) , (a, 0, a) , (a, a, a) and (0, a, 0) , due to this field is 2E_0a^2 · √2 E_0a^2 · E_0a^2 · (E_0a^2)/(√2)",["E = E_0 x","E_0","(0, 0, 0)","(a, 0, a)","(a, a, a)","(0, a, 0)","2E_0a^2","√2 E_0a^2","E_0a^2","(E_0a^2)/(√2)"],""],[0,3,9,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","Shadow method. The field is along x , so we need the projection of the rectangle onto the yz -plane. Dropping the x -coordinate of each corner gives (0, 0) , (0, a) , (a, a) , (a, 0) in the (y, z) plane — a square of side a . So Φ = E_0 × a^2. Vector check. Two adjacent edges of the rectangle are u = (a, 0, a) and v = (0, a, 0) . The area vector is u × v = (−a^2, 0, a^2) , whose x -component has magnitude a^2 . Hence E ⋅ A = E_0a^2 . Answer (C). The rectangle’s true area is √2 a^2 — that is trap (B). It is tilted at 45^∘ to the field, and √2 a^2 cos 45^∘ = a^2 . What is the shadow of the shaded rectangle on a plane perpendicular to the x -axis?",["x","yz","x","(0, 0)","(0, a)","(a, a)","(a, 0)","(y, z)","a","Φ = E_0 × a^2.","u = (a, 0, a)","v = (0, a, 0)"],""],[0,3,10,"sec","Gauss’s law","Surround any collection of charges with any closed surface you like — a sphere, a cube, a potato. Then",[],""],[0,3,11,"text","Gauss’s law","Surround any collection of charges with any closed surface you like — a sphere, a cube, a potato. Then",[],""],[0,3,12,"formula","Gauss’s law","Surround any collection of charges with any closed surface you like — a sphere, a cube, a potato. Then",["∮ E ⋅ dA = (q_(enclosed))/ε_0"],"∮ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> ⋅ d<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">enclosed</span></sub></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span>"],[0,3,13,"text","Gauss’s law","The total flux out of the surface depends on one thing only: the net charge inside.",[],""],[0,3,14,"intuition","Why it has to be true","A charge q is the source of a fixed number of field lines (proportional to q). If it is inside your closed surface, every one of those lines must pierce the surface on its way out — whatever the shape or size. If it is outside, each of its lines that enters the surface must also leave it: −1 going in, +1 coming out, net zero. So only enclosed charge survives the count. For one charge at the centre of a sphere you can check it directly: E ⋅ 4πr^2 = kq/(r^2) ⋅ 4πr^2 = q/ε_0 . The r^2 cancels — which is the inverse-square law doing its job.",["q","q","−1","+1","E ⋅ 4πr^2 = kq/(r^2) ⋅ 4πr^2 = q/ε_0","r^2"],""],[0,3,15,"text","Gauss’s law","Gauss’s law is short, and nearly every word in it is a place where marks are lost:",[],""],[0,3,16,"trap","Four things Gauss’s law does not say","E in the integral is the total field, produced by all charges — inside and outside. Outside charges change E at every point of the surface; they just contribute zero to the net flux. Zero flux does not mean zero field. A closed surface in a uniform field has Φ = 0 with E  = 0 everywhere. Zero enclosed charge does not mean no charges inside — only that they add up to zero (a dipole, for instance). The flux does not depend on where inside the surface the charge sits, nor on the surface’s shape. The field on the surface certainly does.",["E","E","Φ = 0","E  = 0"],""],[0,3,17,"sec","Flux without integration: three symmetry tricks","Most JEE flux questions are solved by sharing, not by integrating.",[],""],[0,3,18,"text","Flux without integration: three symmetry tricks","Most JEE flux questions are solved by sharing, not by integrating.",[],""],[0,3,19,"sec","1. Share by symmetry","A charge q at the centre of a cube: total flux q/ε_0 , and all six faces are equivalent, so each gets q/(6ε_0) .",[],""],[0,3,20,"text","1. Share by symmetry","A charge q at the centre of a cube: total flux q/ε_0 , and all six faces are equivalent, so each gets q/(6ε_0) .",["q","q/ε_0","q/(6ε_0)"],""],[0,3,21,"text","1. Share by symmetry","A charge at a corner of a cube: imagine seven more cubes stacked around that corner to make a big cube with the charge at its centre. The charge is shared by 8 cubes, so the flux through ours is q/(8ε_0) . The same game gives q/(2ε_0) for a charge at the centre of a face and q/(4ε_0) for one at the middle of an edge.",["q/(8ε_0)","q/(2ε_0)","q/(4ε_0)"],""],[0,3,22,"sec","2. Solid angle","A point charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions — q/ε_0 spread over the full solid angle 4π . So the flux through any surface that subtends a solid angle Ω at the charge is",[],""],[0,3,23,"text","2. Solid angle","A point charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions — q/ε_0 spread over the full solid angle 4π . So the flux through any surface that subtends a solid angle Ω at the charge is",["q/ε_0","4π","Ω"],""],[0,3,24,"formula","2. Solid angle","A point charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions — q/ε_0 spread over the full solid angle 4π . So the flux through any surface that subtends a solid angle Ω at the charge is",["Φ = q/ε_0 ⋅ Ω/4π"],"Φ = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Ω</span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span>"],[0,3,25,"text","2. Solid angle","The case you need most: a disc viewed from a point on its axis, where its rim makes a half-angle α with the axis. A cone of half-angle α has Ω = 2π(1 − cos α) , so",["α","α","Ω = 2π(1 − cos α)"],""],[0,3,26,"formula","2. Solid angle","The case you need most: a disc viewed from a point on its axis, where its rim makes a half-angle α with the axis. A cone of half-angle α has Ω = 2π(1 − cos α) , so",["Φ_(disc) = q/(2ε_0) (1 − cos α)"],"Φ<sub><span class=\"fx-t\">disc</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> (1 − cos <i>α</i>)"],[0,3,27,"sec","3. Plane angle (for line charges)","An infinite line charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions around the wire — λL/ε_0 per length L , spread over the full angle 2π . A long strip parallel to the wire that subtends an angle ϕ at the wire therefore catches",[],""],[0,3,28,"text","3. Plane angle (for line charges)","An infinite line charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions around the wire — λL/ε_0 per length L , spread over the full angle 2π . A long strip parallel to the wire that subtends an angle ϕ at the wire therefore catches",["λL/ε_0","L","2π","ϕ"],""],[0,3,29,"formula","3. Plane angle (for line charges)","An infinite line charge sends its flux out uniformly in all directions around the wire — λL/ε_0 per length L , spread over the full angle 2π . A long strip parallel to the wire that subtends an angle ϕ at the wire therefore catches",["Φ = λL/ε_0 ⋅ ϕ/2π"],"Φ = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>λ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ϕ</i></span><span>2<i>π</i></span></span>"],[0,3,30,"q","Charge at a corner · Single correct","A point charge q is placed at one corner of a cube. What is the electric flux through one of the three faces that do not touch that corner? q/(6ε_0) · q/(8ε_0) · q/(24ε_0) · zero",["q","q/(6ε_0)","q/(8ε_0)","q/(24ε_0)"],""],[0,3,30,"sol","Charge at a corner · Single correct","Total flux through the cube is q/(8ε_0) (the charge is shared by 8 such cubes). The three faces that meet at the corner contain the charge in their own plane. The field of a point charge is radial, so it runs along those faces, never through them: zero flux. Everything therefore leaves through the other three faces, which are equivalent by symmetry: Φ_(one face) = 1/3 ⋅ q/(8ε_0) = q/(24ε_0). First find the total flux through this cube. Then ask: can any field line pass through a face that contains the charge?",["q/(8ε_0)","Φ_(one face) = 1/3 ⋅ q/(8ε_0) = q/(24ε_0)."],""],[0,3,31,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · One or more correct","A cubical region of side a has its centre at the origin. It encloses three fixed point charges: −q at (0, −a/4, 0) , +3q at (0, 0, 0) and −q at (0, +a/4, 0) . Choose the correct option(s). The net electric flux crossing the plane x = +a/2 is equal to the net electric flux crossing the plane x = −a/2 . · The net electric flux crossing the plane y = +a/2 is more than the net electric flux crossing the plane y = −a/2 . · The net electric flux crossing the entire region is q/ε_0 . · The net electric flux crossing the plane z = +a/2 is equal to the net electric flux crossing the plane x = +a/2 .",["a","−q","(0, −a/4, 0)","+3q","(0, 0, 0)","−q","(0, +a/4, 0)","x = +a/2","x = −a/2","y = +a/2","y = −a/2","q/ε_0"],""],[0,3,31,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · One or more correct","All three charges lie on the y -axis, symmetric about the origin. Use symmetry face by face. (A) True. Reflecting x → −x leaves every charge where it is, so the faces x = ±a/2 are mirror images with identical flux. (B) False. Reflecting y → −y swaps the two −q charges — the arrangement is unchanged. So the faces y = ±a/2 also carry equal flux. (C) True. Gauss’s law: q_enc = −q + 3q − q = +q , so the total flux is q/ε_0 . (D) True. Rotating the whole picture by 90^∘ about the y -axis (on which all the charges sit) carries the face x = +a/2 onto z = +a/2 without moving any charge. Answer (A), (C), (D). Note that the y -faces do not carry the same flux as the x - and z -faces — the charges are strung out along y — and the question never asks you for those numbers. Do not calculate any flux through a face. Ask which faces the charge arrangement cannot tell apart.",["y","x → −x","x = ±a/2","y → −y","−q","y = ±a/2","q_enc = −q + 3q − q = +q","q/ε_0","90^∘","y","x = +a/2","z = +a/2"],""],[0,3,32,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · Integer answer","An infinitely long uniform line charge distribution of charge per unit length λ lies parallel to the y -axis in the y – z plane at z = (√3)/2a . A rectangular surface ABCD lies in the x – y plane with its centre at the origin; its sides are a (along x) and L (along y). If the magnitude of the flux of the electric field through this rectangle is λL/(nε_0) , find n .",["λ","y","y","z","z = (√3)/2a","ABCD","x","y","a","x","L","y"],""],[0,3,32,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · Integer answer","Look along the y -axis. The wire is a dot at height (√3)/2a directly above the middle of a segment of width a . End-on view: the strip subtends 60^∘ at the wire. Half the strip, a/2 , at a perpendicular distance (√3)/2a : tan θ = (a/2)/(√3a/2) = 1/(√3) ⟹ θ = 30^∘. The full strip subtends 2θ = 60^∘ at the wire. A length L of wire emits flux λL/ε_0 uniformly over 360^∘ , so the strip catches Φ = (60^∘)/(360^∘) ⋅ λL/ε_0 = λL/(6ε_0) ⟹ n = 6. Look along the wire, so that it becomes a dot and the rectangle becomes a line segment of length a . What angle does the segment subtend at the dot?",["y","(√3)/2a","a","60^∘","a/2","(√3)/2a","tan θ = (a/2)/(√3a/2) = 1/(√3) ⟹ θ = 30^∘.","2θ = 60^∘","L","λL/ε_0","360^∘","Φ = (60^∘)/(360^∘) ⋅ λL/ε_0 = λL/(6ε_0) ⟹ n = 6."],""],[0,3,33,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2017 · One or more correct","A point charge +Q is placed just outside an imaginary hemispherical surface of radius R , at the top of its dome, as shown. Which of the following statements is/are correct? The electric flux passing through the curved surface of the hemisphere is −Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2)) . · The component of the electric field normal to the flat surface is constant over the surface. · Total flux through the curved and the flat surfaces is Q/ε_0 . · The circumference of the flat surface is an equipotential.",["+Q","R","−Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2))","Q/ε_0"],""],[0,3,33,"sol","JEE Advanced 2017 · One or more correct","The closed surface encloses nothing. The charge is just outside, so by Gauss’s law the total flux through dome plus flat face is zero — (C) is false — and Φ_(curved) = −Φ_(flat). Flux through the flat face. It is a disc of radius R whose centre is a distance R from the charge, so its rim makes a half-angle α = 45^∘ at the charge. From the disc formula, Φ_(flat) = Q/(2ε_0) (1 − cos 45^∘) = Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2)) , positive because these lines are leaving the enclosed region. Hence Φ_(curved) = −Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2)) : (A) is true. (B) False. At a point of the flat face whose direction from the charge makes an angle θ with the axis, the distance is R/ cos θ and the normal component is (kQ cos^2 θ)/(R^2) ⋅ cos θ ∝ cos^3 θ . It falls from the centre to the rim. (D) True. Every point of the rim is at the same distance √2R from the charge, so they share the potential kQ/(√2R) . Answer (A), (D). The charge is outside the closed surface (dome + flat face). So what must the two fluxes add up to?",["Φ_(curved) = −Φ_(flat).","R","R","α = 45^∘","Φ_(flat) = Q/(2ε_0) (1 − cos 45^∘) = Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2)) ,","Φ_(curved) = −Q/(2ε_0) (1 − 1/(√2))","θ","R/ cos θ","(kQ cos^2 θ)/(R^2) ⋅ cos θ ∝ cos^3 θ","√2R","kQ/(√2R)"],""],[0,3,34,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","A charged shell of radius R carries a total charge Q . Let Φ be the flux of the electric field through a closed cylindrical surface of height h and radius r , whose centre coincides with the centre of the shell. (The centre of the cylinder is the point on its axis equidistant from its top and bottom faces.) Which of the following option(s) is/are correct? If h > 2R and r > R , then Φ = Q/ε_0 · If h < (8R)/5 and r = (3R)/5 , then Φ = 0 · If h > 2R and r = (3R)/5 , then Φ = Q/(5ε_0) · If h > 2R and r = (4R)/5 , then Φ = Q/(5ε_0)",["R","Q","Φ","h","r","h > 2R","r > R","Φ = Q/ε_0","h < (8R)/5","r = (3R)/5","Φ = 0","h > 2R"],""],[0,3,34,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","By Gauss’s law we only need the charge on the part of the shell that lies inside the cylinder. A tall cylinder of radius 3R/5 cuts two polar caps out of the shell. A cap’s share of the charge. A cap of the sphere with polar half-angle θ has area 2πR^2(1 − cos θ) , i.e. a fraction (1 − cos θ)/2 of the shell. A cylinder of radius r meets the shell where sin θ = r/R , at height R cos θ above the centre. (A) r > R , h > 2R : the cylinder swallows the whole shell. Φ = Q/ε_0 . True. (B) r = (3R)/5 gives sin θ = 3/5 , cos θ = 4/5 : the cylinder’s wall meets the shell at height (4R)/5 . If the half-height h/2 < (4R)/5 — that is, h < (8R)/5 — the cylinder ends before it ever reaches the shell. It lies entirely inside, encloses no charge, and Φ = 0 . True. (C) Same radius but h > 2R : now both caps are inside. Charge enclosed = 2 × 21 − 4/5 Q = Q/5 , so Φ = Q/(5ε_0) . True. (D) r = (4R)/5 gives cos θ = 3/5 . Enclosed charge = 2 × 21 − 3/5 Q = (2Q)/5 , so Φ = (2Q)/(5ε_0) , not Q/(5ε_0) . False. Answer (A), (B), (C). The flux is Q_(enclosed)/ε_0 , so the whole question is geometry: how much of the shell’s surface lies inside the cylinder?",["3R/5","θ","2πR^2(1 − cos θ)","(1 − cos θ)/2","r","sin θ = r/R","R cos θ","r > R","h > 2R","Φ = Q/ε_0","r = (3R)/5","sin θ = 3/5"],""],[0,3,35,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","A circular disc of radius R carries surface charge density σ(r) = σ_0 (1 − r/R) , where σ_0 is a constant and r is the distance from the centre of the disc. The electric flux through a large spherical surface that encloses the charged disc completely is ϕ_0 . The electric flux through another spherical surface of radius R/4 , concentric with the disc, is ϕ . Find the ratio ϕ_0/ϕ .",["R","σ(r) = σ_0 (1 − r/R)","σ_0","r","ϕ_0","R/4","ϕ","ϕ_0/ϕ"],""],[0,3,35,"sol","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","Both fluxes are just (enclosed charge) /ε_0 , so ϕ_0/ϕ = (Q_(whole disc))/(Q_(r < R/4)) . The charge on the disc out to radius s (rings of area 2πr dr): Q(s) = ∫_0^s σ_0 (1 − r/R) 2πr dr = 2πσ_0 ((s^2)/2 − (s^3)/(3R)) . Whole disc, s = R : Q = 2πσ_0R^2 (1/2 − 1/3) = (πσ_0R^2)/3 . Inner part, s = R/4 : Q = 2πσ_0R^2 (1/32 − 1/192) = (5πσ_0R^2)/96 . ϕ_0/ϕ = (1/3)/(5/96) = 96/15 = 6.40. Flux ratio = enclosed-charge ratio. The small sphere encloses only the part of the disc with r < R/4 .",["/ε_0","ϕ_0/ϕ = (Q_(whole disc))/(Q_(r < R/4))","s","2πr dr","Q(s) = ∫_0^s σ_0 (1 − r/R) 2πr dr = 2πσ_0 ((s^2)/2 − (s^3)/(3R)) .","s = R","Q = 2πσ_0R^2 (1/2 − 1/3) = (πσ_0R^2)/3","s = R/4","Q = 2πσ_0R^2 (1/32 − 1/192) = (5πσ_0R^2)/96","ϕ_0/ϕ = (1/3)/(5/96) = 96/15 = 6.40.","r < R/4"],""],[0,3,36,"recap","Recap","Φ = ∫ E ⋅ dA ; in a uniform field, flux = E× (shadow area). Gauss: ∮ E ⋅ dA = q_enc/ε_0 . Field is due to all charges; net flux only to those inside. Share flux by symmetry (cube: q/(6ε_0) per face; corner: q/(8ε_0)), by solid angle (disc: q/(2ε_0)(1 − cos α)), or by plane angle for line charges. A charge outside a closed surface: flux in = flux out. Use it to trade a hard surface for an easy one.",["Φ = ∫ E ⋅ dA","= E×","∮ E ⋅ dA = q_enc/ε_0","q/(6ε_0)","q/(8ε_0)","q/(2ε_0)(1 − cos α)","="],""],[0,4,0,"lesson","Gauss’s Law at Work","Spheres, wires, sheets and cavities. With the right symmetry, an integral that would fill a page collapses into one line of algebra.",[],""],[0,4,1,"sec","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","Gauss’s law is always true, but it is only useful for finding E when symmetry lets you pull E out of the integral. That needs a closed surface on which, piece by piece, the field is either",[],""],[0,4,2,"text","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","Gauss’s law is always true, but it is only useful for finding E when symmetry lets you pull E out of the integral. That needs a closed surface on which, piece by piece, the field is either",["E","E"],""],[0,4,3,"text","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","perpendicular to the surface and constant in size (so ∫ E ⋅ dA = EA), or parallel to the surface (so that piece contributes nothing).",["∫ E ⋅ dA = EA"],""],[0,4,4,"text","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","Only three symmetries deliver this, and each has its own “Gaussian surface”:",[],""],[0,4,5,"table","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","Symmetry Charge looks the same if you… Gaussian surface E depends on Spherical rotate about a point concentric sphere r from centre Cylindrical slide along / rotate about an axis coaxial cylinder r from axis Planar slide along a plane pillbox through the plane distance from plane",["E","r","r"],""],[0,4,6,"text","When can Gauss actually hand you the field?","Then the whole method is: (1) spot the symmetry, (2) draw the surface through the point you care about, (3) write flux = E × area , (4) count the enclosed charge, (5) divide.",["= E × area"],""],[0,4,7,"trap","No symmetry, no shortcut","A finite rod, a ring, a disc, a cube of charge: Gauss’s law holds for all of them, but you cannot extract E from it. Use integration (Lesson 2) or superposition of known results.",["E"],""],[0,4,8,"sec","Spheres","A Gaussian sphere of radius r gives E ⋅ 4πr^2 = q_enc/ε_0 .",[],""],[0,4,9,"sec","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R)","A Gaussian sphere of radius r gives E ⋅ 4πr^2 = q_enc/ε_0 .",[],""],[0,4,10,"text","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R)","A Gaussian sphere of radius r gives E ⋅ 4πr^2 = q_enc/ε_0 .",["r","E ⋅ 4πr^2 = q_enc/ε_0"],""],[0,4,11,"formula","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R)","A Gaussian sphere of radius r gives E ⋅ 4πr^2 = q_enc/ε_0 .",["E = ⎩⎨⎧0kQ/(r^2)r < R (nothing enclosed) r > R (as if all of Q sat at the centre)"],"<i>E</i> = ⎩⎨⎧0<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span><i>r</i> &lt; <i>R</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">(nothing enclosed)</span><i>r</i> &gt; <i>R</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">(as if all of </span><i>Q</i><span class=\"fx-t\"> sat at the centre)</span>"],[0,4,12,"text","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R)","Inside a uniformly charged shell there is no field at all. Not “small” — zero, everywhere inside.",[],""],[0,4,13,"sec","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","The field grows linearly from zero at the centre, peaks at the surface with kQ/R^2 , then decays as 1/r^2 .",[],""],[0,4,14,"formula","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","Inside a uniformly charged shell there is no field at all. Not “small” — zero, everywhere inside.",["E = ⎩⎨⎧kQr/(R^3) = ρr/(3ε_0)kQ/(r^2)r ≤ Rr ≥ R"],"<i>E</i> = ⎩⎨⎧<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i> <i>r</i></span><span><i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>r</i></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span><i>r</i> ≤ <i>R</i><i>r</i> ≥ <i>R</i>"],[0,4,15,"text","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","DerivationInside a uniformly charged solid sphere A Gaussian sphere of radius r < R encloses only the charge within it: q_enc = ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 = Q (r^3)/(R^3). By symmetry E is radial and the same all over this sphere, so E ⋅ 4πr^2 = ε_0ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 ⟹ E = ρr/(3ε_0) = kQr/(R^3). The charge outside radius r forms nested shells around the point, and a shell produces no field inside itself — so it contributes nothing.",["r < R","q_enc = ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 = Q (r^3)/(R^3).","E","E ⋅ 4πr^2 = ε_0ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 ⟹ E = ρr/(3ε_0) = kQr/(R^3).","r"],""],[0,4,15,"formula","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","3). By symmetry E is radial and the same all over this sphere, so E ⋅ 4πr^2 = ε_0ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 ⟹ E = ρr/(3ε_0) = kQr/(R^3). The charge outside radius r forms nested shells around the point, and a shell produces no field inside itself — so it contributes nothing.",["q_enc = ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 = Q (r^3)/(R^3)."],"<i>q</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">enc</span></sub> = <i>ρ</i> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>4</span><span>3</span></span><i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>3</sup> = <i>Q</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span><span><i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>."],[0,4,15,"formula","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","3). By symmetry E is radial and the same all over this sphere, so E ⋅ 4πr^2 = ε_0ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 ⟹ E = ρr/(3ε_0) = kQr/(R^3). The charge outside radius r forms nested shells around the point, and a shell produces no field inside itself — so it contributes nothing.",["E ⋅ 4πr^2 = ε_0ρ ⋅ 4/3πr^3 ⟹ E = ρr/(3ε_0) = kQr/(R^3)."],"<i>E</i> ⋅ 4<i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>2</sup> = <i>ε</i><sub>0</sub><i>ρ</i> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>4</span><span>3</span></span><i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>3</sup> ⟹ <i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>r</i></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i> <i>r</i></span><span><i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>."],[0,4,18,"text","Solid sphere (uniform density ρ , total charge Q)","The field grows linearly from zero at the centre, peaks at the surface with kQ/R^2 , then decays as 1/r^2 .",["kQ/R^2","1/r^2"],""],[0,4,19,"key","The vector form that unlocks cavity problems","Inside a uniform sphere of charge, measured from its centre, E = ρ/(3ε_0) r. It points straight out from the centre and is proportional to the position vector. Keep this one within reach — the last section of this lesson is built on it.",["E = ρ/(3ε_0) r."],""],[0,4,19,"formula","The vector form that unlocks cavity problems","Inside a uniform sphere of charge, measured from its centre, E = ρ/(3ε_0) r. It points straight out from the centre and is proportional to the position vector. Keep this one within reach — the last section of this lesson is built on it.",["E = ρ/(3ε_0) r."],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span>."],[0,4,21,"q","Inside versus outside · Single correct","A solid sphere of radius R is uniformly charged. The field at a distance R/2 from its centre is E_0 . What is the field at a distance 2R from the centre? E_0/4 · E_0/2 · E_0 · 2E_0",["R","R/2","E_0","2R","E_0/4","E_0/2","E_0","2E_0"],""],[0,4,21,"sol","Inside versus outside · Single correct","Inside: E_0 = kQ/(R^3) ⋅ R/2 = kQ/(2R^2) . Outside: E(2R) = kQ/((2R)^2) = kQ/(4R^2) = E_0/2 . The two points are not related by a simple inverse-square rule, because one of them is inside the charge.",["E_0 = kQ/(R^3) ⋅ R/2 = kQ/(2R^2)","E(2R) = kQ/((2R)^2) = kQ/(4R^2) = E_0/2"],""],[0,4,22,"sec","Wires and cylinders","For an infinite line charge, wrap a coaxial cylinder of radius r and length L around it. The field is radial: it pierces the curved wall head-on and skims the flat end caps.",[],""],[0,4,23,"text","Wires and cylinders","For an infinite line charge, wrap a coaxial cylinder of radius r and length L around it. The field is radial: it pierces the curved wall head-on and skims the flat end caps.",["r","L"],""],[0,4,24,"formula","Wires and cylinders","For an infinite line charge, wrap a coaxial cylinder of radius r and length L around it. The field is radial: it pierces the curved wall head-on and skims the flat end caps.",["E ⋅ 2πrL = λL/ε_0 ⟹ E = λ/(2πε_0 r)"],"<i>E</i> ⋅ 2<i>π</i><i>r</i><i>L</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>λ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⟹ <i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>λ</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub> <i>r</i></span></span>"],[0,4,25,"text","Wires and cylinders","One line — compare that with the integral in Lesson 2. For a long solid cylinder of radius R and uniform density ρ , the same surface gives",["R","ρ"],""],[0,4,26,"formula","Wires and cylinders","One line — compare that with the integral in Lesson 2. For a long solid cylinder of radius R and uniform density ρ , the same surface gives",["E = ρr/(2ε_0) (r ≤ R) E = (ρR^2)/(2ε_0 r) (r ≥ R)"],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>r</i></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≤ <i>R</i>) <i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub> <i>r</i></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≥ <i>R</i>)"],[0,4,27,"text","Wires and cylinders","Outside, it is just a line charge with λ = ρπR^2 .",["λ = ρπR^2"],""],[0,4,28,"sec","Sheets","For an infinite sheet with charge σ per unit area, use a “pillbox” — a short cylinder poking through the sheet, with end caps of area A on either side. The field is perpendicular to the sheet, so only the two caps carry flux:",[],""],[0,4,29,"text","Sheets","For an infinite sheet with charge σ per unit area, use a “pillbox” — a short cylinder poking through the sheet, with end caps of area A on either side. The field is perpendicular to the sheet, so only the two caps carry flux:",["σ","A"],""],[0,4,30,"formula","Sheets","For an infinite sheet with charge σ per unit area, use a “pillbox” — a short cylinder poking through the sheet, with end caps of area A on either side. The field is perpendicular to the sheet, so only the two caps carry flux:",["2EA = σA/ε_0 ⟹ E = σ/(2ε_0)"],"2<i>E</i><i>A</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i><i>A</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⟹ <i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span>"],[0,4,31,"text","Sheets","directed away from a positive sheet on both sides, and — remarkably — independent of distance.",[],""],[0,4,32,"intuition","Why doesn’t it weaken as you back away?","Close to the sheet you are strongly pushed by the patch right under you, but everything further out pushes almost sideways and cancels. Step back: the nearby patch weakens, but far more of the sheet now pushes nearly straight at you. The two effects balance exactly.",[],""],[0,4,33,"text","Sheets","Because each sheet’s field is uniform, problems with several parallel sheets are pure bookkeeping: at the point of interest, write σ_i/(2ε_0) for each sheet with the right sign (away from positive, towards negative) and add.",["σ_i/(2ε_0)"],""],[0,4,34,"text","Sheets","The most useful special case: two sheets with +σ and −σ . Between them the fields add to σ/ε_0 ; outside they cancel to zero. That is a parallel-plate capacitor.",["+σ","−σ","σ/ε_0"],""],[0,4,35,"q","Two like sheets · Single correct","Two large parallel sheets each carry the same uniform charge density +σ . The electric field is σ/ε_0 between them and zero outside · zero between them and σ/ε_0 outside · σ/2ε_0 everywhere · zero everywhere",["+σ","σ/ε_0","σ/ε_0","σ/2ε_0"],""],[0,4,35,"sol","Two like sheets · Single correct","Each sheet pushes a positive test charge away from itself with σ/2ε_0 . Between the sheets the two pushes are opposite: they cancel, E = 0 . Outside (either side) both push the same way: E = σ/(2ε_0) + σ/(2ε_0) = σ/ε_0 . It is the mirror image of the capacitor case — a favourite one-mark trap.",["σ/2ε_0","E = 0","E = σ/(2ε_0) + σ/(2ε_0) = σ/ε_0"],""],[0,4,36,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2005 · Single correct","Three infinitely long charge sheets are placed as shown: the sheet at z = 3a carries +σ , the sheet at z = 0 carries −2σ and the sheet at z = −a carries −σ . The electric field at the point P , which lies between the upper two sheets, is 2σ/ε_0 k · −2σ/ε_0 k · 4σ/ε_0 k · −4σ/ε_0 k",["z = 3a","+σ","z = 0","−2σ","z = −a","−σ","P","2σ/ε_0 k","−2σ/ε_0 k","4σ/ε_0 k","−4σ/ε_0 k"],""],[0,4,36,"sol","IIT-JEE 2005 · Single correct","Take the three sheets one at a time. At P : Sheet P is… Direction of its field at P Contribution +σ at z = 3a below it away from sheet: −k −σ/(2ε_0)k −2σ at z = 0 above it towards sheet: −k −2σ/(2ε_0)k −σ at z = −a above it towards sheet: −k −σ/(2ε_0)k All three point down, so E_P = −(σ + 2σ + σ)/(2ε_0) k = −2σ/ε_0 k. Answer (B). The distances 3a and a never entered — a sheet’s field does not care how far away you are, only which side you are on.",["P","P","P","+σ","z = 3a","−k","−σ/(2ε_0)k","−2σ","z = 0","−k","−2σ/(2ε_0)k","−σ"],""],[0,4,37,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","Let E_1(r) , E_2(r) and E_3(r) be the respective electric fields at a distance r from a point charge Q , an infinitely long wire with constant linear charge density λ , and an infinite plane with uniform surface charge density σ . If E_1(r_0) = E_2(r_0) = E_3(r_0) at a given distance r_0 , then Q = 4σπr_0^2 · r_0 = λ/2πσ · E_1(r_0/2) = 2E_2(r_0/2) · E_2(r_0/2) = 4E_3(r_0/2)",["E_1(r)","E_2(r)","E_3(r)","r","Q","λ","σ","E_1(r_0) = E_2(r_0) = E_3(r_0)","r_0","Q = 4σπr_0^2","r_0 = λ/2πσ","E_1(r_0/2) = 2E_2(r_0/2)"],""],[0,4,37,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","The three fields: E_1 = Q/(4πε_0r^2) , E_2 = λ/(2πε_0r) , E_3 = σ/(2ε_0) . Call their common value at r_0 simply E . (A) E_1 = E_3 at r_0 : Q/(4πε_0r_0^2) = σ/(2ε_0) ⇒ Q = 2πσr_0^2 . The option has 4π . False. (B) E_2 = E_3 at r_0 : λ/(2πε_0r_0) = σ/(2ε_0) ⇒ r_0 = λ/πσ . False. Now halve the distance and use the scaling: E_1 ∝ 1/r^2 becomes 4E ; E_2 ∝ 1/r becomes 2E ; E_3 stays E . (C) E_1(r_0/2) = 4E = 2 × (2E) = 2E_2(r_0/2) . True. (D) E_2(r_0/2) = 2E = 2E_3 , not 4E_3 . False. Answer (C). You do not need the constants for (C) and (D) — only how each field scales with r .",["E_1 = Q/(4πε_0r^2)","E_2 = λ/(2πε_0r)","E_3 = σ/(2ε_0)","r_0","E","E_1 = E_3","r_0","Q/(4πε_0r_0^2) = σ/(2ε_0) ⇒ Q = 2πσr_0^2","4π","E_2 = E_3","r_0","λ/(2πε_0r_0) = σ/(2ε_0) ⇒ r_0 = λ/πσ"],""],[0,4,38,"sec","Superposition with Gauss: build it from pieces you know","Gauss’s law gives you a handful of standard fields. Superposition lets you combine them into shapes that have no symmetry at all.",[],""],[0,4,39,"text","Superposition with Gauss: build it from pieces you know","Gauss’s law gives you a handful of standard fields. Superposition lets you combine them into shapes that have no symmetry at all.",[],""],[0,4,40,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","Two non-conducting solid spheres of radii R and 2R , having uniform volume charge densities ρ_1 and ρ_2 respectively, touch each other. The net electric field at a distance 2R from the centre of the smaller sphere, along the line joining the centres of the spheres, is zero. The ratio ρ_1/ρ_2 can be −4 · −32/25 · 32/25 · 4",["R","2R","ρ_1","ρ_2","2R","ρ_1/ρ_2","−4","−32/25","32/25","4"],""],[0,4,40,"sol","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","Put the small sphere’s centre at the origin; the big sphere’s centre is then at x = 3R . Two points on the line are 2R from the origin. P_1 is inside the large sphere; P_2 is outside both. The small sphere (charge Q_1 = ρ_1 ⋅ 4/3πR^3) is seen from outside at both points, at distance 2R : E_(small) = (kQ_1)/((2R)^2) = (ρ_1R)/(12ε_0). Point P_1 (x = 2R) is inside the big sphere, a distance R from its centre, so the big sphere’s field there is (ρ_2R)/(3ε_0) . For positive densities the small sphere pushes right and the big one pushes left (away from its own centre). They cancel if (ρ_1R)/(12ε_0) = (ρ_2R)/(3ε_0) ⟹ ρ_1/ρ_2 = 4. Point P_2 (x = −2R) is outside both, a distance 5R from the big sphere’s centre: E_big = (5R)^2k ρ_2 ⋅ 4/3π(2R)^3 = (8ρ_2R)/(75ε_0). Here both fields point the same way if the densities have the same sign, so cancellation needs opposite signs: (ρ_1R)/(12ε_0) + (8ρ_2R)/(75ε_0) = 0 ⟹ ρ_1/ρ_2 = −96/75 = −32/25. Answer (B), (D). The physics is routine; the marks are for noticing the second point and for using the inside formula at P_1 . “At a distance 2R from the centre of the smaller sphere, along the line of centres” describes two points. Where is each one relative to the big sphere?",["x = 3R","2R","P_1","P_2","Q_1 = ρ_1 ⋅ 4/3πR^3","2R","E_(small) = (kQ_1)/((2R)^2) = (ρ_1R)/(12ε_0).","P_1","x = 2R","R","(ρ_2R)/(3ε_0)","(ρ_1R)/(12ε_0) = (ρ_2R)/(3ε_0) ⟹ ρ_1/ρ_2 = 4."],""],[0,4,41,"sec","The cavity trick","Here is one of the prettiest ideas in electrostatics. A uniformly charged sphere has a spherical hole scooped out of it. The symmetry is ruined — but a hole is just the full sphere plus a smaller sphere of opposite charge occupying the same space:",[],""],[0,4,42,"text","The cavity trick","Here is one of the prettiest ideas in electrostatics. A uniformly charged sphere has a spherical hole scooped out of it. The symmetry is ruined — but a hole is just the full sphere plus a smaller sphere of opposite charge occupying the same space:",[],""],[0,4,43,"formula","The cavity trick","Here is one of the prettiest ideas in electrostatics. A uniformly charged sphere has a spherical hole scooped out of it. The symmetry is ruined — but a hole is just the full sphere plus a smaller sphere of opposite charge occupying the same space:",["sphere with cavity = full sphere (+ρ) + small sphere (−ρ)"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">sphere with cavity</span> = <span class=\"fx-t\">full sphere </span>(+<i>ρ</i>) + <span class=\"fx-t\">small sphere </span>(−<i>ρ</i>)"],[0,4,44,"text","The cavity trick","Take a point inside the cavity, at position r from the big sphere’s centre O and r ^′ from the cavity’s centre P , and let a = OP . The point is inside both imaginary spheres, so use the vector form of the inside field for each:",["r","O","r ^′","P","a = OP"],""],[0,4,45,"formula","The cavity trick","Take a point inside the cavity, at position r from the big sphere’s centre O and r ^′ from the cavity’s centre P , and let a = OP . The point is inside both imaginary spheres, so use the vector form of the inside field for each:",["E = ρ/(3ε_0)r + (−ρ)/(3ε_0)r ^′ = ρ/(3ε_0) (r − r ^′) = ρa/(3ε_0)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>(−<i>ρ</i>)</span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span> <sup>′</sup> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> (<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span> − <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>r</i></span> <sup>′</sup>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>a</i></span></span><span>3<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span>"],[0,4,46,"text","The cavity trick","Position has dropped out completely. The field in the cavity is uniform: the same size and direction at every point, parallel to the line from the sphere’s centre to the cavity’s centre. It depends only on ρ and a — not on the radius of either sphere.",["ρ","a"],""],[0,4,47,"intuition","Sanity check","If the cavity is concentric (a = 0) the field inside it is zero — exactly what Gauss’s law says for a hollow, spherically symmetric shell of charge. Good.",["a = 0"],""],[0,4,48,"text","The cavity trick","The same trick works for a cylindrical hole in a charged cylinder (field ρa/(2ε_0)), and for a spherical hole in a cylinder, as you are about to see.",["ρa/(2ε_0)"],""],[0,4,49,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","Consider a uniform spherical charge distribution of radius R_1 centred at the origin O . In this distribution, a spherical cavity of radius R_2 , centred at P with distance OP = a = R_1 − R_2 , is made. If the electric field inside the cavity at position r is E(r) , then the correct statement(s) is (are) E is uniform, its magnitude is independent of R_2 but its direction depends on r · E is uniform, its magnitude depends on R_2 and its direction depends on r · E is uniform, its magnitude is independent of a but its direction depends on a · E is uniform and both its magnitude and direction depend on a",["R_1","O","R_2","P","OP = a = R_1 − R_2","r","E(r)","E","R_2","r","E","R_2"],""],[0,4,49,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","From the cavity trick, at any point inside the cavity E = ρ/(3ε_0) a, a = OP. It contains no r at all, so neither its magnitude nor its direction depends on position: (A) and (B) are out. Its magnitude is ρa/(3ε_0) , proportional to a : (C) is out. Both magnitude and direction are fixed by a : (D). Answer (D).",["E = ρ/(3ε_0) a, a = OP.","r","ρa/(3ε_0)","a","a"],""],[0,4,50,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · Integer answer","An infinitely long solid cylinder of radius R has a uniform volume charge density ρ . It has a spherical cavity of radius R/2 with its centre on the axis of the cylinder. The magnitude of the electric field at the point P , which is at a distance 2R from the axis of the cylinder (on the perpendicular to the axis through the centre of the cavity), is given by the expression (23ρR)/(16kε_0) . Find the value of k .",["R","ρ","R/2","P","2R","(23ρR)/(16kε_0)","k"],""],[0,4,50,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · Integer answer","Treat the object as a complete cylinder of density +ρ plus a sphere of radius R/2 and density −ρ at the cavity. Cylinder (outside point, r = 2R): E_cyl = (ρR^2)/(2ε_0 (2R)) = ρR/(4ε_0) , directed away from the axis. Negative sphere. Its charge has magnitude ρ ⋅ 4/3π (R/2)^3 = (ρπR^3)/6 , and P is 2R from its centre: E_sph = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (ρπR^3/6)/((2R)^2) = ρR/(96ε_0), directed towards the axis (it is negative), along the same line. E_P = ρR/(4ε_0) − ρR/(96ε_0) = (24ρR − ρR)/(96ε_0) = (23ρR)/(96ε_0) = (23ρR)/(16 ⋅ 6 ε_0) ⟹ k = 6. Complete cylinder minus a small sphere. At P , both are seen from outside.",["+ρ","R/2","−ρ","r = 2R","E_cyl = (ρR^2)/(2ε_0 (2R)) = ρR/(4ε_0)","ρ ⋅ 4/3π (R/2)^3 = (ρπR^3)/6","P","2R","E_sph = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (ρπR^3/6)/((2R)^2) = ρR/(96ε_0),","E_P = ρR/(4ε_0) − ρR/(96ε_0) = (24ρR − ρR)/(96ε_0) = (23ρR)/(96ε_0) = (23ρR)/(16 ⋅ 6 ε_0) ⟹ k = 6.","P"],""],[0,4,51,"recap","Recap","Gauss gives E only with spherical, cylindrical or planar symmetry. Shell: 0 inside, kQ/r^2 outside. Solid sphere: ρr/(3ε_0) inside, kQ/r^2 outside. Line: λ/(2πε_0r) . Sheet: σ/(2ε_0) , same at every distance. Opposite sheets: σ/ε_0 between, zero outside. Cavity = full body + negative body. Off-centre spherical cavity: uniform field ρa/(3ε_0) . Before using any formula ask: is this point inside or outside that piece of charge?",["E","0","kQ/r^2","ρr/(3ε_0)","kQ/r^2","λ/(2πε_0r)","σ/(2ε_0)","σ/ε_0","ρa/(3ε_0)"],""],[0,5,0,"lesson","Potential & Potential Energy","Fields are vectors, and vectors are hard work. Potential is just a number at every point — the “height map” of electricity — and energy conservation does the rest.",[],""],[0,5,1,"sec","Electricity has a landscape","Lift a ball up a hill and you store energy; let go and it rolls back down. The hill’s height tells you everything about the energy, without ever mentioning forces.",[],""],[0,5,2,"text","Electricity has a landscape","Lift a ball up a hill and you store energy; let go and it rolls back down. The hill’s height tells you everything about the energy, without ever mentioning forces.",[],""],[0,5,3,"text","Electricity has a landscape","The electrostatic force is conservative, exactly like gravity: the work it does on a charge moving from A to B depends only on the two end points, never on the route. So we can define an electrical “height” at every point — the potential V .",["A","B","V"],""],[0,5,4,"formula","Electricity has a landscape","The electrostatic force is conservative, exactly like gravity: the work it does on a charge moving from A to B depends only on the two end points, never on the route. So we can define an electrical “height” at every point — the potential V .",["V_B − V_A = (W_(by external agent,)A → B)/q_0 = − ∫_A^B E ⋅ dℓ"],"<i>V</i><sub><i>B</i></sub> − <i>V</i><sub><i>A</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>W</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">by external agent, </span></sub><i>A</i> → <i>B</i></span><span><i>q</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> = − ∫<sub><i>A</i></sub><sup><i>B</i></sup> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> ⋅ d<span class=\"fx-vec\">ℓ</span>"],[0,5,5,"text","Electricity has a landscape","In words: the potential difference is the work you must do per unit charge to carry a positive test charge slowly from A to B . The unit is the volt, 1 V = 1 J/C . A charge q sitting at a point of potential V has potential energy",["A","B","1 V = 1 J/C","q","V"],""],[0,5,6,"formula","Electricity has a landscape","In words: the potential difference is the work you must do per unit charge to carry a positive test charge slowly from A to B . The unit is the volt, 1 V = 1 J/C . A charge q sitting at a point of potential V has potential energy",["U = qV."],"<i>U</i> = <i>q</i><i>V</i>."],[0,5,7,"intuition","The height-map picture","V is the height of the land. E points downhill — from high potential to low — and the steeper the slope, the stronger the field. A positive charge rolls downhill, to lower V . A negative charge does the opposite and “falls” up to higher V . Either way, a free charge always moves so that its potential energy qV decreases.",["V","E","V","V","qV"],""],[0,5,8,"text","Electricity has a landscape","Only differences of potential are physical. By convention we choose V = 0 infinitely far away. Then for a point charge Q ,",["V = 0","Q"],""],[0,5,9,"formula","Electricity has a landscape","Only differences of potential are physical. By convention we choose V = 0 infinitely far away. Then for a point charge Q ,",["V = 1/(4πε_0) Q/r"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>4<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span>"],[0,5,10,"text","Electricity has a landscape","Positive near a positive charge, negative near a negative one, falling off as 1/r — more slowly than the field.",["1/r"],""],[0,5,11,"sec","Potentials add like ordinary numbers","Superposition holds for potential too, and here it becomes almost unfairly easy, because V is a scalar:",[],""],[0,5,12,"text","Potentials add like ordinary numbers","Superposition holds for potential too, and here it becomes almost unfairly easy, because V is a scalar:",["V"],""],[0,5,13,"formula","Potentials add like ordinary numbers","Superposition holds for potential too, and here it becomes almost unfairly easy, because V is a scalar:",["V = ∑_i (kq_i)/r_i"],"<i>V</i> = ∑<sub><i>i</i></sub> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span></span>"],[0,5,14,"text","Potentials add like ordinary numbers","No components, no angles, no cancellation by symmetry to argue about. Just distances and signs.",[],""],[0,5,15,"trap","The signs go in","With forces and fields we often work with magnitudes and put the direction in by hand. With V and U you must substitute each charge with its sign. A forgotten minus sign is the number-one error in this lesson.",["V","U"],""],[0,5,16,"key","Any ring, on its axis","Every bit of a ring of radius R is the same distance √(x^2 + R^2) from a point on the axis. So V = kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2)) and at the centre V = kQ/R — and this is true even if the charge is spread unevenly around the ring. The field at the centre would depend on the details; the potential does not.",["R","√(x^2 + R^2)","V = kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2)) and at the centre V = kQ/R"],""],[0,5,16,"formula","Any ring, on its axis"," the same distance √(x^2 + R^2) from a point on the axis. So V = kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2)) and at the centre V = kQ/R — and this is true even if the charge is spread unevenly around the ring. The field at the centre would depend on the details; the potential does not.",["V = kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2)) and at the centre V = kQ/R"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">and at the centre</span> <i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span>"],[0,5,18,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","A non-conducting ring of radius 0.5 m carries a total charge of 1.11 × 10^(−10) C distributed non-uniformly on its circumference, producing an electric field E everywhere in space. The value of the integral ∫_(ℓ = ∞)^(ℓ = 0) −E ⋅ dℓ (where ℓ = 0 is the centre of the ring), in volts, is +2 · −1 · −2 · zero",["0.5 m","1.11 × 10^(−10) C","E","∫_(ℓ = ∞)^(ℓ = 0) −E ⋅ dℓ","ℓ = 0","+2","−1","−2"],""],[0,5,18,"sol","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","− ∫_∞^0 E ⋅ dℓ is exactly V(centre) − V(∞) : the potential at the centre of the ring. Every element of the ring is 0.5 m from the centre, so the uneven distribution is irrelevant: V = kQ/R = ((9 × 10^9)(1.11 × 10^(−10)))/0.5 ≈ +2 V . Answer (A). The phrase “non-uniformly” is there only to make you think the problem is impossible. Recognise the integral. It is the definition of something at the centre of the ring.",["− ∫_∞^0 E ⋅ dℓ","V(centre) − V(∞)","0.5 m","V = kQ/R = ((9 × 10^9)(1.11 × 10^(−10)))/0.5 ≈ +2 V ."],""],[0,5,19,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","A charge +q is fixed at each of the points x = x_0, 3x_0, 5x_0, … (to infinity) on the x -axis, and a charge −q is fixed at each of the points x = 2x_0, 4x_0, 6x_0, … (to infinity). Taking the potential at infinity to be zero, the potential at the origin due to this system of charges is zero · q/(8πε_0x_0 ln 2) · infinite · (q ln 2)/(4πε_0x_0)",["+q","x = x_0, 3x_0, 5x_0, …","x","−q","x = 2x_0, 4x_0, 6x_0, …","q/(8πε_0x_0 ln 2)","(q ln 2)/(4πε_0x_0)"],""],[0,5,19,"sol","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","Potentials add as scalars, signs included: V = kq/x_0 − kq/(2x_0) + kq/(3x_0) − ⋯ = q/(4πε_0x_0) [1 − 1/2 + 1/3 − 1/4 + ⋯ ] . The bracket is the series for ln(1 + x) = x − (x^2)/2 + (x^3)/3 − ⋯ at x = 1 , which sums to ln 2 . Hence V = (q ln 2)/(4πε_0x_0). Answer (D).",["V = kq/x_0 − kq/(2x_0) + kq/(3x_0) − ⋯ = q/(4πε_0x_0) [1 − 1/2 + 1/3 − 1/4 + ⋯ ] .","ln(1 + x) = x − (x^2)/2 + (x^3)/3 − ⋯","x = 1","ln 2","V = (q ln 2)/(4πε_0x_0)."],""],[0,5,20,"sec","From potential back to field","Potential and field carry the same information. To go from E to V you integrate; to go back, you differentiate:",[],""],[0,5,21,"text","From potential back to field","Potential and field carry the same information. To go from E to V you integrate; to go back, you differentiate:",["E","V"],""],[0,5,22,"formula","From potential back to field","Potential and field carry the same information. To go from E to V you integrate; to go back, you differentiate:",["E_x = −(∂V)/(∂x), E_y = −(∂V)/(∂y), E_z = −(∂V)/(∂z)"],"<i>E</i><sub><i>x</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∂<i>V</i></span><span>∂<i>x</i></span></span>, <i>E</i><sub><i>y</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∂<i>V</i></span><span>∂<i>y</i></span></span>, <i>E</i><sub><i>z</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∂<i>V</i></span><span>∂<i>z</i></span></span>"],[0,5,23,"text","From potential back to field","and when everything depends only on the distance r from a centre, simply E_r = −dV/dr .",["r","E_r = −dV/dr"],""],[0,5,24,"text","From potential back to field","The minus sign is the “downhill” rule. In a uniform field this is simply ΔV = −E Δx along the field — move a distance d along E and the potential drops by Ed . (That is where the unit V/m comes from.)",["ΔV = −E Δx","d","E","Ed","V/m"],""],[0,5,25,"text","From potential back to field","An equipotential surface is a contour line of the landscape: a surface on which V is the same everywhere.",["V"],""],[0,5,26,"text","From potential back to field","No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential. Field lines cross equipotentials at right angles (if E had a component along the surface, V would change along it). Where equipotentials are close together, the field is strong. Point charge → concentric spheres. Uniform field → parallel planes. Line charge → coaxial cylinders.",["E","V"],""],[0,5,27,"q","Does zero field mean zero potential? · Single correct","Two equal positive charges are fixed some distance apart. At the point midway between them, E = 0 and V = 0 · E = 0 but V  = 0 · E  = 0 but V = 0 · E  = 0 and V  = 0",["E = 0","V = 0","E = 0","V  = 0","E  = 0","V = 0","E  = 0","V  = 0"],""],[0,5,27,"sol","Does zero field mean zero potential? · Single correct","The two fields at the midpoint are equal and opposite — they cancel, so E = 0 . The two potentials are equal and both positive — they add, so V = (2kq)/(d/2)  = 0 . Swap one charge for −q and the situation flips: now V = 0 at the midpoint while E  = 0 . Neither quantity being zero tells you anything about the other. E is the slope of V , and the midpoint here is like the flat top of a mountain pass — level ground, high altitude.",["E = 0","V = (2kq)/(d/2)  = 0","−q","V = 0","E  = 0","E","V"],""],[0,5,28,"sec","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R). Outside it looks like a point charge. Inside, the field is zero, so the potential cannot change — it stays at its surface value:",[],""],[0,5,29,"text","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R). Outside it looks like a point charge. Inside, the field is zero, so the potential cannot change — it stays at its surface value:",["Q","R"],""],[0,5,30,"formula","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","Thin shell (charge Q , radius R). Outside it looks like a point charge. Inside, the field is zero, so the potential cannot change — it stays at its surface value:",["V = kQ/r (r ≥ R) V = kQ/R (r ≤ R)"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≥ <i>R</i>) <i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≤ <i>R</i>)"],[0,5,31,"text","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","Uniform solid sphere. Inside there is a field, so V keeps rising as you go in:",["V"],""],[0,5,32,"formula","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","Uniform solid sphere. Inside there is a field, so V keeps rising as you go in:",["V = kQ/r (r ≥ R) V = (kQ (3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3) (r ≤ R)"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≥ <i>R</i>) <i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i> (3<i>R</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>r</i><sup>2</sup>)</span><span>2<i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span> (<i>r</i> ≤ <i>R</i>)"],[0,5,33,"text","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","At the centre, V = (3kQ)/(2R) — exactly 1.5 times the surface value.",["V = (3kQ)/(2R)"],""],[0,5,34,"text","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","DerivationPotential inside a solid sphere Start from the surface, where V = kQ/R , and walk inward against the field E = kQr/R^3 : V(r) = V(R) + ∫_r^R E dr^′ = kQ/R + kQ/(R^3) ⋅ (R^2 − r^2)/2 = (kQ (3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3).",["V = kQ/R","E = kQr/R^3","V(r) = V(R) + ∫_r^R E dr^′ = kQ/R + kQ/(R^3) ⋅ (R^2 − r^2)/2 = (kQ (3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3)."],""],[0,5,34,"formula","Spheres: the two results to carry in your head","DerivationPotential inside a solid sphere Start from the surface, where V = kQ/R , and walk inward against the field E = kQr/R^3 : V(r) = V(R) + ∫_r^R E dr^′ = kQ/R + kQ/(R^3) ⋅ (R^2 − r^2)/2 = (kQ (3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3).",["V(r) = V(R) + ∫_r^R E dr^′ = kQ/R + kQ/(R^3) ⋅ (R^2 − r^2)/2 = (kQ (3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3)."],"<i>V</i>(<i>r</i>) = <i>V</i>(<i>R</i>) + ∫<sub><i>r</i></sub><sup><i>R</i></sup> <i>E</i> d<i>r</i><sup>′</sup> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2</span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i> (3<i>R</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>r</i><sup>2</sup>)</span><span>2<i>R</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>."],[0,5,36,"trap","E = 0 inside a shell does not make V = 0 there","Zero field means the potential is constant — flat ground. Flat ground can be at any altitude. Inside a charged shell it sits at kQ/R .",["kQ/R"],""],[0,5,37,"sec","Potential energy of a system of charges","The potential energy of two point charges a distance r apart is",[],""],[0,5,38,"text","Potential energy of a system of charges","The potential energy of two point charges a distance r apart is",["r"],""],[0,5,39,"formula","Potential energy of a system of charges","The potential energy of two point charges a distance r apart is",["U = (kq_1q_2)/r"],"<i>U</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sub>1</sub><i>q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>r</i></span></span>"],[0,5,40,"text","Potential energy of a system of charges","(signs in!). It is the work needed to bring them from infinitely far apart to that separation. Positive U : they repel, and you had to push. Negative U : they attract, and the system is bound — you would have to supply ∣U∣ to tear it apart.",["U","U","∣U∣"],""],[0,5,41,"text","Potential energy of a system of charges","For several charges, add one term for every pair:",[],""],[0,5,42,"formula","Potential energy of a system of charges","For several charges, add one term for every pair:",["U = ∑_(pairs) (kq_iq_j)/(r_ij) (n charges ⇒ (n(n − 1))/2 pairs)"],"<i>U</i> = ∑<sub><span class=\"fx-t\">pairs</span></sub> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub><i>q</i><sub><i>j</i></sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub><i>ij</i></sub></span></span> (<i>n</i><span class=\"fx-t\"> charges</span> ⇒ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i>(<i>n</i> − 1)</span><span>2</span></span><span class=\"fx-t\"> pairs</span>)"],[0,5,43,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2000 · Single correct","Three charges Q , +q and +q are placed at the vertices of a right-angled isosceles triangle as shown: +q sits at the right-angle vertex, and Q and the other +q at the two ends of the hypotenuse. The equal sides have length a . The net electrostatic energy of the configuration is zero if Q is equal to (−q)/(1 + √2) · (−2q)/(2 + √2) · −2q · +q",["Q","+q","+q","+q","Q","+q","a","Q","(−q)/(1 + √2)","(−2q)/(2 + √2)","−2q","+q"],""],[0,5,43,"sol","IIT-JEE 2000 · Single correct","Three charges, three pairs. Q is at distance a from the corner charge and √2 a from the other one; the two +q ’s are a apart. U = k [(q ⋅ q)/a + Qq/a + Qq/(√2 a)] = 0 ⟹ q + Q (1 + 1/(√2)) = 0 Q = −1 + 1/(√2)q = −(√2 q)/(√2 + 1) = −(2q)/(2 + √2). Answer (B). As expected Q is negative: the two +q ’s contribute positive energy, so Q must contribute enough negative energy to cancel it.",["Q","a","√2 a","+q","a","U = k [(q ⋅ q)/a + Qq/a + Qq/(√2 a)] = 0 ⟹ q + Q (1 + 1/(√2)) = 0","Q = −1 + 1/(√2)q = −(√2 q)/(√2 + 1) = −(2q)/(2 + √2).","Q","+q","Q"],""],[0,5,44,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","Two equal point charges are fixed at x = −a and x = +a on the x -axis. Another point charge Q is placed at the origin. The change in the electrical potential energy of Q , when it is displaced by a small distance x along the x -axis, is approximately proportional to x · x^2 · x^3 · 1/x",["x = −a","x = +a","x","Q","Q","x","x","x","x^2","x^3","1/x"],""],[0,5,44,"sol","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","Let each fixed charge be q . With Q at position x it is a − x from one and a + x from the other: U(x) = kqQ [1/(a − x) + 1/(a + x)] = (2kqQ a)/(a^2 − x^2) = (2kqQ)/a (1 − (x^2)/(a^2))^(−1) For x ≪ a , use (1 − ϵ)^(−1) ≈ 1 + ϵ : U(x) ≈ (2kqQ)/a (1 + (x^2)/(a^2)) . ΔU = U(x) − U(0) ≈ (2kqQ)/(a^3) x^2 ∝ x^2. Answer (B). You could have guessed it: the origin is an equilibrium point, so dU/dx = 0 there and the first surviving term in the expansion must be quadratic. Write U(x) exactly, then expand for x ≪ a .",["q","Q","x","a − x","a + x","U(x) = kqQ [1/(a − x) + 1/(a + x)] = (2kqQ a)/(a^2 − x^2) = (2kqQ)/a (1 − (x^2)/(a^2))^(−1)","x ≪ a","(1 − ϵ)^(−1) ≈ 1 + ϵ","U(x) ≈ (2kqQ)/a (1 + (x^2)/(a^2)) .","ΔU = U(x) − U(0) ≈ (2kqQ)/(a^3) x^2 ∝ x^2.","dU/dx = 0","U(x)"],""],[0,5,45,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","Consider a system of three charges q/3 , q/3 and −(2q)/3 placed at points A , B and C respectively, as shown in the figure. O is the centre of the circle of radius R , AB is a diameter and angle CAB = 60^∘ . Which statement is correct? The electric field at point O is q/(8πε_0R^2) directed along the negative x -axis. · The potential energy of the system is zero. · The magnitude of the force between the charges at C and B is (q^2)/(54πε_0R^2) . · The potential at point O is q/(12πε_0R) .",["q/3","q/3","−(2q)/3","A","B","C","O","R","AB","CAB = 60^∘","O","q/(8πε_0R^2)"],""],[0,5,45,"sol","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","Geometry first. AB is a diameter, so the angle at C is 90^∘ (angle in a semicircle). With AB = 2R and ∠CAB = 60^∘ : AC = 2R cos 60^∘ = R, BC = 2R sin 60^∘ = √3 R. Now test each statement. (C) Force between C and B : F = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (√3R)^2((2q)/3) (q/3) = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (2q^2)/(27R^2) = (q^2)/(54πε_0R^2). True. (D) All three charges are at distance R from O , so V_O = k/R (q/3 + q/3 − (2q)/3) = 0 . False. (A) The equal charges at A and B are diametrically opposite, so their fields at O cancel. Only C contributes: E_O = (k (2q/3))/(R^2) = q/(6πε_0R^2) , not q/(8πε_0R^2) . False. (B) U = k [((q/3)(q/3))/(2R) + ((q/3)(−2q/3))/R + ((q/3)(−2q/3))/(√3R)] = (kq^2)/(9R) [1/2 − 2 − 2/(√3)]  = 0 . False. Answer (C). An angle in a semicircle is a right angle. Get the three side lengths first.",["AB","C","90^∘","AB = 2R","∠CAB = 60^∘","AC = 2R cos 60^∘ = R, BC = 2R sin 60^∘ = √3 R.","C","B","F = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (√3R)^2((2q)/3) (q/3) = 1/(4πε_0) ⋅ (2q^2)/(27R^2) = (q^2)/(54πε_0R^2). True.","R","O","V_O = k/R (q/3 + q/3 − (2q)/3) = 0"],""],[0,5,46,"sec","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","Whenever a charge moves under electrostatic forces only,",[],""],[0,5,47,"text","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","Whenever a charge moves under electrostatic forces only,",[],""],[0,5,48,"formula","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","Whenever a charge moves under electrostatic forces only,",["1/2mv^2 + qV = constant"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><i>m</i><i>v</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>q</i><i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-t\">constant</span>"],[0,5,49,"text","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","No forces, no acceleration, no integration over the path. Just “before” and “after”.",[],""],[0,5,50,"text","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","Distance of closest approach. Fire a charge q with kinetic energy K straight at a fixed like charge Q from far away. It slows, stops for an instant at distance r_min , and turns back. At that instant all the kinetic energy has become potential energy:",["q","K","Q","r_min"],""],[0,5,51,"formula","Energy conservation: the fastest tool in the chapter","Distance of closest approach. Fire a charge q with kinetic energy K straight at a fixed like charge Q from far away. It slows, stops for an instant at distance r_min , and turns back. At that instant all the kinetic energy has become potential energy:",["K = kQq/(r_min) ⟹ r_min = kQq/K"],"<i>K</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Qq</i></span><span><i>r</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">min</span></sub></span></span> ⟹ <i>r</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">min</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Qq</i></span><span><i>K</i></span></span>"],[0,5,52,"q","Closest approach · Single correct","An α -particle with kinetic energy K is fired head-on at a heavy nucleus and gets as close as a distance d before turning back. If the kinetic energy is doubled to 2K , the distance of closest approach becomes d/4 · d/2 · d/√2 · 2d",["α","K","d","2K","d/4","d/2","d/√2","2d"],""],[0,5,52,"sol","Closest approach · Single correct","r_min = kQq/K ∝ 1/K . Double the energy, halve the distance: d/2 . (If the speed had been doubled instead, K would be four times larger and the answer would be d/4 — read the question carefully.)",["r_min = kQq/K ∝ 1/K","d/2","K","d/4"],""],[0,5,53,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · Single correct","A thin spherical insulating shell of radius R carries a uniformly distributed charge such that the potential at its surface is V_0 . A hole with a small area α 4πR^2 (α ≪ 1) is made on the shell without affecting the rest of the shell. Which one of the following statements is correct? The potential at the centre of the shell is reduced by 2αV_0 . · The magnitude of the electric field at the centre of the shell is reduced by (αV_0)/(2R) . · The ratio of the potential at the centre of the shell to that at the point at R/2 from the centre towards the hole is (1 − α)/(1 − 2α) . · The magnitude of the electric field at a point on the line through the hole and the shell’s centre, at a distance 2R from the centre, is reduced by (αV_0)/(2R) .",["R","V_0","α 4πR^2","α ≪ 1","2αV_0","(αV_0)/(2R)","R/2","(1 − α)/(1 − 2α)","2R","(αV_0)/(2R)"],""],[0,5,53,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · Single correct","Same idea as the cavity trick. Let the complete shell carry Q , so V_0 = kQ/R . The removed patch is a fraction α of the area, so it carried αQ . Therefore shell with hole = complete shell + point charge (−αQ) at the hole . Potential at the centre. Complete shell: V_0 . Patch, at distance R : −kαQ/R = −αV_0 . V_C = V_0(1 − α). Reduced by αV_0 , not 2αV_0 — (A) false. Potential at R/2 towards the hole. Complete shell: still V_0 (constant inside). Patch, now at distance R/2 : −kαQ/(R/2) = −2αV_0 . V_(R/2) = V_0(1 − 2α) ⟹ V_C/(V_(R/2)) = (1 − α)/(1 − 2α). (C) true. (B) The field at the centre was zero; with the hole it becomes kαQ/(R^2) = (αV_0)/R . It increases. False. (D) The point at 2R on the hole’s side is a distance R from the hole. The original field kQ/(4R^2) is reduced by the patch’s kαQ/(R^2) = (αV_0)/R , not (αV_0)/(2R) . (On the far side the patch is 3R away and the change is (αV_0)/(9R) — also not the stated value.) False. Answer (C). A shell with a hole = complete shell + a small patch of opposite charge where the hole is. How much charge is on the patch?",["Q","V_0 = kQ/R","α","αQ","shell with hole = complete shell + point charge (−αQ) at the hole .","V_0","R","−kαQ/R = −αV_0","V_C = V_0(1 − α).","αV_0","2αV_0","R/2"],""],[0,5,54,"recap","Recap","V is work per unit charge; U = qV ; E points from high V to low V and E = −dV/dr . Potentials are scalars: add them with signs. E = 0 and V = 0 are unrelated conditions. Shell: kQ/R throughout the inside. Solid sphere: centre is 1.5× surface. System energy: one (kq_iq_j)/(r_ij) per pair. Motion problems: 1/2mv^2 + qV = constant. Reach for this before forces.",["V","U = qV","E","V","V","E = −dV/dr","E = 0","V = 0","kQ/R","1.5×","(kq_iq_j)/(r_ij)","1/2mv^2 + qV ="],""],[0,6,0,"lesson","The Electric Dipole","Two equal and opposite charges, close together. It is the simplest neutral object that still has a field — and it is how most of the matter around you actually behaves.",[],""],[0,6,1,"sec","Neutral, but not invisible","A water molecule has no net charge, yet a charged comb can bend a stream of water. How? Inside the molecule the positive and negative charge are slightly separated. Their fields almost cancel — but not quite. What survives is the field of an electric dipole.",[],""],[0,6,2,"text","Neutral, but not invisible","A water molecule has no net charge, yet a charged comb can bend a stream of water. How? Inside the molecule the positive and negative charge are slightly separated. Their fields almost cancel — but not quite. What survives is the field of an electric dipole.",[],""],[0,6,3,"text","Neutral, but not invisible","A dipole is a pair of charges +q and −q a small distance 2a apart. Everything about it is captured by one vector, the dipole moment:",["+q","−q","2a"],""],[0,6,4,"formula","Neutral, but not invisible","A dipole is a pair of charges +q and −q a small distance 2a apart. Everything about it is captured by one vector, the dipole moment:",["p = q (2a) pointing from − q to + q"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> = <i>q</i> (2<i>a</i>) <span class=\"fx-t\">pointing from </span> − <i>q</i><span class=\"fx-t\"> to </span> + <i>q</i>"],[0,6,5,"trap","Direction of p","Field lines run from + to − . The dipole moment points the other way: from − to + . Examiners use this to produce wrong signs in options.",["+","−","−","+"],""],[0,6,6,"sec","The dipole’s own field and potential","Far from the dipole (r ≫ a), at a distance r from its centre:",[],""],[0,6,7,"text","The dipole’s own field and potential","Far from the dipole (r ≫ a), at a distance r from its centre:",["r ≫ a","r"],""],[0,6,8,"table","The dipole’s own field and potential","Where Field Direction Potential On the axis (end-on) (2kp)/(r^3) along p ±kp/(r^2) On the perpendicular bisector (broadside) kp/(r^3) opposite to p 0 General point, at angle θ from p kp/(r^3)√(1 + 3 cos^2 θ) at angle α to r , where tan α = 1/2 tan θ (kp cos θ)/(r^2)",["(2kp)/(r^3)","p","±kp/(r^2)","kp/(r^3)","p","0","θ","p","kp/(r^3)√(1 + 3 cos^2 θ)","α","r","tan α = 1/2 tan θ"],""],[0,6,9,"text","The dipole’s own field and potential","The potential is the cleanest way to hold all of this in your head:",[],""],[0,6,10,"formula","The dipole’s own field and potential","The potential is the cleanest way to hold all of this in your head:",["V = (kp cos θ)/(r^2) = (k p ⋅ r)/(r^2)"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>p</i> cos <i>θ</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>r</i>̂</span></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>"],[0,6,11,"text","The dipole’s own field and potential","and its two derivatives give the field components — radial, and perpendicular to the radius:",[],""],[0,6,12,"formula","The dipole’s own field and potential","and its two derivatives give the field components — radial, and perpendicular to the radius:",["E_r = −(∂V)/(∂r) = (2kp cos θ)/(r^3), E_θ = −1/r(∂V)/(∂θ) = (kp sin θ)/(r^3)"],"<i>E</i><sub><i>r</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∂<i>V</i></span><span>∂<i>r</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>p</i> cos <i>θ</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>, <i>E</i><sub><i>θ</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>∂<i>V</i></span><span>∂<i>θ</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>p</i> sin <i>θ</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>"],[0,6,13,"text","The dipole’s own field and potential","DerivationAxial and equatorial fields from scratch On the axis, at distance r from the centre, the nearer charge is at r − a and the farther at r + a : E = kq/((r − a)^2) − kq/((r + a)^2) = (kq ⋅ 4ar)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) = (2kp r)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) r ≫ a (2kp)/(r^3). On the perpendicular bisector, both charges are at distance √(r^2 + a^2) . The components perpendicular to the dipole axis cancel; the components parallel to it add, each being kq/(r^2 + a^2) ⋅ a/(√(r^2 + a^2)) : E = (2kqa)/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) = kp/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) r ≫ a kp/(r^3), pointing from the +q side towards the −q side — opposite to p .",["r","r − a","r + a","E = kq/((r − a)^2) − kq/((r + a)^2) = (kq ⋅ 4ar)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) = (2kp r)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) r ≫ a (2kp)/(r^3).","√(r^2 + a^2)","kq/(r^2 + a^2) ⋅ a/(√(r^2 + a^2))","E = (2kqa)/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) = kp/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) r ≫ a kp/(r^3),","+q","−q","p"],""],[0,6,13,"formula","The dipole’s own field and potential","s perpendicular to the dipole axis cancel; the components parallel to it add, each being kq/(r^2 + a^2) ⋅ a/(√(r^2 + a^2)) : E = (2kqa)/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) = kp/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) r ≫ a kp/(r^3), pointing from the +q side towards the −q side — opposite to p .",["E = kq/((r − a)^2) − kq/((r + a)^2) = (kq ⋅ 4ar)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) = (2kp r)/((r^2 − a^2)^2) r ≫ a (2kp)/(r^3)."],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i></span><span>(<i>r</i> − <i>a</i>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i></span><span>(<i>r</i> + <i>a</i>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i> ⋅ 4<i>a</i><i>r</i></span><span>(<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>a</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>p</i> <i>r</i></span><span>(<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>a</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> <i>r</i> ≫ <i>a</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>p</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>."],[0,6,13,"formula","The dipole’s own field and potential","s perpendicular to the dipole axis cancel; the components parallel to it add, each being kq/(r^2 + a^2) ⋅ a/(√(r^2 + a^2)) : E = (2kqa)/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) = kp/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) r ≫ a kp/(r^3), pointing from the +q side towards the −q side — opposite to p .",["E = (2kqa)/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) = kp/((r^2 + a^2)^(3/2)) r ≫ a kp/(r^3),"],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>k</i><i>q</i><i>a</i></span><span>(<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>a</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>p</i></span><span>(<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>a</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> <i>r</i> ≫ <i>a</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>p</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>,"],[0,6,16,"intuition","The ladder of fall-offs","The more completely the charge cancels, the faster its influence dies with distance: Source Field Potential Infinite sheet constant ∝ r Infinite line 1/r ∝ ln r Point charge 1/r^2 1/r Dipole 1/r^3 1/r^2 A dipole is two almost-cancelling point charges, so you lose one more power of r . JEE Advanced once set an entire matching question on exactly this table — it is waiting for you in Lesson 8.",["∝ r","1/r","∝ ln r","1/r^2","1/r","1/r^3","1/r^2","r"],""],[0,6,17,"q","End-on versus broadside · Single correct","Points A and B are at the same large distance r from a small dipole: A on its axis, B on its perpendicular bisector. The ratio of field magnitudes E_A : E_B and the potentials are 1 : 1 , with V_A = V_B · 2 : 1 , with V_B = 0 · 2 : 1 , with V_A = 0 · 1 : 2 , with V_B = 0",["A","B","r","A","B","E_A : E_B","1 : 1","V_A = V_B","2 : 1","V_B = 0","2 : 1","V_A = 0"],""],[0,6,17,"sol","End-on versus broadside · Single correct","E_A = (2kp)/(r^3) and E_B = kp/(r^3) , so E_A : E_B = 2 : 1 . Every point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from +q and −q , so their potentials cancel exactly: V_B = 0 . On the axis V_A = ±kp/(r^2)  = 0 . Notice that at B the potential is zero while the field is not — the lesson from the previous chapter, again.",["E_A = (2kp)/(r^3)","E_B = kp/(r^3)","E_A : E_B = 2 : 1","+q","−q","V_B = 0","V_A = ±kp/(r^2)  = 0","B"],""],[0,6,18,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2007 · Single correct","Positive and negative point charges of equal magnitude are kept at (0, 0, a/2) and (0, 0, −a/2) respectively. The work done by the electric field when another positive point charge is moved from (−a, 0, 0) to (0, a, 0) is positive · negative · zero · dependent on the path connecting the initial and final positions",["(0, 0, a/2)","(0, 0, −a/2)","(−a, 0, 0)","(0, a, 0)"],""],[0,6,18,"sol","IIT-JEE 2007 · Single correct","The two charges form a dipole along the z -axis, centred at the origin. Both (−a, 0, 0) and (0, a, 0) lie in the plane z = 0 — the dipole’s perpendicular bisector plane — where every point is equidistant from +q and −q , so V = 0 . Work done by the field = q (V_(initial) − V_(final)) = q (0 − 0) = 0 . Answer (C). (D) can be rejected without any calculation: the electrostatic field is conservative, so the work never depends on the path. Where do the two end points lie relative to the dipole?",["z","(−a, 0, 0)","(0, a, 0)","z = 0","+q","−q","V = 0","= q (V_(initial) − V_(final)) = q (0 − 0) = 0"],""],[0,6,19,"sec","A dipole in someone else’s field","Now place the dipole in an external field E .",[],""],[0,6,20,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","Now place the dipole in an external field E .",["E"],""],[0,6,21,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","Uniform field. The forces +qE and −qE are equal and opposite, so the net force is zero. But they do not act along the same line, so they twist:",["+qE","−qE"],""],[0,6,22,"formula","A dipole in someone else’s field","Uniform field. The forces +qE and −qE are equal and opposite, so the net force is zero. But they do not act along the same line, so they twist:",["τ = p × E (τ = pE sin θ)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>τ</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> (<i>τ</i> = <i>pE</i> sin <i>θ</i>)"],[0,6,23,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","The torque always tries to line p up with E . The corresponding potential energy is",["p","E"],""],[0,6,24,"formula","A dipole in someone else’s field","The torque always tries to line p up with E . The corresponding potential energy is",["U = −p ⋅ E = −pE cos θ"],"<i>U</i> = −<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> = −<i>pE</i> cos <i>θ</i>"],[0,6,25,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","θ = 0 (p ∥ E): U = −pE , the minimum. Stable equilibrium. θ = 180^∘ (p anti-parallel): U = +pE , the maximum. Zero torque, but unstable. Work to turn it slowly from θ_1 to θ_2 : W = pE (cos θ_1 − cos θ_2) . Released at a small angle, it oscillates about the field direction with ω = √(pE/I) , where I is its moment of inertia.",["θ = 0","p ∥ E","U = −pE","θ = 180^∘","p","U = +pE","θ_1","θ_2","W = pE (cos θ_1 − cos θ_2)","ω = √(pE/I)","I"],""],[0,6,26,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","Non-uniform field. Now the two charges sit in slightly different fields, the forces no longer cancel, and there is a net force as well as a torque. For a dipole lying along a field that varies along its own direction,",[],""],[0,6,27,"formula","A dipole in someone else’s field","Non-uniform field. Now the two charges sit in slightly different fields, the forces no longer cancel, and there is a net force as well as a torque. For a dipole lying along a field that varies along its own direction,",["F = p dE/dx"],"<i>F</i> = <i>p</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>d<i>E</i></span><span>d<i>x</i></span></span>"],[0,6,28,"text","A dipole in someone else’s field","A dipole aligned with the field is pulled towards the region where the field is stronger. That is the full story of the comb and the paper: the comb’s field first induces (or aligns) dipoles in the paper, then drags them in.",[],""],[0,6,29,"q","Upside-down dipole · Single correct","A dipole is held in a uniform electric field with its moment p exactly anti-parallel to E . In this position it has zero force, zero torque, and is in stable equilibrium · zero force, zero torque, and is in unstable equilibrium · a non-zero net force along E · a non-zero torque",["p","E","E"],""],[0,6,29,"sol","Upside-down dipole · Single correct","Uniform field → net force is zero for any orientation. τ = pE sin 180^∘ = 0 , so it is an equilibrium position. But U = −pE cos 180^∘ = +pE is the maximum of the potential energy. Tilt it slightly and the torque p × E turns it further away, towards alignment. Unstable — like a pencil balanced on its point.",["τ = pE sin 180^∘ = 0","U = −pE cos 180^∘ = +pE","p × E"],""],[0,6,30,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","An electric dipole with dipole moment p_0/(√2) ( + ) is held fixed at the origin O in the presence of a uniform electric field of magnitude E_0 . If the potential is constant on a circle of radius R centred at the origin, as shown in the figure, then the correct statement(s) is/are (R ≫ dipole size) Point A lies on the circle along the direction of p ; point B lies on the circle perpendicular to it. R = (p_0/(4πε_0E_0))^(1/3) · The magnitude of the total electric field at any two points of the circle is the same. · The total electric field at point A is E_A = √2 E_0 ( + ) . · The total electric field at point B is E_B = 0 .",["p_0/(√2) ( + )","O","E_0","R","R ≫","A","p","B","R = (p_0/(4πε_0E_0))^(1/3)","A","E_A = √2 E_0 ( + )","B"],""],[0,6,30,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","Measure the angle θ from the direction of p (magnitude p_0), and let p be the unit vector along it. Potential on the circle. The dipole contributes (kp_0 cos θ)/(R^2) . A uniform field E_0 contributes −E_0 ⋅ r . For the total to be independent of θ , the second term must cancel the first at every angle — which needs it to vary as cos θ too, so E_0 must be along p : V_(uniform) = −E_0R cos θ . Then V = ((kp_0)/(R^2) − E_0R) cos θ. This is the same at every θ only if the bracket vanishes: (kp_0)/(R^2) = E_0R ⟹ R = (p_0/(4πε_0E_0))^(1/3) . (A) is true, and we now know (kp_0)/(R^3) = E_0 , which makes the rest easy. At A (on the dipole’s axis): the dipole’s field is (2kp_0)/(R^3)p = 2E_0 p , along p . Add the uniform field E_0 p : E_A = 3E_0 p = (3E_0)/(√2) ( + ) . Option (C) has √2E_0 in place of 3/(√2)E_0 . False. At B (on the perpendicular bisector): the dipole’s field is (kp_0)/(R^3) = E_0 , directed opposite to p . Add E_0 p : E_B = −E_0 p + E_0 p = 0. (D) is true. (B) The magnitude is 3E_0 at A and 0 at B . False. Answer (A), (D). A neat cross-check: on an equipotential the field has no tangential component, so E_θ must vanish everywhere on the circle; at B the field is purely tangential, hence zero. Add the potentials of the dipole and of the uniform field on the circle. For the sum to be the same at every angle, which way must the uniform field point?",["θ","p","p_0","p","(kp_0 cos θ)/(R^2)","E_0","−E_0 ⋅ r","θ","cos θ","E_0","p","V_(uniform) = −E_0R cos θ"],""],[0,6,31,"recap","Recap","p = q(2a) , from − to + . Far field: axial (2kp)/(r^3) (along p), equatorial kp/(r^3) (opposite p); V = (kp cos θ)/(r^2) , zero on the whole bisector plane. In a uniform field: F = 0 , τ = p × E , U = −p ⋅ E . Aligned is stable, anti-aligned unstable. In a non-uniform field: also a net force, towards stronger field when aligned.",["p = q(2a)","−","+","(2kp)/(r^3)","p","kp/(r^3)","p","V = (kp cos θ)/(r^2)","F = 0","τ = p × E","U = −p ⋅ E"],""],[0,7,0,"lesson","Conductors in Equilibrium","Give charges the freedom to move and they arrange themselves until there is nothing left to push them. Five consequences follow — and half of JEE’s shell problems with them.",[],""],[0,7,1,"sec","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","A metal contains an enormous number of electrons that are free to drift. If there were any electric field inside the metal, those electrons would feel a force and move. They keep moving — for about a millionth of a billionth of a second — until the field they themselves create cancels whatever was p",[],""],[0,7,2,"text","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","A metal contains an enormous number of electrons that are free to drift. If there were any electric field inside the metal, those electrons would feel a force and move. They keep moving — for about a millionth of a billionth of a second — until the field they themselves create cancels whatever was pushing them. Then everything is still: electrostatic equilibrium.",[],""],[0,7,3,"text","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","Everything in this lesson is a consequence of that one sentence.",[],""],[0,7,4,"text","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","E = 0 everywhere inside the conducting material. (Otherwise charges would still be moving.) Any net charge sits on the surface. Draw a Gaussian surface just inside the metal: E = 0 on it, so it encloses zero net charge. There is nowhere for excess charge to be except the surface. The whole conductor is at one potential. No field inside means no potential difference between any two points of it. Just outside, the field is perpendicular to the surface, with magnitude E = σ/ε_0 where σ is the local surface charge density. (A tangential component would push surface charges along the surface.) Charge crowds onto sharp points. Where the surface curves tightly, σ — and so E — is largest. That is why lightning conductors are pointed.",["E = 0","E = 0","E = σ/ε_0","σ","σ","E"],""],[0,7,4,"formula","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","/ε_0 where σ is the local surface charge density. (A tangential component would push surface charges along the surface.) Charge crowds onto sharp points. Where the surface curves tightly, σ — and so E — is largest. That is why lightning conductors are pointed.",["E = σ/ε_0"],"<i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span>"],[0,7,6,"text","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","DerivationWhy σ/ε_0 here, when a sheet gives σ/2ε_0 ? Take a tiny pillbox straddling the surface: one cap just outside, one inside the metal. Inside, E = 0 ; the side wall is parallel to the field. Only the outer cap has flux: EA = σA/ε_0 ⟹ E = σ/ε_0. Where did the factor of 2 go? The little patch of surface charge, viewed up close, is a sheet and produces σ/(2ε_0) on both sides. All the rest of the conductor’s charge produces another σ/(2ε_0) at that spot, directed outward. Inside the metal the two cancel (zero, as required); outside they add to σ/ε_0 .",["σ/ε_0","σ/2ε_0","E = 0","EA = σA/ε_0 ⟹ E = σ/ε_0.","σ/(2ε_0)","σ/(2ε_0)","σ/ε_0"],""],[0,7,6,"formula","The one idea: charges move until they have no reason to","f surface charge, viewed up close, is a sheet and produces σ/(2ε_0) on both sides. All the rest of the conductor’s charge produces another σ/(2ε_0) at that spot, directed outward. Inside the metal the two cancel (zero, as required); outside they add to σ/ε_0 .",["EA = σA/ε_0 ⟹ E = σ/ε_0."],"<i>E</i><i>A</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i><i>A</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> ⟹ <i>E</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span>."],[0,7,8,"sec","Cavities and shielding","Hollow out a cavity inside a conductor. Two rules, both from a Gaussian surface drawn inside the metal around the cavity (where E = 0 , so the enclosed charge is zero):",[],""],[0,7,9,"text","Cavities and shielding","Hollow out a cavity inside a conductor. Two rules, both from a Gaussian surface drawn inside the metal around the cavity (where E = 0 , so the enclosed charge is zero):",["E = 0"],""],[0,7,10,"text","Cavities and shielding","Empty cavity. No charge appears on the cavity wall and the field inside the cavity is zero — no matter what charges are outside. The metal shields its interior. This is a Faraday cage: why you are safe inside a car in a thunderstorm.",[],""],[0,7,11,"text","Cavities and shielding","Charge q placed inside the cavity. The cavity wall must carry an induced charge −q so that the Gaussian surface encloses zero. If the conductor was neutral, +q is left over and appears on the outer surface.",["q","−q","+q"],""],[0,7,12,"key","Shielding works both ways","The metal isolates the two regions from each other’s details: The charge on the cavity wall arranges itself to cancel the inner charge’s field everywhere outside the cavity. It depends on where q sits in the cavity. The charge on the outer surface arranges itself as if the cavity and its contents did not exist. It does not depend on where q sits. For a spherical conductor with nothing else nearby, it is spread uniformly. So the outside world can find out how much charge is in the cavity, but never where it is.",["q","q"],""],[0,7,13,"q","Off-centre charge in a shell · Single correct","A point charge q is placed inside a neutral, thick spherical conducting shell, but not at its centre. The electric field at a point outside the shell is zero, because the shell shields the charge · the same as that of a point charge q at the centre of the shell · the same as that of a point charge q at its actual position · dependent on the thickness of the shell",["q","q","q"],""],[0,7,13,"sol","Off-centre charge in a shell · Single correct","The inner wall acquires −q , distributed unevenly (denser near the charge) so that q and the wall charge together produce zero field everywhere beyond the inner wall. The outer surface is left with +q . It feels no field from the inside, so by symmetry it spreads uniformly over the outer sphere — and a uniform shell of charge acts, for outside points, like a point charge at its centre. So outside you see a charge q at the centre of the shell, wherever the real charge actually is. (B). The shell hides the charge’s position, not its existence — to cancel the outside field too you would have to earth the shell.",["−q","q","+q","q"],""],[0,7,14,"sec","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Nearly every “concentric shells” problem falls to one fact about a uniformly charged spherical surface of radius R_i carrying Q_i . Its potential at distance r from the centre is",[],""],[0,7,15,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Nearly every “concentric shells” problem falls to one fact about a uniformly charged spherical surface of radius R_i carrying Q_i . Its potential at distance r from the centre is",["R_i","Q_i","r"],""],[0,7,16,"formula","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Nearly every “concentric shells” problem falls to one fact about a uniformly charged spherical surface of radius R_i carrying Q_i . Its potential at distance r from the centre is",["V_i(r) = ⎩⎨⎧(kQ_i)/R_i(kQ_i)/rr ≤ R_i (you are inside it)r ≥ R_i (you are outside it)"],"<i>V</i><sub><i>i</i></sub>(<i>r</i>) = ⎩⎨⎧<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span><span><i>r</i></span></span><i>r</i> ≤ <i>R</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> (<span class=\"fx-t\">you are inside it</span>)<i>r</i> ≥ <i>R</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> (<span class=\"fx-t\">you are outside it</span>)"],[0,7,17,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","The potential anywhere is the sum of one such term per charged surface. Then apply the condition in the question:",[],""],[0,7,18,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Connected by a wire → the two conductors have equal potential. Charge flows until they do. Earthed → that conductor’s potential is zero. Its charge is whatever it needs to be to make that true — not necessarily zero. Isolated → its total charge is fixed.",[],""],[0,7,19,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Worked example — the earthed inner sphere. A conducting sphere of radius a sits inside a concentric shell of radius b carrying charge Q . The inner sphere is earthed. What charge q^′ does it hold?",["a","b","Q","q^′"],""],[0,7,20,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","The inner sphere is inside the shell’s surface and on its own surface, so",[],""],[0,7,21,"formula","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","The inner sphere is inside the shell’s surface and on its own surface, so",["V_(inner) = (kq^′)/a + kQ/b = 0 ⟹ q^′ = −a/b Q."],"<i>V</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">inner</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sup>′</sup></span><span><i>a</i></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>b</i></span></span> = 0 ⟹ <i>q</i><sup>′</sup> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>a</i></span><span><i>b</i></span></span> <i>Q</i>."],[0,7,22,"text","Concentric shells: potential bookkeeping","Earthing did not empty the sphere — it pulled charge onto it from the ground.",[],""],[0,7,23,"q","Which difference survives? · Single correct","A conducting sphere of radius a carries charge q and is surrounded by a concentric conducting shell of radius b carrying charge Q . The potential difference V_(sphere) − V_(shell) depends on both q and Q · depends only on Q · depends only on q · is zero, because both are conductors",["a","q","b","Q","V_(sphere) − V_(shell)","q","Q","Q","q"],""],[0,7,23,"sol","Which difference survives? · Single correct","Bookkeeping: V_(sphere) = kq/a + kQ/b, V_(shell) = kq/b + kQ/b. Subtract: V_(sphere) − V_(shell) = kq (1/a − 1/b) . The shell’s own charge raises the potential of everything inside it by the same amount kQ/b , so it drops out of the difference. Physically: the field in the gap is produced by q alone (Gauss), and the potential difference is the integral of that field across the gap.",["V_(sphere) = kq/a + kQ/b, V_(shell) = kq/b + kQ/b.","V_(sphere) − V_(shell) = kq (1/a − 1/b)","kQ/b","q"],""],[0,7,24,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1989 · Single correct","A solid conducting sphere having a charge Q is surrounded by an uncharged concentric conducting hollow spherical shell. Let the potential difference between the surface of the solid sphere and that of the outer surface of the hollow shell be V . If the shell is now given a charge of −3Q , the new potential difference between the same two surfaces is V · 2V · 4V · −2V",["Q","V","−3Q","V","2V","4V","−2V"],""],[0,7,24,"sol","IIT-JEE 1989 · Single correct","The potential difference between the sphere and the shell is the line integral of the field across the region between them. By Gauss’s law that field is kQ/(r^2) — fixed entirely by the charge Q on the inner sphere (inside the shell’s metal the field is zero either way). Putting −3Q on the shell changes the charge on its outer surface and shifts the potential of both conductors by the same amount. The difference is untouched: it is still V . Answer (A).",["kQ/(r^2)","Q","−3Q","V"],""],[0,7,25,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2009 · Single correct","Three concentric metallic spherical shells of radii R , 2R and 3R are given charges Q_1 , Q_2 and Q_3 respectively. It is found that the surface charge densities on the outer surfaces of the shells are equal. Then the ratio of the charges given to the shells, Q_1 : Q_2 : Q_3 , is 1 : 2 : 3 · 1 : 3 : 5 · 1 : 4 : 9 · 1 : 8 : 18",["R","2R","3R","Q_1","Q_2","Q_3","Q_1 : Q_2 : Q_3","1 : 2 : 3","1 : 3 : 5","1 : 4 : 9","1 : 8 : 18"],""],[0,7,25,"sol","IIT-JEE 2009 · Single correct","Find the outer-surface charges. A Gaussian sphere inside the metal of the second shell must enclose zero net charge, so its inner surface carries −Q_1 , leaving Q_1 + Q_2 on its outer surface. Likewise the third shell has −(Q_1 + Q_2) inside and Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3 outside. Shell Radius Charge on outer surface 1 R Q_1 2 2R Q_1 + Q_2 3 3R Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3 Equal surface densities (σ = charge /4πr^2): Q_1/(R^2) = (Q_1 + Q_2)/(4R^2) = (Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3)/(9R^2). From the first pair, Q_1 + Q_2 = 4Q_1 ⇒ Q_2 = 3Q_1 . From the first and third, Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3 = 9Q_1 ⇒ Q_3 = 5Q_1 . Q_1 : Q_2 : Q_3 = 1 : 3 : 5. Answer (B). What charge sits on the inner surface of the second shell? Use a Gaussian surface inside its metal.",["−Q_1","Q_1 + Q_2","−(Q_1 + Q_2)","Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3","R","Q_1","2R","Q_1 + Q_2","3R","Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3","σ = charge /4πr^2","Q_1/(R^2) = (Q_1 + Q_2)/(4R^2) = (Q_1 + Q_2 + Q_3)/(9R^2)."],""],[0,7,26,"sec","Sharing charge between conductors","Connect two conducting spheres of radii R_1 and R_2 , far apart, with a thin wire. Charge flows until the potentials match:",[],""],[0,7,27,"text","Sharing charge between conductors","Connect two conducting spheres of radii R_1 and R_2 , far apart, with a thin wire. Charge flows until the potentials match:",["R_1","R_2"],""],[0,7,28,"formula","Sharing charge between conductors","Connect two conducting spheres of radii R_1 and R_2 , far apart, with a thin wire. Charge flows until the potentials match:",["(kQ_1)/R_1 = (kQ_2)/R_2 ⟹ Q_1/Q_2 = R_1/R_2"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> ⟹ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[0,7,29,"text","Sharing charge between conductors","The bigger sphere takes more charge. But look at the surface density, σ = Q/4πR^2 :",["σ = Q/4πR^2"],""],[0,7,30,"formula","Sharing charge between conductors","The bigger sphere takes more charge. But look at the surface density, σ = Q/4πR^2 :",["σ_1/σ_2 = R_2/R_1"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>σ</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span>"],[0,7,31,"text","Sharing charge between conductors","The smaller sphere has the denser charge and the stronger surface field. A sharp point is just a very small sphere attached to a big conductor — which is rule 5 from the top of this lesson, now explained.",[],""],[0,7,32,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · One or more correct","A spherical metal shell A of radius R_A and a solid metal sphere B of radius R_B (< R_A) are kept far apart and each is given charge +Q . Now they are connected by a thin metal wire. Then E_A^(inside) = 0 · Q_A > Q_B · σ_A/σ_B = R_B/R_A · E_A^(on surface) < E_B^(on surface)",["A","R_A","B","R_B","< R_A","+Q","E_A^(inside) = 0","Q_A > Q_B","σ_A/σ_B = R_B/R_A","E_A^(on surface) < E_B^(on surface)"],""],[0,7,32,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · One or more correct","Once connected they form one conductor, so V_A = V_B : (kQ_A)/R_A = (kQ_B)/R_B ⟹ Q_A/Q_B = R_A/R_B > 1. (A) True. Inside a charged conducting shell there is no field (Gauss: nothing enclosed). (B) True. Q_A > Q_B , since R_A > R_B . (The total is still 2Q ; it has simply been re-divided.) (C) True. σ_A/σ_B = (Q_A/R_A^2)/(Q_B/R_B^2) = R_A/R_B ⋅ (R_B^2)/(R_A^2) = R_B/R_A . (D) True. The surface field is σ/ε_0 , and σ_A < σ_B . Answer (A), (B), (C), (D) — all four. It is perfectly legal for every option to be correct; do not talk yourself out of one.",["V_A = V_B","(kQ_A)/R_A = (kQ_B)/R_B ⟹ Q_A/Q_B = R_A/R_B > 1.","Q_A > Q_B","R_A > R_B","2Q","σ_A/σ_B = (Q_A/R_A^2)/(Q_B/R_B^2) = R_A/R_B ⋅ (R_B^2)/(R_A^2) = R_B/R_A","σ/ε_0","σ_A < σ_B"],""],[0,7,33,"sec","Electrostatic pressure and energy","The surface is being pushed outward. From the derivation above, each patch of surface charge sits in the field σ/(2ε_0) of all the other charges (a charge cannot push on itself). So the force per unit area on the surface is",[],""],[0,7,34,"text","Electrostatic pressure and energy","The surface is being pushed outward. From the derivation above, each patch of surface charge sits in the field σ/(2ε_0) of all the other charges (a charge cannot push on itself). So the force per unit area on the surface is",["σ/(2ε_0)"],""],[0,7,35,"formula","Electrostatic pressure and energy","The surface is being pushed outward. From the derivation above, each patch of surface charge sits in the field σ/(2ε_0) of all the other charges (a charge cannot push on itself). So the force per unit area on the surface is",["P = σ ⋅ σ/(2ε_0) = (σ^2)/(2ε_0) = 1/2ε_0E^2"],"<i>P</i> = <i>σ</i> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub><i>E</i><sup>2</sup>"],[0,7,36,"text","Electrostatic pressure and energy","always outward, whatever the sign of the charge. A charged soap bubble expands a little.",[],""],[0,7,37,"text","Electrostatic pressure and energy","The field stores energy. Wherever there is an electric field, there is energy, with density",[],""],[0,7,38,"formula","Electrostatic pressure and energy","The field stores energy. Wherever there is an electric field, there is energy, with density",["u = 1/2ε_0E^2 (joules per cubic metre)"],"<i>u</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub><i>E</i><sup>2</sup> (<span class=\"fx-t\">joules per cubic metre</span>)"],[0,7,39,"text","Electrostatic pressure and energy","Add this up over all space around a charged conducting sphere (or shell) of radius R and you get its self-energy — the work needed to assemble the charge:",["R"],""],[0,7,40,"formula","Electrostatic pressure and energy","Add this up over all space around a charged conducting sphere (or shell) of radius R and you get its self-energy — the work needed to assemble the charge:",["U_(shell) = (kQ^2)/(2R) = (Q^2)/(8πε_0R) U_(uniform solid sphere) = (3kQ^2)/(5R)"],"<i>U</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">shell</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>Q</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>R</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>8<i>π</i><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub><i>R</i></span></span> <i>U</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">uniform solid sphere</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>3<i>k</i><i>Q</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>5<i>R</i></span></span>"],[0,7,41,"text","Electrostatic pressure and energy","When two conductors at different potentials are connected, charge flows, the total charge is conserved — and the total electrostatic energy always decreases. The difference is lost as heat in the wire.",[],""],[0,7,42,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2010 · Single correct","A uniformly charged thin spherical shell of radius R carries uniform surface charge density of σ per unit area. It is made of two hemispherical shells, held together by pressing them with force F . F is proportional to 1/ε_0σ^2R^2 · 1/ε_0σ^2R · 1/ε_0(σ^2)/R · 1/ε_0(σ^2)/(R^2)",["R","σ","F","F","1/ε_0σ^2R^2","1/ε_0σ^2R","1/ε_0(σ^2)/R","1/ε_0(σ^2)/(R^2)"],""],[0,7,42,"sol","IIT-JEE 2010 · Single correct","Every patch of the shell feels an outward pressure P = (σ^2)/(2ε_0) , which tries to blow the two halves apart. The net force of a uniform pressure on a hemisphere is the pressure times its projected area πR^2 (the sideways components cancel around the rim — exactly as for the force of a gas on a hemispherical cap): F = (σ^2)/(2ε_0) ⋅ πR^2 ∝ (σ^2R^2)/ε_0. Answer (A). A dimensional check would have got you there too: σ^2/ε_0 is a pressure, and a force needs it multiplied by an area. Electrostatic pressure acts outward on every patch. What is the net force of a uniform pressure on a hemisphere?",["P = (σ^2)/(2ε_0)","πR^2","F = (σ^2)/(2ε_0) ⋅ πR^2 ∝ (σ^2R^2)/ε_0.","σ^2/ε_0"],""],[0,7,43,"recap","Recap","In equilibrium: E = 0 in the metal, charge on the surface, one potential throughout, E = σ/ε_0 just outside and perpendicular. Charge q in a cavity → −q on the cavity wall, +q pushed to the outer surface. The outside never learns where q is. Concentric shells: add kQ_i/R_i (inside that shell) or kQ_i/r (outside it). Wire → equal V . Earth → V = 0 . Connected spheres: Q ∝ R but σ ∝ 1/R . Pressure (σ^2)/(2ε_0) outward; energy density 1/2ε_0E^2 .",["E = 0","E = σ/ε_0","q","−q","+q","q","kQ_i/R_i","kQ_i/r","V","V = 0","Q ∝ R","σ ∝ 1/R"],""],[0,8,0,"lesson","Revision & Mixed PYQs","The whole chapter on one screen — which tool to pick, the formulas worth memorising, the traps examiners reuse — and three problems that mix it all together.",[],""],[0,8,1,"sec","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Read the question, find what is being asked, and choose:",[],""],[0,8,2,"text","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Read the question, find what is being asked, and choose:",[],""],[0,8,3,"table","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","The question asks for… Reach for… Force or field from a few point charges Coulomb + vector superposition. Look for symmetry first. Field of a rod, arc, ring or disc Chop → aim → add (integrate the surviving component). Field of something with spherical, cylindrical or planar symmetry Gauss’s law — one line. A flux Gauss, or share by symmetry / solid angle. Never integrate first. A body with a hole or cavity Whole body + negative copy of the missing piece. Speed, work, closest approach, energy Potential and energy conservation. Avoid forces. Anything involving metal E = 0 in the metal; one potential; Gaussian surface inside the metal. “Small oscillations” Find the net force near equilibrium; show F = −(const) x .",["E = 0","F = −(const) x"],""],[0,8,4,"sec","The formulas worth memorising","With k = 1/(4πε_0) = 9 × 10^9 in SI units.",[],""],[0,8,5,"text","The formulas worth memorising","With k = 1/(4πε_0) = 9 × 10^9 in SI units.",["k = 1/(4πε_0) = 9 × 10^9"],""],[0,8,6,"table","The formulas worth memorising","Source Field E Potential V Point charge kQ/(r^2) kQ/r Ring, on axis kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2)) kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2)) Infinite line (2kλ)/r −2kλ ln r + const Infinite sheet σ/(2ε_0) −σ/(2ε_0) d + const Shell, outside / inside kQ/(r^2) / 0 kQ/r / kQ/R Solid sphere, outside / inside kQ/(r^2) / kQr/(R^3) kQ/r / (kQ(3R^2 − r^2))/(2R^3) Dipole, axial / equatorial (2kp)/(r^3) / kp/(r^3) kp/(r^2) / 0 Conductor surface σ/ε_0 constant",["E","V","kQ/(r^2)","kQ/r","kQx/((x^2 + R^2)^(3/2))","kQ/(√(x^2 + R^2))","(2kλ)/r","−2kλ ln r + const","σ/(2ε_0)","−σ/(2ε_0) d + const","kQ/(r^2)","0"],""],[0,8,7,"text","The formulas worth memorising","And the relations that tie them together:",[],""],[0,8,8,"formula","The formulas worth memorising","And the relations that tie them together:",["∮ E ⋅ dA = (q_enc)/ε_0 E_r = −dV/dr U = ∑_(pairs) (kq_iq_j)/(r_ij) 1/2mv^2 + qV = const"],"∮ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> ⋅ d<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">enc</span></sub></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <i>E</i><sub><i>r</i></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>d<i>V</i></span><span>d<i>r</i></span></span> <i>U</i> = ∑<sub><span class=\"fx-t\">pairs</span></sub> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub><i>q</i><sub><i>j</i></sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub><i>ij</i></sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><i>m</i><i>v</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>q</i><i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-t\">const</span>"],[0,8,9,"formula","The formulas worth memorising","And the relations that tie them together:",["τ = p × E U_(dipole) = −p ⋅ E P = (σ^2)/(2ε_0) u = 1/2ε_0E^2"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>τ</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> <i>U</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">dipole</span></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>p</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> <i>P</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>σ</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>ε</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> <i>u</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><i>ε</i><sub>0</sub><i>E</i><sup>2</sup>"],[0,8,10,"sec","Ten traps that keep coming back","",[],""],[0,8,11,"text","Ten traps that keep coming back","Inside or outside? A point can be outside one sphere and inside another. Decide separately for every piece of charge. Signs in V and U . Substitute charges with their signs. Always. E = 0  ⇒ V = 0 , and vice versa. Inside a shell E = 0 but V = kQ/R . Gauss’s E is the total field; only the net flux ignores outside charges. Earthed does not mean uncharged. It means V = 0 . p points from − to + — against the field lines between the charges. Sheet: σ/2ε_0 . Conductor surface: σ/ε_0 . Know which one you have. Equilibrium of a system means every charge is balanced, not just the convenient one. “At distance d along the line” may describe two points. Check both sides. Units and powers of ten: μ C , nC , cm . Convert before you substitute.",["V","U","E = 0  ⇒ V = 0","E = 0","V = kQ/R","E","V = 0","p","−","+","σ/2ε_0","σ/ε_0"],""],[0,8,12,"sec","Mixed practice: three questions that span the chapter","",[],""],[0,8,13,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2018 · Single correct","The electric field E is measured at a point P(0, 0, d) generated due to various charge distributions, and the dependence of E on d is found to be different for different charge distributions. List-I contains different relations between E and d . List-II describes different electric charge distributions, along with their locations. Match the functions in List-I with the related charge distributions in List-II. List-I List-II P. E is independent of d 1. A point charge Q at the origin Q. E ∝ 1/d 2. A small dipole with point charges Q at (0, 0, l) and −Q at (0, 0, −l) ; take 2l ≪ d R. E ∝ 1/(d^2) 3. An infinite line charge coincident with the x -axis, with uniform linear charge density λ S. E ∝ 1/(d^3) 4. Two infinite wires carrying uniform linear charge density, parallel to the x -axis: the one along (y = 0, z = l) has charge density +λ and the one along (y = 0, z = −l) has −λ ; take 2l ≪ d 5. An infinite plane charge coincident with the xy -plane, with uniform surface charge density P → 5; Q → 3, 4; R → 1; S → 2 · P → 5; Q → 3; R → 1, 4; S → 2 · P → 5; Q → 3; R → 1, 2; S → 4 · P → 4; Q → 2, 3; R → 1; S → 5",["E","P(0, 0, d)","E","d","E","d","E","d","Q","E ∝ 1/d","Q","(0, 0, l)"],""],[0,8,13,"sol","JEE Advanced 2018 · Single correct","Go down List-II and write the d -dependence of each field at P . 1 · Point charge: E = kQ/(d^2) ∝ 1/(d^2) → R 2 · Dipole (on its axis): E = (2kp)/(d^3) ∝ 1/(d^3) → S 3 · Infinite line: E = (2kλ)/d ∝ 1/d → Q 5 · Infinite plane: E = σ/(2ε_0) , independent of d → P 4 · Two opposite wires. P is at distance d − l from the +λ wire and d + l from the −λ wire, and both fields lie along z : E = 2kλ [1/(d − l) − 1/(d + l)] = (4kλ l)/(d^2 − l^2) ≈ (4kλ l)/(d^2) ∝ 1/(d^2) → R This is a “line dipole”: just as pairing two point charges turns 1/d^2 into 1/d^3 , pairing two opposite wires turns 1/d into 1/d^2 . So P → 5; Q → 3; R → 1, 4; S → 2. Answer (B). Four of the five are standard results. For the pair of opposite wires, subtract two 1/r fields at distances d − l and d + l .",["d","P","E = kQ/(d^2) ∝ 1/(d^2)","E = (2kp)/(d^3) ∝ 1/(d^3)","E = (2kλ)/d ∝ 1/d","E = σ/(2ε_0)","d","P","d − l","+λ","d + l","−λ"],""],[0,8,14,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2018 · One or more correct","An infinitely long thin non-conducting wire is parallel to the z -axis and carries a uniform line charge density λ . It pierces a thin non-conducting spherical shell of radius R in such a way that the arc PQ subtends an angle 120^∘ at the centre O of the spherical shell, as shown in the figure. The permittivity of free space is ε_0 . Which of the following statements is (are) true? The electric flux through the shell is (√3 Rλ)/ε_0 . · The z -component of the electric field is zero at all the points on the surface of the shell. · The electric flux through the shell is (√2 Rλ)/ε_0 . · The electric field is normal to the surface of the shell at all points.",["z","λ","R","PQ","120^∘","O","ε_0","(√3 Rλ)/ε_0","z","(√2 Rλ)/ε_0"],""],[0,8,14,"sol","JEE Advanced 2018 · One or more correct","Flux. By Gauss’s law we need the charge on the length of wire inside the sphere — the chord PQ . A chord subtending 120^∘ at the centre has length PQ = 2R sin 60^∘ = √3 R, so q_enc = √3Rλ and Φ = (√3 Rλ)/ε_0 . (A) true, (C) false. Field. The shell is non-conducting and merely “imaginary” as far as the field is concerned: the field everywhere is that of an infinite straight wire, pointing radially away from the wire, perpendicular to z . So E_z = 0 at every point of the shell — (B) true. That radial-from-the-wire direction is generally not along the sphere’s own radius, so the field is not normal to the shell — (D) false. Answer (A), (B). The flux needs only the length of wire inside the shell. The field itself is still just the field of an infinite wire.",["PQ","120^∘","PQ = 2R sin 60^∘ = √3 R,","q_enc = √3Rλ","Φ = (√3 Rλ)/ε_0","z","E_z = 0"],""],[0,8,15,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","Charges Q , 2Q and 4Q are uniformly distributed in three dielectric solid spheres 1, 2 and 3 of radii R/2 , R and 2R respectively. If the magnitudes of the electric fields at a point P at a distance R from the centre of spheres 1, 2 and 3 are E_1 , E_2 and E_3 respectively, then E_1 > E_2 > E_3 · E_3 > E_1 > E_2 · E_2 > E_1 > E_3 · E_3 > E_2 > E_1",["Q","2Q","4Q","R/2","R","2R","P","R","E_1","E_2","E_3","E_1 > E_2 > E_3"],""],[0,8,15,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","The point is at the same distance R from each centre, but its position relative to each sphere differs. Sphere 1 (radius R/2): the point is outside. E_1 = kQ/(R^2) . Sphere 2 (radius R): the point is on the surface. E_2 = (k(2Q))/(R^2) = (2kQ)/(R^2) . Sphere 3 (radius 2R): the point is inside. E_3 = (k(4Q) R)/((2R)^3) = kQ/(2R^2) . So E_2 > E_1 > E_3 . Answer (C). The sphere with the most charge gives the smallest field, because only one-eighth of its charge lies inside radius R . Trap number 1. For each sphere: is a point at distance R from the centre inside, on, or outside it?",["R","R/2","E_1 = kQ/(R^2)","R","E_2 = (k(2Q))/(R^2) = (2kQ)/(R^2)","2R","E_3 = (k(4Q) R)/((2R)^3) = kQ/(2R^2)","E_2 > E_1 > E_3","R","R"],""],[0,8,16,"story","Where next","You now own the four tools — force, field, flux, potential — and you have used them on thirty real JEE problems. The next chapter, Capacitance, puts them to work: two conductors, a potential difference, and the energy stored in the field between them. Every idea there is one you have already met here. Before moving on, go back through the lessons and redo — on paper, without looking — every question you marked wrong or revealed. That second pass is where the learning actually happens.",[],""],[1,1,0,"lesson","Electric Current & Drift Velocity","Flip a switch and the light is on before you can blink — yet the electrons in the wire crawl along slower than a snail. Solve that mystery and you understand what a current really is.",[],""],[1,1,1,"sec","The great switch mystery","Flip the switch by the door. The tube light on the far wall, five metres of wire away, comes on at once.",[],""],[1,1,2,"text","The great switch mystery","Flip the switch by the door. The tube light on the far wall, five metres of wire away, comes on at once.",[],""],[1,1,3,"text","The great switch mystery","Here is the strange part. The electrons in that copper wire drift along at well under a tenth of a millimetre per second. At that pace, an electron leaving the switch would need the best part of a day to reach the lamp. A garden snail at full tilt (about a centimetre a second) would beat it a hundred times over.",[],""],[1,1,4,"text","The great switch mystery","So nothing travelled from the switch to the lamp in the time it took the light to come on. And yet the lamp lit. What actually moved?",[],""],[1,1,5,"text","The great switch mystery","Hold that question. By the end of this lesson you will be able to answer it — and to calculate that snail-beating speed yourself.",[],""],[1,1,6,"story","The plan for this chapter","In Electrostatics, charges sat still and we asked what they do to the space around them. Now we let them move. Five ideas, in order: what a current is (this lesson) → what resists it (Ohm’s law) → how to analyse any circuit (combinations, cells, Kirchhoff) → where the energy goes (heating and power) → how to measure it all without ruining it (meters and bridges). JEE loves this chapter because one small circuit can test all five at once.",[],""],[1,1,7,"sec","Current: counting charge on the move","Stand at any cross-section of a wire and count the charge passing through. The rate at which it passes is the electric current:",[],""],[1,1,8,"text","Current: counting charge on the move","Stand at any cross-section of a wire and count the charge passing through. The rate at which it passes is the electric current:",[],""],[1,1,9,"formula","Current: counting charge on the move","Stand at any cross-section of a wire and count the charge passing through. The rate at which it passes is the electric current:",["I = dq/dt unit: ampere (A) = coulomb per second"],"<i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>q</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>t</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: ampere (A)</span> = <span class=\"fx-t\">coulomb per second</span>"],[1,1,10,"text","Current: counting charge on the move","For a steady current this is simply I = q/t . One ampere means one coulomb every second — about 6.25 × 10^18 electrons streaming past each second.",["I = q/t","6.25 × 10^18"],""],[1,1,11,"text","Current: counting charge on the move","If the current changes with time, turn the definition around: the charge that has passed is the area under the I – t graph,",["I","t"],""],[1,1,12,"formula","Current: counting charge on the move","If the current changes with time, turn the definition around: the charge that has passed is the area under the I – t graph,",["q = ∫ I dt."],"<i>q</i> = ∫ <i>I</i> <i>d</i><i>t</i>."],[1,1,13,"text","Current: counting charge on the move","Which way does it point? By convention, current flows in the direction a positive charge would move — from higher potential to lower potential through the wire. In a metal the charges that actually move are electrons, which are negative, so they travel the opposite way. (The convention was fixed long before anyone knew electrons existed. It costs us nothing: negative charge moving left is, for every purpose in this chapter, the same thing as positive charge moving right.)",[],""],[1,1,14,"intuition","Getting a feel for the ampere","An indicator LED sips about 20 mA . A phone charger delivers one to three amperes. A typical lightning stroke carries around 30, 000 A — but only for a tiny fraction of a second.",["20 mA","30, 000 A"],""],[1,1,15,"trap","Current has a direction, but it is not a vector","Vectors add by the parallelogram law. Currents do not. If 3 A and 4 A flow into a junction along two wires, 7 A flows out — whatever the angle between the wires. Current is a scalar; the arrow on a circuit diagram only tells you the sense of flow along the wire.",["3 A","4 A","7 A"],""],[1,1,16,"q","Area under the graph · Integer answer","The current in a wire varies with time as I = (2t + 3t^2) A , where t is in seconds. How many coulombs of charge pass through a cross-section between t = 0 and t = 2 s ?",["I = (2t + 3t^2) A","t","t = 0","t = 2 s"],""],[1,1,16,"sol","Area under the graph · Integer answer","q = ∫_0^2(2t + 3t^2) dt = [t^2 + t^3]_0^2 = 4 + 8 = 12 C . Multiplying the final current (16 A) by 2 s gives 32 C — far too much, because the current was smaller than that for the whole journey. The current is not constant, so q = It will not do. Integrate.",["q = ∫_0^2(2t + 3t^2) dt = [t^2 + t^3]_0^2 = 4 + 8 = 12 C .","16 A","2 s","32 C","q = It"],""],[1,1,17,"sec","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","A current of 1 A in a thick cable is a gentle affair. The same 1 A squeezed through a hair-thin filament is violent enough to make it glow. What differs is the current per unit area, the current density:",[],""],[1,1,18,"text","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","A current of 1 A in a thick cable is a gentle affair. The same 1 A squeezed through a hair-thin filament is violent enough to make it glow. What differs is the current per unit area, the current density:",["1 A","1 A"],""],[1,1,19,"formula","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","A current of 1 A in a thick cable is a gentle affair. The same 1 A squeezed through a hair-thin filament is violent enough to make it glow. What differs is the current per unit area, the current density:",["J = I/A unit: A m ^(−2)"],"<i>J</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span><i>A</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: </span><span class=\"fx-t\">A m</span><sup>−2</sup>"],[1,1,20,"text","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","Unlike I , current density is a vector, J . It points along the flow of positive charge at each point (so along the electric field inside a conductor), and in general",["I","J"],""],[1,1,21,"formula","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","Unlike I , current density is a vector, J . It points along the flow of positive charge at each point (so along the electric field inside a conductor), and in general",["I = ∫ J ⋅ dA."],"<i>I</i> = ∫ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>J</i></span> ⋅ <i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span>."],[1,1,22,"text","Current density: how crowded is the flow?","Keep the two apart: I belongs to the whole wire, J to a point inside it. If a wire gets narrower along its length, the same I flows through every cross-section (charge cannot pile up in a steady state) — but J rises where the wire is thin.",["I","J","I","J"],""],[1,1,23,"sec","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","A metal is a lattice of positive ions swimming in a sea of free electrons — about one per atom in copper, which makes an enormous number: n ≈ 8.5 × 10^28 per cubic metre.",[],""],[1,1,24,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","A metal is a lattice of positive ions swimming in a sea of free electrons — about one per atom in copper, which makes an enormous number: n ≈ 8.5 × 10^28 per cubic metre.",["n ≈ 8.5 × 10^28"],""],[1,1,25,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","No battery connected. The free electrons are anything but still. They race about at roughly a million metres per second, bouncing off the vibrating ions and changing direction at random. But random is the key word: as many go left as go right. The average velocity is zero, so there is no current.",[],""],[1,1,26,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","Battery connected. Now there is an electric field E inside the wire, and every electron feels a force −eE . Between two collisions an electron accelerates steadily, opposite to the field:",["E","−eE"],""],[1,1,27,"formula","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","Battery connected. Now there is an electric field E inside the wire, and every electron feels a force −eE . Between two collisions an electron accelerates steadily, opposite to the field:",["a = eE/m."],"<i>a</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>E</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>."],[1,1,28,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","Then it hits an ion, loses all memory of the little extra velocity it had gained, and starts again. Speed up, crash, forget. Speed up, crash, forget.",[],""],[1,1,29,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","The result is a slow, steady slide superimposed on the frantic random motion. That slide is the drift velocity. If τ is the average time between collisions (the relaxation time), the average extra velocity an electron has picked up is",["τ"],""],[1,1,30,"formula","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","The result is a slow, steady slide superimposed on the frantic random motion. That slide is the drift velocity. If τ is the average time between collisions (the relaxation time), the average extra velocity an electron has picked up is",["v_d = eEτ/m"],"<i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>E</i><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>"],[1,1,31,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","directed opposite to E .",["E"],""],[1,1,32,"text","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","DerivationWhy the average comes out as eEτ/m Take one electron. Just after its last collision it had some random velocity u . A time t later its velocity is v = u − eE/m t. Now average over all the electrons. The u ’s point every which way, so their average is zero. The average of t — the time since the last collision — is, by definition, the relaxation time τ . Hence v_d = ⟨v⟩ = −eE/m τ. The minus sign says electrons drift against the field.",["eEτ/m","u","t","v = u − eE/m t.","u","t","τ","v_d = ⟨v⟩ = −eE/m τ."],""],[1,1,32,"formula","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","ver all the electrons. The u ’s point every which way, so their average is zero. The average of t — the time since the last collision — is, by definition, the relaxation time τ . Hence v_d = ⟨v⟩ = −eE/m τ. The minus sign says electrons drift against the field.",["v = u − eE/m t."],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>u</i></span> − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span></span><span><i>m</i></span></span> <i>t</i>."],[1,1,32,"formula","Inside a wire: chaos with a tiny bias","ver all the electrons. The u ’s point every which way, so their average is zero. The average of t — the time since the last collision — is, by definition, the relaxation time τ . Hence v_d = ⟨v⟩ = −eE/m τ. The minus sign says electrons drift against the field.",["v_d = ⟨v⟩ = −eE/m τ."],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span><sub><i>d</i></sub> = ⟨<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span>⟩ = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span></span><span><i>m</i></span></span> <i>τ</i>."],[1,1,35,"intuition","A swarm of bees in a breeze","Picture a swarm of bees buzzing furiously in all directions — and a faint breeze nudging the whole swarm slowly down the garden. The buzzing is the thermal motion. The slow slide of the swarm is the drift. Only the slide carries any net charge anywhere.",[],""],[1,1,36,"sec","From drift to current","How much charge does that slow slide carry? Look at a wire of cross-section A . In a time dt every free electron shifts a distance v_d dt . So all the electrons in a slab of length v_d dt — volume A v_d dt — cross the end face. There are n of them per unit volume, each carrying charge e :",[],""],[1,1,37,"text","From drift to current","How much charge does that slow slide carry? Look at a wire of cross-section A . In a time dt every free electron shifts a distance v_d dt . So all the electrons in a slab of length v_d dt — volume A v_d dt — cross the end face. There are n of them per unit volume, each carrying charge e :",["A","dt","v_d dt","v_d dt","A v_d dt","n","e"],""],[1,1,38,"formula","From drift to current","w slide carry? Look at a wire of cross-section A . In a time dt every free electron shifts a distance v_d dt . So all the electrons in a slab of length v_d dt — volume A v_d dt — cross the end face. There are n of them per unit volume, each carrying charge e :",["dq = n e A v_d dt ⟹ I = neAv_d J = nev_d"],"<i>d</i><i>q</i> = <i>n</i> <i>e</i> <i>A</i> <i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> <i>d</i><i>t</i> ⟹ <i>I</i> = <i>n</i><i>e</i><i>A</i><i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> <i>J</i> = <i>n</i><i>e</i><i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub>"],[1,1,39,"text","From drift to current","Now put in numbers. A copper wire of cross-section 1 mm ^2 = 10^(−6) m ^2 carries 1 A :",["1 mm ^2 = 10^(−6) m ^2","1 A"],""],[1,1,40,"formula","From drift to current","Now put in numbers. A copper wire of cross-section 1 mm ^2 = 10^(−6) m ^2 carries 1 A :",["v_d = I/neA = 1/((8.5 × 10^28)(1.6 × 10^(−19))(10^(−6))) ≈ 7 × 10^(−5) m s ^(−1)."],"<i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span><i>n</i><i>e</i><i>A</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>(8.5 × 10<sup>28</sup>)(1.6 × 10<sup>−19</sup>)(10<sup>−6</sup>)</span></span> ≈ 7 × 10<sup>−5</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">m s</span><sup>−1</sup>."],[1,1,41,"text","From drift to current","That is 0.07 millimetres per second. To cover one metre the electron needs nearly four hours.",["0.07"],""],[1,1,42,"text","From drift to current","The reason the speed is so tiny is that n is so huge. With 8.5 × 10^28 carriers in every cubic metre, they barely have to move to deliver an ampere.",["n","8.5 × 10^28"],""],[1,1,43,"q","Thicker wire, bigger current · Single correct","A copper wire carries a current I and its electrons have drift speed v . It is replaced by a copper wire of twice the diameter carrying a current 2I . The drift speed is now 2v · v · v/2 · v/4",["I","v","2I","2v","v","v/2","v/4"],""],[1,1,43,"sol","Thicker wire, bigger current · Single correct","From I = neAv_d , the drift speed is v_d = I/(neA) — proportional to I/A . Doubling the diameter makes the area four times larger. So v_d^′ = (2I)/(ne(4A)) = v/2. Answer (C). The slip to avoid is doubling the area instead of quadrupling it: area goes as the square of the diameter.",["I = neAv_d","v_d = I/(neA)","I/A","v_d^′ = (2I)/(ne(4A)) = v/2."],""],[1,1,44,"sec","So why does the light come on instantly?","Because the wire was never empty.",[],""],[1,1,45,"text","So why does the light come on instantly?","Because the wire was never empty.",[],""],[1,1,46,"text","So why does the light come on instantly?","Every millimetre of the wire is already packed with free electrons. When you close the switch, what races down the wire is not electrons but the electric field — the news that “there is a push now”. That news travels at close to the speed of light. Within a fraction of a microsecond every free electron along the whole circuit, including the ones already sitting inside the lamp’s filament, begins to drift.",[],""],[1,1,47,"text","So why does the light come on instantly?","Think of a garden hose that is already full of water. Open the tap and water comes out of the far end at once — not the water that has just left the tap, but the water that was already waiting at the nozzle.",[],""],[1,1,48,"key","Three speeds — never mix them up","Typical size What it is Random (thermal) speed ∼ 10^6 m s ^(−1) frantic motion between collisions; averages to zero Drift speed ∼ 10^(−4) m s ^(−1) the tiny net slide that is the current Signal speed ∼ 10^8 m s ^(−1) how fast the field (the “go!” command) spreads along the wire (The simple classical picture used in JEE estimates the thermal speed as √(3kT/m) ≈ 10^5 m s ^(−1) at room temperature; the true quantum value for copper is about ten times larger. Either way it dwarfs the drift speed by nine or ten orders of magnitude.)",["∼ 10^6 m s ^(−1)","∼ 10^(−4) m s ^(−1)","∼ 10^8 m s ^(−1)","√(3kT/m) ≈ 10^5 m s ^(−1)"],""],[1,1,49,"text","So why does the light come on instantly?","A bonus fact to enjoy. The mains supply in your house is alternating: the field reverses direction 100 times a second (a 50 Hz supply). So the electrons in your wiring do not even crawl forward — they shuffle back and forth by a few thousandths of a millimetre at most and never leave home. What you pay the electricity board for is not electrons. It is energy.",[],""],[1,1,50,"lab","Watch the electrons crawl","A short length of copper wire, zoomed in until you can see the free electrons. The battery’s field points to the right, so conventional current flows right — and the electrons drift left. Try these, in order: Set the current to zero. The electrons still dash about madly, but the crowd as a whole goes nowhere. No net flow, no current. Raise the current slowly. Watch the whole crowd begin to slide to the left. Read the drift speed — and how long an electron would need to cover one metre. Tap Follow one electron. Its path is a drunkard’s walk with a slight lean. That lean is the entire current. Keep the current fixed and make the wire thinner. Same charge per second through a smaller door, so each electron must hurry: v_d = I/(neA) . Switch Thermal motion off to see the bare mechanism: speed up, collide, forget, repeat. In a real wire the random motion is about ten thousand million times faster than the drift. The lab slows it down enormously so that you can see both at once.",["v_d = I/(neA)"],""],[1,1,51,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","A steady current flows in a metallic conductor of non-uniform cross-section. The quantity or quantities that remain constant along the length of the conductor is/are current, electric field and drift speed · drift speed only · current and drift speed · current only",[],""],[1,1,51,"sol","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","In a steady state, charge cannot pile up anywhere. So the charge crossing every cross-section per second is the same: the current is constant along the conductor. Everything else depends on the area: J = I/A changes where the area changes, v_d = J/(ne) follows J — the electrons hurry through the narrow parts, v_d = eEτ/m means E ∝ v_d , so the field is stronger in the narrow parts too. Answer (D). A river behaves the same way: the same volume of water passes every point each second, but the water runs fastest where the channel is narrowest.",["J = I/A","v_d = J/(ne)","J","v_d = eEτ/m","E ∝ v_d"],""],[1,1,52,"sec","Mobility","How easily do the carriers respond to a field? That is measured by the mobility — drift speed per unit field:",[],""],[1,1,53,"text","Mobility","How easily do the carriers respond to a field? That is measured by the mobility — drift speed per unit field:",[],""],[1,1,54,"formula","Mobility","How easily do the carriers respond to a field? That is measured by the mobility — drift speed per unit field:",["μ = v_d/E = eτ/m unit: m ^2 V ^(−1) s ^(−1)"],"<i>μ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub></span><span><i>E</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: </span><span class=\"fx-t\">m</span><sup>2</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">V</span><sup>−1</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">s</span><sup>−1</sup>"],[1,1,55,"text","Mobility","With it, the current density reads J = nev_d = neμE . Mobility is the natural language for semiconductors, where both electrons and holes carry current and each has its own μ .",["J = nev_d = neμE","μ"],""],[1,1,56,"text","Mobility","Notice what v_d = eEτ/m says for a uniform wire of length L with a potential difference V across it. The field is E = V/L , so",["v_d = eEτ/m","L","V","E = V/L"],""],[1,1,57,"formula","Mobility","Notice what v_d = eEτ/m says for a uniform wire of length L with a potential difference V across it. The field is E = V/L , so",["v_d = eτ/m ⋅ V/L."],"<i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span>."],[1,1,58,"text","Mobility","The drift speed is set by the field — by V and L — and by the material (through τ). The thickness of the wire does not appear at all. That makes for a favourite examiner’s question:",["V","L","τ"],""],[1,1,59,"q","Who controls the drift speed? · One or more correct","A potential difference V is maintained across a uniform copper wire of length L and diameter d . The drift speed of the electrons is v . The temperature stays constant. Which of the following is/are correct? If V is doubled (same L , d), the drift speed becomes 2v . · If L is doubled (same V , d), the drift speed becomes v/2 . · If d is doubled (same V , L), the drift speed stays v . · If d is doubled (same V , L), the current doubles.",["V","L","d","v","V","L","d","2v","L","V","d","v/2"],""],[1,1,59,"sol","Who controls the drift speed? · One or more correct","v_d = eEτ/m = eτ/m ⋅ V/L. (A) v_d ∝ V — doubling V doubles the field and the drift speed. Correct. (B) v_d ∝ 1/L — the same voltage spread over twice the length gives half the field. Correct. (C) The diameter does not appear. A fatter wire has the same field, so each electron drifts exactly as before. Correct. (D) The current is I = neAv_d . With v_d unchanged and A four times larger, the current becomes four times larger, not two. Wrong. Answer (A), (B), (C). A fat wire carries more current not because its electrons move faster, but because more of them are moving. Write v_d in terms of the electric field, and the field in terms of V and L . Does the area appear?",["v_d = eEτ/m = eτ/m ⋅ V/L.","v_d ∝ V","V","v_d ∝ 1/L","I = neAv_d","v_d","A","v_d","V","L"],""],[1,1,60,"recap","Recap","Current I = dq/dt is a scalar; charge passed is the area under the I – t graph. Current density J (J = I/A) is a vector. Free electrons have a huge random speed that averages to zero, plus a tiny drift opposite to the field: v_d = eEτ/m . I = neAv_d and J = nev_d . Drift speeds are of the order of 0.1 mm s ^(−1) because n is enormous. The lamp lights at once because the field spreads at nearly the speed of light and sets every electron drifting together. In a wire of varying thickness the current is the same everywhere; J , v_d and E are all larger where the wire is thinner. Mobility μ = v_d/E = eτ/m . @end",["I = dq/dt","I","t","J","J = I/A","v_d = eEτ/m","I = neAv_d","J = nev_d","0.1 mm s ^(−1)","n","J","v_d"],""],[1,2,0,"lesson","Ohm’s Law, Resistance & Resistivity","Why does a longer wire fight the current harder, a fatter one less — and a hot one more? One tiny collision time explains Ohm’s law, resistance, and why a bulb is most likely to blow at the moment you switch it on.",[],""],[1,2,1,"sec","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Lesson 1 gave us two facts about the electrons in a wire:",[],""],[1,2,2,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Lesson 1 gave us two facts about the electrons in a wire:",[],""],[1,2,3,"formula","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Lesson 1 gave us two facts about the electrons in a wire:",["J = nev_d and v_d = eEτ/m."],"<i>J</i> = <i>n</i><i>e</i><i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> <span class=\"fx-t\">and</span> <i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>E</i><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>."],[1,2,4,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Put the second into the first:",[],""],[1,2,5,"formula","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Put the second into the first:",["J = ((ne^2τ)/m) E."],"<i>J</i> = (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>e</i><sup>2</sup><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>) <i>E</i>."],[1,2,6,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Everything in the bracket belongs to the material: how many carriers it has, and how long they survive between collisions. Call the bracket the conductivity σ , and its reciprocal the resistivity ρ :",["σ","ρ"],""],[1,2,7,"formula","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Everything in the bracket belongs to the material: how many carriers it has, and how long they survive between collisions. Call the bracket the conductivity σ , and its reciprocal the resistivity ρ :",["J = σE σ = (ne^2τ)/m, ρ = 1/σ = m/(ne^2τ)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>J</i></span> = <i>σ</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> <i>σ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>e</i><sup>2</sup><i>τ</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>, <i>ρ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>σ</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i></span><span><i>n</i><i>e</i><sup>2</sup><i>τ</i></span></span>"],[1,2,8,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","This is Ohm’s law in its purest form: at every point in the material, the current density is proportional to the field.",[],""],[1,2,9,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Now apply it to a whole wire of length L and cross-section A with potential difference V across it. The field is E = V/L and the current density is J = I/A , so",["L","A","V","E = V/L","J = I/A"],""],[1,2,10,"formula","Ohm’s law, from the inside","Now apply it to a whole wire of length L and cross-section A with potential difference V across it. The field is E = V/L and the current density is J = I/A , so",["I/A = 1/ρ ⋅ V/L ⟹ V = I (ρL/A) = IR."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span><i>A</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>ρ</i></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> ⟹ <i>V</i> = <i>I</i> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>A</i></span></span>) = <i>I</i><i>R</i>."],[1,2,11,"text","Ohm’s law, from the inside","There it is: V = IR , with nothing assumed except that τ does not depend on the field.",["V = IR","τ"],""],[1,2,12,"story","The schoolteacher nobody believed","Georg Simon Ohm was a school mathematics teacher in Cologne when he published this law in 1827, after years of patient experiments with apparatus he built himself. The reception was so cold that he resigned his post. Recognition came fourteen years later, when the Royal Society awarded him its Copley Medal — and eventually the unit of resistance took his name.",[],""],[1,2,13,"sec","Resistance = material × geometry","Resistivity has one of the widest ranges of any physical property:",[],""],[1,2,14,"formula","Resistance = material × geometry","",["R = ρ L/A unit: ohm (Ω) = V A ^(−1)"],"<i>R</i> = <i>ρ</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>L</i></span><span><i>A</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: ohm </span>(Ω) = <span class=\"fx-t\">V A</span><sup>−1</sup>"],[1,2,15,"text","Resistance = material × geometry","Longer wire → more collisions along the way → more resistance. Fatter wire → more lanes for the traffic → less resistance. ρ (unit Ω m) is the part that belongs to the material, whatever its shape.",["ρ","Ω m"],""],[1,2,16,"text","Resistance = material × geometry","Resistivity has one of the widest ranges of any physical property:",[],""],[1,2,17,"table","Resistance = material × geometry","Material Resistivity at 20 ^∘ C (Ω m) Silver 1.6 × 10^(−8) the best conductor Copper 1.7 × 10^(−8) house wiring Aluminium 2.7 × 10^(−8) overhead power lines Tungsten 5.6 × 10^(−8) lamp filaments Nichrome (alloy) ≈ 1 × 10^(−6) heater coils Silicon (pure) ≈ 2 × 10^3 a semiconductor Glass 10^10 to 10^14 an insulator Fused quartz ≈ 10^16 about the best insulator there is",["20 ^∘ C","Ω m","1.6 × 10^(−8)","1.7 × 10^(−8)","2.7 × 10^(−8)","5.6 × 10^(−8)","≈ 1 × 10^(−6)","≈ 2 × 10^3","10^10","10^14","≈ 10^16"],""],[1,2,18,"text","Resistance = material × geometry","From silver to quartz is a factor of about 10^24 . Few quantities in physics span such a range.",["10^24"],""],[1,2,19,"text","Resistance = material × geometry","The same block, three different resistors. Resistance is not a property of an object alone — it depends on which way the current goes through it. Take a slab measuring 1 cm × 2 cm × 4 cm . Send the current along the 4 cm edge and it sees a long, thin path (L = 4 , A = 1 × 2). Send it along the 1 cm edge and the path is short and fat (L = 1 , A = 2 × 4). The two resistances differ by a factor of",["1 cm × 2 cm × 4 cm","4 cm","L = 4","A = 1 × 2","1 cm","L = 1","A = 2 × 4"],""],[1,2,20,"formula","Resistance = material × geometry","ough it. Take a slab measuring 1 cm × 2 cm × 4 cm . Send the current along the 4 cm edge and it sees a long, thin path (L = 4 , A = 1 × 2). Send it along the 1 cm edge and the path is short and fat (L = 1 , A = 2 × 4). The two resistances differ by a factor of",["(4/2)/(1/8) = 16."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>4/2</span><span>1/8</span></span> = 16."],[1,2,21,"key","Stretching a wire","Pull a wire to n times its length. Its volume cannot change, so the area shrinks to A/n : R^′ = ρ nL/(A/n) = n^2R. Longer and thinner, so the resistance goes up as the square. For a small stretch, a 1% increase in length gives a 2% increase in resistance.",["n","A/n","R^′ = ρ nL/(A/n) = n^2R.","1%","2%"],""],[1,2,21,"formula","Stretching a wire","Pull a wire to n times its length. Its volume cannot change, so the area shrinks to A/n : R^′ = ρ nL/(A/n) = n^2R. Longer and thinner, so the resistance goes up as the square. For a small stretch, a 1% increase in length gives a 2% increase in resistance.",["R^′ = ρ nL/(A/n) = n^2R."],"<i>R</i><sup>′</sup> = <i>ρ</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>A</i>/<i>n</i></span></span> = <i>n</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i>."],[1,2,23,"q","Through the die · Integer answer","A wire of resistance 5 Ω is drawn through a die until it is three times as long. Its volume and resistivity do not change. What is its new resistance, in ohms?",["5 Ω"],""],[1,2,23,"sol","Through the die · Integer answer","Volume = AL is fixed, so tripling L cuts the area to A/3 : R^′ = ρ (3L)/(A/3) = 9 ρL/A = 9 × 5 = 45 Ω. Answering 15 Ω means you let the wire get longer but forgot it also got thinner.",["= AL","L","A/3","R^′ = ρ (3L)/(A/3) = 9 ρL/A = 9 × 5 = 45 Ω.","15 Ω"],""],[1,2,24,"sec","When the path is not a simple wire","R = ρL/A assumes the same area all the way along. When the area changes, slice the conductor into thin slabs perpendicular to the current, each one a tiny resistor dR = ρ dx/A(x) , and add them in series:",[],""],[1,2,25,"text","When the path is not a simple wire","R = ρL/A assumes the same area all the way along. When the area changes, slice the conductor into thin slabs perpendicular to the current, each one a tiny resistor dR = ρ dx/A(x) , and add them in series:",["R = ρL/A","dR = ρ dx/A(x)"],""],[1,2,26,"formula","When the path is not a simple wire","R = ρL/A assumes the same area all the way along. When the area changes, slice the conductor into thin slabs perpendicular to the current, each one a tiny resistor dR = ρ dx/A(x) , and add them in series:",["R = ∫ ρdx/(A(x))."],"<i>R</i> = ∫ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i></span><span><i>A</i>(<i>x</i>)</span></span>."],[1,2,27,"text","When the path is not a simple wire","Two results worth knowing how to get:",[],""],[1,2,28,"text","When the path is not a simple wire","DerivationA tapered wire (a truncated cone), end radii a and b , length L At a distance x from the narrow end the radius is r = a + (b − a) x/L , so dx = L/(b − a) dr and R = ∫_0^L ρdx/(πr^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) ∫_a^b dr/(r^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) (1/a − 1/b) = ρL/πab. A neat check: put a = b and you are back to ρL/(πa^2) . (The slicing treats each slab as carrying uniform current, which is a good approximation for a gentle taper — and it is the answer JEE expects.)",["a","b","L","x","r = a + (b − a) x/L","dx = L/(b − a) dr","R = ∫_0^L ρdx/(πr^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) ∫_a^b dr/(r^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) (1/a − 1/b) = ρL/πab.","a = b","ρL/(πa^2)"],""],[1,2,28,"formula","When the path is not a simple wire"," − a)) ∫_a^b dr/(r^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) (1/a − 1/b) = ρL/πab. A neat check: put a = b and you are back to ρL/(πa^2) . (The slicing treats each slab as carrying uniform current, which is a good approximation for a gentle taper — and it is the answer JEE expects.)",["R = ∫_0^L ρdx/(πr^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) ∫_a^b dr/(r^2) = ρL/(π(b − a)) (1/a − 1/b) = ρL/πab."],"<i>R</i> = ∫<sub>0</sub><sup><i>L</i></sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i></span><span><i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>π</i>(<i>b</i> − <i>a</i>)</span></span> ∫<sub><i>a</i></sub><sup><i>b</i></sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>r</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>π</i>(<i>b</i> − <i>a</i>)</span></span> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>a</i></span></span> − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>b</i></span></span>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>π</i><i>ab</i></span></span>."],[1,2,30,"text","When the path is not a simple wire","DerivationCurrent flowing radially through a cylindrical shell A hollow cylinder of length L , inner radius a , outer radius b . Current flows from the inner surface outwards to the outer surface (think of leakage through the insulation of a cable). Now the slices are thin coaxial shells: thickness dr along the current, area 2πrL across it. R = ∫_a^b ρdr/(2πrL) = ρ/2πL ln b/a.",["L","a","b","dr","2πrL","R = ∫_a^b ρdr/(2πrL) = ρ/2πL ln b/a."],""],[1,2,30,"formula","When the path is not a simple wire","adius b . Current flows from the inner surface outwards to the outer surface (think of leakage through the insulation of a cable). Now the slices are thin coaxial shells: thickness dr along the current, area 2πrL across it. R = ∫_a^b ρdr/(2πrL) = ρ/2πL ln b/a.",["R = ∫_a^b ρdr/(2πrL) = ρ/2πL ln b/a."],"<i>R</i> = ∫<sub><i>a</i></sub><sup><i>b</i></sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i> <i>d</i><i>r</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>r</i><i>L</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>L</i></span></span> ln <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>b</i></span><span><i>a</i></span></span>."],[1,2,32,"text","When the path is not a simple wire","And when two materials lie side by side along the current — each offering its own path from one end to the other — they are simply resistors in parallel. That is the whole idea behind the next problem.",[],""],[1,2,33,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · Single correct","In an aluminium (Al) bar of square cross-section, a square hole is drilled and is filled with iron (Fe) as shown in the figure. The electrical resistivities of Al and Fe are 2.7 × 10^(−8) Ω m and 1.0 × 10^(−7) Ω m respectively. The electrical resistance between the two faces P and Q of the composite bar is (2475)/64 μΩ · (1875)/64 μΩ · (1875)/49 μΩ · (2475)/132 μΩ",["2.7 × 10^(−8) Ω m","1.0 × 10^(−7) Ω m","(2475)/64 μΩ","(1875)/64 μΩ","(1875)/49 μΩ","(2475)/132 μΩ"],""],[1,2,33,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · Single correct","Each metal provides its own path from face P to face Q, so the iron core and the aluminium sleeve are in parallel. Areas. Iron: 2 × 2 = 4 mm ^2 . Aluminium: 7 × 7 − 4 = 45 mm ^2 . Length of each: 50 mm . R_Al = ((2.7 × 10^(−8))(50 × 10^(−3)))/(45 × 10^(−6)) = 30 μΩ R_Fe = ((1.0 × 10^(−7))(50 × 10^(−3)))/(4 × 10^(−6)) = 1250 μΩ R = (30 × 1250)/(30 + 1250) = (37500)/(1280) = (1875)/64 μΩ ≈ 29.3 μΩ. Answer (B). A sanity check: a parallel combination must be less than its smaller member, 30 μΩ . Only (B) and (D) pass that test. Current flows along the length of the bar. Both metals run the full length from P to Q. Are they in series or in parallel?",["2 × 2 = 4 mm ^2","7 × 7 − 4 = 45 mm ^2","50 mm","R_Al = ((2.7 × 10^(−8))(50 × 10^(−3)))/(45 × 10^(−6)) = 30 μΩ","R_Fe = ((1.0 × 10^(−7))(50 × 10^(−3)))/(4 × 10^(−6)) = 1250 μΩ","R = (30 × 1250)/(30 + 1250) = (37500)/(1280) = (1875)/64 μΩ ≈ 29.3 μΩ.","30 μΩ"],""],[1,2,34,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2006 · Single correct","Consider the cylindrical element shown in the figure: the first half AB has twice the radius of the second half BC, and each half has the same length. A current I flows through the element, which is made of a material of resistivity ρ . Choose the correct option. The power loss in the second half is four times the power loss in the first half. · The voltage drop in the first half is twice the voltage drop in the second half. · The current density in both halves is equal. · The electric field in both halves is equal.",["I","ρ"],""],[1,2,34,"sol","IIT-JEE 2006 · Single correct","The two halves are in series, so the same current I flows through both. Compare everything else through the area: the first half has radius 2r , hence four times the area of the second. Resistance: R ∝ 1/A , so R_BC = 4R_AB . Power = I^2R (same I): P_BC = 4P_AB . (A) is correct. Voltage drop = IR : the drop across the first half is a quarter of that across the second, not twice. (B) is wrong. Current density J = I/A : four times larger in the thin half. (C) is wrong. Field E = ρJ : four times larger in the thin half. (D) is wrong. Answer (A). The thin half is the bottleneck: higher J , higher E , higher voltage drop, more heat.",["I","2r","R ∝ 1/A","R_BC = 4R_AB","= I^2R","I","P_BC = 4P_AB","= IR","J = I/A","E = ρJ","J","E"],""],[1,2,35,"sec","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Look again at ρ = m/(ne^2τ) . Only two things in it can change with temperature: the number of carriers n and the collision time τ .",[],""],[1,2,36,"text","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Look again at ρ = m/(ne^2τ) . Only two things in it can change with temperature: the number of carriers n and the collision time τ .",["ρ = m/(ne^2τ)","n","τ"],""],[1,2,37,"text","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Metals. n is fixed — the free electrons are already free. But heat the metal and its ions vibrate more violently, so an electron gets a shorter clear run before the next collision. τ falls, and the resistivity rises. Over a moderate range the rise is nearly linear:",["n","τ"],""],[1,2,38,"formula","Temperature: the collision time fights back","s. n is fixed — the free electrons are already free. But heat the metal and its ions vibrate more violently, so an electron gets a shorter clear run before the next collision. τ falls, and the resistivity rises. Over a moderate range the rise is nearly linear:",["ρ_T = ρ_0 [1 + α (T − T_0)] ⟹ R_T = R_0 [1 + α (T − T_0)]"],"<i>ρ</i><sub><i>T</i></sub> = <i>ρ</i><sub>0</sub> [1 + <i>α</i> (<i>T</i> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>)] ⟹ <i>R</i><sub><i>T</i></sub> = <i>R</i><sub>0</sub> [1 + <i>α</i> (<i>T</i> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>)]"],[1,2,39,"text","Temperature: the collision time fights back","where α is the temperature coefficient of resistivity (about 0.004 per ^∘ C for most pure metals).",["α","0.004","^∘ C"],""],[1,2,40,"text","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Semiconductors. Here heating shakes more electrons loose, and n climbs steeply — far faster than τ falls. So the resistivity drops as the temperature rises: α is negative.",["n","τ","α"],""],[1,2,41,"text","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Alloys such as manganin and constantan. Their α is practically zero, which makes them ideal for standard resistors and for the wires of bridges and potentiometers. Nichrome has a resistivity about sixty times copper’s and survives red heat without oxidising away — perfect for heater coils.",["α"],""],[1,2,42,"figure","Temperature: the collision time fights back","Resistivity against temperature: a metal, a semiconductor, and a superconductor.",[],""],[1,2,43,"intuition","Why a bulb blows when you switch it on","A 100 W , 220 V bulb has a working resistance of V^2/P = 484 Ω — with its tungsten filament at around 2500 ^∘ C . Cold, the same filament measures only about a fifteenth of that: thirty-odd ohms. So for the first few hundredths of a second after switch-on, the current is many times larger than normal. If the filament has a thin, weak spot, that is the moment it gives up.",["100 W","220 V","V^2/P = 484 Ω","2500 ^∘ C"],""],[1,2,44,"story","Zero. Not “very small” — zero.","In 1911 Heike Kamerlingh Onnes cooled mercury with liquid helium and watched its resistance fall smoothly… and then, at 4.2 K , vanish altogether. He had discovered superconductivity. A current started in a superconducting ring keeps circulating for years with no battery at all.",["4.2 K"],""],[1,2,45,"trap","Which temperature does α belong to?","In R_T = R_0[1 + α(T − T_0)] , R_0 is the resistance at the reference temperature T_0 for which α is quoted (usually 0 ^∘ C in JEE problems). If you are given the resistance at two other temperatures, do not jump from one straight to the other — write the formula once for each and divide: R_1/R_2 = (1 + αt_1)/(1 + αt_2).",["R_T = R_0[1 + α(T − T_0)]","R_0","T_0","α","0 ^∘ C","R_1/R_2 = (1 + αt_1)/(1 + αt_2)."],""],[1,2,45,"formula","Which temperature does α belong to?","ference temperature T_0 for which α is quoted (usually 0 ^∘ C in JEE problems). If you are given the resistance at two other temperatures, do not jump from one straight to the other — write the formula once for each and divide: R_1/R_2 = (1 + αt_1)/(1 + αt_2).",["R_1/R_2 = (1 + αt_1)/(1 + αt_2)."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1 + <i>α</i><i>t</i><sub>1</sub></span><span>1 + <i>α</i><i>t</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>."],[1,2,47,"q","A wire that reads temperature · Numerical answer","The platinum coil of a resistance thermometer has a resistance of 10.0 Ω in melting ice (0 ^∘ C) and 13.9 Ω in steam (100 ^∘ C). Lowered into a furnace, it reads 25.6 Ω . Assuming the resistance varies linearly with temperature, find the furnace temperature in ^∘ C .",["10.0 Ω","0 ^∘ C","13.9 Ω","100 ^∘ C","25.6 Ω","^∘ C"],""],[1,2,47,"sol","A wire that reads temperature · Numerical answer","From 0 to 100 ^∘ C the resistance rises by 3.9 Ω — that is 0.039 Ω per degree. In the furnace it has risen by 25.6 − 10.0 = 15.6 Ω : t = (R_t − R_0)/(R_100 − R_0) × 100 = (15.6)/3.9 × 100 = 400 ^∘ C . Notice you never needed α itself (it is 0.0039 per ^∘ C here). The two fixed points do the calibrating — exactly the “every scale is a straight line” idea from the Thermodynamics chapter. A linear scale means equal changes in resistance for equal changes in temperature. How many ohms per degree?",["0","100 ^∘ C","3.9 Ω","0.039 Ω","25.6 − 10.0 = 15.6 Ω","t = (R_t − R_0)/(R_100 − R_0) × 100 = (15.6)/3.9 × 100 = 400 ^∘ C .","α","0.0039","^∘ C"],""],[1,2,48,"sec","Ohmic or not? Read the graph","Ohm’s law is not a law of nature in the way that charge conservation is. It is a statement about some materials under some conditions: that V/I stays constant. A conductor that obeys it is called ohmic, and its I – V graph is a straight line through the origin.",[],""],[1,2,49,"text","Ohmic or not? Read the graph","Ohm’s law is not a law of nature in the way that charge conservation is. It is a statement about some materials under some conditions: that V/I stays constant. A conductor that obeys it is called ohmic, and its I – V graph is a straight line through the origin.",["V/I","I","V"],""],[1,2,50,"text","Ohmic or not? Read the graph","Plenty of useful devices are not ohmic:",[],""],[1,2,51,"text","Ohmic or not? Read the graph","A filament lamp. As the current rises the filament heats up, its resistance climbs, and the graph bends over towards the V -axis. A diode. It conducts readily one way and hardly at all the other. Reverse the voltage and you do not get the same current reversed. Some devices even have a region where the current falls as the voltage rises.",["V"],""],[1,2,52,"text","Ohmic or not? Read the graph","You can always define a resistance as R = V/I at any point of any graph. Ohm’s law is the extra claim that this number is the same at every point.",["R = V/I"],""],[1,2,53,"trap","Check the axes before you read the slope","On an I – V graph (current on the vertical axis) the slope is 1/R : a steeper line means a smaller resistance. On a V – I graph it is the other way round. Examiners switch the axes on purpose.",["I","V","1/R","V","I"],""],[1,2,54,"q","Hot line, cold line · Single correct","The figure shows the I – V graphs of the same metallic wire at two temperatures T_1 and T_2 . Which statement is correct? T_1 > T_2 · T_1 < T_2 · T_1 = T_2 · Nothing can be said without knowing the metal",["I","V","T_1","T_2","T_1 > T_2","T_1 < T_2","T_1 = T_2"],""],[1,2,54,"sol","Hot line, cold line · Single correct","Current is on the vertical axis, so the slope of each line is I/V = 1/R . The line for T_1 is steeper: at T_1 the wire has the smaller resistance. For a metal, resistance rises with temperature. Smaller resistance means lower temperature, so T_1 < T_2 . Answer (B). Had the axes been swapped (V vertical), the steeper line would have been the hotter one.",["I/V = 1/R","T_1","T_1","T_1 < T_2","V"],""],[1,2,55,"pyq","JEE Main 2015 · Single correct","When a potential difference of 5 V is applied across a wire of length 0.1 m , the drift speed of the electrons is 2.5 × 10^(−4) m s ^(−1) . If the electron density in the wire is 8 × 10^28 m ^(−3) , the resistivity of the material is close to 1.6 × 10^(−8) Ω m · 1.6 × 10^(−7) Ω m · 1.6 × 10^(−6) Ω m · 1.6 × 10^(−5) Ω m",["5 V","0.1 m","2.5 × 10^(−4) m s ^(−1)","8 × 10^28 m ^(−3)","1.6 × 10^(−8) Ω m","1.6 × 10^(−7) Ω m","1.6 × 10^(−6) Ω m","1.6 × 10^(−5) Ω m"],""],[1,2,55,"sol","JEE Main 2015 · Single correct","The field in the wire is E = V/L = 5/0.1 = 50 V m ^(−1) . The current density is J = nev_d = (8 × 10^28)(1.6 × 10^(−19))(2.5 × 10^(−4)) = 3.2 × 10^6 A m ^(−2) . ρ = E/J = 50/(3.2 × 10^6) ≈ 1.6 × 10^(−5) Ω m . Answer (D). When a problem mixes drift speed with resistivity and gives no cross-section, J = σE is the bridge between them. You are given no area, so R = ρL/A is a dead end. Use the point form: J = E/ρ .",["E = V/L = 5/0.1 = 50 V m ^(−1)","J = nev_d = (8 × 10^28)(1.6 × 10^(−19))(2.5 × 10^(−4)) = 3.2 × 10^6 A m ^(−2)","ρ = E/J = 50/(3.2 × 10^6) ≈ 1.6 × 10^(−5) Ω m .","J = σE","R = ρL/A","J = E/ρ"],""],[1,2,56,"recap","Recap","Ohm’s law, point form: J = σE with σ = ne^2τ/m and ρ = 1/σ . For a wire this becomes V = IR . R = ρL/A : material × geometry. Stretch a wire to n times its length and R → n^2R . Non-uniform conductor: slice across the current and integrate dR = ρ dx/A . Side-by-side paths are in parallel. Metals: τ falls on heating, so R_T = R_0[1 + α(T − T_0)] with α > 0 . Semiconductors: n rises, so α < 0 . Manganin and constantan: α ≈ 0 . Ohmic means a straight I – V line through the origin. On an I – V graph the slope is 1/R . @end",["J = σE","σ = ne^2τ/m","ρ = 1/σ","V = IR","R = ρL/A","n","R → n^2R","dR = ρ dx/A","τ","R_T = R_0[1 + α(T − T_0)]","α > 0","n"],""],[1,3,0,"lesson","Combining Resistors","Series, parallel — and the three tricks that crack the networks which are neither: spotting equal-potential points, using symmetry, and folding ladders. Most “impossible” JEE networks are easy ones in disguise.",[],""],[1,3,1,"sec","Two rules run almost everything","Series: one path, same current. The resistors sit one after another, so whatever current enters the first must pass through them all. The voltages add:",[],""],[1,3,2,"text","Two rules run almost everything","Series: one path, same current. The resistors sit one after another, so whatever current enters the first must pass through them all. The voltages add:",[],""],[1,3,3,"formula","Two rules run almost everything","Series: one path, same current. The resistors sit one after another, so whatever current enters the first must pass through them all. The voltages add:",["V = IR_1 + IR_2 + … ⟹ R_s = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + …"],"<i>V</i> = <i>I</i><i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>I</i><i>R</i><sub>2</sub> + … ⟹ <i>R</i><sub><i>s</i></sub> = <i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>2</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>3</sub> + …"],[1,3,4,"text","Two rules run almost everything","Parallel: same two ends, same voltage. Each resistor is connected across the same pair of points, so each has the same potential difference. The currents add:",[],""],[1,3,5,"formula","Two rules run almost everything","Parallel: same two ends, same voltage. Each resistor is connected across the same pair of points, so each has the same potential difference. The currents add:",["I = V/R_1 + V/R_2 + … ⟹ 1/R_p = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2 + 1/R_3 + …"],"<i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> + … ⟹ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub><i>p</i></sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>3</sub></span></span> + …"],[1,3,6,"text","Two rules run almost everything","Three shortcuts you should never have to derive in the exam hall:",[],""],[1,3,7,"text","Two rules run almost everything","Two in parallel: R_p = (R_1R_2)/(R_1 + R_2) (“product over sum”). n equal resistors R : series gives nR , parallel gives R/n . A parallel combination is always smaller than its smallest member. A series one is larger than its largest.",["R_p = (R_1R_2)/(R_1 + R_2)","n","R","nR","R/n"],""],[1,3,8,"intuition","Toll plazas","Resistors in series are toll booths one after another on the same road: every car must queue at each. Resistors in parallel are extra lanes at one plaza: open another lane — any lane, however slow — and the traffic gets through more easily. That is why adding a resistor in parallel always lowers the total.",[],""],[1,3,9,"text","Two rules run almost everything","How the voltage and current share out. In series the same I flows, so the voltage divides in proportion to resistance — the big resistor takes the big share. In parallel the same V applies, so the current divides in inverse proportion — the small resistor takes the big share:",["I","V"],""],[1,3,10,"formula","Two rules run almost everything","and current share out. In series the same I flows, so the voltage divides in proportion to resistance — the big resistor takes the big share. In parallel the same V applies, so the current divides in inverse proportion — the small resistor takes the big share:",["series: V_1 = V R_1/(R_1 + R_2) parallel: I_1 = I R_2/(R_1 + R_2)"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">series: </span><i>V</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>V</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">parallel: </span><i>I</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[1,3,11,"text","Two rules run almost everything","Look closely at that second formula: the current through R_1 has the other resistor on top.",["R_1"],""],[1,3,12,"q","Ring road · Single correct","A uniform wire of resistance 12 Ω is bent into a circle. What is the resistance between two diametrically opposite points on it? 3 Ω · 6 Ω · 12 Ω · 24 Ω",["12 Ω","3 Ω","6 Ω","12 Ω","24 Ω"],""],[1,3,12,"sol","Ring road · Single correct","Current entering at one point has two ways round to the opposite point: the left half-circle and the right half-circle. Each half is half the wire, 6 Ω , and the two are in parallel: R = (6 × 6)/(6 + 6) = 3 Ω. Answer (A). Try the follow-up yourself: between two points a quarter of the way round, it is 3 Ω in parallel with 9 Ω , which is 2.25 Ω .",["6 Ω","R = (6 × 6)/(6 + 6) = 3 Ω.","3 Ω","9 Ω","2.25 Ω"],""],[1,3,13,"sec","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","An ideal connecting wire has no resistance, so there is no potential drop along it. Every point on it is at the same potential — electrically it is a single point, however long and winding it looks on the page.",[],""],[1,3,14,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","An ideal connecting wire has no resistance, so there is no potential drop along it. Every point on it is at the same potential — electrically it is a single point, however long and winding it looks on the page.",[],""],[1,3,15,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","That gives a method that never fails:",[],""],[1,3,16,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","Name the nodes. Give a letter to every junction. Two junctions joined by a bare wire get the same letter. List each resistor by the two letters it connects. Redraw. Resistors sharing the same pair of letters are in parallel.",[],""],[1,3,17,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","Try it on this innocent-looking circuit of three equal resistors R :",["R"],""],[1,3,18,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","At first sight they are in series: 3R . Now name the nodes. The upper wire joins A straight to Q, so Q is A. The lower wire joins P straight to B, so P is B. List the resistors:",["3R"],""],[1,3,19,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","first: between A and P → between A and B second: between P and Q → between B and A third: between Q and B → between A and B",[],""],[1,3,20,"text","Trick 1: find the points that are really one point","All three connect the same two points. They are in parallel, and the answer is R/3 — nine times smaller than the first guess.",["R/3"],""],[1,3,21,"trap","A resistor with both ends on the same node is dead","If a bare wire is connected across a resistor, both its ends are at the same potential. No potential difference means no current: the resistor is short-circuited and you may erase it. The same goes for any piece of circuit that dangles off a single node with nowhere else to go.",[],""],[1,3,22,"q","One wire fewer · Single correct","In the circuit above, each resistor is 6 Ω . The lower wire (from P to B) is removed, leaving only the upper wire from A to Q. The resistance between A and B is now 2 Ω · 6 Ω · 9 Ω · 18 Ω",["6 Ω","2 Ω","6 Ω","9 Ω","18 Ω"],""],[1,3,22,"sol","One wire fewer · Single correct","The upper wire still makes Q the same node as A. So: first resistor: between A and P second resistor: between P and Q = between P and A third resistor: between Q = A and B The first two both run from A to P — and P now leads nowhere else. Both ends of that little loop sit at A’s potential, so no current enters it. Those two resistors are dead. All the current goes through the wire to Q and then through the third resistor alone: R_AB = 6 Ω . Answer (B). Option (C), 9 Ω , is what you get by putting the first two “in parallel” (3 Ω) and then adding the third — forgetting that a parallel pair with no way out carries nothing. Name the nodes again. Which resistors now have both ends on the same node, or lead nowhere?",["=","=","R_AB = 6 Ω","9 Ω","3 Ω"],""],[1,3,23,"sec","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","If a network looks the same after you flip or rotate it — with the input and output terminals staying where they are — then points that swap places under the flip must be at the same potential.",[],""],[1,3,24,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","If a network looks the same after you flip or rotate it — with the input and output terminals staying where they are — then points that swap places under the flip must be at the same potential.",[],""],[1,3,25,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","Two equal-potential points can be treated in either of two ways, whichever is more convenient:",[],""],[1,3,26,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","Join them (a wire between them carries no current, so it changes nothing), or Remove any resistor connected between them (it carries no current anyway).",[],""],[1,3,27,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","The balanced bridge. The most important example is this five-resistor network, which is neither series nor parallel:",[],""],[1,3,28,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","If P/Q = R/S , the points B and D are at the same potential (each divides the voltage in the same ratio). The middle resistor carries no current, so throw it away. What remains is trivial: (P + Q) in parallel with (R + S) . This is the Wheatstone bridge, and it returns as a measuring instrument in Lesson 8.",["P/Q = R/S","(P + Q)","(R + S)"],""],[1,3,29,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","The cube. Twelve equal resistors r form the edges of a cube. Current I enters at one corner and leaves at the diagonally opposite corner.",["r","I"],""],[1,3,30,"figure","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","Every edge is a resistor r . By symmetry the current splits I/3 → I/6 → I/3 .",["r","I/3 → I/6 → I/3","r","I/3 → I/6 → I/3"],""],[1,3,31,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","At the entry corner three edges look exactly alike, so the current splits into I/3 in each. Each of these reaches a corner where two onward edges look alike: I/6 in each. Finally three edges, each carrying I/3 , meet at the exit. Walk along any path from entry to exit and add the voltage drops:",["I/3","I/6","I/3"],""],[1,3,32,"formula","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","k exactly alike, so the current splits into I/3 in each. Each of these reaches a corner where two onward edges look alike: I/6 in each. Finally three edges, each carrying I/3 , meet at the exit. Walk along any path from entry to exit and add the voltage drops:",["V = I/3 r + I/6 r + I/3 r = 5/6 Ir ⟹ R_(body diagonal) = 5/6 r."],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span>3</span></span> <i>r</i> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span>6</span></span> <i>r</i> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span>3</span></span> <i>r</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>5</span><span>6</span></span> <i>I</i><i>r</i> ⟹ <i>R</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">body diagonal</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>5</span><span>6</span></span> <i>r</i>."],[1,3,33,"text","Trick 2: let symmetry do the work","The same cube gives 3/4r across a face diagonal and 7/12r across one edge.",["3/4r","7/12r"],""],[1,3,34,"q","The bridge that isn’t there · Numerical answer","In the bridge network above, P = 2 Ω , Q = 4 Ω , R = 3 Ω , S = 6 Ω and the middle resistor is 10 Ω . Find the equivalent resistance between A and C, in ohms.",["P = 2 Ω","Q = 4 Ω","R = 3 Ω","S = 6 Ω","10 Ω"],""],[1,3,34,"sol","The bridge that isn’t there · Numerical answer","P/Q = 2/4 = 1/2 and R/S = 3/6 = 1/2 . The bridge is balanced: B and D are at the same potential and the 10 Ω resistor carries no current. Remove it. Upper branch: 2 + 4 = 6 Ω . Lower branch: 3 + 6 = 9 Ω . In parallel: R_AC = (6 × 9)/(6 + 9) = 3.6 Ω. The 10 Ω could have been 1 Ω or a million ohms; the answer would not change. Before any algebra, compare P/Q with R/S .",["P/Q = 2/4 = 1/2","R/S = 3/6 = 1/2","10 Ω","2 + 4 = 6 Ω","3 + 6 = 9 Ω","R_AC = (6 × 9)/(6 + 9) = 3.6 Ω.","10 Ω","1 Ω","P/Q","R/S"],""],[1,3,35,"q","Corner to corner · Integer answer","Twelve identical 6 Ω resistors form the edges of a cube. What is the resistance, in ohms, between two corners at opposite ends of a body diagonal?",["6 Ω"],""],[1,3,35,"sol","Corner to corner · Integer answer","Using the symmetry argument above, R = 5/6 r = 5/6 × 6 = 5 Ω . If you want to see it another way: the three corners next to the entry are at one potential, and the three next to the exit are at another. Join each trio. Now the cube is three resistors in parallel (r/3), then six in parallel (r/6), then three in parallel (r/3), all in series: r/3 + r/6 + r/3 = (5r)/6.",["R = 5/6 r = 5/6 × 6 = 5 Ω","r/3","r/6","r/3","r/3 + r/6 + r/3 = (5r)/6."],""],[1,3,36,"sec","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","An infinite ladder repeats the same section for ever:",[],""],[1,3,37,"text","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","An infinite ladder repeats the same section for ever:",[],""],[1,3,38,"text","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","Let its resistance be x . Here is the trick: chop off the first section. What is left is still an infinite ladder — so it also has resistance x . The whole thing is therefore the first series resistor R_1 , followed by R_2 in parallel with x :",["x","x","R_1","R_2","x"],""],[1,3,39,"formula","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","Let its resistance be x . Here is the trick: chop off the first section. What is left is still an infinite ladder — so it also has resistance x . The whole thing is therefore the first series resistor R_1 , followed by R_2 in parallel with x :",["x = R_1 + (R_2 x)/(R_2 + x)."],"<i>x</i> = <i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub> <i>x</i></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub> + <i>x</i></span></span>."],[1,3,40,"text","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","That is just a quadratic in x . With every resistor equal to 1 Ω it becomes x^2 − x − 1 = 0 , so",["x","1 Ω","x^2 − x − 1 = 0"],""],[1,3,41,"formula","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","That is just a quadratic in x . With every resistor equal to 1 Ω it becomes x^2 − x − 1 = 0 , so",["x = (1 + √5)/2 ≈ 1.618 Ω"],"<i>x</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1 + √<span class=\"fx-sq\">5</span></span><span>2</span></span> ≈ 1.618 Ω"],[1,3,42,"text","Trick 3: ladders — add one more rung and nothing changes","— the golden ratio, of all things, hiding in a circuit.",[],""],[1,3,43,"q","An endless ladder · Integer answer","In the infinite ladder above, every series resistor is R_1 = 1 Ω and every rung is R_2 = 2 Ω . Find the resistance between the input terminals, in ohms.",["R_1 = 1 Ω","R_2 = 2 Ω"],""],[1,3,43,"sol","An endless ladder · Integer answer","x = 1 + (2x)/(2 + x) ⟹ x(2 + x) = (2 + x) + 2x ⟹ x^2 − x − 2 = 0. So (x − 2)(x + 1) = 0 , and since a resistance cannot be negative, x = 2 Ω . Check it: one rung of 2 Ω in parallel with the rest (2 Ω) gives 1 Ω ; add the series 1 Ω and you get 2 Ω again. The ladder reproduces itself.",["x = 1 + (2x)/(2 + x) ⟹ x(2 + x) = (2 + x) + 2x ⟹ x^2 − x − 2 = 0.","(x − 2)(x + 1) = 0","x = 2 Ω","2 Ω","2 Ω","1 Ω","1 Ω","2 Ω"],""],[1,3,44,"sec","Finite ladders: fold from the far end","A ladder that ends is folded up from the far end: the last few resistors are always a plain series group. Combine them, then go parallel with the nearest rung, then series again, and so on back to the battery. To find a particular current, unfold again, halving (or otherwise splitting) the current a",[],""],[1,3,45,"text","Finite ladders: fold from the far end","A ladder that ends is folded up from the far end: the last few resistors are always a plain series group. Combine them, then go parallel with the nearest rung, then series again, and so on back to the battery. To find a particular current, unfold again, halving (or otherwise splitting) the current at each junction.",[],""],[1,3,46,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","In the circuit shown in the figure, the current through the 3 Ω resistor is 0.50 A · the 3 Ω resistor is 0.25 A · the 4 Ω resistor is 0.50 A · the 4 Ω resistor is 0.25 A",["3 Ω","0.50 A","3 Ω","0.25 A","4 Ω","0.50 A","4 Ω","0.25 A"],""],[1,3,46,"sol","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","Fold from the far end. The last loop is 2 + 4 + 2 = 8 Ω in series. That sits in parallel with the second 8 Ω rung: 4 Ω . Add the two 2 Ω resistors on either side of it: 2 + 4 + 2 = 8 Ω . That is in parallel with the first 8 Ω rung: 4 Ω again. Finally add the 3 Ω and the 2 Ω next to the battery: 3 + 4 + 2 = 9 Ω . I = (9 V)/9Ω = 1 A . Unfold. All of this 1 A passes through the 3 Ω resistor — so (A) and (B) are both wrong. At the first rung it meets two equal 8 Ω paths and splits into 0.5 A each. At the second rung the 0.5 A again meets two equal 8 Ω paths: 0.25 A each. So the 4 Ω resistor carries 0.25 A . Answer (D). Start folding from the right-hand end. Keep an eye out for an 8 Ω meeting another 8 Ω .",["2 + 4 + 2 = 8 Ω","8 Ω","4 Ω","2 Ω","2 + 4 + 2 = 8 Ω","8 Ω","4 Ω","3 Ω","2 Ω","3 + 4 + 2 = 9 Ω","I = (9 V)/9Ω = 1 A .","1 A"],""],[1,3,47,"sec","Putting it to work","One more pair of formulas, borrowed from Class 10 and treated properly in Lesson 6: a resistor carrying current I with voltage V across it turns electrical energy into heat at the rate",[],""],[1,3,48,"text","Putting it to work","One more pair of formulas, borrowed from Class 10 and treated properly in Lesson 6: a resistor carrying current I with voltage V across it turns electrical energy into heat at the rate",["I","V"],""],[1,3,49,"formula","Putting it to work","One more pair of formulas, borrowed from Class 10 and treated properly in Lesson 6: a resistor carrying current I with voltage V across it turns electrical energy into heat at the rate",["P = VI = I^2R = (V^2)/R."],"<i>P</i> = <i>V</i><i>I</i> = <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>R</i></span></span>."],[1,3,50,"text","Putting it to work","That is all you need for the next problem, which is really a test of how voltage divides.",[],""],[1,3,51,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2009 · One or more correct","For the circuit shown in the figure, the current I through the battery is 7.5 mA · the potential difference across R_L is 18 V · the ratio of the powers dissipated in R_1 and R_2 is 3 · if R_1 and R_2 are interchanged, the magnitude of the power dissipated in R_L will decrease by a factor of 9",["I","7.5 mA","R_L","18 V","R_1","R_2","3","R_1","R_2","R_L","9"],""],[1,3,51,"sol","IIT-JEE 2009 · One or more correct","Reduce. R_2 and R_L are in parallel: (6 × 1.5)/(6 + 1.5) = 1.2 k Ω, and this is in series with R_1 = 2 k Ω : total 3.2 k Ω . (A) I = (24 V)/(3.2 k Ω) = 7.5 mA . Correct. (B) The parallel pair takes 7.5 mA × 1.2 k Ω = 9 V , not 18 V . Wrong. (The other 15 V is across R_1 .) (C) P_1 = (15^2)/(2 k Ω) = 112.5 mW and P_2 = (9^2)/(6 k Ω) = 13.5 mW . The ratio is 25/3 , not 3 . Wrong. (D) Swap them. Now 2 k Ω is in parallel with R_L : (2 × 1.5)/3.5 = 6/7 k Ω , in series with 6 k Ω . The voltage across R_L becomes 24 × (6/7)/(6 + 6/7) = 24 × 1/8 = 3 V . It was 9 V ; now it is 3 V . Power goes as V^2 , so it falls by a factor of 9 . Correct. Answer (A), (D).",["R_2","R_L","(6 × 1.5)/(6 + 1.5) = 1.2 k Ω,","R_1 = 2 k Ω","3.2 k Ω","I = (24 V)/(3.2 k Ω) = 7.5 mA","7.5 mA × 1.2 k Ω = 9 V","18 V","15 V","R_1","P_1 = (15^2)/(2 k Ω) = 112.5 mW","P_2 = (9^2)/(6 k Ω) = 13.5 mW"],""],[1,3,52,"recap","Recap","Series: same current, R_s = ∑ R , voltage divides ∝ R . Parallel: same voltage, 1/R_p = ∑ 1/R , current divides ∝ 1/R . A bare wire is one node. Name the nodes, list each resistor by its two letters, redraw. A resistor with both ends at the same potential carries nothing: delete it. Symmetry → equal-potential points → join them or remove what lies between. Balanced bridge: P/Q = R/S . Cube of equal resistors r : 5/6r (body diagonal), 3/4r (face diagonal), 7/12r (edge). Infinite ladder: call it x , chop off one section, it is still x . Finite ladder: fold from the far end. @end",["R_s = ∑ R","∝ R","1/R_p = ∑ 1/R","∝ 1/R","P/Q = R/S","r","5/6r","3/4r","7/12r","x","x"],""],[1,4,0,"lesson","Cells, EMF & Internal Resistance","A battery is a pump that lifts charge uphill — and every real pump leaks a little. Meet emf, the resistance hidden inside every cell, the load that squeezes out the most power, and what happens when cells team up.",[],""],[1,4,1,"sec","What a battery actually does","Left to itself, positive charge in a wire rolls “downhill” — from high potential to low — and current would stop the moment the hill flattened out. To keep a current going round a circuit, something must carry the charge back up the hill again.",[],""],[1,4,2,"text","What a battery actually does","Left to itself, positive charge in a wire rolls “downhill” — from high potential to low — and current would stop the moment the hill flattened out. To keep a current going round a circuit, something must carry the charge back up the hill again.",[],""],[1,4,3,"text","What a battery actually does","That something is the cell. Inside it, chemical reactions grab charge at the low-potential terminal and push it to the high-potential terminal, against the electric field. It is a charge pump — the ski lift that carries skiers back to the top so they can keep coming down the slope.",[],""],[1,4,4,"text","What a battery actually does","The emf ε of a cell is the work its chemistry does per unit charge pumped:",["ε"],""],[1,4,5,"formula","What a battery actually does","The emf ε of a cell is the work its chemistry does per unit charge pumped:",["ε = W/q unit: volt"],"<i>ε</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>W</i></span><span><i>q</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: volt</span>"],[1,4,6,"text","What a battery actually does","Despite the old name “electromotive force”, emf is not a force. It is energy per unit charge, measured in volts, just like potential difference. A 1.5 V cell gives 1.5 J to every coulomb it lifts.",["1.5 V","1.5 J"],""],[1,4,7,"story","A stack of coins that changed the world","In 1800 Alessandro Volta piled up discs of zinc and silver separated by brine-soaked card and got something nobody had ever had before: a steady current, on tap. Until then electricity meant sparks. Every circuit in this chapter is a descendant of that pile — and the volt carries his name.",[],""],[1,4,8,"sec","Every real cell has a resistance inside","The charge being pumped has to push its way through the cell’s own chemicals, and they resist. So a real cell behaves like an ideal pump of emf ε in series with a small internal resistance r , sealed in the same box:",[],""],[1,4,9,"text","Every real cell has a resistance inside","The charge being pumped has to push its way through the cell’s own chemicals, and they resist. So a real cell behaves like an ideal pump of emf ε in series with a small internal resistance r , sealed in the same box:",["ε","r"],""],[1,4,10,"figure","Every real cell has a resistance inside","A real cell = ideal emf ε + internal resistance r , sealed in one box.",["ε","r","ε","r"],""],[1,4,11,"text","Every real cell has a resistance inside","Connect an external resistance R . The same current flows through R and r :",["R","R","r"],""],[1,4,12,"formula","Every real cell has a resistance inside","Connect an external resistance R . The same current flows through R and r :",["I = ε/(R + r)"],"<i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i></span><span><i>R</i> + <i>r</i></span></span>"],[1,4,13,"text","Every real cell has a resistance inside","What a voltmeter across the cell’s terminals reads — the terminal voltage V — is what is left after the internal drop:",["V"],""],[1,4,14,"formula","Every real cell has a resistance inside","What a voltmeter across the cell’s terminals reads — the terminal voltage V — is what is left after the internal drop:",["V = ε − Ir (cell delivering current)"],"<i>V</i> = <i>ε</i> − <i>I</i><i>r</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">(cell delivering current)</span>"],[1,4,15,"text","Every real cell has a resistance inside","Four situations cover most questions:",[],""],[1,4,16,"table","Every real cell has a resistance inside","Situation Current Terminal voltage Open circuit (nothing connected) 0 V = ε Delivering current (discharging) ε/(R + r) V = ε − Ir < ε Short circuit (R = 0) ε/r , the maximum possible V = 0 Being charged (current forced in backwards) set by the charger V = ε + Ir > ε",["0","V = ε","ε/(R + r)","V = ε − Ir < ε","R = 0","ε/r","V = 0","V = ε + Ir > ε"],""],[1,4,17,"text","Every real cell has a resistance inside","Plot V against I and you get a straight line: the intercept on the V -axis is ε , and the slope is −r . That graph is the standard experiment for finding both.",["V","I","V","ε","−r"],""],[1,4,18,"intuition","Why headlights dim when the engine starts","A car battery’s internal resistance is tiny — of the order of a hundredth of an ohm. But the starter motor gulps well over a hundred amperes. Even a hundredth of an ohm then swallows a volt or two of the battery’s twelve, and for that moment everything else on the battery, headlights included, gets less.",[],""],[1,4,19,"trap","Terminal voltage is not always less than the emf","While a cell is being charged, current is driven through it from its positive terminal to its negative terminal — backwards. The internal drop now adds: V = ε + Ir . A “ 12 V ” battery on charge can show 14 V across its terminals.",["V = ε + Ir","12 V","14 V"],""],[1,4,20,"q","Two readings, one hidden resistor · Numerical answer","A voltmeter of very high resistance connected across a battery reads 12.0 V . When the battery delivers 4.0 A to a lamp, the same voltmeter reads 10.0 V . Find the internal resistance of the battery, in ohms.",["12.0 V","4.0 A","10.0 V"],""],[1,4,20,"sol","Two readings, one hidden resistor · Numerical answer","With nothing but a high-resistance voltmeter attached, practically no current flows, so the first reading is the emf: ε = 12.0 V . Under load, V = ε − Ir : 10.0 = 12.0 − 4.0 r ⟹ r = 0.5 Ω. The “missing” 2 V has not gone anywhere mysterious: it is the drop across r , inside the battery, warming it up.",["ε = 12.0 V","V = ε − Ir","10.0 = 12.0 − 4.0 r ⟹ r = 0.5 Ω.","2 V","r"],""],[1,4,21,"sec","How much power can you squeeze out?","The power delivered to the load R is",[],""],[1,4,22,"text","How much power can you squeeze out?","The power delivered to the load R is",["R"],""],[1,4,23,"formula","How much power can you squeeze out?","The power delivered to the load R is",["P = I^2R = (ε^2R)/((R + r)^2)."],"<i>P</i> = <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i></span><span>(<i>R</i> + <i>r</i>)<sup>2</sup></span></span>."],[1,4,24,"text","How much power can you squeeze out?","Think about the two extremes. If R is very small, the current is large but there is hardly any voltage across the load: little power. If R is very large, there is plenty of voltage but hardly any current: little power again. Somewhere in between there must be a best value.",["R","R"],""],[1,4,25,"text","How much power can you squeeze out?","DerivationThe maximum power transfer theorem Differentiate P with respect to R and set the result to zero: dP/dR = ε^2 ((R + r)^2 − 2R(R + r))/((R + r)^4) = ε^2 (r − R)/((R + r)^3) = 0 ⟹ R = r. For R < r the derivative is positive and for R > r it is negative, so this is a maximum. Substituting back, P_max = (ε^2 r)/((2r)^2) = (ε^2)/(4r).",["P","R","dP/dR = ε^2 ((R + r)^2 − 2R(R + r))/((R + r)^4) = ε^2 (r − R)/((R + r)^3) = 0 ⟹ R = r.","R < r","R > r","P_max = (ε^2 r)/((2r)^2) = (ε^2)/(4r)."],""],[1,4,25,"formula","How much power can you squeeze out?","nd set the result to zero: dP/dR = ε^2 ((R + r)^2 − 2R(R + r))/((R + r)^4) = ε^2 (r − R)/((R + r)^3) = 0 ⟹ R = r. For R < r the derivative is positive and for R > r it is negative, so this is a maximum. Substituting back, P_max = (ε^2 r)/((2r)^2) = (ε^2)/(4r).",["dP/dR = ε^2 ((R + r)^2 − 2R(R + r))/((R + r)^4) = ε^2 (r − R)/((R + r)^3) = 0 ⟹ R = r."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>P</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>R</i></span></span> = <i>ε</i><sup>2</sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>(<i>R</i> + <i>r</i>)<sup>2</sup> − 2<i>R</i>(<i>R</i> + <i>r</i>)</span><span>(<i>R</i> + <i>r</i>)<sup>4</sup></span></span> = <i>ε</i><sup>2</sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>r</i> − <i>R</i></span><span>(<i>R</i> + <i>r</i>)<sup>3</sup></span></span> = 0 ⟹ <i>R</i> = <i>r</i>."],[1,4,25,"formula","How much power can you squeeze out?","nd set the result to zero: dP/dR = ε^2 ((R + r)^2 − 2R(R + r))/((R + r)^4) = ε^2 (r − R)/((R + r)^3) = 0 ⟹ R = r. For R < r the derivative is positive and for R > r it is negative, so this is a maximum. Substituting back, P_max = (ε^2 r)/((2r)^2) = (ε^2)/(4r).",["P_max = (ε^2 r)/((2r)^2) = (ε^2)/(4r)."],"<i>P</i><sub>max</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sup>2</sup> <i>r</i></span><span>(2<i>r</i>)<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>4<i>r</i></span></span>."],[1,4,28,"key","Maximum power transfer","The load receives the greatest power when it matches the internal resistance: R = r, P_max = (ε^2)/(4r). At that point exactly half the cell’s output is wasted inside the cell — the efficiency is only 50% . Maximum power and maximum efficiency are different goals.",["R = r, P_max = (ε^2)/(4r).","50%"],""],[1,4,28,"formula","Maximum power transfer","load receives the greatest power when it matches the internal resistance: R = r, P_max = (ε^2)/(4r). At that point exactly half the cell’s output is wasted inside the cell — the efficiency is only 50% . Maximum power and maximum efficiency are different goals.",["R = r, P_max = (ε^2)/(4r)."],"<i>R</i> = <i>r</i>, <i>P</i><sub>max</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>4<i>r</i></span></span>."],[1,4,30,"text","How much power can you squeeze out?","There is a pretty corollary. Every power less than the maximum is delivered by two different loads, one on each side of the peak. Setting P(R_1) = P(R_2) and simplifying gives",["P(R_1) = P(R_2)"],""],[1,4,31,"formula","How much power can you squeeze out?","There is a pretty corollary. Every power less than the maximum is delivered by two different loads, one on each side of the peak. Setting P(R_1) = P(R_2) and simplifying gives",["R_1R_2 = r^2."],"<i>R</i><sub>1</sub><i>R</i><sub>2</sub> = <i>r</i><sup>2</sup>."],[1,4,32,"lab","One cell, one load","The curve shows the power delivered to the load as you change its resistance R . The bar on top shows where every watt of the cell’s output goes. Try these, in order: Drag the dot along the curve from left to right. The power climbs, peaks, and falls away. Read off the R at the peak and compare it with r . At the peak, look at the energy bar: exactly half goes to the load, half is lost inside the cell. Push R far to the right. The efficiency keeps rising towards 100% — but the power you actually get keeps shrinking. That is the trade-off. Turn on Twin load. For each R the lab marks the other resistance that draws the same power. Multiply the pair: you always get r^2 . Slide R to zero: a short circuit. The current is ε/r , the terminal voltage is zero, and all the power is burnt inside the cell.",["R","R","r","R","100%","R","r^2","R","ε/r"],""],[1,4,33,"q","Same heat from two loads · Integer answer","A cell produces heat at the same rate in an external resistor whether that resistor is 1 Ω or 4 Ω . What is the internal resistance of the cell, in ohms?",["1 Ω","4 Ω"],""],[1,4,33,"sol","Same heat from two loads · Integer answer","1/((1 + r)^2) = 4/((4 + r)^2) ⟹ 4 + r = 2(1 + r) ⟹ r = 2 Ω. Or use the shortcut: two loads giving equal power satisfy R_1R_2 = r^2 , so r = √(1 × 4) = 2 Ω . The two loads sit on opposite sides of the peak at R = r = 2 Ω — one too small, one too large. Write P = ε^2R/(R + r)^2 for the two loads and equate. The emf cancels.",["1/((1 + r)^2) = 4/((4 + r)^2) ⟹ 4 + r = 2(1 + r) ⟹ r = 2 Ω.","R_1R_2 = r^2","r = √(1 × 4) = 2 Ω","R = r = 2 Ω","P = ε^2R/(R + r)^2"],""],[1,4,34,"sec","Teams of cells","In series (the positive of one joined to the negative of the next). The same current runs through all, and each lifts the charge by its own emf. Emfs add, and so do the internal resistances:",[],""],[1,4,35,"text","Teams of cells","In series (the positive of one joined to the negative of the next). The same current runs through all, and each lifts the charge by its own emf. Emfs add, and so do the internal resistances:",[],""],[1,4,36,"formula","Teams of cells","In series (the positive of one joined to the negative of the next). The same current runs through all, and each lifts the charge by its own emf. Emfs add, and so do the internal resistances:",["ε_eq = ε_1 + ε_2 + … , r_eq = r_1 + r_2 + …"],"<i>ε</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">eq</span></sub> = <i>ε</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>ε</i><sub>2</sub> + … , <i>r</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">eq</span></sub> = <i>r</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>r</i><sub>2</sub> + …"],[1,4,37,"text","Teams of cells","If one cell is put in backwards, its emf subtracts — but its internal resistance still adds. With n identical cells and m of them reversed, the net emf is (n − 2m) ε while the resistance stays nr .",["n","m","(n − 2m) ε","nr"],""],[1,4,38,"text","Teams of cells","In parallel (all positives joined, all negatives joined):",[],""],[1,4,39,"formula","Teams of cells","In parallel (all positives joined, all negatives joined):",["ε_eq = 1/r_1 + 1/r_2ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2 , 1/(r_eq) = 1/r_1 + 1/r_2"],"<i>ε</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">eq</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> , <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">eq</span></sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[1,4,40,"text","Teams of cells","DerivationWhere the parallel formula comes from Let the common terminal voltage be V , and let the cells supply currents I_1 and I_2 . For each cell V = ε − Ir , so I_1 = (ε_1 − V)/r_1, I_2 = (ε_2 − V)/r_2. The total current delivered is I = I_1 + I_2 = (ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2) − V (1/r_1 + 1/r_2) . Rearrange into the form V = ε_eq − I r_eq and read off the two results above.",["V","I_1","I_2","V = ε − Ir","I_1 = (ε_1 − V)/r_1, I_2 = (ε_2 − V)/r_2.","I = I_1 + I_2 = (ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2) − V (1/r_1 + 1/r_2) .","V = ε_eq − I r_eq"],""],[1,4,40,"formula","Teams of cells","urrents I_1 and I_2 . For each cell V = ε − Ir , so I_1 = (ε_1 − V)/r_1, I_2 = (ε_2 − V)/r_2. The total current delivered is I = I_1 + I_2 = (ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2) − V (1/r_1 + 1/r_2) . Rearrange into the form V = ε_eq − I r_eq and read off the two results above.",["I_1 = (ε_1 − V)/r_1, I_2 = (ε_2 − V)/r_2."],"<i>I</i><sub>1</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>V</i></span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span>, <i>I</i><sub>2</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>2</sub> − <i>V</i></span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>."],[1,4,40,"formula","Teams of cells","urrents I_1 and I_2 . For each cell V = ε − Ir , so I_1 = (ε_1 − V)/r_1, I_2 = (ε_2 − V)/r_2. The total current delivered is I = I_1 + I_2 = (ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2) − V (1/r_1 + 1/r_2) . Rearrange into the form V = ε_eq − I r_eq and read off the two results above.",["I = I_1 + I_2 = (ε_1/r_1 + ε_2/r_2) − V (1/r_1 + 1/r_2) ."],"<i>I</i> = <i>I</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>I</i><sub>2</sub> = (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>) − <i>V</i> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>r</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>) ."],[1,4,43,"intuition","A tug of war settled by a weighted average","The equivalent emf of cells in parallel is a weighted average of their emfs, each weighted by 1/r . It always lies between the individual emfs — closer to the cell with the smaller internal resistance, which has the louder voice. For identical cells it is just ε , with internal resistance r/n : parallel cells do not raise the voltage, they let you draw more current.",["1/r","ε","r/n"],""],[1,4,44,"text","Teams of cells","When to use which? For n identical cells driving a load R :",["n","R"],""],[1,4,45,"formula","Teams of cells","When to use which? For n identical cells driving a load R :",["I_(series) = nε/(R + nr), I_(parallel) = ε/(R + r/n)."],"<i>I</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">series</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>ε</i></span><span><i>R</i> + <i>n</i><i>r</i></span></span>, <i>I</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">parallel</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i></span><span><i>R</i> + <i>r</i>/<i>n</i></span></span>."],[1,4,46,"text","Teams of cells","If R ≫ r , series wins (more voltage is what you need). If R ≪ r , parallel wins (the internal resistance is the bottleneck, so cut it down).",["R ≫ r","R ≪ r"],""],[1,4,47,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · Integer answer","Two batteries of different emfs and different internal resistances are connected as shown. Find the voltage across AB, in volts.",[],""],[1,4,47,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · Integer answer","Method 1 — follow the loop. The two emfs oppose each other around the loop, so the net emf driving the circulating current is 6 − 3 = 3 V , through a total resistance of 1 + 2 = 3 Ω : I = 1 A , flowing out of the 6 V battery and into the 3 V one. Now read the voltage across AB using either branch: V_AB = 6 − (1)(1) = 5 V or V_AB = 3 + (1)(2) = 5 V . Method 2 — the parallel formula. V_AB = (6/1 + 3/2)/(1/1 + 1/2) = 7.5/1.5 = 5 V . Answer: 5. The stronger battery is discharging; the weaker one is being charged, which is why its terminal voltage (5 V) is above its emf. Nothing else is connected between A and B. So the only current is the one circulating round the loop formed by the two batteries.",["6 − 3 = 3 V","1 + 2 = 3 Ω","I = 1 A ,","6 V","3 V","V_AB = 6 − (1)(1) = 5 V or V_AB = 3 + (1)(2) = 5 V .","V_AB = (6/1 + 3/2)/(1/1 + 1/2) = 7.5/1.5 = 5 V .","5 V"],""],[1,4,48,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","When two identical batteries of internal resistance 1 Ω each are connected in series across a resistor R , the rate of heat produced in R is J_1 . When the same batteries are connected in parallel across R , the rate is J_2 . If J_1 = 2.25 J_2 , find the value of R in ohms.",["1 Ω","R","R","J_1","R","J_2","J_1 = 2.25 J_2","R"],""],[1,4,48,"sol","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","Heat rate in R is I^2R , so J_1/J_2 = (I_1/I_2)^2 = 2.25 , which means I_1/I_2 = 1.5 . Series: emf 2ε , internal resistance 2 Ω : I_1 = 2ε/(R + 2) . Parallel: emf ε , internal resistance 0.5 Ω : I_2 = ε/(R + 0.5) . I_1/I_2 = (2(R + 0.5))/(R + 2) = 1.5 ⟹ 2R + 1 = 1.5R + 3 ⟹ R = 4 Ω. Answer: 4.",["R","I^2R","J_1/J_2 = (I_1/I_2)^2 = 2.25","I_1/I_2 = 1.5","2ε","2 Ω","I_1 = 2ε/(R + 2)","ε","0.5 Ω","I_2 = ε/(R + 0.5)","I_1/I_2 = (2(R + 0.5))/(R + 2) = 1.5 ⟹ 2R + 1 = 1.5R + 3 ⟹ R = 4 Ω."],""],[1,4,49,"pyq","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","Two sources of equal emf are connected in series to an external resistance R . The internal resistances of the two sources are R_1 and R_2 (R_2 > R_1). If the potential difference across the source having internal resistance R_2 is zero, then R = (R_1R_2)/(R_1 + R_2) · R = (R_1R_2)/(R_2 − R_1) · R = (R_2 (R_1 + R_2))/(R_2 − R_1) · R = R_2 − R_1",["R","R_1","R_2","R_2 > R_1","R_2","R = (R_1R_2)/(R_1 + R_2)","R = (R_1R_2)/(R_2 − R_1)","R = (R_2 (R_1 + R_2))/(R_2 − R_1)","R = R_2 − R_1"],""],[1,4,49,"sol","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","Let each emf be ε . In series the current is I = 2ε/(R + R_1 + R_2). The terminal voltage of the second source is ε − IR_2 . For this to be zero, I = ε/R_2 : 2ε/(R + R_1 + R_2) = ε/R_2 ⟹ R + R_1 + R_2 = 2R_2 ⟹ R = R_2 − R_1. Answer (D). That cell is working as hard as if it were short-circuited: every volt it generates is lost across its own internal resistance.",["ε","I = 2ε/(R + R_1 + R_2).","ε − IR_2","I = ε/R_2","2ε/(R + R_1 + R_2) = ε/R_2 ⟹ R + R_1 + R_2 = 2R_2 ⟹ R = R_2 − R_1."],""],[1,4,50,"q","Two cells the wrong way round · Numerical answer","Ten identical cells, each of emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.2 Ω , are connected in series to a 4 Ω resistor. By mistake, two of the cells are connected the wrong way round. Find the current in the circuit, in amperes.",["1.5 V","0.2 Ω","4 Ω"],""],[1,4,50,"sol","Two cells the wrong way round · Numerical answer","Eight cells push one way and two push the other. The two reversed cells each cancel one good cell, so the net emf is that of 10 − 2 × 2 = 6 cells: ε_net = 6 × 1.5 = 9 V . But all ten internal resistances are still in the circuit: 10 × 0.2 = 2 Ω . I = 9/(4 + 2) = 1.5 A . Connected correctly the current would have been 15/6 = 2.5 A . A reversed cell hurts twice: it subtracts its emf and keeps its resistance.",["10 − 2 × 2 = 6","ε_net = 6 × 1.5 = 9 V .","10 × 0.2 = 2 Ω","I = 9/(4 + 2) = 1.5 A .","15/6 = 2.5 A"],""],[1,4,51,"recap","Recap","Emf ε = work done by the cell per unit charge. It is energy per charge (volts), not a force. Terminal voltage: V = ε − Ir when delivering current, V = ε + Ir when being charged, V = ε on open circuit. I = ε/(R + r) ; short-circuit current ε/r . The V – I line has intercept ε and slope −r . Maximum power to the load at R = r : P_max = ε^2/4r , at 50% efficiency. Equal power for two loads: R_1R_2 = r^2 . Series: emfs add (a reversed cell subtracts), resistances always add. Parallel: ε_eq = (∑ ε_i/r_i)/(∑ 1/r_i) , 1/r_eq = ∑ 1/r_i . @end",["ε","V = ε − Ir","V = ε + Ir","V = ε","I = ε/(R + r)","ε/r","V","I","ε","−r","R = r","P_max = ε^2/4r"],""],[1,5,0,"lesson","Kirchhoff’s Laws","When a circuit refuses to reduce to series and parallel, two bookkeeping rules — one for charge, one for energy — solve it anyway. Learn the sign ritual once and no network can scare you.",[],""],[1,5,1,"sec","Two laws, no new physics","Some circuits simply will not reduce. Two batteries in different branches, an unbalanced bridge — stare as long as you like, nothing is in series or parallel with anything.",[],""],[1,5,2,"text","Two laws, no new physics","Some circuits simply will not reduce. Two batteries in different branches, an unbalanced bridge — stare as long as you like, nothing is in series or parallel with anything.",[],""],[1,5,3,"text","Two laws, no new physics","In 1845 a 21-year-old student in Königsberg, Gustav Kirchhoff, wrote down two rules that handle every such circuit. The beautiful thing is that neither is a new law of nature. They are two conservation laws you already trust, dressed for circuit work:",[],""],[1,5,4,"table","Two laws, no new physics","Says Is really Junction rule current in = current out conservation of charge Loop rule potential changes round a closed loop add to zero conservation of energy",[],""],[1,5,5,"sec","The junction rule","At any junction in a steady circuit, charge cannot pile up and cannot be created. So what flows in must flow out:",[],""],[1,5,6,"text","The junction rule","At any junction in a steady circuit, charge cannot pile up and cannot be created. So what flows in must flow out:",[],""],[1,5,7,"formula","The junction rule","At any junction in a steady circuit, charge cannot pile up and cannot be created. So what flows in must flow out:",["∑ I_in = ∑ I_out"],"∑ <i>I</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">in</span></sub> = ∑ <i>I</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">out</span></sub>"],[1,5,8,"text","The junction rule","Use it early and it saves you unknowns. If I_1 and I_2 flow into a junction, do not invent an I_3 for the third wire — write I_1 + I_2 straight onto the diagram.",["I_1","I_2","I_3","I_1 + I_2"],""],[1,5,9,"sec","The loop rule and the sign ritual","Potential is like height on a hill. Walk any closed path on a hill and come back to your starting point: the total climb equals the total descent. In a circuit:",[],""],[1,5,10,"text","The loop rule and the sign ritual","Potential is like height on a hill. Walk any closed path on a hill and come back to your starting point: the total climb equals the total descent. In a circuit:",[],""],[1,5,11,"formula","The loop rule and the sign ritual","Potential is like height on a hill. Walk any closed path on a hill and come back to your starting point: the total climb equals the total descent. In a circuit:",["∑_(closed loop) ΔV = 0"],"∑<sub><span class=\"fx-t\">closed loop</span></sub> Δ<i>V</i> = 0"],[1,5,12,"text","The loop rule and the sign ritual","The whole art is getting the signs right. Pick a direction to walk round the loop, and as you cross each element ask one question: did I just go up or down in potential?",[],""],[1,5,13,"text","The loop rule and the sign ritual","Resistor, walking with the current: current flows from high to low potential, so you go down: −IR . Resistor, walking against the current: you go up: +IR . Cell, walking from − to + : you go up by ε : +ε . Cell, walking from + to − : you go down: −ε .",["−IR","+IR","−","+","ε","+ε","+","−","−ε"],""],[1,5,14,"text","The loop rule and the sign ritual","For a cell, only the direction you walk matters — not the direction of the current.",[],""],[1,5,15,"key","The five-step recipe","Label a current in every branch, with an arrow. Guess the directions freely. A wrong guess simply comes out negative. Use the junction rule at once to keep the number of unknowns small. Choose as many independent loops as you have unknowns. Walk each loop, writing ±IR and ±ε by the ritual above. Solve, then check with a loop you have not used.",["±IR","±ε"],""],[1,5,16,"sec","A worked example, slowly","Two batteries share a load. Find all three currents.",[],""],[1,5,17,"text","A worked example, slowly","Two batteries share a load. Find all three currents.",[],""],[1,5,18,"text","A worked example, slowly","Take ε_1 = 10 V , ε_2 = 6 V , R_1 = R_2 = 2 Ω and R_3 = 1 Ω .",["ε_1 = 10 V","ε_2 = 6 V","R_1 = R_2 = 2 Ω","R_3 = 1 Ω"],""],[1,5,19,"text","A worked example, slowly","Steps 1 and 2. Let I_1 flow up through the left battery and I_2 up through the right one. Both arrive at the top junction A, so the middle branch carries I_1 + I_2 downwards.",["I_1","I_2","I_1 + I_2"],""],[1,5,20,"text","A worked example, slowly","Steps 3 and 4. Walk the left loop clockwise, starting from the bottom junction B: up through ε_1 (from − to +), through R_1 with the current, then down through R_3 with the current.",["ε_1","−","+","R_1","R_3"],""],[1,5,21,"formula","A worked example, slowly","Steps 3 and 4. Walk the left loop clockwise, starting from the bottom junction B: up through ε_1 (from − to +), through R_1 with the current, then down through R_3 with the current.",["+10 − 2I_1 − 1 (I_1 + I_2) = 0"],"+10 − 2<i>I</i><sub>1</sub> − 1 (<i>I</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>I</i><sub>2</sub>) = 0"],[1,5,22,"text","A worked example, slowly","Walk the right loop anticlockwise from B: up through ε_2 , through R_2 , down through R_3 .",["ε_2","R_2","R_3"],""],[1,5,23,"formula","A worked example, slowly","Walk the right loop anticlockwise from B: up through ε_2 , through R_2 , down through R_3 .",["+6 − 2I_2 − 1 (I_1 + I_2) = 0"],"+6 − 2<i>I</i><sub>2</sub> − 1 (<i>I</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>I</i><sub>2</sub>) = 0"],[1,5,24,"text","A worked example, slowly","Step 5. Subtract the second from the first: 4 − 2I_1 + 2I_2 = 0 , so I_1 = I_2 + 2 . Put that back into the second equation:",["4 − 2I_1 + 2I_2 = 0","I_1 = I_2 + 2"],""],[1,5,25,"formula","A worked example, slowly","Step 5. Subtract the second from the first: 4 − 2I_1 + 2I_2 = 0 , so I_1 = I_2 + 2 . Put that back into the second equation:",["6 − 2I_2 − (2I_2 + 2) = 0 ⟹ I_2 = 1 A , I_1 = 3 A , I_1 + I_2 = 4 A ."],"6 − 2<i>I</i><sub>2</sub> − (2<i>I</i><sub>2</sub> + 2) = 0 ⟹ <i>I</i><sub>2</sub> = 1 <span class=\"fx-t\">A</span>, <i>I</i><sub>1</sub> = 3 <span class=\"fx-t\">A</span>, <i>I</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>I</i><sub>2</sub> = 4 <span class=\"fx-t\">A</span>."],[1,5,26,"text","A worked example, slowly","Check with the big outer loop (up the left side, down the right): +10 − 2(3) + 2(1) − 6 = 0 . ✓",["+10 − 2(3) + 2(1) − 6 = 0"],""],[1,5,27,"intuition","What a negative current is telling you","Repeat the example with a weaker second battery, ε_2 = 2 V . You will find I_2 = −0.5 A . Nothing has gone wrong: the minus sign says the current in that branch flows opposite to your arrow — into the positive terminal of the weak battery. The strong battery is charging the weak one. Never go back and redraw; just read the sign.",["ε_2 = 2 V","I_2 = −0.5 A"],""],[1,5,28,"sec","The node-voltage shortcut","There is a faster way to run the same physics, and it is the one to use in the exam.",[],""],[1,5,29,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","There is a faster way to run the same physics, and it is the one to use in the exam.",[],""],[1,5,30,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","Call one junction zero volts (you are free to: only differences of potential matter). Give the potential of each remaining junction a name. Then write the junction rule, with every current expressed through Ohm’s law as “(potential difference across the resistor) ÷ resistance”.",[],""],[1,5,31,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","In the example, take B as zero and let A be at potential V . Go up the left branch from B: the battery lifts you to 10 V , so the resistor R_1 has 10 V at one end and V at the other, and it sends a current (10 − V)/2 into A. The right branch works the same way. Now say “in = out” at A:",["V","10 V","R_1","10 V","V","(10 − V)/2"],""],[1,5,32,"formula","The node-voltage shortcut","zero and let A be at potential V . Go up the left branch from B: the battery lifts you to 10 V , so the resistor R_1 has 10 V at one end and V at the other, and it sends a current (10 − V)/2 into A. The right branch works the same way. Now say “in = out” at A:",["from the left (10 − V)/2 + from the right (6 − V)/2 = down the middle (V − 0)/1"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">from the left</span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>10 − <i>V</i></span><span>2</span></span> + <span class=\"fx-t\">from the right</span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>6 − <i>V</i></span><span>2</span></span> = <span class=\"fx-t\">down the middle</span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i> − 0</span><span>1</span></span>"],[1,5,33,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","One equation, one unknown: 16 − 2V = 2V , so V = 4 V . Then I_1 = (10 − 4)/2 = 3 A , I_2 = (6 − 4)/2 = 1 A , and the middle branch carries 4/1 = 4 A . Same answers, a third of the work.",["16 − 2V = 2V","V = 4 V","I_1 = (10 − 4)/2 = 3 A","I_2 = (6 − 4)/2 = 1 A","4/1 = 4 A"],""],[1,5,34,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","Solving that equation for V in general gives a formula you have already met:",["V"],""],[1,5,35,"formula","The node-voltage shortcut","Solving that equation for V in general gives a formula you have already met:",["V = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2 + 1/R_3ε_1/R_1 + ε_2/R_2"],"<i>V</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>3</sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[1,5,36,"text","The node-voltage shortcut","— the parallel-cells formula of Lesson 4, with the load R_3 counted as a “cell” of zero emf.",["R_3"],""],[1,5,37,"lab","Two batteries, one load","The circuit of the worked example, alive. The moving dots show conventional current; their speed follows the size of the current in each branch. Try these, in order: Start with the default values and check the readouts against the worked example: 3 A , 1 A , 4 A . Slowly lower ε_2 . Watch the dots in the right-hand branch slow down, stop, and then run backwards. At the moment they stop, compare ε_2 with the junction voltage V_A . With I_2 negative, the right-hand battery is being charged: the junction voltage V_A has climbed above ε_2 , so current is pushed into its positive terminal. Whatever you set, the junction rule holds: the top readout always shows I_1 + I_2 = I_3 . Make R_3 very large. Hardly any current reaches the load, and the two batteries simply fight each other round the outer loop.",["3 A","1 A","4 A","ε_2","ε_2","V_A","I_2","V_A","ε_2","I_1 + I_2 = I_3","R_3"],""],[1,5,38,"q","Push and push back · Single correct","A 12 V battery of internal resistance 1 Ω and a 6 V battery of internal resistance 2 Ω are connected in a single loop with a 3 Ω resistor, so that their emfs oppose each other. The potential difference across the terminals of the 6 V battery is 4 V · 6 V · 8 V · 10 V",["12 V","1 Ω","6 V","2 Ω","3 Ω","6 V","4 V","6 V","8 V","10 V"],""],[1,5,38,"sol","Push and push back · Single correct","The emfs oppose, so the net emf round the loop is 12 − 6 = 6 V . The total resistance is 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 Ω : I = 1 A , in the direction the stronger battery wants. That current is forced backwards through the 6 V battery — it is being charged — so its terminal voltage is V = ε + Ir = 6 + (1)(2) = 8 V . Answer (C). Option (A), 4 V , comes from using ε − Ir for a battery that is not delivering current but receiving it.",["12 − 6 = 6 V","1 + 2 + 3 = 6 Ω","I = 1 A ,","6 V","V = ε + Ir = 6 + (1)(2) = 8 V .","4 V","ε − Ir"],""],[1,5,39,"q","Three roads to one junction · Integer answer","Three resistors of 2 Ω , 3 Ω and 6 Ω meet at a junction O. Their other ends are held at potentials of 12 V , 6 V and 0 V respectively, as shown. Find the potential of O, in volts.",["2 Ω","3 Ω","6 Ω","12 V","6 V","0 V"],""],[1,5,39,"sol","Three roads to one junction · Integer answer","Junction rule at O, with every current written as (potential difference) ÷ (resistance): (V − 12)/2 + (V − 6)/3 + (V − 0)/6 = 0. Multiply through by 6 : 3(V − 12) + 2(V − 6) + V = 0 ⟹ 6V = 48 ⟹ V = 8 V . Answer: 8. One equation, and every current follows: 2 A arrives from the 12 V point, while 2/3 A leaves towards the 6 V point and 4/3 A leaves towards the 0 V point. In equals out. Let the potential of O be V . The total current flowing out of O along the three resistors must be zero.",["(V − 12)/2 + (V − 6)/3 + (V − 0)/6 = 0.","6","3(V − 12) + 2(V − 6) + V = 0 ⟹ 6V = 48 ⟹ V = 8 V .","2 A","12 V","2/3 A","6 V","4/3 A","0 V","V"],""],[1,5,40,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","Two ideal batteries of emf V_1 and V_2 and three resistances R_1 , R_2 and R_3 are connected as shown in the figure. The current in resistance R_2 would be zero if V_1 = V_2 and R_1 = R_2 = R_3 · V_1 = V_2 and R_1 = 2R_2 = R_3 · V_1 = 2V_2 and 2R_1 = 2R_2 = R_3 · 2V_1 = V_2 and 2R_1 = R_2 = R_3",["V_1","V_2","R_1","R_2","R_3","R_2","V_1 = V_2","R_1 = R_2 = R_3","V_1 = V_2","R_1 = 2R_2 = R_3","V_1 = 2V_2","2R_1 = 2R_2 = R_3"],""],[1,5,40,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","Suppose the current in R_2 is zero. Two things follow. (i) With nothing going down the middle, the same current I circulates round the outer loop, driven by both batteries together: I = (V_1 + V_2)/(R_1 + R_3). (ii) No current through R_2 means no voltage across it: its two ends are at the same potential. So going round the left loop alone, the battery’s rise must be exactly used up in R_1 : V_1 = IR_1. Combine the two: V_1(R_1 + R_3) = (V_1 + V_2)R_1 ⟹ V_1R_3 = V_2R_1 Notice that R_2 does not appear at all. Now test the options: (A) V_1 = V_2 , R_1 = R_3 : satisfied. ✓ (B) V_1 = V_2 , R_1 = R_3 again (the value of R_2 is irrelevant): satisfied. ✓ (C) V_1 = 2V_2 , R_3 = 2R_1 : V_1R_3 = 4V_2R_1  = V_2R_1 . ✗ (D) V_2 = 2V_1 , R_3 = 2R_1 : V_1(2R_1) = (2V_1)R_1 . ✓ Answer (A), (B), (D). If R_2 carries no current, there is no potential difference across it — and the same current must flow through R_1 and R_3 .",["R_2","I","I = (V_1 + V_2)/(R_1 + R_3).","R_2","R_1","V_1 = IR_1.","V_1(R_1 + R_3) = (V_1 + V_2)R_1 ⟹ V_1R_3 = V_2R_1","R_2","V_1 = V_2","R_1 = R_3","V_1 = V_2","R_1 = R_3"],""],[1,5,41,"sec","Finding the potential difference between any two points","Once the currents are known, the potential difference between any two points is found by walking from one to the other along any path, adding rises and drops with the same sign ritual:",[],""],[1,5,42,"text","Finding the potential difference between any two points","Once the currents are known, the potential difference between any two points is found by walking from one to the other along any path, adding rises and drops with the same sign ritual:",[],""],[1,5,43,"formula","Finding the potential difference between any two points","Once the currents are known, the potential difference between any two points is found by walking from one to the other along any path, adding rises and drops with the same sign ritual:",["V_A − V_B = ∑(drops) − ∑(rises) walking from A to B."],"<i>V</i><sub><i>A</i></sub> − <i>V</i><sub><i>B</i></sub> = ∑(<span class=\"fx-t\">drops</span>) − ∑(<span class=\"fx-t\">rises</span>) <span class=\"fx-t\">walking from A to B.</span>"],[1,5,44,"text","Finding the potential difference between any two points","Every path gives the same answer — that is the loop rule again.",[],""],[1,5,45,"trap","Zero current does not mean zero voltage — and vice versa","A bare wire can carry a large current with no potential difference across it. A branch carrying no current has no drop across its resistors — but there can still be a potential difference across a cell or an open switch in it. Earthing one point of a circuit fixes that point at zero potential. It does not change any current, because a single earth connection gives the charge no loop to go round.",[],""],[1,5,46,"q","The battery that takes instead of gives · One or more correct","In the two-battery circuit of the worked example, the values are changed to ε_1 = 10 V , ε_2 = 5 V , R_1 = R_2 = 1 Ω and R_3 = 2 Ω . Which of the following is/are correct? The potential difference between the junctions A and B is 6 V . · The current through R_3 is 3 A . · The 5 V battery is being charged. · The 5 V battery supplies 5 W to the rest of the circuit.",["ε_1 = 10 V","ε_2 = 5 V","R_1 = R_2 = 1 Ω","R_3 = 2 Ω","6 V","R_3","3 A","5 V","5 V","5 W"],""],[1,5,46,"sol","The battery that takes instead of gives · One or more correct","Junction rule at A: (10 − V)/1 + (5 − V)/1 = V/2 ⟹ 15 = 2.5 V ⟹ V = 6 V . (A) V_A − V_B = 6 V . Correct. (B) I_3 = 6/2 = 3 A . Correct. (C) I_2 = (5 − 6)/1 = −1 A . Negative: one ampere flows into the positive terminal of the 5 V battery. It is being charged. Correct. (D) A battery being charged absorbs energy: here ε_2∣I_2∣ = 5 W is being stored in it, not supplied by it. Wrong. Answer (A), (B), (C). Energy check: the 10 V battery supplies 10 × 4 = 40 W . The resistors burn 4^2(1) + 1^2(1) + 3^2(2) = 35 W , and 5 W goes into charging. It balances. Use the node-voltage method: B at zero, A at V .",["(10 − V)/1 + (5 − V)/1 = V/2 ⟹ 15 = 2.5 V ⟹ V = 6 V .","V_A − V_B = 6 V","I_3 = 6/2 = 3 A","I_2 = (5 − 6)/1 = −1 A","5 V","ε_2∣I_2∣ = 5 W","10 V","10 × 4 = 40 W","4^2(1) + 1^2(1) + 3^2(2) = 35 W","5 W","V"],""],[1,5,47,"q","A shortcut across the middle · Numerical answer","An ideal 12 V battery is connected across two parallel branches. The first branch has 2 Ω followed by 4 Ω , meeting at P. The second has 4 Ω followed by 2 Ω , meeting at Q. A resistanceless wire now joins P and Q. Find the current in that wire, in amperes.",["12 V","2 Ω","4 Ω","4 Ω","2 Ω"],""],[1,5,47,"sol","A shortcut across the middle · Numerical answer","The wire makes P and Q a single node. The two upper resistors (2 Ω and 4 Ω) are now in parallel, and so are the two lower ones (4 Ω and 2 Ω). Each pair is 4/3 Ω , so by symmetry the node sits at half the battery voltage: 6 V . Now do the bookkeeping at P: arriving through the upper 2 Ω : (12 − 6)/2 = 3 A leaving through the lower 4 Ω : (6 − 0)/4 = 1.5 A The difference, 3 − 1.5 = 1.5 A , has only one place to go: along the wire from P to Q. Answer: 1.5 A. A wire with no voltage across it, carrying a perfectly healthy current. (Before the wire was added, P was at 8 V and Q at 4 V — which is why current flows from P to Q once they are joined.) With the wire in place, P and Q are one node. Find its potential first; then look at how much current arrives at P and how much leaves it downwards.",["2 Ω","4 Ω","4 Ω","2 Ω","4/3 Ω","6 V","2 Ω","(12 − 6)/2 = 3 A","4 Ω","(6 − 0)/4 = 1.5 A","3 − 1.5 = 1.5 A","8 V"],""],[1,5,48,"recap","Recap","Junction rule (charge): ∑ I_in = ∑ I_out . Loop rule (energy): ∑ ΔV = 0 round any closed loop. Signs while walking: with the current through R → −IR ; against → +IR ; cell from − to + → +ε ; from + to − → −ε . Guess current directions freely. A negative answer means “the other way”. Node-voltage method: set one junction to zero, write the junction rule in terms of potentials. Usually the fastest route. A battery with current entering its positive terminal is being charged: it absorbs power εI . V_A − V_B is the same along every path between A and B. @end",["∑ I_in = ∑ I_out","∑ ΔV = 0","R","−IR","+IR","−","+","+ε","+","−","−ε","εI"],""],[1,6,0,"lesson","Energy, Power & the Heating Effect","Every coulomb that falls through a volt hands over a joule. Follow the energy and you will know why a 40 W bulb can outshine a 100 W one, how to read the label on any appliance, and why power lines carry lakhs of volts.",[],""],[1,6,1,"sec","Where does the energy go?","A charge dq moves through a device from the high-potential end to the low-potential end, falling through a potential difference V . Like a ball rolling downhill, it loses potential energy V dq . With a current I , charge dq = I dt makes this fall every dt seconds, so energy is handed to the device a",[],""],[1,6,2,"text","Where does the energy go?","A charge dq moves through a device from the high-potential end to the low-potential end, falling through a potential difference V . Like a ball rolling downhill, it loses potential energy V dq . With a current I , charge dq = I dt makes this fall every dt seconds, so energy is handed to the device at the rate",["dq","V","V dq","I","dq = I dt","dt"],""],[1,6,3,"formula","Where does the energy go?","otential end to the low-potential end, falling through a potential difference V . Like a ball rolling downhill, it loses potential energy V dq . With a current I , charge dq = I dt makes this fall every dt seconds, so energy is handed to the device at the rate",["P = VI unit: watt (W) = J s ^(−1)"],"<i>P</i> = <i>V</i><i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">unit: watt (W)</span> = <span class=\"fx-t\">J s</span><sup>−1</sup>"],[1,6,4,"text","Where does the energy go?","This is true for any device: a resistor, a motor, a battery on charge. What the device does with the energy is its own business — a motor turns most of it into mechanical work, a charging battery stores it chemically.",[],""],[1,6,5,"text","Where does the energy go?","A resistor has only one thing it can do: turn it all into heat. Remember the electrons of Lesson 1 — speed up, crash, forget? Every crash dumps the energy the electron gained from the field into the vibrating lattice. The wire warms up. Using V = IR :",["V = IR"],""],[1,6,6,"formula","Where does the energy go?","A resistor has only one thing it can do: turn it all into heat. Remember the electrons of Lesson 1 — speed up, crash, forget? Every crash dumps the energy the electron gained from the field into the vibrating lattice. The wire warms up. Using V = IR :",["P = VI = I^2R = (V^2)/R and in a time t : H = I^2Rt."],"<i>P</i> = <i>V</i><i>I</i> = <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-t\">and in a time </span><i>t</i> : <i>H</i> = <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i><i>t</i>."],[1,6,7,"text","Where does the energy go?","This is Joule’s law of heating, found by James Joule in the early 1840s by dunking current-carrying wires in water and patiently reading a thermometer.",[],""],[1,6,8,"intuition","What your electricity bill actually counts","The “unit” on your bill is the kilowatt-hour: the energy used by a 1 kW appliance running for one hour. 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10^6 J . It is a unit of energy, not of power. A 100 W bulb takes ten hours to use one unit; a 2 kW geyser takes half an hour.",["1 kW","1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10^6 J .","100 W","2 kW"],""],[1,6,8,"formula","What your electricity bill actually counts","e “unit” on your bill is the kilowatt-hour: the energy used by a 1 kW appliance running for one hour. 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10^6 J . It is a unit of energy, not of power. A 100 W bulb takes ten hours to use one unit; a 2 kW geyser takes half an hour.",["1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10^6 J ."],"1 <span class=\"fx-t\">kWh</span> = 1000 <span class=\"fx-t\">W</span> × 3600 <span class=\"fx-t\">s</span> = 3.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">J</span>."],[1,6,10,"sec","I^2R or V^2/R ? Ask what is shared","P = I^2R says a bigger resistance gets hotter. P = V^2/R says a smaller one does. Both are right — they answer different questions.",[],""],[1,6,11,"text","I^2R or V^2/R ? Ask what is shared","P = I^2R says a bigger resistance gets hotter. P = V^2/R says a smaller one does. Both are right — they answer different questions.",["P = I^2R","P = V^2/R"],""],[1,6,12,"text","I^2R or V^2/R ? Ask what is shared","In series the resistors share the same current. Compare them with P = I^2R : the larger resistance takes more power. In parallel they share the same voltage. Compare them with P = V^2/R : the smaller resistance takes more power.",["P = I^2R","P = V^2/R"],""],[1,6,13,"text","I^2R or V^2/R ? Ask what is shared","So before comparing powers, always ask: what is the same for these two — the current or the voltage?",[],""],[1,6,14,"sec","Reading the label on a bulb","A bulb marked “ 100 W , 220 V ” is making a promise: if you give me 220 V , I will use 100 W . What the label really tells you is the bulb’s resistance:",[],""],[1,6,15,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","A bulb marked “ 100 W , 220 V ” is making a promise: if you give me 220 V , I will use 100 W . What the label really tells you is the bulb’s resistance:",["100 W","220 V","220 V","100 W"],""],[1,6,16,"formula","Reading the label on a bulb","A bulb marked “ 100 W , 220 V ” is making a promise: if you give me 220 V , I will use 100 W . What the label really tells you is the bulb’s resistance:",["R = (V_(rated)^2)/(P_(rated)) = (220^2)/100 = 484 Ω."],"<i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">rated</span></sub><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">rated</span></sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>220<sup>2</sup></span><span>100</span></span> = 484 Ω."],[1,6,17,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","Two things follow at once.",[],""],[1,6,18,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","Higher wattage means lower resistance. At the same rated voltage, a 100 W bulb has a quarter of the resistance of a 25 W bulb — a thicker filament.",["100 W","25 W"],""],[1,6,19,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","Rated power is not actual power. Give the bulb a different voltage V and (treating R as constant) it uses",["V","R"],""],[1,6,20,"formula","Reading the label on a bulb","Rated power is not actual power. Give the bulb a different voltage V and (treating R as constant) it uses",["P = (V^2)/R = P_(rated) (V/(V_(rated)))^2 ."],"<i>P</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> = <i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">rated</span></sub> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>V</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">rated</span></sub></span></span>)<sup>2</sup> ."],[1,6,21,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","At half the rated voltage a bulb delivers only a quarter of its rated power.",[],""],[1,6,22,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","Two bulbs together (both rated for the supply voltage, with rated powers P_1 and P_2):",["P_1","P_2"],""],[1,6,23,"text","Reading the label on a bulb","In parallel across the supply, each gets its full rated voltage: total power P_1 + P_2 . This is how your house is wired. In series across the supply, the total power is (P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2) — less than either bulb alone. And because the current is shared, the bulb with the larger resistance — the lower wattage — glows brighter.",["P_1 + P_2","(P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2)"],""],[1,6,24,"lab","Which bulb wins?","Two filament bulbs on a 220 V supply. Set the wattage printed on each, then choose how to wire them. The glow shows the power each bulb actually gets. (Filament resistances are treated as constant.) Try these, in order: Parallel, 25 W and 100 W . Each bulb gets the full 220 V and delivers exactly what its label says. Switch to Series. The 100 W bulb fades almost to nothing while the 25 W one stays fairly bright. Same current through both — and the 25 W bulb has four times the resistance. Add up the two actual powers in series and compare with (P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2) . Make the two ratings equal, in series. Each bulb gets half the voltage — and only a quarter of its rated power. In series, push one rating up to 200 W . The better the bulb on the label, the dimmer it gets. Why?",["220 V","25 W","100 W","220 V","100 W","25 W","25 W","(P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2)","200 W"],""],[1,6,25,"q","The underdog · Single correct","A 25 W , 220 V bulb and a 100 W , 220 V bulb are connected in series across a 220 V supply. Treating their resistances as constant, the 100 W bulb is brighter and uses 16 W · the 25 W bulb is brighter and uses 16 W · the 25 W bulb is brighter and uses 25 W · both are equally bright and use 20 W each",["25 W","220 V","100 W","220 V","220 V","100 W","16 W","25 W","16 W","25 W","25 W","20 W"],""],[1,6,25,"sol","The underdog · Single correct","Resistances: R_25 = (220^2)/25 = 1936 Ω and R_100 = (220^2)/100 = 484 Ω . Current (the same through both): I = 220/(1936 + 484) = 1/11 A . Actual powers: P_25 = I^2R_25 = (1936)/121 = 16 W , P_100 = I^2R_100 = 484/121 = 4 W . The 25 W bulb takes four times the power of the 100 W one. Answer (B). Check with the shortcut: total = (25 × 100)/125 = 20 W , split 4 : 1 in the ratio of the resistances.",["R_25 = (220^2)/25 = 1936 Ω","R_100 = (220^2)/100 = 484 Ω","I = 220/(1936 + 484) = 1/11 A","P_25 = I^2R_25 = (1936)/121 = 16 W , P_100 = I^2R_100 = 484/121 = 4 W .","25 W","100 W","= (25 × 100)/125 = 20 W","4 : 1"],""],[1,6,26,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","A 100 W bulb B_1 and two 60 W bulbs B_2 and B_3 (all rated for 250 V) are connected to a 250 V source as shown in the figure. W_1 , W_2 and W_3 are the output powers of the bulbs B_1 , B_2 and B_3 respectively. Then W_1 > W_2 = W_3 · W_1 > W_2 > W_3 · W_1 < W_2 = W_3 · W_1 < W_2 < W_3",["100 W","B_1","60 W","B_2","B_3","250 V","250 V","W_1","W_2","W_3","B_1","B_2"],""],[1,6,26,"sol","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","B_3 sits directly across 250 V , its rated voltage, so it delivers its full W_3 = 60 W . B_1 and B_2 are in series, sharing the 250 V between them. Neither gets its rated voltage, so both run below their ratings; in particular W_2 < 60 W = W_3 . They carry the same current, so the one with the larger resistance takes more power. From R = V^2/P , the 60 W bulb has the larger resistance. Hence W_2 > W_1 . W_1 < W_2 < W_3. Answer (D). With numbers: R ∝ 1/P gives R_1 : R_2 = 3 : 5 , so B_1 and B_2 get 3/8 and 5/8 of the voltage, and W_1 = 100 (3/8)^2 ≈ 14 W , W_2 = 60 (5/8)^2 ≈ 23 W , W_3 = 60 W .",["B_3","250 V","W_3 = 60 W","B_1","B_2","250 V","W_2 < 60 W = W_3","R = V^2/P","60 W","W_2 > W_1","W_1 < W_2 < W_3.","R ∝ 1/P"],""],[1,6,27,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2010 · Single correct","Incandescent bulbs are designed by keeping in mind that the resistance of their filament increases with increase in temperature. If at room temperature, 100 W , 60 W and 40 W bulbs have filament resistances R_100 , R_60 and R_40 respectively, the relation between these resistances is 1/(R_100) = 1/(R_40) + 1/(R_60) · R_100 = R_40 + R_60 · R_100 > R_60 > R_40 · 1/(R_100) > 1/(R_60) > 1/(R_40)",["100 W","60 W","40 W","R_100","R_60","R_40","1/(R_100) = 1/(R_40) + 1/(R_60)","R_100 = R_40 + R_60","R_100 > R_60 > R_40","1/(R_100) > 1/(R_60) > 1/(R_40)"],""],[1,6,27,"sol","IIT-JEE 2010 · Single correct","All three bulbs are made for the same supply voltage, so for the hot filaments R = V^2/P : the higher the wattage, the lower the resistance. At room temperature every filament has a much smaller resistance than when hot — but a thicker filament is still a thicker filament. The ordering survives: R_100 < R_60 < R_40 ⟹ 1/(R_100) > 1/(R_60) > 1/(R_40). Answer (D). Why not (A)? For the hot resistances 1/R ∝ P exactly, and 100 = 40 + 60 , so (A) would be true of the working resistances. But the question asks about room temperature. The three filaments do not run at exactly the same temperature, so their resistances do not all shrink by the same factor on cooling, and the exact equality is lost. That is precisely what the opening sentence of the question is warning you about. The rating fixes the resistance of the hot filament. The question asks about the cold one. What survives the change of temperature — an exact equation, or just an ordering?",["R = V^2/P","R_100 < R_60 < R_40 ⟹ 1/(R_100) > 1/(R_60) > 1/(R_40).","1/R ∝ P","100 = 40 + 60"],""],[1,6,28,"sec","Heaters, kettles and fuses","A heater on the mains has a fixed voltage across it, so its power is V^2/R . To make a more powerful heater you need less resistance — a shorter or thicker element. The time needed to supply a given amount of heat H is",[],""],[1,6,29,"text","Heaters, kettles and fuses","A heater on the mains has a fixed voltage across it, so its power is V^2/R . To make a more powerful heater you need less resistance — a shorter or thicker element. The time needed to supply a given amount of heat H is",["V^2/R","H"],""],[1,6,30,"formula","Heaters, kettles and fuses","A heater on the mains has a fixed voltage across it, so its power is V^2/R . To make a more powerful heater you need less resistance — a shorter or thicker element. The time needed to supply a given amount of heat H is",["t = H/P = HR/(V^2) ∝ R."],"<i>t</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>H</i></span><span><i>P</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>H</i><i>R</i></span><span><i>V</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> ∝ <i>R</i>."],[1,6,31,"text","Heaters, kettles and fuses","So for a kettle with two heating coils that would boil the water in times t_1 and t_2 separately:",["t_1","t_2"],""],[1,6,32,"text","Heaters, kettles and fuses","coils in series (resistances add): t = t_1 + t_2 coils in parallel: 1/t = 1/t_1 + 1/t_2",["t = t_1 + t_2","1/t = 1/t_1 + 1/t_2"],""],[1,6,33,"text","Heaters, kettles and fuses","The fuse turns Joule heating into a safety device. It is a short piece of wire with a low melting point, placed in series with the appliance. If the current climbs too high, the fuse melts first and breaks the circuit.",[],""],[1,6,34,"text","Heaters, kettles and fuses","DerivationWhy the rating of a fuse does not depend on its length In the steady state, the heat produced in the fuse wire each second equals the heat it loses from its surface. For a wire of radius r and length L : I^2 ρL/(πr^2) = h (2πrL) where h is the heat lost per second from each unit of surface area at the melting temperature. The length cancels: I^2 = (2π^2h)/ρ r^3 ⟹ I_max ∝ r^(3/2). A longer fuse makes more heat but has proportionally more surface to lose it from. Only the thickness matters.",["r","L","I^2 ρL/(πr^2) = h (2πrL)","h","I^2 = (2π^2h)/ρ r^3 ⟹ I_max ∝ r^(3/2)."],""],[1,6,34,"formula","Heaters, kettles and fuses","e h is the heat lost per second from each unit of surface area at the melting temperature. The length cancels: I^2 = (2π^2h)/ρ r^3 ⟹ I_max ∝ r^(3/2). A longer fuse makes more heat but has proportionally more surface to lose it from. Only the thickness matters.",["I^2 ρL/(πr^2) = h (2πrL)"],"<i>I</i><sup>2</sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i></span><span><i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> = <i>h</i> (2<i>π</i><i>r</i><i>L</i>)"],[1,6,34,"formula","Heaters, kettles and fuses","e h is the heat lost per second from each unit of surface area at the melting temperature. The length cancels: I^2 = (2π^2h)/ρ r^3 ⟹ I_max ∝ r^(3/2). A longer fuse makes more heat but has proportionally more surface to lose it from. Only the thickness matters.",["I^2 = (2π^2h)/ρ r^3 ⟹ I_max ∝ r^(3/2)."],"<i>I</i><sup>2</sup> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>π</i><sup>2</sup><i>h</i></span><span><i>ρ</i></span></span> <i>r</i><sup>3</sup> ⟹ <i>I</i><sub>max</sub> ∝ <i>r</i><sup>3/2</sup>."],[1,6,37,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","The heater of an electric kettle is made of a wire of length L and diameter d . It takes 4 minutes to raise the temperature of 0.5 kg of water by 40 K . This heater is replaced by a new heater having two wires of the same material, each of length L and diameter 2d . The way these wires are connected is given in the options. How much time, in minutes, will it take to raise the temperature of the same amount of water by 40 K ? 4 if the wires are in parallel · 2 if the wires are in series · 1 if the wires are in series · 0.5 if the wires are in parallel",["L","d","4","0.5 kg","40 K","L","2d","40 K","4","2","1","0.5"],""],[1,6,37,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · One or more correct","The heat required is the same in every case, and the supply voltage is the same, so t = HR/(V^2) ∝ R. The new wires. Same length, twice the diameter, so four times the area: each new wire has resistance R/4 , where R is the resistance of the old heater. In series: R/4 + R/4 = R/2 . Half the resistance, half the time: 2 minutes. (B) is correct. In parallel: 1/2 ⋅ R/4 = R/8 . One-eighth of the time: 0.5 minute. (D) is correct. Answer (B), (D). The mass of water and the 40 K never needed to be used. Same water, same temperature rise, same mains voltage. So the heat needed is the same and the time is proportional to the resistance.",["t = HR/(V^2) ∝ R.","R/4","R","R/4 + R/4 = R/2","2","1/2 ⋅ R/4 = R/8","0.5","40 K"],""],[1,6,38,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2001 · Single correct","A wire of length L and 3 identical cells of negligible internal resistance are connected in series. Due to the current, the temperature of the wire is raised by ΔT in a time t . A number N of similar cells is now connected in series with a wire of the same material and cross-section but of length 2L . The temperature of the wire is raised by the same amount ΔT in the same time t . The value of N is 4 · 6 · 8 · 9",["L","3","ΔT","t","N","2L","ΔT","t","N","4","6","8"],""],[1,6,38,"sol","IIT-JEE 2001 · Single correct","Let each cell have emf ε , and let the first wire have resistance R and mass m . The heat produced goes into raising the wire’s temperature: ((3ε)^2)/R t = mc ΔT. The second wire is twice as long: resistance 2R and mass 2m — there is twice as much metal to heat. ((Nε)^2)/(2R) t = (2m)c ΔT. Divide the second equation by the first: (N^2)/(2 × 9) = 2 ⟹ N^2 = 36 ⟹ N = 6. Answer (B). Forget that the mass doubles and you get N^2 = 18 — not a whole number, which is your warning that something was missed. The longer wire has twice the resistance. What else about it has doubled?",["ε","R","m","((3ε)^2)/R t = mc ΔT.","2R","2m","((Nε)^2)/(2R) t = (2m)c ΔT.","(N^2)/(2 × 9) = 2 ⟹ N^2 = 36 ⟹ N = 6.","N^2 = 18"],""],[1,6,39,"sec","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","A power station must deliver a fixed power P to a distant town. It is free to choose the voltage V at which it sends the power; the current in the cables is then I = P/V . The cables have resistance R , and the power wasted in heating them is",[],""],[1,6,40,"text","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","A power station must deliver a fixed power P to a distant town. It is free to choose the voltage V at which it sends the power; the current in the cables is then I = P/V . The cables have resistance R , and the power wasted in heating them is",["P","V","I = P/V","R"],""],[1,6,41,"formula","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","A power station must deliver a fixed power P to a distant town. It is free to choose the voltage V at which it sends the power; the current in the cables is then I = P/V . The cables have resistance R , and the power wasted in heating them is",["P_(loss) = I^2R = (P/V)^2 R."],"<i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">loss</span></sub> = <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i> = (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span>)<sup>2</sup> <i>R</i>."],[1,6,42,"text","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","The loss goes as 1/V^2 . Send the same power at ten times the voltage and the cables waste a hundred times less.",["1/V^2"],""],[1,6,43,"text","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","Try some numbers. Sending 22 kW through cables of total resistance 2 Ω :",["22 kW","2 Ω"],""],[1,6,44,"table","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","Sent at Current Lost in the cables 220 V 100 A 20 kW — nearly everything 22 kV 1 A 2 W — nothing worth mentioning",["220 V","100 A","20 kW","22 kV","1 A","2 W"],""],[1,6,45,"text","Why power lines carry lakhs of volts","That is why long-distance lines in India run at 400 kV and even 765 kV , and why transformers step the voltage back down near your home.",["400 kV","765 kV"],""],[1,6,46,"trap","“But P = V^2/R says high voltage means more loss!”","The V in P = V^2/R must be the voltage across the resistor in question. The cables do not have the full transmission voltage across them — almost all of that appears across the town at the far end. The drop along the cables is only IR , and it is tiny precisely because the current is small. When in doubt on a transmission problem, use I^2R with I = P/V .",["V","P = V^2/R","IR","I^2R","I = P/V"],""],[1,6,47,"q","Counting the loss · Integer answer","A small power station sends 22 kW of electrical power to a factory through cables of total resistance 2 Ω . If the power is sent at 2200 V , how many watts are wasted as heat in the cables?",["22 kW","2 Ω","2200 V"],""],[1,6,47,"sol","Counting the loss · Integer answer","The current in the cables is fixed by the power and the voltage: I = P/V = (22000)/(2200) = 10 A . P_(loss) = I^2R = 10^2 × 2 = 200 W . That is under 1% of the power sent. The tempting wrong answer is V^2/R = 2200^2/2 ≈ 2.4 MW — more than a hundred times the power the station produces, which should ring every alarm bell.",["I = P/V = (22000)/(2200) = 10 A .","P_(loss) = I^2R = 10^2 × 2 = 200 W .","1%","V^2/R = 2200^2/2 ≈ 2.4 MW"],""],[1,6,48,"recap","Recap","Any device: P = VI . A resistor: P = I^2R = V^2/R , heat H = I^2Rt . 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10^6 J — a unit of energy. Series (same I): larger R takes more power. Parallel (same V): smaller R takes more power. A bulb’s label gives its resistance: R = V_(rated)^2/P_(rated) . Actual power = P_(rated)(V/V_(rated))^2 . Two bulbs in series across the rated voltage: total (P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2) , and the lower-wattage bulb is brighter. Fixed voltage: heating time ∝ R . Fuse: I_max ∝ r^(3/2) , independent of length. Transmission loss = (P/V)^2R : high voltage, low loss. @end",["P = VI","P = I^2R = V^2/R","H = I^2Rt","1 kWh = 3.6 × 10^6 J","I","R","V","R","R = V_(rated)^2/P_(rated)","= P_(rated)(V/V_(rated))^2","(P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2)","∝ R"],""],[1,7,0,"lesson","Ammeters, Voltmeters & the Galvanometer","The moment you connect a meter, it becomes part of the circuit it is trying to measure. Learn how one delicate galvanometer is turned into both an ammeter and a voltmeter — and how to tell which way a real meter lies to you.",[],""],[1,7,1,"sec","Every measurement disturbs","Until now our meters have been magic: ammeters that add no resistance, voltmeters that draw no current. Real instruments are made of wire, and wire has resistance. Connect one and you have built a slightly different circuit from the one you meant to measure.",[],""],[1,7,2,"text","Every measurement disturbs","Until now our meters have been magic: ammeters that add no resistance, voltmeters that draw no current. Real instruments are made of wire, and wire has resistance. Connect one and you have built a slightly different circuit from the one you meant to measure.",[],""],[1,7,3,"text","Every measurement disturbs","So two questions run through this lesson. How is each meter built? And how wrong is its reading?",[],""],[1,7,4,"table","Every measurement disturbs","Ammeter Voltmeter Measures current through a branch potential difference between two points Connected in series — break the branch and insert it in parallel — across the two points Ideal resistance zero (so it does not choke the current) infinite (so it draws no current) Reality small, but not zero large, but not infinite",[],""],[1,7,5,"intuition","A turnstile and a spirit level","An ammeter is a turnstile at a stadium gate: it must stand in the stream to count everyone passing, and a good one barely slows the crowd. A voltmeter is a surveyor comparing the heights of two points: it stands beside the path and should not let any of the traffic take a short cut through it.",[],""],[1,7,6,"trap","Connect them the wrong way round and…","An ammeter in parallel offers the current an almost resistance-free short cut. A huge current rushes through it — and may well burn it out. A voltmeter in series adds an enormous resistance to the branch. The current practically stops. The voltmeter survives, and reads almost the full emf of the battery, because nearly all the voltage now appears across it.",[],""],[1,7,7,"sec","The galvanometer: sensitive and delicate","At the heart of both instruments is a galvanometer: a coil that turns in a magnetic field when current passes through it, with a pointer attached. (How the coil feels a torque is a story for the Magnetism chapter.) For now, two numbers describe it completely:",[],""],[1,7,8,"text","The galvanometer: sensitive and delicate","At the heart of both instruments is a galvanometer: a coil that turns in a magnetic field when current passes through it, with a pointer attached. (How the coil feels a torque is a story for the Magnetism chapter.) For now, two numbers describe it completely:",[],""],[1,7,9,"text","The galvanometer: sensitive and delicate","its resistance G — typically tens of ohms; its full-scale current I_g — the current that swings the pointer right to the end of the scale. This is tiny: microamperes to a few milliamperes.",["G","I_g"],""],[1,7,10,"text","The galvanometer: sensitive and delicate","The deflection is proportional to the current through the coil. And because it has resistance G , full-scale deflection also corresponds to a small voltage I_gG across it. So a bare galvanometer is already an ammeter that reads up to I_g and a voltmeter that reads up to I_gG . Both ranges are uselessly small. The trick is to extend them.",["G","I_gG","I_g","I_gG"],""],[1,7,11,"sec","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","To measure a large current I , only the tiny I_g may pass through the coil. The rest must be led around it, through a low resistance connected in parallel — a shunt S .",[],""],[1,7,12,"text","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","To measure a large current I , only the tiny I_g may pass through the coil. The rest must be led around it, through a low resistance connected in parallel — a shunt S .",["I","I_g","S"],""],[1,7,13,"text","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","The coil and the shunt are in parallel, so they have the same voltage across them:",[],""],[1,7,14,"formula","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","The coil and the shunt are in parallel, so they have the same voltage across them:",["I_gG = (I − I_g) S ⟹ S = (I_g G)/(I − I_g)"],"<i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub><i>G</i> = (<i>I</i> − <i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub>) <i>S</i> ⟹ <i>S</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub> <i>G</i></span><span><i>I</i> − <i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub></span></span>"],[1,7,15,"text","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","The resistance of the finished ammeter is G in parallel with S :",["G","S"],""],[1,7,16,"formula","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","The resistance of the finished ammeter is G in parallel with S :",["R_A = GS/(G + S) = (I_gG)/I"],"<i>R</i><sub><i>A</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>GS</i></span><span><i>G</i> + <i>S</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub><i>G</i></span><span><i>I</i></span></span>"],[1,7,17,"text","Galvanometer → ammeter: give the extra current a bypass","— smaller than the already-small shunt. That is exactly what an ammeter needs. The second form is worth noticing: full-scale voltage ÷ full-scale current.",[],""],[1,7,18,"sec","Galvanometer → voltmeter: make the current work harder","To measure a large voltage V , the coil must still carry only I_g at full scale. So put a large resistance R in series with it — often called a multiplier — to take up the excess voltage.",[],""],[1,7,19,"text","Galvanometer → voltmeter: make the current work harder","To measure a large voltage V , the coil must still carry only I_g at full scale. So put a large resistance R in series with it — often called a multiplier — to take up the excess voltage.",["V","I_g","R"],""],[1,7,20,"formula","Galvanometer → voltmeter: make the current work harder","To measure a large voltage V , the coil must still carry only I_g at full scale. So put a large resistance R in series with it — often called a multiplier — to take up the excess voltage.",["V = I_g (G + R) ⟹ R = V/I_g − G"],"<i>V</i> = <i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub> (<i>G</i> + <i>R</i>) ⟹ <i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>I</i><sub><i>g</i></sub></span></span> − <i>G</i>"],[1,7,21,"text","Galvanometer → voltmeter: make the current work harder","The resistance of the finished voltmeter is R_V = G + R = V/I_g : large, as a voltmeter’s should be.",["R_V = G + R = V/I_g"],""],[1,7,22,"key","Low resistance in parallel, high resistance in series","Ammeter = galvanometer + small shunt in parallel. To multiply the range by n , the shunt must be G/(n − 1) . Voltmeter = galvanometer + large resistance in series. To multiply the range by n , add (n − 1)G in series. In both cases the galvanometer itself still carries exactly I_g at full scale. Nothing about the coil has changed; you have only changed how much of the outside world it gets to see.",["=","+","n","G/(n − 1)","=","+","n","(n − 1)G","I_g"],""],[1,7,23,"q","Building a voltmeter · Integer answer","A galvanometer has a resistance of 50 Ω and gives full-scale deflection for a current of 1 mA . What resistance, in ohms, must be connected in series with it to turn it into a voltmeter reading up to 10 V ?",["50 Ω","1 mA","10 V"],""],[1,7,23,"sol","Building a voltmeter · Integer answer","At full scale the coil carries 1 mA while 10 V is applied across the whole instrument: R_V = V/I_g = 10/(1 × 10^(−3)) = 10 000 Ω. The coil already provides 50 Ω of that, so the series resistance needed is 10 000 − 50 = 9950 Ω . Without it, the galvanometer alone would hit full scale at just I_gG = 0.05 V .",["1 mA","10 V","R_V = V/I_g = 10/(1 × 10^(−3)) = 10 000 Ω.","50 Ω","10 000 − 50 = 9950 Ω","I_gG = 0.05 V"],""],[1,7,24,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2005 · Single correct","A moving-coil galvanometer of resistance 100 Ω is used as an ammeter using a resistance of 0.1 Ω . The maximum deflection current in the galvanometer is 100 μ A . Find the minimum current in the circuit so that the ammeter shows maximum deflection. 100.1 mA · 1000.1 mA · 10.01 mA · 1.01 mA",["100 Ω","0.1 Ω","100 μ A","100.1 mA","1000.1 mA","10.01 mA","1.01 mA"],""],[1,7,24,"sol","IIT-JEE 2005 · Single correct","The 0.1 Ω resistance is the shunt, in parallel with the coil. At full deflection the coil carries I_g = 100 μ A = 0.1 mA , and the voltage across coil and shunt is the same: I_gG = I_sS ⟹ I_s = ((0.1 mA)(100))/0.1 = 100 mA . The circuit current is the sum of the two: I = I_s + I_g = 100 + 0.1 = 100.1 mA . Answer (A). The shunt has a thousandth of the coil’s resistance, so it carries a thousand times the coil’s current.",["0.1 Ω","I_g = 100 μ A = 0.1 mA","I_gG = I_sS ⟹ I_s = ((0.1 mA)(100))/0.1 = 100 mA .","I = I_s + I_g = 100 + 0.1 = 100.1 mA ."],""],[1,7,25,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · Integer answer","A galvanometer gives full-scale deflection with a current of 0.006 A . By connecting it to a 4990 Ω resistance, it can be converted into a voltmeter of range 0 – 30 V . If connected to a (2n)/249 Ω resistance, it becomes an ammeter of range 0 – 1.5 A . Find the value of n .",["0.006 A","4990 Ω","0","30 V","(2n)/249 Ω","0","1.5 A","n"],""],[1,7,25,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · Integer answer","Voltmeter → find G . At full scale, 0.006 A flows through G and 4990 Ω in series, with 30 V across them: G + 4990 = 30/(0.006) = 5000 ⟹ G = 10 Ω. Ammeter → find the shunt. S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) = ((0.006)(10))/(1.5 − 0.006) = (0.06)/(1.494) = 10/249 Ω. Comparing with (2n)/249 : 2n = 10 , so n = 5 . Answer: 5. The voltmeter information is there to give you the one thing you are not told: the galvanometer’s own resistance.",["G","0.006 A","G","4990 Ω","30 V","G + 4990 = 30/(0.006) = 5000 ⟹ G = 10 Ω.","S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) = ((0.006)(10))/(1.5 − 0.006) = (0.06)/(1.494) = 10/249 Ω.","(2n)/249","2n = 10","n = 5"],""],[1,7,26,"sec","Loading: how a real meter lies","A voltmeter lowers the voltage it is measuring. Connecting it across a resistor puts its own resistance R_V in parallel with that resistor. The combination is smaller than the resistor alone, so it takes a smaller share of the circuit’s voltage. A real voltmeter always reads low — badly so if R_V is",[],""],[1,7,27,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","A voltmeter lowers the voltage it is measuring. Connecting it across a resistor puts its own resistance R_V in parallel with that resistor. The combination is smaller than the resistor alone, so it takes a smaller share of the circuit’s voltage. A real voltmeter always reads low — badly so if R_V is not much larger than the resistance it is across.",["R_V","R_V"],""],[1,7,28,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","An ammeter lowers the current it is measuring. It adds R_A in series, so the current falls. A real ammeter also reads low.",["R_A"],""],[1,7,29,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","In the circuit above, two 10 k Ω resistors share 12 V equally: 6 V each. Now connect a voltmeter whose own resistance is 10 k Ω across one of them. That half becomes 10 ∥ 10 = 5 k Ω , and the meter reads",["10 k Ω","12 V","6 V","10 k Ω","10 ∥ 10 = 5 k Ω"],""],[1,7,30,"formula","Loading: how a real meter lies","In the circuit above, two 10 k Ω resistors share 12 V equally: 6 V each. Now connect a voltmeter whose own resistance is 10 k Ω across one of them. That half becomes 10 ∥ 10 = 5 k Ω , and the meter reads",["12 × 5/(5 + 10) = 4 V ."],"12 × <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>5</span><span>5 + 10</span></span> = 4 <span class=\"fx-t\">V</span>."],[1,7,31,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","It is reporting, quite truthfully, the voltage that exists while it is connected. It is just not the voltage you wanted to know.",[],""],[1,7,32,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Measuring a resistance with both meters. To find R you need the voltage across it and the current through it. With real meters you cannot get both exactly right at the same time:",["R"],""],[1,7,33,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Voltmeter directly across R (left). The voltmeter reads the true voltage across R , but the ammeter reads the current through R plus the current through the voltmeter. So",["R","R","R"],""],[1,7,34,"formula","Loading: how a real meter lies","Voltmeter directly across R (left). The voltmeter reads the true voltage across R , but the ammeter reads the current through R plus the current through the voltmeter. So",["R_(measured) = V/I = (R R_V)/(R + R_V) < R."],"<i>R</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">measured</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i></span><span><i>I</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i> <i>R</i><sub><i>V</i></sub></span><span><i>R</i> + <i>R</i><sub><i>V</i></sub></span></span> &lt; <i>R</i>."],[1,7,35,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Good when R ≪ R_V — that is, for small resistances.",["R ≪ R_V"],""],[1,7,36,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Voltmeter across R and the ammeter together (right). The ammeter reads the true current, but the voltmeter includes the drop across the ammeter:",["R"],""],[1,7,37,"formula","Loading: how a real meter lies","Voltmeter across R and the ammeter together (right). The ammeter reads the true current, but the voltmeter includes the drop across the ammeter:",["R_(measured) = R + R_A > R."],"<i>R</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">measured</span></sub> = <i>R</i> + <i>R</i><sub><i>A</i></sub> &gt; <i>R</i>."],[1,7,38,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Good when R ≫ R_A — for large resistances.",["R ≫ R_A"],""],[1,7,39,"text","Loading: how a real meter lies","Notice that neither result involves the battery. Its emf and internal resistance change V and I together; the ratio V/I depends only on what lies between the voltmeter’s leads.",["V","I","V/I"],""],[1,7,40,"q","The meter that changes the answer · Single correct","Two 10 k Ω resistors are connected in series across an ideal 12 V battery. A voltmeter of resistance 20 k Ω is connected across one of them. What does it read? 6.0 V · 4.8 V · 4.0 V · 7.2 V",["10 k Ω","12 V","20 k Ω","6.0 V","4.8 V","4.0 V","7.2 V"],""],[1,7,40,"sol","The meter that changes the answer · Single correct","The voltmeter is in parallel with one resistor: (10 × 20)/(10 + 20) = 20/3 k Ω. This is in series with the other 10 k Ω across 12 V : V = 12 × (20/3)/(20/3 + 10) = 12 × 20/50 = 4.8 V . Answer (B). The “true” value, with no meter, is 6 V . Even a voltmeter with twice the resistance of the resistor reads 20% low. The other resistor, meanwhile, now has 7.2 V across it — option (D).",["(10 × 20)/(10 + 20) = 20/3 k Ω.","10 k Ω","12 V","V = 12 × (20/3)/(20/3 + 10) = 12 × 20/50 = 4.8 V .","6 V","20%","7.2 V"],""],[1,7,41,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","Two identical moving-coil galvanometers have 10 Ω resistance and full-scale deflection at 2 μ A current. One of them is converted into a voltmeter of 100 mV full-scale reading and the other into an ammeter of 1 mA full-scale current, using appropriate resistors. These are then used to measure the voltage and current in an Ohm’s-law experiment with an R = 1000 Ω resistor, using an ideal cell. Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct? The resistance of the voltmeter will be 100 k Ω . · The resistance of the ammeter will be 0.02 Ω (rounded to the second decimal place). · The measured value of R will be 978 Ω < R < 982 Ω . · If the ideal cell is replaced by a cell having internal resistance of 5 Ω , then the measured value of R will be more than 1000 Ω .",["10 Ω","2 μ A","100 mV","1 mA","R = 1000 Ω","100 k Ω","0.02 Ω","R","978 Ω < R < 982 Ω","5 Ω","R","1000 Ω"],""],[1,7,41,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","(A) Voltmeter. Full scale means 2 μ A through the instrument with 100 mV across it: R_V = 0.1/(2 × 10^(−6)) = 50 k Ω. Not 100 k Ω . Wrong. (B) Ammeter. At full scale the coil carries 2 μ A , so the voltage across it (and across the whole ammeter) is 2 μ A × 10 Ω = 20 μ V , while 1 mA flows through the instrument: R_A = (20 × 10^(−6))/(1 × 10^(−3)) = 0.02 Ω. Correct. (C) The measurement. In the standard Ohm’s-law arrangement the voltmeter is connected directly across R and the ammeter is in series with the pair. The ammeter then reads the current through R and the voltmeter, so R_(measured) = (R R_V)/(R + R_V) = (1000 × 50 000)/(51 000) ≈ 980.4 Ω. Correct. (D) An internal resistance in the cell lowers both the voltage and the current, but their ratio is still R ∥ R_V ≈ 980 Ω . It can never exceed 1000 Ω . Wrong. Answer (B), (C). For each meter: resistance = full-scale voltage ÷ full-scale current. Then ask what the voltmeter is in parallel with.",["2 μ A","100 mV","R_V = 0.1/(2 × 10^(−6)) = 50 k Ω.","100 k Ω","2 μ A","2 μ A × 10 Ω = 20 μ V","1 mA","R_A = (20 × 10^(−6))/(1 × 10^(−3)) = 0.02 Ω.","R","R","R_(measured) = (R R_V)/(R + R_V) = (1000 × 50 000)/(51 000) ≈ 980.4 Ω.","R ∥ R_V ≈ 980 Ω"],""],[1,7,42,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2016 · One or more correct","Consider two identical galvanometers and two identical resistors with resistance R . If the internal resistance of the galvanometers R_C < R/2 , which of the following statement(s) about any one of the galvanometers is/are true? The maximum voltage range is obtained when all the components are connected in series. · The maximum voltage range is obtained when the two resistors and one galvanometer are connected in series, and the second galvanometer is connected in parallel to the first galvanometer. · The maximum current range is obtained when all the components are connected in parallel. · The maximum current range is obtained when the two galvanometers are connected in series and the combination is connected in parallel with both the resistors.",["R","R_C < R/2"],""],[1,7,42,"sol","JEE Advanced 2016 · One or more correct","Let the full-scale current of each galvanometer be I_g . In every arrangement, find the voltage across (or the current into) the whole combination when a galvanometer carries exactly I_g . Voltage range (A) All four in series. The same I_g flows through everything: V_A = I_g (2R_C + 2R). (B) Two galvanometers in parallel, then both resistors in series. Each galvanometer carries I_g , so 2I_g flows through the resistors: V_B = I_gR_C + 2I_g(2R) = I_g (R_C + 4R). V_B − V_A = I_g (2R − R_C) , which is positive since R_C < R/2 . (B) gives the larger voltage range. Current range (C) All four in parallel. The voltage across everything is I_gR_C , so each resistor carries I_gR_C/R : I_C = 2I_g + (2I_gR_C)/R. (D) Galvanometers in series, in parallel with both resistors. The voltage is now 2I_gR_C , so each resistor carries 2I_gR_C/R : I_D = I_g + (4I_gR_C)/R. I_C − I_D = I_g (1 − (2R_C)/R) , which is positive exactly when R_C < R/2 — the condition given. (C) gives the larger current range. Answer (B), (C). The oddly specific condition R_C < R/2 is the giveaway that you were meant to compare (C) with (D) this way. “Range” means the total voltage across (or total current into) the whole arrangement at the moment the galvanometer reaches its full-scale current I_g . Work that out for each of the four arrangements.",["I_g","I_g","I_g","V_A = I_g (2R_C + 2R).","I_g","2I_g","V_B = I_gR_C + 2I_g(2R) = I_g (R_C + 4R).","V_B − V_A = I_g (2R − R_C)","R_C < R/2","I_gR_C","I_gR_C/R","I_C = 2I_g + (2I_gR_C)/R."],""],[1,7,43,"recap","Recap","Ammeter: in series, ideally zero resistance. Voltmeter: in parallel, ideally infinite resistance. A galvanometer is described by G and I_g ; its own ranges are I_g and I_gG . To make an ammeter: shunt S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) in parallel; R_A = I_gG/I . To make a voltmeter: resistance R = V/I_g − G in series; R_V = V/I_g . Real meters load the circuit: a voltmeter and an ammeter both read low. Measuring R as V/I : voltmeter straight across R gives R ∥ R_V (too small); voltmeter across R and ammeter gives R + R_A (too large). The battery does not matter. @end",["G","I_g","I_g","I_gG","S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g)","R_A = I_gG/I","R = V/I_g − G","R_V = V/I_g","R","V/I","R","R ∥ R_V"],""],[1,8,0,"lesson","Wheatstone Bridge, Metre Bridge & Potentiometer","The most accurate electrical measurements are made by reading nothing at all. Balance a bridge until the galvanometer falls silent, and the unknown drops out as a simple ratio — no calibration, no loading, no trust in the battery required.",[],""],[1,8,1,"sec","Measuring by silence","Lesson 7 ended on a sour note: every real meter disturbs the circuit, and every pointer has to be trusted to be accurately calibrated.",[],""],[1,8,2,"text","Measuring by silence","Lesson 7 ended on a sour note: every real meter disturbs the circuit, and every pointer has to be trusted to be accurately calibrated.",[],""],[1,8,3,"text","Measuring by silence","There is a cleverer way. Arrange the circuit so that the quantity you want is balanced against a known one, and adjust until a galvanometer between them reads zero. This is a null method, and it has three great strengths:",[],""],[1,8,4,"text","Measuring by silence","At balance the galvanometer carries no current, so it cannot load anything. You only need to detect whether there is a current, not measure how much. The galvanometer needs to be sensitive, not accurate. The answer turns out to be a ratio of resistances or lengths. The battery’s emf — and its slow fading — does not matter.",[],""],[1,8,5,"text","Measuring by silence","It is the difference between weighing sugar on a spring balance that might be worn, and on a pair of scales against standard weights.",[],""],[1,8,6,"sec","The Wheatstone bridge","Four resistors form a diamond. A battery is connected across one diagonal and a galvanometer across the other:",[],""],[1,8,7,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","Four resistors form a diamond. A battery is connected across one diagonal and a galvanometer across the other:",[],""],[1,8,8,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","Adjust one resistor until the galvanometer shows no deflection. Then B and D are at the same potential, and no current crosses between them. So the current I_1 in P continues undivided into Q , and the current I_2 in R continues into S .",["I_1","P","Q","I_2","R","S"],""],[1,8,9,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","Since V_B = V_D , the drops from A must match, and so must the drops to C:",["V_B = V_D"],""],[1,8,10,"formula","The Wheatstone bridge","Since V_B = V_D , the drops from A must match, and so must the drops to C:",["I_1P = I_2R and I_1Q = I_2S."],"<i>I</i><sub>1</sub><i>P</i> = <i>I</i><sub>2</sub><i>R</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">and</span> <i>I</i><sub>1</sub><i>Q</i> = <i>I</i><sub>2</sub><i>S</i>."],[1,8,11,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","Divide one by the other:",[],""],[1,8,12,"formula","The Wheatstone bridge","Divide one by the other:",["P/Q = R/S"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>Q</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i></span><span><i>S</i></span></span>"],[1,8,13,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","Know three of the resistances and you have the fourth. Things to notice:",[],""],[1,8,14,"text","The Wheatstone bridge","The emf of the battery does not appear, nor does its internal resistance, nor the resistance of the galvanometer. Swap the battery and the galvanometer and the bridge is still balanced: the condition PS = QR is symmetric. The bridge is most sensitive when all four resistances are of the same order.",["PS = QR"],""],[1,8,15,"story","The bridge Wheatstone did not invent","The circuit was devised in 1833 by Samuel Hunter Christie. Charles Wheatstone came across it a decade later, saw how useful it was for measuring resistance, and made it famous in 1843 — carefully giving Christie the credit. The name stuck to the wrong man anyway.",[],""],[1,8,16,"q","Swap the battery and the galvanometer · Single correct","A Wheatstone bridge is balanced. The battery and the galvanometer are then interchanged, with nothing else touched. The galvanometer will now show no deflection · a deflection in the same direction as it would have before balance · a deflection that depends on the emf of the battery · a deflection that depends on the resistance of the galvanometer",[],""],[1,8,16,"sol","Swap the battery and the galvanometer · Single correct","Before the swap the bridge is balanced, so P/Q = R/S , that is, PS = QR . After the swap, the battery is across B and D, and the galvanometer across A and C. Seen from the battery, the two branches are now P with R (through A) and Q with S (through C). The new balance condition is P/R = Q/S — which is PS = QR once more. The same equation, so the bridge is still balanced. Answer (A). Balance is a property of the four resistors alone.",["P/Q = R/S","PS = QR","P","R","Q","S","P/R = Q/S","PS = QR"],""],[1,8,17,"sec","The metre bridge","A Wheatstone bridge needs a ratio P/Q that can be varied smoothly. The metre bridge gets it from a one-metre wire of uniform cross-section, with a sliding contact — the jockey — that can touch it anywhere:",[],""],[1,8,18,"text","The metre bridge","A Wheatstone bridge needs a ratio P/Q that can be varied smoothly. The metre bridge gets it from a one-metre wire of uniform cross-section, with a sliding contact — the jockey — that can touch it anywhere:",["P/Q"],""],[1,8,19,"text","The metre bridge","The unknown X and a known resistance R sit in the two gaps. The wire on either side of the jockey supplies the other two arms. Because the wire is uniform, its resistance is proportional to its length, so if balance is found with the jockey at a distance l (in cm) from the end A:",["X","R","l"],""],[1,8,20,"formula","The metre bridge","known resistance R sit in the two gaps. The wire on either side of the jockey supplies the other two arms. Because the wire is uniform, its resistance is proportional to its length, so if balance is found with the jockey at a distance l (in cm) from the end A:",["X/R = l/(100 − l)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>X</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>l</i></span><span>100 − <i>l</i></span></span>"],[1,8,21,"text","The metre bridge","(with X in the gap nearer A). The resistance per centimetre of the wire cancels out. A thicker wire, a different alloy — none of it changes the balance point, as long as the wire is uniform.",["X"],""],[1,8,22,"text","The metre bridge","Where should the balance point be? A small error Δl in reading the jockey position gives",["Δl"],""],[1,8,23,"formula","The metre bridge","Where should the balance point be? A small error Δl in reading the jockey position gives",["ΔX/X = Δl/l + Δl/(100 − l) = (100 Δl)/(l (100 − l))."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>X</i></span><span><i>X</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>l</i></span><span><i>l</i></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>l</i></span><span>100 − <i>l</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>100 Δ<i>l</i></span><span><i>l</i> (100 − <i>l</i>)</span></span>."],[1,8,24,"text","The metre bridge","The denominator is largest at l = 50 . So the experiment is most accurate when the balance point is near the middle of the wire — which means choosing R close to X .",["l = 50","R","X"],""],[1,8,25,"text","The metre bridge","End corrections. The thick copper strips and the soldered joints at the ends add a little resistance that the scale does not show. It is allowed for by adding small lengths α and β to the two sides:",["α","β"],""],[1,8,26,"formula","The metre bridge","End corrections. The thick copper strips and the soldered joints at the ends add a little resistance that the scale does not show. It is allowed for by adding small lengths α and β to the two sides:",["X/R = (l + α)/((100 − l) + β)."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>X</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>l</i> + <i>α</i></span><span>(100 − <i>l</i>) + <i>β</i></span></span>."],[1,8,27,"text","The metre bridge","Repeating the experiment with X and R interchanged and averaging removes most of this error — another reason to work near the middle.",["X","R"],""],[1,8,28,"lab","Find the mystery resistor","A metre bridge with an unknown resistor X in the left-hand gap and a known resistance R in the right. Your job: find X . Try these, in order: Drag the jockey along the wire and watch the galvanometer needle. On one side of the balance point it swings left, on the other side right. Home in on the spot where it sits at zero. (With the keyboard: click the wire, then use the arrow keys for fine steps.) Read the balance length l and work out X = R l/(100 − l) yourself. Then tap Reveal X. Change the known resistance R . The balance point moves — but your value of X should not. Choose R so that the balance point is close to 50 cm , then try a value that pushes it out near one end. Near the end, a one-millimetre slip of the jockey changes your answer far more. Change the battery’s emf. The needle becomes livelier or lazier, but the balance point does not move at all. Tap New mystery X to play again.",["X","R","X","l","X = R l/(100 − l)","R","X","R","50 cm"],""],[1,8,29,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","A metre bridge is set up to determine an unknown resistance X using a standard 10 Ω resistor, with X in the gap nearer end A of the wire. The galvanometer shows a null point when the tapping key is at the 52 cm mark. The end corrections are 1 cm and 2 cm respectively for the ends A and B. The determined value of X is 10.2 Ω · 10.6 Ω · 10.8 Ω · 11.1 Ω",["X","10 Ω","X","52 cm","1 cm","2 cm","X","10.2 Ω","10.6 Ω","10.8 Ω","11.1 Ω"],""],[1,8,29,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","Add each end correction to the length on its own side: X/10 = (52 + 1)/((100 − 52) + 2) = 53/50 ⟹ X = 10.6 Ω. Answer (B). Ignore the corrections and you get 10 × 52/48 = 10.8 Ω — option (C), waiting for anyone in a hurry.",["X/10 = (52 + 1)/((100 − 52) + 2) = 53/50 ⟹ X = 10.6 Ω.","10 × 52/48 = 10.8 Ω"],""],[1,8,30,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","During an experiment with a metre bridge, the galvanometer shows a null point when the jockey is pressed at 40.0 cm , using a standard resistance of 90 Ω in the gap farther from the zero end; the unknown resistance R is in the other gap. The least count of the scale used in the metre bridge is 1 mm . The unknown resistance is 60 ± 0.15 Ω · 135 ± 0.56 Ω · 60 ± 0.25 Ω · 135 ± 0.23 Ω",["40.0 cm","90 Ω","R","1 mm","60 ± 0.15 Ω","135 ± 0.56 Ω","60 ± 0.25 Ω","135 ± 0.23 Ω"],""],[1,8,30,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","The value. R/90 = (40.0)/(60.0) ⟹ R = 60 Ω. The uncertainty. R = 90 l/(100 − l) . An error Δl = 0.1 cm in l is equally an error in (100 − l) , and in the worst case the two fractional errors add: ΔR/R = Δl/l + Δl/(100 − l) = 0.1/40 + 0.1/60 = 1/240. ΔR = 60/240 = 0.25 Ω. Answer (C): R = 60 ± 0.25 Ω . The uncertainty in the jockey position, 0.1 cm , affects both lengths. Add the fractional errors.",["R/90 = (40.0)/(60.0) ⟹ R = 60 Ω.","R = 90 l/(100 − l)","Δl = 0.1 cm","l","(100 − l)","ΔR/R = Δl/l + Δl/(100 − l) = 0.1/40 + 0.1/60 = 1/240.","ΔR = 60/240 = 0.25 Ω.","R = 60 ± 0.25 Ω","0.1 cm"],""],[1,8,31,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2007 · Single correct","A resistance of 2 Ω is connected across one gap of a metre bridge (the length of the wire is 100 cm) and an unknown resistance, greater than 2 Ω , is connected across the other gap. When these resistances are interchanged, the balance point shifts by 20 cm . Neglecting any corrections, the unknown resistance is 3 Ω · 4 Ω · 5 Ω · 6 Ω",["2 Ω","100 cm","2 Ω","20 cm","3 Ω","4 Ω","5 Ω","6 Ω"],""],[1,8,31,"sol","IIT-JEE 2007 · Single correct","Let the balance length on the side of the 2 Ω resistor be l . Since the unknown X is larger, l < 50 . Interchanging the resistors simply mirrors the picture: the balance point moves from l to 100 − l . The shift is (100 − l) − l = 20 ⟹ l = 40 cm . 2/X = 40/60 ⟹ X = 3 Ω. Answer (A). Interchanging the two resistors reflects the balance point about the middle of the wire.",["2 Ω","l","X","l < 50","l","100 − l","(100 − l) − l = 20 ⟹ l = 40 cm .","2/X = 40/60 ⟹ X = 3 Ω."],""],[1,8,32,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2003 · Single correct","In a metre bridge experiment with a wire AB, the null deflection of the galvanometer is obtained with the jockey at a point C, where AC = x . What would the value of AC be if the radius of the wire AB were doubled? x · x/4 · 4x · 2x",["AC = x","AC","x","x/4","4x","2x"],""],[1,8,32,"sol","IIT-JEE 2003 · Single correct","The balance condition compares the resistances of the two parts of the same wire: (R_AC)/(R_CB) = x/(100 − x). Doubling the radius cuts the resistance of every centimetre of the wire to a quarter — but it does so for both parts alike. Their ratio is untouched, so the balance point stays exactly where it was. Answer (A). Only a non-uniform wire would move the null point.",["(R_AC)/(R_CB) = x/(100 − x)."],""],[1,8,33,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","Statement 1: In a metre bridge experiment, the null point for an unknown resistance is measured. Now the unknown resistance is put inside an enclosure maintained at a higher temperature. The null point can be obtained at the same point as before by decreasing the value of the standard resistance. Statement 2: The resistance of a metal increases with increase in temperature. Both statements are true, and Statement 2 is a correct explanation of Statement 1. · Both statements are true, but Statement 2 is not a correct explanation of Statement 1. · Statement 1 is true; Statement 2 is false. · Statement 1 is false; Statement 2 is true.",[],""],[1,8,33,"sol","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","Statement 2 is true: in a metal, heating shortens the time between collisions, so the resistance rises. Statement 1. At balance, X/(R_std) = l/(100 − l) . To keep the null point where it was, this ratio must stay the same. Heating makes X larger, so the standard resistance must be made larger too — not smaller. Statement 1 is false. Answer (D).",["X/(R_std) = l/(100 − l)","X"],""],[1,8,34,"sec","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","A voltmeter across a cell always reads a little less than the emf, because it draws a current and some voltage is lost inside the cell. To read the true emf you need a voltmeter of infinite resistance. The potentiometer is exactly that — built from a long wire and a null method.",[],""],[1,8,35,"text","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","A voltmeter across a cell always reads a little less than the emf, because it draws a current and some voltage is lost inside the cell. To read the true emf you need a voltmeter of infinite resistance. The potentiometer is exactly that — built from a long wire and a null method.",[],""],[1,8,36,"text","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","A steady driver cell sends a constant current through a long uniform wire AB. The potential falls steadily along the wire, by the same amount for every centimetre. That fall per unit length is the potential gradient:",[],""],[1,8,37,"formula","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","A steady driver cell sends a constant current through a long uniform wire AB. The potential falls steadily along the wire, by the same amount for every centimetre. That fall per unit length is the potential gradient:",["k = (V_AB)/L ⟹ V_AJ = k l."],"<i>k</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sub><i>A</i><i>B</i></sub></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> ⟹ <i>V</i><sub><i>A</i><i>J</i></sub> = <i>k</i> <i>l</i>."],[1,8,38,"text","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","The wire is a ruler marked in volts. Now connect the cell under test with its positive terminal to A (the same end as the driver’s positive) and its other terminal, through a galvanometer, to the jockey. Slide the jockey until the galvanometer reads zero. At that point the potential drop along the length l of wire exactly equals the cell’s emf — and since no current flows through the cell, there is no internal drop to spoil it:",["l"],""],[1,8,39,"formula","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","h a galvanometer, to the jockey. Slide the jockey until the galvanometer reads zero. At that point the potential drop along the length l of wire exactly equals the cell’s emf — and since no current flows through the cell, there is no internal drop to spoil it:",["ε = k l."],"<i>ε</i> = <i>k</i> <i>l</i>."],[1,8,40,"text","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","Comparing two emfs. Balance each cell in turn on the same wire:",[],""],[1,8,41,"formula","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","Comparing two emfs. Balance each cell in turn on the same wire:",["ε_1/ε_2 = l_1/l_2."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ε</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>ε</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>l</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>l</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>."],[1,8,42,"text","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","Internal resistance of a cell. First balance the cell on open circuit: ε = k l_1 . Then connect a known resistance R across the cell so that it delivers current, and balance again: now the wire is matching the terminal voltage, V = k l_2 . Since V = εR/(R + r) ,",["ε = k l_1","R","V = k l_2","V = εR/(R + r)"],""],[1,8,43,"formula","The potentiometer: a voltmeter that draws no current","ternal resistance of a cell. First balance the cell on open circuit: ε = k l_1 . Then connect a known resistance R across the cell so that it delivers current, and balance again: now the wire is matching the terminal voltage, V = k l_2 . Since V = εR/(R + r) ,",["l_1/l_2 = (R + r)/R ⟹ r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>l</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>l</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i> + <i>r</i></span><span><i>R</i></span></span> ⟹ <i>r</i> = <i>R</i> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>l</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>l</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> − 1)"],[1,8,44,"key","Making a potentiometer work — and work well","Positive terminals to the same end, and the driver’s voltage across the wire must exceed the emf being measured. Otherwise there is no balance point anywhere on the wire. Sensitivity means a small potential gradient: then a tiny voltage corresponds to a long, easily measured length. Get it with a longer wire, or by reducing the current in the wire with a series resistance. The driver’s current must stay constant throughout, and the wire must be uniform.",[],""],[1,8,45,"intuition","Why it beats a voltmeter","A voltmeter measures by taking a sip of current, and the sip itself changes what it is tasting. At its balance point the potentiometer takes nothing at all from the cell under test. Measuring an emf with it is like checking that two water tanks are at the same level by opening a pipe between them and seeing that nothing flows.",[],""],[1,8,46,"pyq","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","In a potentiometer experiment the balancing length with a cell is 240 cm . On shunting the cell with a resistance of 2 Ω , the balancing length becomes 120 cm . The internal resistance of the cell is 4 Ω · 2 Ω · 1 Ω · 0.5 Ω",["240 cm","2 Ω","120 cm","4 Ω","2 Ω","1 Ω","0.5 Ω"],""],[1,8,46,"sol","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","On open circuit the wire balances the emf: ε ∝ 240 . With the 2 Ω shunt it balances the terminal voltage: V ∝ 120 . r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1) = 2 (240/120 − 1) = 2 Ω. Answer (B). It makes sense: the terminal voltage is exactly half the emf, so the internal resistance must be dropping as much voltage as the external 2 Ω .",["ε ∝ 240","2 Ω","V ∝ 120","r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1) = 2 (240/120 − 1) = 2 Ω.","2 Ω"],""],[1,8,47,"q","A ruler marked in millivolts · Integer answer","A potentiometer wire is 100 cm long and has a resistance of 5 Ω . It is connected in series with a resistance R and a driver cell of emf 3 V and negligible internal resistance. A source of emf 6 mV is balanced by 60 cm of the wire. Find R , in ohms.",["100 cm","5 Ω","R","3 V","6 mV","60 cm","R"],""],[1,8,47,"sol","A ruler marked in millivolts · Integer answer","Potential gradient. 6 mV is balanced by 60 cm , so k = 0.1 mV per cm. Voltage across the whole wire. 100 cm × 0.1 mV cm ^(−1) = 10 mV . Current in the wire. I = (10 mV)/5Ω = 2 mA . The series resistance. The 3 V driver sends this current through R and the wire: R + 5 = 3/(2 × 10^(−3)) = 1500 ⟹ R = 1495 Ω. Almost all of the driver’s 3 V is dropped across R on purpose — leaving a very gentle gradient along the wire, so that a few millivolts can be measured to within a fraction of a percent. Find the potential gradient first, then the voltage across the whole wire, then the current in it.",["6 mV","60 cm","k = 0.1 mV","100 cm × 0.1 mV cm ^(−1) = 10 mV","I = (10 mV)/5Ω = 2 mA","3 V","R","R + 5 = 3/(2 × 10^(−3)) = 1500 ⟹ R = 1495 Ω.","3 V","R"],""],[1,8,48,"recap","Recap","Null methods read zero, so the meter cannot load the circuit and the battery’s emf drops out. Wheatstone bridge: balanced when P/Q = R/S . Unchanged if battery and galvanometer are swapped. Metre bridge: X/R = l/(100 − l) ; best near the middle; end corrections add to each length; the wire’s thickness does not matter. Potentiometer: potential gradient k = V_AB/L ; at balance ε = kl with no current drawn from the cell. Compare emfs: ε_1/ε_2 = l_1/l_2 . Internal resistance: r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1) . Smaller potential gradient → more sensitive potentiometer. @end",["P/Q = R/S","X/R = l/(100 − l)","k = V_AB/L","ε = kl","ε_1/ε_2 = l_1/l_2","r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1)"],""],[1,9,0,"lesson","Revision & Mixed PYQs","The whole chapter on one screen — which method to reach for, the formulas worth memorising, the traps examiners reuse — and three JEE Advanced problems that stitch it all together.",[],""],[1,9,1,"sec","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Most marks in this chapter are lost in the first thirty seconds, by starting to write equations before deciding on a method. Run down this list first.",[],""],[1,9,2,"text","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Most marks in this chapter are lost in the first thirty seconds, by starting to write equations before deciding on a method. Run down this list first.",[],""],[1,9,3,"table","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","If you see… Reach for… drift speed, electron density, a field inside a wire I = neAv_d and J = σE — no need for R a conductor whose cross-section changes slice across the current, integrate dR = ρ dx/A materials lying side by side along the current they are in parallel a network of resistors only name the nodes → look for symmetry → series/parallel five resistors in a diamond test P/Q = R/S first; if balanced, delete the middle one an endless repeating pattern call it x ; remove one section; it is still x two or more batteries in different branches node-voltage method (junction rule in terms of potentials) “current through … is zero” no drop across that resistor: its ends are at the same potential bulbs, heaters, “which is brighter?” find R = V_(rated)^2/P_(rated) ; then ask whether I or V is shared power sent over cables P_(loss) = (P/V)^2R a galvanometer being converted at full scale the coil carries I_g ; write one Ohm’s-law equation a meter with finite resistance treat it as one more resistor in the circuit a null point no current in the galvanometer branch → write a ratio",["I = neAv_d","J = σE","R","dR = ρ dx/A","P/Q = R/S","x","x","R = V_(rated)^2/P_(rated)","I","V","P_(loss) = (P/V)^2R","I_g"],""],[1,9,4,"sec","The formulas worth memorising","",[],""],[1,9,5,"table","The formulas worth memorising","Idea Formula Current, current density I = dq/dt = neAv_d, J = I/A = nev_d Drift velocity, mobility v_d = eEτ/m, μ = v_d/E = eτ/m Ohm’s law (point form) J = σE, ρ = 1/σ = m/(ne^2τ) Resistance R = ρL/A ; stretched to n times the length: n^2R Temperature R_T = R_0 [1 + α (T − T_0)] Series / parallel R_s = ∑ R_i, 1/R_p = ∑ 1/R_i Cell V = ε − Ir (discharging), V = ε + Ir (charging) Maximum power at R = r : P_max = (ε^2)/(4r) ; equal power when R_1R_2 = r^2 Cells in parallel ε_eq = (∑ ε_i/r_i)/(∑ 1/r_i), 1/(r_eq) = ∑ 1/r_i Kirchhoff ∑ I_in = ∑ I_out, ∑_(loop) ΔV = 0 Power, heat P = VI = I^2R = (V^2)/R, H = I^2Rt Rated bulb R = (V_(rated)^2)/(P_(rated)) ; two in series: (P_1P_2)/(P_1 + P_2) Ammeter shunt S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) Voltmeter multiplier R = V/I_g − G Wheatstone / metre bridge P/Q = R/S, X/R = l/(100 − l) Potentiometer ε_1/ε_2 = l_1/l_2, r = R (l_1/l_2 − 1)",["I = dq/dt = neAv_d, J = I/A = nev_d","v_d = eEτ/m, μ = v_d/E = eτ/m","J = σE, ρ = 1/σ = m/(ne^2τ)","R = ρL/A","n","n^2R","R_T = R_0 [1 + α (T − T_0)]","R_s = ∑ R_i, 1/R_p = ∑ 1/R_i","V = ε − Ir","V = ε + Ir","R = r","P_max = (ε^2)/(4r)"],""],[1,9,6,"sec","Ten traps that keep coming back","",[],""],[1,9,7,"text","Ten traps that keep coming back","Three speeds. Random (∼ 10^6 m s ^(−1)), drift (∼ 10^(−4)), signal (∼ 10^8). Only the drift is the current. Current is a scalar; current density is the vector. Non-uniform wire: the current is the same everywhere. J , E and v_d are not. Stretching changes length and area together: R ∝ L^2 , not L . I – V or V – I ? On an I – V graph a steeper line means a smaller resistance. Terminal voltage can exceed the emf — when the cell is being charged. A reversed cell subtracts its emf but still adds its internal resistance. Rated power is not actual power. In series, the lower-wattage bulb is the brighter one. V^2/R needs the voltage across that resistor — not the supply voltage, and certainly not the transmission voltage. A real voltmeter is a resistor in parallel; a real ammeter is a resistor in series. And V/I from two meters never depends on the battery.",["∼ 10^6 m s ^(−1)","∼ 10^(−4)","∼ 10^8","J","E","v_d","R ∝ L^2","L","I","V","V","I"],""],[1,9,8,"sec","Mixed practice: three problems that span the chapter","Each of these needs ideas from more than one lesson. Try each on paper before you look at anything.",[],""],[1,9,9,"text","Mixed practice: three problems that span the chapter","Each of these needs ideas from more than one lesson. Try each on paper before you look at anything.",[],""],[1,9,10,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2016 · One or more correct","An incandescent bulb has a thin filament of tungsten that is heated to high temperature by passing an electric current. The hot filament emits black-body radiation. The filament is observed to break up at random locations after a sufficiently long time of operation due to non-uniform evaporation of tungsten from the filament. If the bulb is powered at constant voltage, which of the following statement(s) is/are true? The temperature distribution over the filament is uniform. · The resistance over small sections of the filament decreases with time. · The filament emits more light at a higher band of frequencies before it breaks up. · The filament consumes less electrical power towards the end of the life of the bulb.",[],""],[1,9,10,"sol","JEE Advanced 2016 · One or more correct","The filament is a single wire, so the same current passes through every part of it. (A) Tungsten evaporates unevenly, so some sections become thinner than others. A thin section has more resistance per unit length, so with the same current it produces more heat (I^2R) — and it has less surface to radiate that heat away. It runs hotter than its neighbours. The temperature is not uniform. Wrong. (B) Evaporation removes material: every section gets thinner with time, and a hotter filament has a higher resistivity as well. The resistance of a small section increases. Wrong. (C) A hot spot is a vicious circle: hotter means faster evaporation, which makes it thinner, which makes it hotter still. Just before the filament breaks, that spot is at a very high temperature — and by Wien’s displacement law (from the Thermodynamics chapter), a hotter black body emits more of its light at higher frequencies. Correct. (D) The supply voltage is constant, so the power is P = V^2/R . Over the bulb’s life the filament’s total resistance has risen, so the power it draws falls. Correct. Answer (C), (D). One question, four lessons: resistance and geometry, temperature dependence, heating, and choosing V^2/R because the voltage is what is fixed. Evaporation makes parts of the filament thinner. The whole filament is one series circuit, so every part carries the same current. What happens at a thin spot",["I^2R","P = V^2/R","V^2/R"],""],[1,9,11,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","In the balanced condition, the values of the resistances of the four arms of a Wheatstone bridge are as shown in the figure. The resistance R_3 has a temperature coefficient of 0.0004 ^∘ C ^(−1) . If the temperature of R_3 is increased by 100 ^∘ C , find the voltage developed between S and T, in volts.",["R_3","0.0004 ^∘ C ^(−1)","R_3","100 ^∘ C"],""],[1,9,11,"sol","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","Before heating. R_1/R_3 = 60/300 and R_2/R_4 = 100/500 — both 1/5 . The two sides divide the 50 V in the same ratio, so S and T are at the same potential. Balanced, as stated. After heating. R_3^′ = 300 (1 + 0.0004 × 100) = 312 Ω. Now treat each side as a voltage divider and measure potentials from the lower end: V_S = 50 × 312/(60 + 312) = 41.94 V , V_T = 50 × 500/(100 + 500) = 41.67 V . V_S − V_T ≈ 0.27 V . Answer: 0.27 V. A 4% change in one arm produces a voltage that is easy to measure. This is exactly how a resistance thermometer or a strain gauge is read: put the sensor in one arm of a balanced bridge and watch the bridge go out of balance. The galvanometer branch is not asked about — S and T are just two points. Each side of the bridge is a simple voltage divider across the 50 V supply.",["R_1/R_3 = 60/300","R_2/R_4 = 100/500","1/5","50 V","R_3^′ = 300 (1 + 0.0004 × 100) = 312 Ω.","V_S = 50 × 312/(60 + 312) = 41.94 V , V_T = 50 × 500/(100 + 500) = 41.67 V .","V_S − V_T ≈ 0.27 V .","4%","50 V"],""],[1,9,12,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2022 · Integer answer","Two resistances R_1 = X Ω and R_2 = 1 Ω are connected to a wire AB of uniform resistivity, as shown in the figure. The radius of the wire varies linearly along its axis from 0.2 mm at A to 1 mm at B. A galvanometer (G) connected to the centre of the wire, 50 cm from each end along its axis, shows zero deflection when A and B are connected to a battery. Find the value of X .",["R_1 = X Ω","R_2 = 1 Ω","0.2 mm","1 mm","50 cm","X"],""],[1,9,12,"sol","JEE Advanced 2022 · Integer answer","Zero deflection means the bridge is balanced: R_1/R_2 = (R_AC)/(R_CB), where C is the mid-point of the wire. The halves have equal length, but the wire is far thinner near A, so their resistances differ. Radius at the centre. The radius grows linearly from 0.2 mm to 1 mm , so at the middle it is 0.6 mm . Resistance of a tapered piece. From Lesson 2, a length ℓ of wire tapering from radius a to radius b has resistance ρℓ/πab . Both halves have the same ρ and the same ℓ , so (R_AC)/(R_CB) = (1/(0.2 × 0.6))/(1/(0.6 × 1)) = (0.6 × 1)/(0.2 × 0.6) = 5. X/1 = 5 ⟹ X = 5. Answer: 5. The thin half of the wire has five times the resistance of the thick half, even though both are 50 cm long. This is why Lesson 8 insisted that a metre-bridge wire be uniform. It is a metre bridge with a non-uniform wire. The lengths of the two halves are equal — but their resistances are not. You met the resistance of a tapered wire in Lesson 2.",["R_1/R_2 = (R_AC)/(R_CB),","0.2 mm","1 mm","0.6 mm","ℓ","a","b","ρℓ/πab","ρ","ℓ","(R_AC)/(R_CB) = (1/(0.2 × 0.6))/(1/(0.6 × 1)) = (0.6 × 1)/(0.2 × 0.6) = 5.","X/1 = 5 ⟹ X = 5."],""],[1,9,13,"story","Back to the switch","We began with a puzzle: electrons that crawl, and a lamp that lights at once. You can now tell the whole story. The battery, a charge pump, keeps one end of the circuit at a higher potential. The field it sets up spreads along the wire at nearly the speed of light, and every free electron begins its slow drift. In the filament — thin, long, deliberately resistive — those electrons collide furiously, and I^2R heats the tungsten until it glows. Nothing travelled from the switch to the lamp except the news. And a moving charge has one more trick up its sleeve: it makes a magnetic field. That is where the story goes next.",["I^2R"],""],[2,1,0,"lesson","The Magnetic Force on a Moving Charge","In 1820 a compass needle twitched beside a live wire, and physics was never the same. Meet the strangest force in the syllabus — it ignores charges at rest, always pushes sideways, and can never speed anything up.",[],""],[2,1,1,"sec","The twitch that changed physics","Copenhagen, 1820. Hans Christian Ørsted connects a battery to a wire lying near a compass — and the needle swings. Switch the current off and the needle swings back.",[],""],[2,1,2,"text","The twitch that changed physics","Copenhagen, 1820. Hans Christian Ørsted connects a battery to a wire lying near a compass — and the needle swings. Switch the current off and the needle swings back.",[],""],[2,1,3,"text","The twitch that changed physics","That is all. A needle moved. But nobody had ever seen electricity push on a magnet before. Electric charges and magnets had been two separate subjects for two thousand years, and in one afternoon they became one.",[],""],[2,1,4,"text","The twitch that changed physics","Within a few months André-Marie Ampère in Paris had shown that two wires carrying currents push and pull on each other — no magnet anywhere. The conclusion was unavoidable: magnetism is what moving charges do to other moving charges.",[],""],[2,1,5,"story","The plan for this chapter","Electrostatics was about charges sitting still. This chapter is about what changes when they move. It comes in four parts. What a magnetic field does to a moving charge (Lessons 1–2) → where fields come from: Biot–Savart and Ampère (Lessons 3–4) → forces and torques on currents, the physics of every motor and meter (Lessons 5–7) → magnets and materials (Lesson 8). The same two equations do almost all of the work.",[],""],[2,1,6,"sec","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","Fill a region of space with a magnetic field B . Send a charge q through it with velocity v . Experiment says it feels a force",[],""],[2,1,7,"text","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","Fill a region of space with a magnetic field B . Send a charge q through it with velocity v . Experiment says it feels a force",["B","q","v"],""],[2,1,8,"formula","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","Fill a region of space with a magnetic field B . Send a charge q through it with velocity v . Experiment says it feels a force",["F = q v × B"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = <i>q</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>"],[2,1,9,"text","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","That cross product is the whole story, and it has three strange consequences:",[],""],[2,1,10,"text","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","A charge at rest feels nothing. No velocity, no force. A magnet cannot pick up a stationary charged balloon by magnetism. A charge moving along the field feels nothing either. The size of the force is F = qvB sin θ , with θ the angle between v and B . It is largest when the charge cuts straight across the field. The force is sideways. It is perpendicular to both v and B . It never pushes a charge along its path, only off it.",["F = qvB sin θ","θ","v","B","v","B"],""],[2,1,11,"text","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","The SI unit of B follows from F = qvB : newtons per (coulomb × metre per second), which is newtons per ampere-metre. It is called the tesla:",["B","F = qvB"],""],[2,1,12,"formula","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","The SI unit of B follows from F = qvB : newtons per (coulomb × metre per second), which is newtons per ampere-metre. It is called the tesla:",["1 T = 1 N/(A ⋅ m), 1 gauss = 10^(−4) T"],"1 <span class=\"fx-t\">T</span> = 1 <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><span class=\"fx-t\">N</span></span><span><span class=\"fx-t\">A</span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-t\">m</span></span></span>, 1 <span class=\"fx-t\">gauss</span> = 10<sup>−4</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">T</span>"],[2,1,13,"text","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","A tesla is a lot of field:",[],""],[2,1,14,"table","The magnetic field, defined by what it does","Where Typical B Earth’s field at the surface 25–65 μ T A fridge magnet about 5 mT The surface of a neodymium magnet about 1 T A hospital MRI scanner 1.5–3 T A magnetar (a kind of neutron star) 10^9 – 10^11 T",["B","μ","10^9","10^11"],""],[2,1,15,"sec","Which way? The right-hand rule","A cross product has a direction, and you will need it in almost every question in this chapter. One rule, used the same way every time:",[],""],[2,1,16,"text","Which way? The right-hand rule","A cross product has a direction, and you will need it in almost every question in this chapter. One rule, used the same way every time:",[],""],[2,1,17,"text","Which way? The right-hand rule","Point the fingers of your right hand along v . Curl them towards B through the smaller angle. Your thumb points along v × B — the force on a positive charge.",["v","B","v × B"],""],[2,1,18,"text","Which way? The right-hand rule","For a negative charge, such as an electron, find the direction for a positive charge and then reverse it.",[],""],[2,1,19,"text","Which way? The right-hand rule","Diagrams are flat, so we need a way to draw arrows that point out of or into the page. Think of an arrow flying at you: you see its point, ⊙ (out of the page). Flying away from you, you see the tail feathers, ⊗ (into the page).",["⊙","⊗"],""],[2,1,20,"figure","Which way? The right-hand rule","Same velocity, same field, opposite charges — opposite forces. Check both with your hand.",[],""],[2,1,21,"text","Which way? The right-hand rule","When the vectors lie along the axes, the cross products are worth memorising as a cycle: x → y → z → x . Going forwards gives the third one, going backwards gives minus it:",["x → y → z → x"],""],[2,1,22,"formula","Which way? The right-hand rule","When the vectors lie along the axes, the cross products are worth memorising as a cycle: x → y → z → x . Going forwards gives the third one, going backwards gives minus it:",["x × y = z, y × z = x, z × x = y, y × x = −z (and so on)"],"<span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>x</i>̂</span> × <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>y</i>̂</span> = <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>z</i>̂</span>, <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>y</i>̂</span> × <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>z</i>̂</span> = <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>x</i>̂</span>, <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>z</i>̂</span> × <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>x</i>̂</span> = <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>y</i>̂</span>, <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>y</i>̂</span> × <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>x</i>̂</span> = −<span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>z</i>̂</span> <span class=\"fx-t\">(and so on)</span>"],[2,1,23,"trap","Do the rule for a positive charge, then think","The commonest lost mark in this chapter is an electron pushed the wrong way. Never try to “use the left hand for electrons”. Use the right hand, get the answer for +q , and flip it.",["+q"],""],[2,1,24,"q","Electron in Earth’s field · Single correct","An electron moves due north in a horizontal plane. The magnetic field there points vertically downward. In which direction is the magnetic force on the electron? West · East · North · Vertically upward",[],""],[2,1,24,"sol","Electron in Earth’s field · Single correct","With east = x , north = y , up = z : the velocity is along y and the field along −z . v × B ∝ y × (−z) = −x (west) That is the force on a positive charge. The electron is negative, so its force points the other way: east. Answer (B). Notice the force is horizontal and perpendicular to the velocity — it would curve the electron round in the horizontal plane, never up or down. Take east, north and up as x , y , z . Work out y × (−z) first, then remember the charge is negative.",["= x","= y","= z","y","−z","v × B ∝ y × (−z) = −x (west)","x","y","z","y × (−z)"],""],[2,1,25,"lab","Right-hand-rule sprint","Ten seconds of thinking per question is too slow for the exam. This drill builds the reflex. Each round shows a charge, its velocity v (an arrow, or ⊙ / ⊗ drawn on the charge) and the field B (field lines, or a grid of dots or crosses). Tap the direction of the force. Do it with your actual hand for the first few rounds. Fingers along v , curl to B , thumb. Watch the sign of the charge — about one round in three is an electron. Sometimes v is parallel to B . Then the answer is no force. Aim for a streak of ten. Then try to keep it while answering in under two seconds.",["v","⊙","⊗","B","v","B","v","B"],""],[2,1,26,"sec","The force that never does any work","Here is the most useful fact in the lesson. The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so the rate at which it does work is",[],""],[2,1,27,"text","The force that never does any work","Here is the most useful fact in the lesson. The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so the rate at which it does work is",[],""],[2,1,28,"formula","The force that never does any work","Here is the most useful fact in the lesson. The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so the rate at which it does work is",["P = F ⋅ v = q (v × B) ⋅ v = 0"],"<i>P</i> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> = <i>q</i> (<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>) ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> = 0"],[2,1,29,"text","The force that never does any work","A magnetic force can never change a particle’s speed or its kinetic energy. It can only bend the path. However long the particle stays in the field, however twisty its path, it comes out exactly as fast as it went in.",[],""],[2,1,30,"intuition","A stone on a string","Whirl a stone round your head on a string. The string pulls on the stone all the time, yet the stone never speeds up — the pull is always at right angles to its motion. The magnetic force on a charge is that string. It steers; it never pushes.",[],""],[2,1,31,"sec","Add an electric field: the Lorentz force","If an electric field is present too, the forces just add. The total is the Lorentz force:",[],""],[2,1,32,"text","Add an electric field: the Lorentz force","If an electric field is present too, the forces just add. The total is the Lorentz force:",[],""],[2,1,33,"formula","Add an electric field: the Lorentz force","If an electric field is present too, the forces just add. The total is the Lorentz force:",["F = q (E + v × B)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = <i>q</i> (<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>E</i></span> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>)"],[2,1,34,"text","Add an electric field: the Lorentz force","The two parts have very different personalities. The electric part acts whether the charge moves or not, and it does work — it can speed a charge up or slow it down. The magnetic part only acts on moving charges, and it only steers.",[],""],[2,1,35,"text","Add an electric field: the Lorentz force","Pause & predictA charge is released from rest where both E and B exist. Which field gets it moving? Only E . At the instant of release v = 0 , so q v × B = 0 . The electric force starts it moving along E ; only then can the magnetic force begin to act — and how it acts depends on the angle between E and B . Try the next question with that in mind.",["E","B","E","v = 0","q v × B = 0","E","E","B"],""],[2,1,36,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1999 · Single correct","A charged particle is released from rest in a region of steady and uniform electric and magnetic fields which are parallel to each other. The particle will move in a straight line · circle · helix · cycloid",[],""],[2,1,36,"sol","IIT-JEE 1999 · Single correct","At release the particle is at rest, so the magnetic force is zero. The electric force qE accelerates it along E . But E is parallel to B , so the velocity it gains is along B too — and a charge moving along the field feels no magnetic force (sin 0^∘ = 0). That stays true for ever: the velocity only ever grows along the same line. Answer (A): a straight line along the fields, with steadily increasing speed. If the fields were perpendicular instead, the answer would be the cycloid — the path of a point on the rim of a rolling wheel.",["qE","E","E","B","B","sin 0^∘ = 0"],""],[2,1,37,"sec","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","Now arrange E and B at right angles, both perpendicular to a beam of charged particles. Pick the directions so that the two forces oppose each other:",[],""],[2,1,38,"text","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","Now arrange E and B at right angles, both perpendicular to a beam of charged particles. Pick the directions so that the two forces oppose each other:",["E","B"],""],[2,1,39,"text","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","The electric force qE pushes the particle down; the magnetic force qvB pushes it up. They cancel when",["qE","qvB"],""],[2,1,40,"formula","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","The electric force qE pushes the particle down; the magnetic force qvB pushes it up. They cancel when",["qE = qvB ⟹ v = E/B"],"<i>q</i><i>E</i> = <i>q</i><i>v</i><i>B</i> ⟹ <i>v</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>E</i></span><span><i>B</i></span></span>"],[2,1,41,"text","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","Only particles with exactly this speed fly straight through to the slit. Faster ones feel a bigger magnetic force and curve up; slower ones curve down. And notice what is missing from the result: the charge and the mass. A proton, an electron (with both fields’ forces reversed together) and a uranium ion all pass if — and only if — their speed is E/B .",["E/B"],""],[2,1,42,"text","Crossed fields: the velocity selector","In vector form, the selected velocity is v = (E × B)/(B^2) — perpendicular to both fields, with size E/B .",["v = (E × B)/(B^2)","E/B"],""],[2,1,43,"story","The first particle ever discovered","In 1897 J. J. Thomson used exactly this trick on the “cathode rays” in a glass tube. He balanced an electric deflection against a magnetic one to find their speed, then used the magnetic bending alone to get their charge-to-mass ratio. It came out more than a thousand times larger than for a hydrogen ion. The rays were a stream of tiny new particles: electrons.",[],""],[2,1,44,"q","Tuning a velocity selector · Integer answer","Two parallel plates 1.0 cm apart have 300 V across them, and a uniform magnetic field of 0.050 T fills the gap at right angles to the electric field. Ions are fired into the gap perpendicular to both fields. Ions of what speed pass through undeflected? Give your answer in units of 10^5 m/s.",["10^5"],""],[2,1,44,"sol","Tuning a velocity selector · Integer answer","The field between the plates is E = V/d = (300 V)/(0.010 m) = 3.0 × 10^4 V/m Undeflected ions have v = E/B = (3.0 × 10^4)/(0.050) = 6.0 × 10^5 m/s Answer: 6. Doubly charged ions of the same speed also pass — both forces double together. Only the speed is selected. Find E from the plates first: E = V/d .",["E = V/d = (300 V)/(0.010 m) = 3.0 × 10^4 V/m","v = E/B = (3.0 × 10^4)/(0.050) = 6.0 × 10^5 m/s","E","E = V/d"],""],[2,1,45,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2000 · Single correct","An ionised gas contains both positive and negative ions. It is subjected simultaneously to an electric field along the +x direction and a magnetic field along the +z direction. Then positive ions deflect towards +y and negative ions towards −y · all ions deflect towards +y · all ions deflect towards −y · positive ions deflect towards −y and negative ions towards +y",["+x","+z","+y","−y","+y","−y","−y","+y"],""],[2,1,45,"sol","IIT-JEE 2000 · Single correct","The electric field starts the ions moving: positive ions along +x , negative ions along −x . Positive ion: q > 0 , v along +x . F ∝ (+) (x × z) = −y Negative ion: q < 0 , v along −x . F ∝ (−) (−x × z) = (+)(x × z) = −y Two sign flips cancel. Answer (C): every ion drifts towards −y . This is a deep little result. In crossed fields, positive and negative charges drift the same way, so the gas as a whole drifts along E × B — here x × z = −y . Plasma physicists call it the E × B drift. The electric field sets the ions moving first. Which way does each kind move — and what happens to the sign in q v × B when both q and v flip?",["+x","−x","q > 0","v","+x","F ∝ (+) (x × z) = −y","q < 0","v","−x","F ∝ (−) (−x × z) = (+)(x × z) = −y","−y","E × B"],""],[2,1,46,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1991 · One or more correct","A particle of charge +q and mass m moves under the influence of a uniform electric field E i and a uniform magnetic field B k , and follows the path from P to Q shown. Its velocity at P is v i and at Q is −2v j . Which of the following is/are correct? E = 3/4 (mv^2)/qa · The rate of work done by the electric field at P is 3/4 (mv^3)/a · The rate of work done by the electric field at P is zero · The rate of work done by both fields at Q is zero",["+q","m","E i","B k","v i","−2v j","E = 3/4 (mv^2)/qa","3/4 (mv^3)/a"],""],[2,1,46,"sol","IIT-JEE 1991 · One or more correct","Energy. The magnetic force does no work, so the whole change in kinetic energy comes from the electric field. Between P and Q the particle moves 2a along i , the direction of E : qE(2a) = 1/2m(2v)^2 − 1/2mv^2 = 3/2mv^2 ⟹ E = 3/4 (mv^2)/qa So (A) is correct. Power at P. The electric force qE i is parallel to the velocity v i : P = qEv = 3/4 (mv^3)/a So (B) is correct and (C) is wrong. Power at Q. The velocity −2v j is perpendicular to the electric force qE i , so the electric field does no work at that instant. The magnetic field never does work. So (D) is correct. Answer (A), (B), (D). The lesson: whenever B appears in an energy question, cross it out — it contributes nothing. Only the electric field can change the kinetic energy. How far does the particle move along E between P and Q?",["2a","i","E","qE(2a) = 1/2m(2v)^2 − 1/2mv^2 = 3/2mv^2 ⟹ E = 3/4 (mv^2)/qa","qE i","v i","P = qEv = 3/4 (mv^3)/a","−2v j","qE i","B","E"],""],[2,1,47,"story","Curtains of light over the poles","The solar wind is a stream of charged particles blowing out from the Sun. Most are steered round the Earth by its magnetic field. But near the poles the field lines plunge down into the atmosphere, and particles that spiral along them crash into air molecules a hundred kilometres or more up. Excited oxygen atoms glow green; higher up they glow red. That is the aurora. Next lesson you will see exactly why the particles spiral.",[],""],[2,1,48,"recap","Recap","F = q v × B , size qvB sin θ . Zero for a charge at rest or moving along B . Always perpendicular to both v and B . Right hand: fingers along v , curl to B , thumb = force on +q . Reverse for negative charges. ⊙ out of the page, ⊗ into it. 1 T = 1 N A ^(−1) m ^(−1) ; 1 G = 10^(−4) T. Earth’s field is a few tens of microtesla. The magnetic force does no work. Speed and kinetic energy never change in a pure magnetic field. Lorentz force: F = q(E + v × B) . Crossed fields pass only v = E/B , whatever the charge or mass.",["F = q v × B","qvB sin θ","B","v","B","v","B","+q","⊙","⊗","1 T = 1 N A ^(−1) m ^(−1)","1 G = 10^(−4)"],""],[2,2,0,"lesson","Circles, Helices & the Cyclotron","A magnetic field can only steer, so a charge in a uniform field runs round a perfect circle — and every lap takes exactly the same time, however fast it goes. That one fact builds particle accelerators, paints the aurora, and cracks most JEE trajectory problems.",[],""],[2,2,1,"sec","Why a circle?","Fire a charge at right angles into a uniform magnetic field. Two things are true at every instant:",[],""],[2,2,2,"text","Why a circle?","Fire a charge at right angles into a uniform magnetic field. Two things are true at every instant:",[],""],[2,2,3,"text","Why a circle?","the speed never changes (the magnetic force does no work), so the force qvB has a constant size; the force is always perpendicular to the velocity.",["qvB"],""],[2,2,4,"text","Why a circle?","A force of constant size, always at right angles to the motion — that is exactly the recipe for uniform circular motion. The magnetic force supplies the centripetal force:",[],""],[2,2,5,"formula","Why a circle?","A force of constant size, always at right angles to the motion — that is exactly the recipe for uniform circular motion. The magnetic force supplies the centripetal force:",["qvB = (mv^2)/r ⟹ r = mv/qB = p/qB"],"<i>q</i><i>v</i><i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><i>v</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> ⟹ <i>r</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><i>v</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>p</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span>"],[2,2,6,"text","Why a circle?","More momentum means a wider circle; a stronger field or a bigger charge pulls it tighter. JEE often hands you the kinetic energy K or the accelerating voltage V instead, so keep these forms ready:",["K","V"],""],[2,2,7,"formula","Why a circle?","More momentum means a wider circle; a stronger field or a bigger charge pulls it tighter. JEE often hands you the kinetic energy K or the accelerating voltage V instead, so keep these forms ready:",["r = (√(2mK))/qB = 1/B√((2mV)/q) (using K = qV)"],"<i>r</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\">2<i>m</i><i>K</i></span></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>B</i></span></span>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>mV</i></span><span><i>q</i></span></span></span> (<span class=\"fx-t\">using </span><i>K</i> = <i>q</i><i>V</i>)"],[2,2,8,"sec","The clock that ignores speed","How long does one lap take?",[],""],[2,2,9,"text","The clock that ignores speed","How long does one lap take?",[],""],[2,2,10,"formula","The clock that ignores speed","How long does one lap take?",["T = 2πr/v = 2πm/qB, f = qB/2πm, ω = qB/m"],"<i>T</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>π</i><i>r</i></span><span><i>v</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>π</i><i>m</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span>, <i>f</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>m</i></span></span>, <i>ω</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>"],[2,2,11,"text","The clock that ignores speed","The speed has cancelled out. A fast particle runs a bigger circle, but exactly in proportion, so it gets round in the same time as a slow one. This is called the cyclotron frequency, and it depends only on the particle (q/m) and the field.",["q/m"],""],[2,2,12,"intuition","Every runner finishes the lap together","Imagine runners on a track where each one is forced into a lane whose size matches their speed — twice as fast, twice the radius. Twice the speed over twice the distance: they all cross the start line together, lap after lap. That is a cloud of identical charges in a magnetic field.",[],""],[2,2,13,"story","A million laps a second","An electron in Earth’s field (take B = 50 μ T) has f = eB/2πm_e ≈ 1.4 MHz. If nothing knocked it off course, it would circle about 1.4 million times every second — whatever its speed.",["B = 50 μ T","f = eB/2πm_e ≈ 1.4"],""],[2,2,14,"q","Four times the energy · Single correct","A proton moves in a circle in a uniform magnetic field. Its kinetic energy is now made four times larger, in the same field. What happens to the radius r and the time period T ? r doubles and T doubles · r doubles and T is unchanged · r is unchanged and T halves · r becomes four times larger and T is unchanged",["r","T","r","T","r","T","r","T","r","T"],""],[2,2,14,"sol","Four times the energy · Single correct","Four times the kinetic energy means twice the speed (K ∝ v^2), so twice the momentum. Then r = mv/qB → 2r, T = 2πm/qB → unchanged Answer (B). The trap is option (D): the radius follows the momentum, and momentum goes as √K .",["K ∝ v^2","r = mv/qB → 2r, T = 2πm/qB → unchanged","√K"],""],[2,2,15,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","A proton, a deuteron and an α -particle having the same kinetic energy move in circular trajectories in a constant magnetic field. If r_p , r_d and r_α are the radii of their trajectories, then r_α = r_p < r_d · r_α > r_d > r_p · r_α = r_d > r_p · r_p = r_d = r_α",["α","r_p","r_d","r_α","r_α = r_p < r_d","r_α > r_d > r_p","r_α = r_d > r_p","r_p = r_d = r_α"],""],[2,2,15,"sol","IIT-JEE 1997 · Single correct","With the same K and B , r = (√(2mK))/qB ∝ (√m)/q Particle m q √m/q proton 1 1 1 deuteron 2 1 √2 α 4 2 1 So r_p : r_d : r_α = 1 : √2 : 1 . Answer (A). The alpha particle is four times heavier, but its double charge exactly makes up for it. With K and B fixed, r = √(2mK)/qB ∝ √m/q . Write down m and q for each particle in units of the proton’s.",["K","B","r = (√(2mK))/qB ∝ (√m)/q","m","q","√m/q","√2","α","r_p : r_d : r_α = 1 : √2 : 1","K","B","r = √(2mK)/qB ∝ √m/q"],""],[2,2,16,"sec","Into a field — and out again","Real fields have edges. Suppose the field fills a band of width d , and a charge enters at right angles to the edge. Its path inside is an arc of a circle of radius r . What happens next depends only on how r compares with d :",[],""],[2,2,17,"text","Into a field — and out again","Real fields have edges. Suppose the field fills a band of width d , and a charge enters at right angles to the edge. Its path inside is an arc of a circle of radius r . What happens next depends only on how r compares with d :",["d","r","r","d"],""],[2,2,18,"figure","Into a field — and out again","Small radius: a half-circle and straight back out. Large radius: through, bent by θ with sin θ = d/r .",["θ","sin θ = d/r","θ","sin θ = d/r"],""],[2,2,19,"text","Into a field — and out again","r < d — a U-turn. The charge completes a half circle and leaves the way it came, travelling backwards, shifted sideways by 2r . Time inside: half a period, T/2 = πm/qB — the same for every speed. r = d — just grazing. The half-circle just touches the far edge. This is the longest possible path in the field. r > d — through. It crosses the band and leaves deflected by an angle θ with sin θ = d/r . The time inside is θ/ω .",["r < d","2r","T/2 = πm/qB","r = d","r > d","θ","sin θ = d/r","θ/ω"],""],[2,2,20,"text","Into a field — and out again","If the charge enters at a slant, one piece of geometry saves the day: a circle meets a straight line symmetrically. A charge that enters making an angle α with the boundary leaves making the same angle α with it, like a ball bouncing off a wall.",["α","α"],""],[2,2,21,"lab","Steer a charge","A beam of protons enters a band of magnetic field from the left. You control the speed, the field and the width of the band. Try these, in order: Start with the settings as they are and watch the U-turn. Read the time in field. Now change the speed: the semicircle grows or shrinks, but the time stays the same. Slowly raise the speed until the path just grazes the far edge — r = d . A touch faster and it escapes. Drag the launch arrow to fire in at a slant. Check that the charge leaves at the same angle to the edge. Switch the particle to an alpha or an antiproton. Which way does each curve, and how big is the circle? Turn on the electric field and tune it until the beam flies straight through. You have built the velocity selector from Lesson 1: read off E/B and compare it with the speed.",["r = d","E/B"],""],[2,2,22,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2008 · One or more correct","A particle of mass m and charge q , moving with velocity v , enters Region II normal to the boundary as shown. Region II has a uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to the plane of the paper. The length of Region II is l . Choose the correct choice(s). The particle enters Region III only if its velocity v > qlB/m · The particle enters Region III only if its velocity v < qlB/m · The path length of the particle in Region II is maximum when v = qlB/m · The time spent in Region II is the same for any velocity v , as long as the particle returns to Region I",["m","q","v","B","l","v > qlB/m","v < qlB/m","v = qlB/m","v"],""],[2,2,22,"sol","IIT-JEE 2008 · One or more correct","Inside Region II the particle moves on a circle of radius r = mv/qB , which starts tangent to the direction of entry. It reaches Region III only if the circle reaches across the band: r > l , i.e. v > qlB/m . (A) is correct, (B) is wrong. While r ≤ l the path is a half circle of length πr , which grows with v . Once r > l the particle escapes along an arc shorter than that. So the longest path is when r = l , i.e. v = qlB/m . (C) is correct. Every particle that turns back has done exactly half a revolution, taking T/2 = πm/qB — independent of v . (D) is correct. Answer (A), (C), (D).",["r = mv/qB","r > l","v > qlB/m","r ≤ l","πr","v","r > l","r = l","v = qlB/m","T/2 = πm/qB","v"],""],[2,2,23,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","A particle of mass M and positive charge Q , moving with a constant velocity u_1 = 4i m/s, enters a region of uniform static magnetic field normal to the x – y plane. The field fills the region from x = 0 to x = L for all values of y . After passing through this region, the particle emerges on the other side after 10 milliseconds with velocity u_2 = 2(√3 i + j) m/s. The correct statement(s) is/are The magnetic field points in the −z direction · The magnetic field points in the +z direction · The magnitude of the magnetic field is (50πM)/(3Q) units · The magnitude of the magnetic field is (100πM)/(3Q) units",["M","Q","u_1 = 4i","x","y","x = 0","x = L","y","u_2 = 2(√3 i + j)","−z","+z","(50πM)/(3Q)"],""],[2,2,23,"sol","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","Speed check. ∣u_2∣ = 2√(3 + 1) = 4 m/s. Unchanged, as it must be. Direction. The velocity has turned from i towards +j , so the force on the entering particle was along +j . For a positive charge moving along i : i × (−k) = +j so B points along −z . (A) is correct, (B) is wrong. Size. u_2 makes an angle tan^(−1)(1/√3) = 30^∘ = π/6 with i . The velocity rotates at the constant rate ω = QB/M , so QB/M (10 × 10^(−3) s) = π/6 ⟹ B = πM/(6Q × 0.01) = (50πM)/(3Q) (C) is correct. Answer (A), (C). Notice that we never needed L : the angle turned and the time taken were enough. Check the speed first — is it the same? Then find the angle through which the velocity has turned, and use ω = QB/M .",["∣u_2∣ = 2√(3 + 1) = 4","i","+j","+j","i","i × (−k) = +j","B","−z","u_2","tan^(−1)(1/√3) = 30^∘ = π/6","i","ω = QB/M"],""],[2,2,24,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · One or more correct","An electron and a proton are moving on straight parallel paths with the same velocity. They enter a semi-infinite region of uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the velocity. Which of the following statement(s) is/are true? They will never come out of the magnetic field region · They will come out travelling along parallel paths · They will come out at the same time · They will come out at different times",[],""],[2,2,24,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · One or more correct","Each charge moves on a circular arc and must come back to the boundary, because the field fills the whole half-space (so (A) is wrong). Exit direction. A circle meets a straight line symmetrically, so each charge leaves at the same angle to the boundary as it entered — its exit velocity is the mirror image of its entry velocity in the boundary. Both entered with the same velocity, so both leave with the same velocity: parallel paths. (B) is correct. Time. The two charges curve in opposite senses. If they enter at angle α to the boundary, one turns through 2α and the other through 2π − 2α . On top of that, ω = eB/m is about 1836 times larger for the electron. Their times θ/ω are different. (D) is correct, (C) is wrong. Answer (B), (D). A circle meets a straight line symmetrically. And the two charges curve in opposite senses.",["α","2α","2π − 2α","ω = eB/m","θ/ω"],""],[2,2,25,"sec","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","What if a charge enters a field at an angle θ to it? Split the velocity into two parts:",[],""],[2,2,26,"text","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","What if a charge enters a field at an angle θ to it? Split the velocity into two parts:",["θ"],""],[2,2,27,"text","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","v_∥ = v cos θ , along B — the field cannot touch it, so it carries on unchanged; v_⊥ = v sin θ , across B — this part goes round in a circle, exactly as before.",["v_∥ = v cos θ","B","v_⊥ = v sin θ","B"],""],[2,2,28,"text","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","A steady glide plus a circle is a helix, like the thread of a screw:",[],""],[2,2,29,"formula","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","A steady glide plus a circle is a helix, like the thread of a screw:",["r = (mv sin θ)/qB, T = 2πm/qB, p = v_∥ T = (2πmv cos θ)/qB"],"<i>r</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><i>v</i> sin <i>θ</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span>, <i>T</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>π</i><i>m</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span>, <i>p</i> = <i>v</i><sub> ∥ </sub> <i>T</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>π</i><i>m</i><i>v</i> cos <i>θ</i></span><span><i>q</i><i>B</i></span></span>"],[2,2,30,"text","Helices: when the velocity is not square to the field","The distance p moved along the field in one turn is the pitch. The period is the same as before — it never depended on speed.",["p"],""],[2,2,31,"story","Why the aurora is at the poles","A particle arriving from the Sun spirals round Earth’s field lines like a bead threaded on a wire. Near the equator the field lines run parallel to the ground, so the particles stay high up. Near the poles the lines dive steeply into the atmosphere — and the spiralling particles are led straight down with them. That is why auroras crowd into rings around the magnetic poles.",[],""],[2,2,32,"q","Pitch versus radius · Numerical answer","An electron enters a uniform magnetic field with its velocity at 60^∘ to the field. Find the ratio of the pitch of its helical path to the radius, p/r .",["60^∘","p/r"],""],[2,2,32,"sol","Pitch versus radius · Numerical answer","p/r = (2πmv cos θ/qB)/(mv sin θ/qB) = 2π cot θ = 2π/(√3) ≈ 3.63 Answer: 3.63. Everything about the particle cancels — the ratio depends only on the angle. At 90^∘ the pitch is zero (a flat circle); at small angles the helix is stretched out like a slinky.",["p/r = (2πmv cos θ/qB)/(mv sin θ/qB) = 2π cot θ = 2π/(√3) ≈ 3.63","90^∘"],""],[2,2,33,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · One or more correct","Consider the motion of a positive point charge in a region where there are simultaneous uniform electric and magnetic fields E = E_0 j and B = B_0 j . At time t = 0 this charge has velocity v in the x – y plane, making an angle θ with the x -axis. Which of the following option(s) is/are correct for time t > 0 ? If θ = 0^∘ , the charge moves in a circular path in the x – z plane · If θ = 0^∘ , the charge undergoes helical motion with constant pitch along the y -axis · If θ = 10^∘ , the charge undergoes helical motion with its pitch increasing with time, along the y -axis · If θ = 90^∘ , the charge undergoes linear but accelerated motion along the y -axis",["E = E_0 j","B = B_0 j","t = 0","v","x","y","θ","x","t > 0","θ = 0^∘","x","z"],""],[2,2,33,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · One or more correct","Both fields are along j . Split the motion: Across the fields (in the x – z plane): v_⊥ goes round in a circle of fixed radius, as always. Along the fields (y): the magnetic force is zero, but E gives a constant acceleration qE_0/m along +y . (A) At θ = 0^∘ there is no initial v_y , but the electric field soon creates one. The circle in the x – z plane is dragged along y faster and faster: a helix whose pitch grows. Not a circle — wrong. For the same reason the pitch is not constant: (B) is wrong. (C) At θ = 10^∘ : circle across y , accelerating drift along y — a helix with increasing pitch. Correct. (D) At θ = 90^∘ the velocity is along j , parallel to B , so there is no magnetic force, ever. The electric field just accelerates it along y . Correct. Answer (C), (D). Both fields point along y . Which part of the velocity does each field affect?",["j","x","z","v_⊥","y","E","qE_0/m","+y","θ = 0^∘","v_y","x","z"],""],[2,2,34,"sec","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","The lap time not depending on speed is not just a curiosity. In 1929 Ernest Lawrence realised it could be used to build a particle accelerator.",[],""],[2,2,35,"text","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","The lap time not depending on speed is not just a curiosity. In 1929 Ernest Lawrence realised it could be used to build a particle accelerator.",[],""],[2,2,36,"text","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","Two hollow D-shaped electrodes (“dees”) sit in a strong magnetic field, with an alternating voltage across the gap between them. Inside a dee there is no electric field, so the particle simply coasts round a half circle. Each time it reaches the gap, the voltage has just flipped to give it a kick across. It speeds up, swings round a slightly bigger half circle — which takes exactly the same time — and arrives back at the gap just as the voltage flips again.",[],""],[2,2,37,"text","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","So the voltage can oscillate at one fixed frequency, the cyclotron frequency f = qB/2πm , and stay in step for ever. The particle spirals outward until it reaches the rim, radius R , where",["f = qB/2πm","R"],""],[2,2,38,"formula","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","So the voltage can oscillate at one fixed frequency, the cyclotron frequency f = qB/2πm , and stay in step for ever. The particle spirals outward until it reaches the rim, radius R , where",["v_max = qBR/m, K_max = (q^2B^2R^2)/(2m)"],"<i>v</i><sub>max</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>B</i><i>R</i></span><span><i>m</i></span></span>, <i>K</i><sub>max</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><sup>2</sup><i>B</i><sup>2</sup><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>m</i></span></span>"],[2,2,39,"text","The cyclotron: a particle on a merry-go-round","Strangely, the accelerating voltage does not appear in K_max . A bigger voltage just gets the particle out in fewer laps.",["K_max"],""],[2,2,40,"trap","Two limits examiners love","A cyclotron cannot accelerate neutrons (no charge, nothing to push). And it struggles with electrons: they are so light that they approach the speed of light at quite low energies, their effective mass grows, the lap time stretches, and they fall out of step with the fixed-frequency voltage.",[],""],[2,2,41,"q","How much energy can it give? · Integer answer","A cyclotron has dees of radius 0.50 m in a field of 0.50 T. What is the maximum kinetic energy it can give to protons, in MeV? (Take m_p = 1.67 × 10^(−27) kg and e = 1.6 × 10^(−19) C; round to the nearest integer.)",["m_p = 1.67 × 10^(−27)","e = 1.6 × 10^(−19)"],""],[2,2,41,"sol","How much energy can it give? · Integer answer","K_max = (q^2B^2R^2)/(2m) = ((1.6 × 10^(−19))^2(0.50)^2(0.50)^2)/(2 × 1.67 × 10^(−27)) ≈ 4.8 × 10^(−13) J Since 1 MeV = 1.6 × 10^(−13) J, K_max ≈ 3.0 MeV. Answer: 3. The voltage must alternate at f = qB/2πm ≈ 7.6 MHz — a radio frequency, which is why cyclotrons are driven by radio transmitters. Use K_max = q^2B^2R^2/2m , then divide by 1.6 × 10^(−13) J per MeV.",["K_max = (q^2B^2R^2)/(2m) = ((1.6 × 10^(−19))^2(0.50)^2(0.50)^2)/(2 × 1.67 × 10^(−27)) ≈ 4.8 × 10^(−13) J","1 MeV = 1.6 × 10^(−13)","K_max ≈ 3.0","f = qB/2πm ≈ 7.6","K_max = q^2B^2R^2/2m","1.6 × 10^(−13)"],""],[2,2,42,"story","An accelerator in the palm of your hand","On 2 January 1931 Lawrence and his student M. Stanley Livingston ran a cyclotron about 11 cm across and pushed hydrogen ions to 80 keV. Bigger machines soon passed a million electron-volts. Lawrence won the 1939 Nobel Prize for the idea. Hospitals today use cyclotrons to make the short-lived radioactive tracers for PET scans.",[],""],[2,2,43,"recap","Recap","In a uniform field, perpendicular entry gives a circle: r = mv/qB = (√(2mK))/qB . Period T = 2πm/qB — independent of speed and radius. ω = qB/m . A band of field of width d : U-turn if r < d (time T/2 , any speed); through if r > d , deflected by sin θ = d/r . A circle meets a straight edge symmetrically. At an angle to B : a helix with r = (mv sin θ)/qB and pitch p = (2πmv cos θ)/qB . Add E ∥ B and the pitch grows; release from rest and the motion is a straight line. Cyclotron: fixed frequency qB/2πm ; K_max = q^2B^2R^2/2m , independent of the voltage.",["r = mv/qB = (√(2mK))/qB","T = 2πm/qB","ω = qB/m","d","r < d","T/2","r > d","sin θ = d/r","B","r = (mv sin θ)/qB","p = (2πmv cos θ)/qB","E ∥ B"],""],[2,3,0,"lesson","The Biot–Savart Law","Every tiny piece of a current-carrying wire adds its own little whirl of field. Add up the whirls and you can find the field of any wire you can draw — straight, bent, looped or coiled into a spiral.",[],""],[2,3,1,"sec","Currents make fields","Go back to Ørsted’s compass. Move it around a long straight wire carrying a steady current and map where the needle points. The field lines are circles centred on the wire, lying in planes perpendicular to it. Sprinkle iron filings on a card pierced by the wire and you can see them.",[],""],[2,3,2,"text","Currents make fields","Go back to Ørsted’s compass. Move it around a long straight wire carrying a steady current and map where the needle points. The field lines are circles centred on the wire, lying in planes perpendicular to it. Sprinkle iron filings on a card pierced by the wire and you can see them.",[],""],[2,3,3,"figure","Currents make fields","Right-hand grip: thumb along the current, fingers curl the way the field circulates.",[],""],[2,3,4,"text","Currents make fields","The direction follows the right-hand grip rule: grip the wire with your right thumb along the current; your fingers curl the way the field goes round. Seen from above, a current coming up towards you makes anticlockwise field lines.",[],""],[2,3,5,"text","Currents make fields","How strong is the field, and how do we get it for wires that are not straight? For that we need the law that plays the role Coulomb’s law played in electrostatics.",[],""],[2,3,6,"sec","The Biot–Savart law","Chop the wire into tiny pieces. A piece of length dl , carrying current I in the direction dl , produces at a point P (at displacement r from it) the field",[],""],[2,3,7,"text","The Biot–Savart law","Chop the wire into tiny pieces. A piece of length dl , carrying current I in the direction dl , produces at a point P (at displacement r from it) the field",["dl","I","dl","P","r"],""],[2,3,8,"formula","The Biot–Savart law","Chop the wire into tiny pieces. A piece of length dl , carrying current I in the direction dl , produces at a point P (at displacement r from it) the field",["dB = μ_0/4π (I dl × r)/(r^2), ∣dB∣ = μ_0/4π (I dl sin θ)/(r^2)"],"<i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i> <i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>l</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>r</i>̂</span></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>, ∣<i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>∣ = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i> <i>d</i><i>l</i> sin <i>θ</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>"],[2,3,9,"text","The Biot–Savart law","Here θ is the angle between dl and r . The constant is the permeability of free space:",["θ","dl","r"],""],[2,3,10,"formula","The Biot–Savart law","Here θ is the angle between dl and r . The constant is the permeability of free space:",["μ_0 = 4π × 10^(−7) T m A ^(−1), μ_0/4π = 10^(−7) T m A ^(−1)"],"<i>μ</i><sub>0</sub> = 4<i>π</i> × 10<sup>−7</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">T m A</span><sup>−1</sup>, <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> = 10<sup>−7</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">T m A</span><sup>−1</sup>"],[2,3,11,"intuition","Coulomb’s law with a twist","Put it next to dE = 1/(4πε_0)dq/(r^2) . Both fall off as 1/r^2 and both add up by superposition. The difference is the cross product: a charge’s field points away from it, but a current element’s field wraps around it. And one more gift from the sin θ : a current element produces no field at all along its own line.",["dE = 1/(4πε_0)dq/(r^2)","1/r^2","sin θ"],""],[2,3,12,"story","A constant that stopped being exact","Until 2019, μ_0 was exactly 4π × 10^(−7) by definition. Since the SI units were redefined on 20 May 2019, the elementary charge is fixed exactly instead, and μ_0 has become a measured number. It still agrees with 4π × 10^(−7) to better than one part in a billion, so in every exam you use 4π × 10^(−7) without a second thought.",["μ_0","4π × 10^(−7)","μ_0","4π × 10^(−7)","4π × 10^(−7)"],""],[2,3,13,"sec","A straight wire of any length","Take a straight wire and a point P at perpendicular distance d from its line. Let the foot of the perpendicular be N , and let the two ends of the wire be seen from P at angles α and β on either side of PN :",[],""],[2,3,14,"text","A straight wire of any length","Take a straight wire and a point P at perpendicular distance d from its line. Let the foot of the perpendicular be N , and let the two ends of the wire be seen from P at angles α and β on either side of PN :",["P","d","N","P","α","β","PN"],""],[2,3,15,"text","A straight wire of any length","Adding up all the elements gives",[],""],[2,3,16,"formula","A straight wire of any length","Adding up all the elements gives",["B = (μ_0I)/4πd (sin α + sin β)"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>d</i></span></span> (sin <i>α</i> + sin <i>β</i>)"],[2,3,17,"text","A straight wire of any length","directed by the right-hand grip rule (here, into the page at P). If both ends lie on the same side of N , the contributions partly cancel: use sin α − sin β instead.",["P","N","sin α − sin β"],""],[2,3,18,"text","A straight wire of any length","DerivationAdding up the elements Put N at the origin, with the element dx at height x on the wire. Its distance to P is r = √(d^2 + x^2) , and the sine of the angle between dl and r is d/r . Every element’s field at P points the same way, so magnitudes simply add: B = (μ_0I)/4π ∫ ddx/((d^2 + x^2)^(3/2)) Substitute x = d tan ϕ , so dx = d sec^2 ϕ dϕ and (d^2 + x^2)^(3/2) = d^3 sec^3 ϕ : B = (μ_0I)/4πd ∫_(−β)^α cos ϕ dϕ = (μ_0I)/4πd (sin α + sin β)",["N","dx","x","P","r = √(d^2 + x^2)","dl","r","d/r","P","B = (μ_0I)/4π ∫ ddx/((d^2 + x^2)^(3/2))","x = d tan ϕ","dx = d sec^2 ϕ dϕ"],""],[2,3,18,"formula","A straight wire of any length"," Every element’s field at P points the same way, so magnitudes simply add: B = (μ_0I)/4π ∫ ddx/((d^2 + x^2)^(3/2)) Substitute x = d tan ϕ , so dx = d sec^2 ϕ dϕ and (d^2 + x^2)^(3/2) = d^3 sec^3 ϕ : B = (μ_0I)/4πd ∫_(−β)^α cos ϕ dϕ = (μ_0I)/4πd (sin α + sin β)",["B = (μ_0I)/4π ∫ ddx/((d^2 + x^2)^(3/2))"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> ∫ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i></span><span>(<i>d</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>"],[2,3,18,"formula","A straight wire of any length"," Every element’s field at P points the same way, so magnitudes simply add: B = (μ_0I)/4π ∫ ddx/((d^2 + x^2)^(3/2)) Substitute x = d tan ϕ , so dx = d sec^2 ϕ dϕ and (d^2 + x^2)^(3/2) = d^3 sec^3 ϕ : B = (μ_0I)/4πd ∫_(−β)^α cos ϕ dϕ = (μ_0I)/4πd (sin α + sin β)",["B = (μ_0I)/4πd ∫_(−β)^α cos ϕ dϕ = (μ_0I)/4πd (sin α + sin β)"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>d</i></span></span> ∫<sub>−<i>β</i></sub><sup><i>α</i></sup> cos <i>ϕ</i> <i>d</i><i>ϕ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>d</i></span></span> (sin <i>α</i> + sin <i>β</i>)"],[2,3,21,"text","A straight wire of any length","Three special cases do most of the work in exams:",[],""],[2,3,22,"table","A straight wire of any length","Where is P ? Angles Field Beside an infinitely long wire α = β = 90^∘ (μ_0I)/2πd Level with one end of a semi-infinite wire α = 90^∘, β = 0 (μ_0I)/4πd Anywhere on the line of the wire d → 0 , but sin θ = 0 for every element 0",["P","α = β = 90^∘","(μ_0I)/2πd","α = 90^∘, β = 0","(μ_0I)/4πd","d → 0","sin θ = 0","0"],""],[2,3,23,"q","Ørsted’s needle, quantified · Integer answer","A long straight wire carries a current of 10 A. What is the magnetic field 5.0 cm from it, in microtesla?",[],""],[2,3,23,"sol","Ørsted’s needle, quantified · Integer answer","B = (μ_0I)/2πd = (2 × 10^(−7) × 10)/(0.050) = 4.0 × 10^(−5) T = 40 μ T Answer: 40. That is about the size of Earth’s own field — which is why a compass a few centimetres from an ordinary current swings so visibly.",["B = (μ_0I)/2πd = (2 × 10^(−7) × 10)/(0.050) = 4.0 × 10^(−5) T = 40 μ T"],""],[2,3,24,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","A long straight wire along the z -axis carries a current I in the negative z direction. The magnetic field B at a point with coordinates (x, y) in the z = 0 plane is (μ_0I (yi − xj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2)) · (μ_0I (xi + yj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2)) · (μ_0I (xj − yi))/(2π(x^2 + y^2)) · (μ_0I (xi − yj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))",["z","I","z","B","(x, y)","z = 0","(μ_0I (yi − xj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))","(μ_0I (xi + yj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))","(μ_0I (xj − yi))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))","(μ_0I (xi − yj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))"],""],[2,3,24,"sol","IIT-JEE 2002 · Single correct","The size is μ_0I/2πr with r = √(x^2 + y^2) . The direction is (current direction) × (outward radial direction): B = (μ_0I)/2πr (−k) × (xi + yj)/r = (μ_0I)/(2πr^2) (−x k × i − y k × j) = (μ_0I (yi − xj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2)) using k × i = j and k × j = −i . Answer (A). Quick check: at the point (1, 0) the field is along −j . A current going down the z -axis circulates clockwise when seen from above — and at the point on the +x axis, clockwise means −j . The direction of B is along (current direction) × (unit vector from the wire to the point).",["μ_0I/2πr","r = √(x^2 + y^2)","B = (μ_0I)/2πr (−k) × (xi + yj)/r = (μ_0I)/(2πr^2) (−x k × i − y k × j) = (μ_0I (yi − xj))/(2π(x^2 + y^2))","k × i = j","k × j = −i","(1, 0)","−j","z","+x","−j","B","×"],""],[2,3,25,"lab","Map the field of currents","Two long straight wires run straight through the screen. Each is marked ⊙ (current towards you) or ⊗ (current away). The cyan curves are field lines, drawn so that they crowd together where the field is strong. Try these, in order: At first only wire 1 carries current. Its field lines are perfect circles, bunched near the wire and spreading out — that is the 1/d fall-off. Drag the probe P and read B ; double the distance and watch it halve. Give wire 2 a current in the same direction. A ring marks the spot between them where the field vanishes. Then make one current bigger — which wire does the null point move towards? Reverse one current. Now there is no null point between the wires: the fields add there. Watch the probe’s two coloured arrows: they are the fields of each wire, added tip to tail. Superposition, live.",["⊙","⊗","1/d","B"],""],[2,3,26,"sec","Polygons: one side at a time","A loop made of straight sides is just several finite wires. Find each side’s contribution with (μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β) , check directions with the grip rule, and add.",[],""],[2,3,27,"text","Polygons: one side at a time","A loop made of straight sides is just several finite wires. Find each side’s contribution with (μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β) , check directions with the grip rule, and add.",["(μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β)"],""],[2,3,28,"text","Polygons: one side at a time","For example, at the centre of a square of side a , each side is at distance a/2 and subtends 45^∘ either way:",["a","a/2","45^∘"],""],[2,3,29,"formula","Polygons: one side at a time","For example, at the centre of a square of side a , each side is at distance a/2 and subtends 45^∘ either way:",["B = 4 × (μ_0I)/(4π(a/2)) (sin 45^∘ + sin 45^∘) = (2√2 μ_0I)/πa"],"<i>B</i> = 4 × <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i>(<i>a</i>/2)</span></span> (sin 45<sup> ∘ </sup> + sin 45<sup> ∘ </sup>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2√<span class=\"fx-sq\">2</span> <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span><i>π</i><i>a</i></span></span>"],[2,3,30,"text","Polygons: one side at a time","All four contributions point the same way, because the current circulates the same way round the centre on every side.",[],""],[2,3,31,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2009 · Integer answer","A steady current I goes through a wire loop PQR shaped as a right-angled triangle with PQ = 3x , PR = 4x and QR = 5x . If the magnitude of the magnetic field at P due to this loop is k ((μ_0I)/(48πx)) , find the value of k .",["I","PQ = 3x","PR = 4x","QR = 5x","k ((μ_0I)/(48πx))","k"],""],[2,3,31,"sol","IIT-JEE 2009 · Integer answer","Sides PQ and PR both pass through P, so they contribute nothing there. Only QR counts. The perpendicular distance from P to QR is d = (PQ ⋅ PR)/QR = (3x ⋅ 4x)/(5x) = (12x)/5 The perpendicular from P makes angles with PQ and PR whose cosines are d/PQ = 0.8 and d/PR = 0.6 , so the sines of the angles subtended at P by the two parts of QR are 0.6 and 0.8 : B = (μ_0I)/(4π (12x/5)) (0.6 + 0.8) = (5 × 1.4 μ_0I)/(48πx) = 7 ((μ_0I)/(48πx)) Answer: 7. Two of the three sides pass straight through P. What does a wire contribute at a point on its own line?",["d = (PQ ⋅ PR)/QR = (3x ⋅ 4x)/(5x) = (12x)/5","d/PQ = 0.8","d/PR = 0.6","0.6","0.8","B = (μ_0I)/(4π (12x/5)) (0.6 + 0.8) = (5 × 1.4 μ_0I)/(48πx) = 7 ((μ_0I)/(48πx))"],""],[2,3,32,"pyq","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","What is the magnitude of the magnetic field at the centre of an equilateral triangular loop of side 1 m carrying a current of 10 A? (Take μ_0 = 4π × 10^(−7) N A⁻².) 18 μ T · 9 μ T · 3 μ T · 1 μ T",["μ_0 = 4π × 10^(−7)","μ","μ","μ","μ"],""],[2,3,32,"sol","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","The centre is at distance d = a/(2√3) from each side, and each side subtends 60^∘ either way: B = 3 × (μ_0I)/4πd (sin 60^∘ + sin 60^∘) = 3 × (10^(−7) × 10 × 2√3)/1 × √3 = 18 × 10^(−6) T Answer (A): 18 μ T.",["d = a/(2√3)","60^∘","B = 3 × (μ_0I)/4πd (sin 60^∘ + sin 60^∘) = 3 × (10^(−7) × 10 × 2√3)/1 × √3 = 18 × 10^(−6) T","μ"],""],[2,3,33,"pyq","AIEEE 2011 · Single correct","A current I flows along an infinitely long wire whose cross-section is a thin semicircular ring of radius R (like a long pipe cut in half lengthwise). The magnitude of the magnetic field on its axis is (μ_0I)/2πR · (μ_0I)/4πR · (μ_0I)/(π^2R) · (μ_0I)/(2π^2R)",["I","R","(μ_0I)/2πR","(μ_0I)/4πR","(μ_0I)/(π^2R)","(μ_0I)/(2π^2R)"],""],[2,3,33,"sol","AIEEE 2011 · Single correct","Slice the half-pipe into long thin strips. The strip at angle θ (measured round the semicircle) has width R dθ , so it carries dI = I/π dθ and acts as an infinite straight wire at distance R : dB = (μ_0 dI)/2πR = (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) dθ Pair each strip with its mirror image across the vertical line through O: their field components perpendicular to the diameter cancel, and the components along the diameter add. Each strip’s field is perpendicular to its own radius, so the surviving component is dB sin θ : B = ∫_0^π (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) sin θ dθ = (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) × 2 = (μ_0I)/(π^2R) Answer (C). Slice the half-pipe into thin straight strips, each an infinite wire. By symmetry, only one component of their fields survives.",["θ","R dθ","dI = I/π dθ","R","dB = (μ_0 dI)/2πR = (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) dθ","dB sin θ","B = ∫_0^π (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) sin θ dθ = (μ_0I)/(2π^2R) × 2 = (μ_0I)/(π^2R)"],""],[2,3,34,"sec","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","At the centre of a circular arc, the geometry could not be friendlier. Every element is at the same distance R , and every element is perpendicular to the line joining it to the centre (sin θ = 1). So the elements’ fields all point the same way and simply add:",[],""],[2,3,35,"text","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","At the centre of a circular arc, the geometry could not be friendlier. Every element is at the same distance R , and every element is perpendicular to the line joining it to the centre (sin θ = 1). So the elements’ fields all point the same way and simply add:",["R","sin θ = 1"],""],[2,3,36,"formula","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","At the centre of a circular arc, the geometry could not be friendlier. Every element is at the same distance R , and every element is perpendicular to the line joining it to the centre (sin θ = 1). So the elements’ fields all point the same way and simply add:",["B = (μ_0I)/(4πR^2) × (arc length) = (μ_0I θ)/4πR"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> × (<span class=\"fx-t\">arc length</span>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i> <i>θ</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>R</i></span></span>"],[2,3,37,"text","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","with θ the angle of the arc in radians. The cases you will meet over and over:",["θ"],""],[2,3,38,"formula","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","with θ the angle of the arc in radians. The cases you will meet over and over:",["full loop: (μ_0I)/(2R), N turns: (μ_0NI)/(2R), semicircle: (μ_0I)/(4R), quarter circle: (μ_0I)/(8R)"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">full loop: </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>2<i>R</i></span></span>, <i>N</i><span class=\"fx-t\"> turns: </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>N</i><i>I</i></span><span>2<i>R</i></span></span>, <span class=\"fx-t\">semicircle: </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>4<i>R</i></span></span>, <span class=\"fx-t\">quarter circle: </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>8<i>R</i></span></span>"],[2,3,39,"text","Arcs and loops: the centre of a circle","For the direction, curl the fingers of your right hand along the current round the loop: your thumb points along B at the centre. Anticlockwise on the page gives a field out of the page.",["B"],""],[2,3,40,"trap","Straight lines aimed at the centre","In loops built from arcs and radial straight pieces (sector shapes), the straight pieces point straight at the centre, so they contribute zero there. Only the arcs count. Many students waste minutes calculating these.",[],""],[2,3,41,"q","The hairpin · Single correct","A long wire is bent into a hairpin: two long parallel straight sections joined by a semicircle of radius R , as shown. It carries current I . What is the magnetic field at O, the centre of the semicircle? (μ_0I)/(4R) · (μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 2/π) · (μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 1/π) · (μ_0I)/(2R) (1 + 1/π)",["R","I","(μ_0I)/(4R)","(μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 2/π)","(μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 1/π)","(μ_0I)/(2R) (1 + 1/π)"],""],[2,3,41,"sol","The hairpin · Single correct","Three pieces, and all three fields at O point the same way (into the page here — check with your grip): the semicircle: (μ_0I)/(4R) ; each straight section is semi-infinite, and O is level with its end at distance R : (μ_0I)/4πR each. B = (μ_0I)/(4R) + 2 × (μ_0I)/4πR = (μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 2/π) Answer (B).",["(μ_0I)/(4R)","R","(μ_0I)/4πR","B = (μ_0I)/(4R) + 2 × (μ_0I)/4πR = (μ_0I)/(4R) (1 + 2/π)"],""],[2,3,42,"pyq","AIEEE 2004 · Single correct","A long wire carries a steady current. It is bent into a circle of one turn, and the magnetic field at the centre of the coil is B . It is then bent into a circular coil of n turns. The magnetic field at the centre of the coil will be nB · n^2B · 2nB · 2n^2B",["B","n","nB","n^2B","2nB","2n^2B"],""],[2,3,42,"sol","AIEEE 2004 · Single correct","The wire has a fixed length L . One turn: R = L/2π . With n turns, each turn has radius R/n . B^′ = (μ_0nI)/(2(R/n)) = n^2 (μ_0I)/(2R) = n^2B Answer (B). One factor of n from the extra turns, another from each turn being smaller.",["L","R = L/2π","n","R/n","B^′ = (μ_0nI)/(2(R/n)) = n^2 (μ_0I)/(2R) = n^2B","n"],""],[2,3,43,"sec","On the axis of a loop","Away from the centre, along the axis, the elements’ fields no longer line up. Each element’s dB is tilted; but pair it with the element on the opposite side of the loop and the sideways parts cancel. Only the parts along the axis survive:",[],""],[2,3,44,"text","On the axis of a loop","Away from the centre, along the axis, the elements’ fields no longer line up. Each element’s dB is tilted; but pair it with the element on the opposite side of the loop and the sideways parts cancel. Only the parts along the axis survive:",["dB"],""],[2,3,45,"formula","On the axis of a loop","Away from the centre, along the axis, the elements’ fields no longer line up. Each element’s dB is tilted; but pair it with the element on the opposite side of the loop and the sideways parts cancel. Only the parts along the axis survive:",["B(x) = (μ_0IR^2)/(2 (R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))"],"<i>B</i>(<i>x</i>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2 (<i>R</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>"],[2,3,46,"text","On the axis of a loop","At x = 0 this gives back μ_0I/2R . Far away (x ≫ R) it becomes",["x = 0","μ_0I/2R","x ≫ R"],""],[2,3,47,"formula","On the axis of a loop","At x = 0 this gives back μ_0I/2R . Far away (x ≫ R) it becomes",["B ≈ (μ_0IR^2)/(2x^3) = μ_0/4π (2 (IπR^2))/(x^3)"],"<i>B</i> ≈ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2<i>x</i><sup>3</sup></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2 (<i>I</i><i>π</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup>)</span><span><i>x</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>"],[2,3,48,"text","On the axis of a loop","The combination IπR^2 — current times area — will turn out to be the loop’s magnetic moment in Lesson 6. Hold on to the 1/x^3 : it is the same fall-off as an electric dipole’s field.",["IπR^2","1/x^3"],""],[2,3,49,"text","On the axis of a loop","DerivationThe field on the axis An element dl on the loop is at distance r = √(R^2 + x^2) from P , and dl is perpendicular to r , so dB = (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) . This dB is perpendicular to r , so it makes an angle with the axis whose cosine is R/r . Only that part survives the pairing: B = ∮ (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) ⋅ R/r = (μ_0IR)/(4πr^3) (2πR) = (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))",["dl","r = √(R^2 + x^2)","P","dl","r","dB = (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2)","dB","r","R/r","B = ∮ (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) ⋅ R/r = (μ_0IR)/(4πr^3) (2πR) = (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))"],""],[2,3,49,"formula","On the axis of a loop","erpendicular to r , so dB = (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) . This dB is perpendicular to r , so it makes an angle with the axis whose cosine is R/r . Only that part survives the pairing: B = ∮ (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) ⋅ R/r = (μ_0IR)/(4πr^3) (2πR) = (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))",["B = ∮ (μ_0I dl)/(4πr^2) ⋅ R/r = (μ_0IR)/(4πr^3) (2πR) = (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))"],"<i>B</i> = ∮ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i> <i>d</i><i>l</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><i>R</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span> (2<i>π</i><i>R</i>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2(<i>R</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>"],[2,3,51,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","A long insulated copper wire is closely wound as a flat spiral of N turns. The spiral has inner radius a and outer radius b , lies in the x – y plane, and carries a steady current I . The z -component of the magnetic field at the centre of the spiral is (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ln b/a · (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) · (μ_0NI)/(2b) · (μ_0NI (b − a))/(2ab)",["N","a","b","x","y","I","z","(μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ln b/a","(μ_0NI)/(2(b − a))","(μ_0NI)/(2b)","(μ_0NI (b − a))/(2ab)"],""],[2,3,51,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","The N turns are spread evenly over the width b − a , so a thin ring between r and r + dr contains dN = N/(b − a) dr turns. Each acts as a circular loop at the centre: dB = (μ_0I dN)/(2r) = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) dr/r B = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ∫_a^b dr/r = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ln b/a Answer (A). Sanity check: if b → a , ln(b/a) ≈ (b − a)/a and the answer becomes μ_0NI/2a — N turns all of radius a . Treat the spiral as many concentric rings. How many turns lie between radius r and r + dr ?",["N","b − a","r","r + dr","dN = N/(b − a) dr","dB = (μ_0I dN)/(2r) = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) dr/r","B = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ∫_a^b dr/r = (μ_0NI)/(2(b − a)) ln b/a","b → a","ln(b/a) ≈ (b − a)/a","μ_0NI/2a","N","a"],""],[2,3,52,"recap","Recap","Biot–Savart: dB = μ_0/4π(I dl × r)/(r^2) ; μ_0/4π = 10^(−7) T m A⁻¹. No field along an element’s own line. Straight wire: (μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β) ; infinite (μ_0I)/2πd ; level with the end of a semi-infinite wire (μ_0I)/4πd . Arc at its centre: (μ_0Iθ)/4πR ; loop (μ_0I)/(2R) ; N turns (μ_0NI)/(2R) . Radial straight pieces add nothing at the centre. Loop axis: (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2)) , falling as 1/x^3 far away. Directions: right-hand grip. Always check that the pieces really point the same way before adding magnitudes.",["dB = μ_0/4π(I dl × r)/(r^2)","μ_0/4π = 10^(−7)","(μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β)","(μ_0I)/2πd","(μ_0I)/4πd","(μ_0Iθ)/4πR","(μ_0I)/(2R)","N","(μ_0NI)/(2R)","(μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2))","1/x^3"],""],[2,4,0,"lesson","Ampère’s Law, Solenoids & Toroids","Gauss’s law let you skip Coulomb’s integrals whenever a problem was symmetric. Ampère’s law does the same for magnetism — and hands you the fields inside thick wires, solenoids and toroids in a single line.",[],""],[2,4,1,"sec","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Biot–Savart always works, but it can mean a hard integral. For electric fields you had a shortcut for symmetric problems: Gauss’s law. Magnetism has its own, and it is just as powerful.",[],""],[2,4,2,"text","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Biot–Savart always works, but it can mean a hard integral. For electric fields you had a shortcut for symmetric problems: Gauss’s law. Magnetism has its own, and it is just as powerful.",[],""],[2,4,3,"text","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Draw any closed loop in space (an Amperian loop). Walk round it, adding up the component of B along each little step dl . Ampère’s circuital law says the total depends only on the current threading through the loop:",["B","dl"],""],[2,4,4,"formula","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Draw any closed loop in space (an Amperian loop). Walk round it, adding up the component of B along each little step dl . Ampère’s circuital law says the total depends only on the current threading through the loop:",["∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0 I_enc"],"∮ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> ⋅ <i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>l</i></span> = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub> <i>I</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">enc</span></sub>"],[2,4,5,"text","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Signs. Curl the fingers of your right hand along the direction you walk round the loop. Currents along your thumb count as positive, currents the other way as negative. For the loop above (anticlockwise, thumb out of the page): ∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0(I_1 − I_2) . The current I_3 does not appear at all.",["∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0(I_1 − I_2)","I_3"],""],[2,4,6,"trap","The field in the integral is the total field","I_3 is missing from the right-hand side, but its field is certainly there on the loop! Ampère’s law uses the total B . The outside current simply contributes zero to the integral — its field adds as much going one way round as it takes away coming back. Never conclude “ I_enc = 0 , so B = 0 everywhere on the loop” unless symmetry tells you B is the same all the way round.",["I_3","B","I_enc = 0","B = 0","B"],""],[2,4,7,"text","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","First test: the straight wire. By symmetry, B has the same size all round a circle of radius r centred on the wire, and it points along the circle. So the integral is just B × 2πr :",["B","r","B × 2πr"],""],[2,4,8,"formula","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","First test: the straight wire. By symmetry, B has the same size all round a circle of radius r centred on the wire, and it points along the circle. So the integral is just B × 2πr :",["B (2πr) = μ_0I ⟹ B = (μ_0I)/2πr"],"<i>B</i> (2<i>π</i><i>r</i>) = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i> ⟹ <i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>r</i></span></span>"],[2,4,9,"text","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","The Biot–Savart answer, in one line.",[],""],[2,4,10,"table","A shortcut, just like Gauss’s law","Electrostatics Magnetostatics Coulomb’s law: always works, may need an integral Biot–Savart: always works, may need an integral Gauss: ∮ E ⋅ dA = q_enc/ε_0 Ampère: ∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0I_enc useful for spheres, cylinders, planes useful for long wires, solenoids, toroids, sheets",["∮ E ⋅ dA = q_enc/ε_0","∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0I_enc"],""],[2,4,11,"sec","Inside a thick wire","A long solid cylinder of radius R carries current I spread evenly over its cross-section. Take a circle of radius r centred on the axis.",[],""],[2,4,12,"text","Inside a thick wire","A long solid cylinder of radius R carries current I spread evenly over its cross-section. Take a circle of radius r centred on the axis.",["R","I","r"],""],[2,4,13,"text","Inside a thick wire","Outside (r ≥ R), the circle encloses all the current: B = (μ_0I)/2πr , as if all the current ran along the axis.",["r ≥ R","B = (μ_0I)/2πr"],""],[2,4,14,"text","Inside a thick wire","Inside (r < R), it encloses only the fraction r^2/R^2 :",["r < R","r^2/R^2"],""],[2,4,15,"formula","Inside a thick wire","Inside (r < R), it encloses only the fraction r^2/R^2 :",["B (2πr) = μ_0I (r^2)/(R^2) ⟹ B = (μ_0I r)/(2πR^2)"],"<i>B</i> (2<i>π</i><i>r</i>) = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>r</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> ⟹ <i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i> <i>r</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>"],[2,4,16,"text","Inside a thick wire","The field grows linearly from zero on the axis to its maximum at the surface, then falls off as 1/r .",["1/r"],""],[2,4,17,"text","Inside a thick wire","A hollow pipe (current only in a thin wall) is even simpler. Any circle inside the pipe encloses no current, and by symmetry B is the same all the way round it — so B = 0 everywhere inside.",["B","B = 0"],""],[2,4,18,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1993 · Single correct","A current I flows along the length of an infinitely long, straight, thin-walled pipe. Then the magnetic field at all points inside the pipe is the same, but not zero · the magnetic field is zero only on the axis of the pipe · the magnetic field is different at different points inside the pipe · the magnetic field at any point inside the pipe is zero",["I"],""],[2,4,18,"sol","IIT-JEE 1993 · Single correct","Take an Amperian circle of any radius r smaller than the pipe’s, centred on the axis. The current runs entirely in the wall, so it encloses none: B (2πr) = 0 ⟹ B = 0 The symmetry argument (same B all round the circle, pointing along it) holds for every circle inside, so the field is zero at every point inside, not just on the axis. Answer (D). Biot–Savart agrees: think of the pipe as many thin straight strips round a circle. Inside, the fields of strips on opposite sides cancel exactly.",["r","B (2πr) = 0 ⟹ B = 0","B"],""],[2,4,19,"q","Half-way in, twice as far out · Single correct","A long solid wire of radius R carries a current spread uniformly over its cross-section. The field at distance R/2 from the axis is B_1 ; at distance 2R it is B_2 . Then B_1/B_2 is 1/4 · 1/2 · 1 · 4",["R","R/2","B_1","2R","B_2","B_1/B_2","1/4","1/2","1","4"],""],[2,4,19,"sol","Half-way in, twice as far out · Single correct","B_1 = (μ_0I (R/2))/(2πR^2) = (μ_0I)/4πR, B_2 = (μ_0I)/(2π(2R)) = (μ_0I)/4πR Answer (C): they are equal. Half-way in, the field has fallen to half its surface value (linear inside); twice as far out, it has also fallen to half (as 1/r outside).",["B_1 = (μ_0I (R/2))/(2πR^2) = (μ_0I)/4πR, B_2 = (μ_0I)/(2π(2R)) = (μ_0I)/4πR","1/r"],""],[2,4,20,"sec","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","Wind a wire into a long, tightly packed coil — a solenoid — with n turns per unit length. Close to the wire, each turn’s field circles it; but inside the coil, the fields of all the turns add up along the axis, and outside they very nearly cancel:",[],""],[2,4,21,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","Wind a wire into a long, tightly packed coil — a solenoid — with n turns per unit length. Close to the wire, each turn’s field circles it; but inside the coil, the fields of all the turns add up along the axis, and outside they very nearly cancel:",["n"],""],[2,4,22,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","Use the rectangle shown. Along the inside edge, B is parallel to the path and uniform: contribution BL . Along the outside edge B ≈ 0 , and along the short ends B is perpendicular to the path (inside) or zero (outside). The rectangle is threaded by nL turns, each carrying I :",["B","BL","B ≈ 0","B","nL","I"],""],[2,4,23,"formula","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","e shown. Along the inside edge, B is parallel to the path and uniform: contribution BL . Along the outside edge B ≈ 0 , and along the short ends B is perpendicular to the path (inside) or zero (outside). The rectangle is threaded by nL turns, each carrying I :",["BL = μ_0 (nL) I ⟹ B = μ_0nI"],"<i>B</i><i>L</i> = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub> (<i>n</i><i>L</i>) <i>I</i> ⟹ <i>B</i> = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>n</i><i>I</i>"],[2,4,24,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","That is remarkable. The answer does not depend on where you are inside (centre or near the wall), nor on the radius of the coil. A long solenoid is a bottle of uniform field — which is why MRI scanners are giant solenoids.",[],""],[2,4,25,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","At the ends the field is weaker. On the axis, exactly at one end of a long solenoid,",[],""],[2,4,26,"formula","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","At the ends the field is weaker. On the axis, exactly at one end of a long solenoid,",["B_end = (μ_0nI)/2"],"<i>B</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">end</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>n</i><i>I</i></span><span>2</span></span>"],[2,4,27,"intuition","Why exactly half?","Put two long solenoids end to end: together they make one long solenoid, with μ_0nI at the joint. By symmetry, each half supplies the same amount there. So each, on its own, gives μ_0nI/2 at its end.",["μ_0nI","μ_0nI/2"],""],[2,4,28,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","For a solenoid of finite length, at a point on the axis inside it, the result from Biot–Savart is",[],""],[2,4,29,"formula","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","For a solenoid of finite length, at a point on the axis inside it, the result from Biot–Savart is",["B = (μ_0nI)/2 (cos θ_1 + cos θ_2)"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>n</i><i>I</i></span><span>2</span></span> (cos <i>θ</i><sub>1</sub> + cos <i>θ</i><sub>2</sub>)"],[2,4,30,"text","The solenoid: a bottle of uniform field","where θ_1 and θ_2 are the angles between the axis and the lines from the point to the rims of the two ends. Deep inside a long coil both angles are tiny: B → μ_0nI . At one end, one angle is 90^∘ : B → μ_0nI/2 .",["θ_1","θ_2","B → μ_0nI","90^∘","B → μ_0nI/2"],""],[2,4,31,"lab","Stretch the solenoid","The top picture is a solenoid with its field lines (a cross-sectional sketch). The graph underneath is the exact field along the axis of a real cylindrical solenoid, as a fraction of μ_0nI . Try these, in order: Start with a short, fat coil — length about equal to its diameter. The field peaks in the middle and is nowhere near uniform. Stretch it. The graph flattens into a plateau at exactly μ_0nI : the bottle of uniform field. Drag the probe to the end of a long coil. Read the field: half. Push the probe outside the coil and watch the field die away. Double the turns per metre, or the current. The plateau doubles too: B = μ_0nI .",["μ_0nI","μ_0nI","B = μ_0nI"],""],[2,4,32,"q","Inside a lab solenoid · Numerical answer","A solenoid 50 cm long has 500 turns and carries 2.0 A. What is the magnetic field near its centre, in millitesla? (Treat it as long.)",[],""],[2,4,32,"sol","Inside a lab solenoid · Numerical answer","n = 500/(0.50 m) = 1000 m ^(−1), B = μ_0nI = 4π × 10^(−7) × 1000 × 2.0 = 2.51 × 10^(−3) T Answer: 2.51 mT — about fifty times Earth’s field, from a modest bench coil.",["n = 500/(0.50 m) = 1000 m ^(−1), B = μ_0nI = 4π × 10^(−7) × 1000 × 2.0 = 2.51 × 10^(−3) T"],""],[2,4,33,"sec","The toroid: a solenoid bent into a doughnut","Bend a solenoid round until its ends meet, and you have a toroid. Now there are no ends for the field to leak out of.",[],""],[2,4,34,"text","The toroid: a solenoid bent into a doughnut","Bend a solenoid round until its ends meet, and you have a toroid. Now there are no ends for the field to leak out of.",[],""],[2,4,35,"text","The toroid: a solenoid bent into a doughnut","With N turns in all, an Amperian circle of radius r inside the windings is threaded N times by the current:",["N","r","N"],""],[2,4,36,"formula","The toroid: a solenoid bent into a doughnut","With N turns in all, an Amperian circle of radius r inside the windings is threaded N times by the current:",["B (2πr) = μ_0NI ⟹ B = (μ_0NI)/2πr"],"<i>B</i> (2<i>π</i><i>r</i>) = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>N</i><i>I</i> ⟹ <i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>N</i><i>I</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>r</i></span></span>"],[2,4,37,"text","The toroid: a solenoid bent into a doughnut","A circle in the central hole encloses no current; a circle outside the whole toroid is threaded by each turn twice, once going up and once coming down — net zero. The field is completely trapped inside the windings.",[],""],[2,4,38,"q","Field in a toroid · Integer answer","A toroid of mean radius 10 cm has 500 turns and carries a current of 2.0 A. What is the field on its mean circle, in millitesla? (The cross-section is small compared with the mean radius.)",[],""],[2,4,38,"sol","Field in a toroid · Integer answer","B = (μ_0NI)/2πr = (2 × 10^(−7) × 500 × 2.0)/(0.10) = 2.0 × 10^(−3) T Answer: 2. Notice N/2πr is just the number of turns per unit length round the ring, so this is μ_0nI again — a toroid is a solenoid with no ends.",["B = (μ_0NI)/2πr = (2 × 10^(−7) × 500 × 2.0)/(0.10) = 2.0 × 10^(−3) T","N/2πr","μ_0nI"],""],[2,4,39,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","A steady current I flows along an infinitely long hollow cylindrical conductor of radius R . This cylinder is placed coaxially inside an infinite solenoid of radius 2R . The solenoid has n turns per unit length and carries a steady current I . Consider a point P at distance r from the common axis. The correct statement(s) is/are In the region 0 < r < R , the magnetic field is non-zero · In the region R < r < 2R , the magnetic field is along the common axis · In the region R < r < 2R , the magnetic field is tangential to the circle of radius r centred on the axis · In the region r > 2R , the magnetic field is non-zero",["I","R","2R","n","I","r","0 < r < R","R < r < 2R","R < r < 2R","r","r > 2R"],""],[2,4,39,"sol","JEE Advanced 2013 · One or more correct","Treat the two sources separately and add. Region Hollow cylinder Solenoid Total r < R 0 (no current enclosed) μ_0nI , along the axis axial, non-zero R < r < 2R μ_0I/2πr , circling the axis μ_0nI , along the axis a mixture of both directions r > 2R μ_0I/2πr , circling the axis 0 circling, non-zero (A) Inside the cylinder the solenoid’s field is still there. Correct. (B), (C) Between them, the total field has both an axial and a circular part — it is neither purely along the axis nor purely tangential. The field lines are helices. Both wrong. (D) Outside the solenoid its field vanishes, but the cylinder’s does not. Correct. Answer (A), (D). Find the field of each object separately in each region, then add. One is always axial, the other always circles the axis.",["r < R","μ_0nI","R < r < 2R","μ_0I/2πr","μ_0nI","r > 2R","μ_0I/2πr"],""],[2,4,40,"sec","A flat sheet of current","One more shape completes the set. An infinite flat sheet carrying a surface current K (amperes per metre of width) makes a uniform field",[],""],[2,4,41,"text","A flat sheet of current","One more shape completes the set. An infinite flat sheet carrying a surface current K (amperes per metre of width) makes a uniform field",["K"],""],[2,4,42,"formula","A flat sheet of current","One more shape completes the set. An infinite flat sheet carrying a surface current K (amperes per metre of width) makes a uniform field",["B = (μ_0K)/2"],"<i>B</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>K</i></span><span>2</span></span>"],[2,4,43,"text","A flat sheet of current","on both sides, parallel to the sheet and perpendicular to the current, pointing in opposite directions on the two sides. (Use a rectangular Amperian loop that pierces the sheet.) It is the magnetic twin of the charged sheet’s E = σ/2ε_0 .",["E = σ/2ε_0"],""],[2,4,44,"text","A flat sheet of current","Two parallel sheets with opposite currents give μ_0K between them and zero outside — and a solenoid is exactly that, rolled up, with K = nI . Everything fits.",["μ_0K","K = nI"],""],[2,4,45,"recap","Recap","Ampère: ∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0I_enc . Always true; useful only when symmetry makes B constant along the loop. Signs by the right-hand rule. Solid wire: B = (μ_0Ir)/(2πR^2) inside, (μ_0I)/2πr outside, maximum at the surface. Hollow pipe: zero inside. Long solenoid: B = μ_0nI , uniform inside, zero outside; μ_0nI/2 at an end. Finite: (μ_0nI)/2(cos θ_1 + cos θ_2) . Toroid: B = (μ_0NI)/2πr inside the windings, zero in the hole and outside. Current sheet: B = μ_0K/2 on each side.",["∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0I_enc","B","B = (μ_0Ir)/(2πR^2)","(μ_0I)/2πr","B = μ_0nI","μ_0nI/2","(μ_0nI)/2(cos θ_1 + cos θ_2)","B = (μ_0NI)/2πr","B = μ_0K/2"],""],[2,5,0,"lesson","Forces on Wires & Between Currents","Put a current in a magnetic field and the whole wire gets shoved sideways — the push behind every electric motor and loudspeaker. Bend the wire any way you like and only its two ends matter; and two currents side by side pull together, which is exactly how the ampere was once defined.",[],""],[2,5,1,"sec","From one charge to a whole wire","A current is just a crowd of charges drifting along a wire. Each one feels q v_d × B , and the force is passed on to the wire itself. Add them up over a length L of wire with cross-section A and n carriers per unit volume:",[],""],[2,5,2,"text","From one charge to a whole wire","A current is just a crowd of charges drifting along a wire. Each one feels q v_d × B , and the force is passed on to the wire itself. Add them up over a length L of wire with cross-section A and n carriers per unit volume:",["q v_d × B","L","A","n"],""],[2,5,3,"formula","From one charge to a whole wire","A current is just a crowd of charges drifting along a wire. Each one feels q v_d × B , and the force is passed on to the wire itself. Add them up over a length L of wire with cross-section A and n carriers per unit volume:",["F = (nAL) q v_d × B = (nqv_dA) L × B = I L × B"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = (<i>n</i><i>A</i><i>L</i>) <i>q</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>v</i></span><sub><i>d</i></sub> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = (<i>n</i><i>q</i><i>v</i><sub><i>d</i></sub><i>A</i>) <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>L</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = <i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>L</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>"],[2,5,4,"text","From one charge to a whole wire","where L points along the current. So",["L"],""],[2,5,5,"formula","From one charge to a whole wire","where L points along the current. So",["F = I L × B, F = BIL sin θ"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = <i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>L</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>, <i>F</i> = <i>B</i><i>I</i><i>L</i> sin <i>θ</i>"],[2,5,6,"text","From one charge to a whole wire","Same right-hand rule as before, with the current in place of the velocity: fingers along I , curl to B , thumb along F . In the figure, x × z = −y : the wire is pushed down the page.",["I","B","F","x × z = −y"],""],[2,5,7,"intuition","Fleming’s left-hand rule is the same rule","If you learnt Fleming’s left-hand rule at school (First finger: Field, seCond finger: Current, thuMb: Motion), it gives exactly the same direction. Use whichever you trust — but the cross product also handles every angle and every 3D question without a new rule.",[],""],[2,5,8,"q","A wire at an angle · Numerical answer","A straight wire 50 cm long carries a current of 4.0 A. It lies at 30^∘ to a uniform magnetic field of 0.20 T. What is the magnitude of the force on it, in newtons?",["30^∘"],""],[2,5,8,"sol","A wire at an angle · Numerical answer","F = BIL sin θ = 0.20 × 4.0 × 0.50 × sin 30^∘ = 0.20 N Answer: 0.2 N, perpendicular to both the wire and the field. Lay the wire along the field and the force vanishes; turn it square to the field and it doubles to 0.40 N.",["F = BIL sin θ = 0.20 × 4.0 × 0.50 × sin 30^∘ = 0.20 N"],""],[2,5,9,"sec","Bent wires: only the ends matter","For a wire of any shape in a uniform field, add the forces on all its little pieces:",[],""],[2,5,10,"text","Bent wires: only the ends matter","For a wire of any shape in a uniform field, add the forces on all its little pieces:",[],""],[2,5,11,"formula","Bent wires: only the ends matter","For a wire of any shape in a uniform field, add the forces on all its little pieces:",["F = I (∫ dl) × B = I L_AB × B"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>F</i></span> = <i>I</i> (∫ <i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>l</i></span>) × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = <i>I</i> <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>L</i></span><sub><i>A</i><i>B</i></sub> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>"],[2,5,12,"text","Bent wires: only the ends matter","The sum of all the little steps dl along the wire is simply the straight arrow L_AB from its start to its end. Any wire between A and B feels the same force as a straight wire from A to B.",["dl","L_AB"],""],[2,5,13,"text","Bent wires: only the ends matter","Close the wire into a loop and A meets B: L_AB = 0 . The net force on any closed current loop in a uniform field is zero. (It can still feel a torque — that is Lesson 6.)",["L_AB = 0"],""],[2,5,14,"q","The semicircle · Single correct","A wire bent into a semicircle of radius R carries current I and lies in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B . The magnitude of the net magnetic force on it is πIRB · 2IRB · IRB · zero",["R","I","B","πIRB","2IRB","IRB"],""],[2,5,14,"sol","The semicircle · Single correct","Replace the semicircle by the straight diameter joining its ends: length 2R , perpendicular to B . F = BI(2R) = 2IRB Answer (B). Option (A) uses the arc length πR — but the pieces of the arc push in different directions, and their sideways parts cancel.",["2R","B","F = BI(2R) = 2IRB","πR"],""],[2,5,15,"pyq","JEE Main 2014 · Numerical answer","A conductor lies along the z -axis for −1.5 ≤ z < 1.5 m and carries a fixed current of 10.0 A in the −z direction. The magnetic field is B = 3.0 × 10^(−4) e^(−0.2x) y T. Find the power (in watts) required to move the conductor at constant speed to x = 2.0 m, y = 0 , in 5 × 10^(−3) s. Assume the motion is parallel to the x -axis.",["z","−1.5 ≤ z < 1.5","−z","B = 3.0 × 10^(−4) e^(−0.2x) y","x = 2.0","y = 0","5 × 10^(−3)","x"],""],[2,5,15,"sol","JEE Main 2014 · Numerical answer","Force. L = 3.0 (−z) m, so F = IL × B = 10 × 3.0 × 3.0 × 10^(−4)e^(−0.2x) (−z × y) = 9.0 × 10^(−3)e^(−0.2x) x N Work. To move the conductor at constant speed, the agent must balance this force all the way from x = 0 to x = 2 : ∣W∣ = ∫_0^2 9.0 × 10^(−3)e^(−0.2x) dx = (9.0 × 10^(−3))/0.2 (1 − e^(−0.4)) = 0.045 × 0.3297 ≈ 1.48 × 10^(−2) J Power. P = (∣W∣)/t = (1.48 × 10^(−2))/(5 × 10^(−3)) ≈ 2.97 W Answer: 2.97 W. (Strictly, the magnetic force here points along +x , so the agent is holding the rod back; the question asks for the magnitude of the rate of working.) The field gets weaker with x , so the force is not constant. Integrate F(x) dx to get the work, then divide by the time.",["L = 3.0 (−z)","F = IL × B = 10 × 3.0 × 3.0 × 10^(−4)e^(−0.2x) (−z × y) = 9.0 × 10^(−3)e^(−0.2x) x N","x = 0","x = 2","∣W∣ = ∫_0^2 9.0 × 10^(−3)e^(−0.2x) dx = (9.0 × 10^(−3))/0.2 (1 − e^(−0.4)) = 0.045 × 0.3297 ≈ 1.48 × 10^(−2) J","P = (∣W∣)/t = (1.48 × 10^(−2))/(5 × 10^(−3)) ≈ 2.97 W","+x","x","F(x) dx"],""],[2,5,16,"sec","Wires that hold themselves up","A horizontal rod of mass m and length L lies across a horizontal field B . Pass the right current in the right direction and the magnetic force points straight up. It floats when",[],""],[2,5,17,"text","Wires that hold themselves up","A horizontal rod of mass m and length L lies across a horizontal field B . Pass the right current in the right direction and the magnetic force points straight up. It floats when",["m","L","B"],""],[2,5,18,"formula","Wires that hold themselves up","A horizontal rod of mass m and length L lies across a horizontal field B . Pass the right current in the right direction and the magnetic force points straight up. It floats when",["BIL = mg ⟹ I = mg/BL"],"<i>B</i><i>I</i><i>L</i> = <i>m</i><i>g</i> ⟹ <i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><i>g</i></span><span><i>B</i><i>L</i></span></span>"],[2,5,19,"text","Wires that hold themselves up","Pause & predictA 20 g rod, 50 cm long, sits in a horizontal field of 0.40 T. Roughly what current makes it float? I = mg/BL = (0.020 × 9.8)/(0.40 × 0.50) ≈ 0.98 A About one ampere — no more than a phone charger supplies. Magnetic forces on currents are not small.",["I = mg/BL = (0.020 × 9.8)/(0.40 × 0.50) ≈ 0.98 A"],""],[2,5,19,"formula","Wires that hold themselves up","se & predictA 20 g rod, 50 cm long, sits in a horizontal field of 0.40 T. Roughly what current makes it float? I = mg/BL = (0.020 × 9.8)/(0.40 × 0.50) ≈ 0.98 A About one ampere — no more than a phone charger supplies. Magnetic forces on currents are not small.",["I = mg/BL = (0.020 × 9.8)/(0.40 × 0.50) ≈ 0.98 A"],"<i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><i>g</i></span><span><i>B</i><i>L</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>0.020 × 9.8</span><span>0.40 × 0.50</span></span> ≈ 0.98 <span class=\"fx-t\">A</span>"],[2,5,21,"q","The wire that becomes a circle · Single correct","A thin flexible wire of length L has its two ends fixed to two points very close together. It carries a current I clockwise, and the whole set-up is placed in a uniform magnetic field B pointing into the page. The wire is pulled into a circle. What is the tension in the wire? IBL · IBL/π · IBL/2π · IBL/4π",["L","I","B","IBL","IBL/π","IBL/2π","IBL/4π"],""],[2,5,21,"sol","The wire that becomes a circle · Single correct","Why a circle. At the top of the loop the current flows to the right (clockwise), and x × (−z) = +y : the force points outward. The same is true everywhere — every piece is pushed outward, so the wire balloons into a circle of radius R = L/2π . Balance a small arc. Take an arc subtending 2θ at the centre, of length 2Rθ . The tensions T at its two ends each tilt inward by θ , giving an inward resultant 2T sin θ ≈ 2Tθ . This balances the outward magnetic force BI(2Rθ) : 2Tθ = 2BIRθ ⟹ T = BIR = IBL/2π Answer (C). It is the same maths as a spinning ring or the hoop stress in a pressure pipe. Look at a small arc that subtends an angle 2θ at the centre. The two tension forces at its ends are nearly opposite; what is their small resultant?",["x × (−z) = +y","R = L/2π","2θ","2Rθ","T","θ","2T sin θ ≈ 2Tθ","BI(2Rθ)","2Tθ = 2BIRθ ⟹ T = BIR = IBL/2π","2θ"],""],[2,5,22,"sec","Parallel currents: like attracts like","Now put two long straight wires side by side, distance d apart. Wire 1 makes a field at wire 2:",[],""],[2,5,23,"text","Parallel currents: like attracts like","Now put two long straight wires side by side, distance d apart. Wire 1 makes a field at wire 2:",["d"],""],[2,5,24,"formula","Parallel currents: like attracts like","Now put two long straight wires side by side, distance d apart. Wire 1 makes a field at wire 2:",["B_1 = (μ_0I_1)/2πd"],"<i>B</i><sub>1</sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><sub>1</sub></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>d</i></span></span>"],[2,5,25,"text","Parallel currents: like attracts like","and wire 2, carrying I_2 , sits in that field. The force on a length L of it is I_2LB_1 :",["I_2","L","I_2LB_1"],""],[2,5,26,"formula","Parallel currents: like attracts like","and wire 2, carrying I_2 , sits in that field. The force on a length L of it is I_2LB_1 :",["F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>F</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><sub>1</sub><i>I</i><sub>2</sub></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>d</i></span></span>"],[2,5,27,"text","Parallel currents: like attracts like","Do the right-hand rule and you find:",[],""],[2,5,28,"text","Parallel currents: like attracts like","currents in the same direction attract; currents in opposite directions repel.",[],""],[2,5,29,"text","Parallel currents: like attracts like","That is the reverse of electric charges, where likes repel. And the forces on the two wires are equal and opposite, as Newton’s third law demands.",[],""],[2,5,30,"story","How long is an ampere?","From 1948 until 2019 the ampere was defined by this formula: the steady current which, flowing in two infinitely long, thin, parallel wires one metre apart in vacuum, makes them push on each other with 2 × 10^(−7) newton per metre of length. Put I_1 = I_2 = 1 A and d = 1 m into μ_0I_1I_2/2πd and you get exactly that. Since 20 May 2019 the ampere is defined instead by fixing the charge of an electron at exactly 1.602176634 × 10^(−19) C — an ampere is a fixed number of electrons per second.",["2 × 10^(−7)","I_1 = I_2 = 1","d = 1","μ_0I_1I_2/2πd","1.602176634 × 10^(−19)"],""],[2,5,31,"q","Two cables · Integer answer","Two long parallel wires 10 cm apart carry 5.0 A and 10 A in opposite directions. What is the force per metre on each, in units of 10^(−4) N/m? Do they attract or repel?",["10^(−4)"],""],[2,5,31,"sol","Two cables · Integer answer","F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd = (2 × 10^(−7) × 5.0 × 10)/(0.10) = 1.0 × 10^(−4) N/m Answer: 1. Opposite currents repel. Note that the 5 A wire feels exactly as much force as the 10 A wire — the formula is symmetric in I_1 and I_2 .",["F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd = (2 × 10^(−7) × 5.0 × 10)/(0.10) = 1.0 × 10^(−4) N/m","I_1","I_2"],""],[2,5,32,"lab","Wires that pull and push","The same two wires as in Lesson 3, now with the forces drawn: the arrow on each wire shows the force per metre on it. Try these: Same-direction currents: the arrows point at each other. Reverse one current: they point apart. Make one current five times the other. Are the two force arrows still the same length? (Newton’s third law says they must be.) Drag the wires apart and watch the force fall as 1/d — halve the gap, double the force.",["1/d"],""],[2,5,33,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","Two very long, straight, parallel wires carry steady currents I and −I respectively. The distance between the wires is d . At a certain instant, a point charge q is at a point equidistant from the two wires, in the plane of the wires. Its instantaneous velocity v is perpendicular to this plane. The magnitude of the force due to the magnetic field acting on the charge at this instant is (μ_0Iqv)/2πd · (μ_0Iqv)/πd · (2μ_0Iqv)/πd · 0",["I","−I","d","q","v","(μ_0Iqv)/2πd","(μ_0Iqv)/πd","(2μ_0Iqv)/πd","0"],""],[2,5,33,"sol","IIT-JEE 1998 · Single correct","Midway between two wires carrying opposite currents, the two fields add (they do not cancel). But their direction is what matters: each wire’s field at a point in the plane of the wires is perpendicular to that plane. The charge’s velocity is also perpendicular to that plane. So v is parallel (or antiparallel) to B , and F = q v × B = 0 Answer (D). The field is not zero — the force is. Always check the angle before reaching for a formula. Don’t calculate B yet. Which direction does it point, compared with v ?",["v","B","F = q v × B = 0","B","v"],""],[2,5,34,"sec","A wire and a loop","A rectangular loop sits beside a long straight wire, with two of its sides parallel to the wire:",[],""],[2,5,35,"text","A wire and a loop","A rectangular loop sits beside a long straight wire, with two of its sides parallel to the wire:",[],""],[2,5,36,"text","A wire and a loop","The two short sides (perpendicular to the wire) carry opposite currents through identical fields: their forces cancel. The near side (current parallel to I_1) is attracted; the far side (antiparallel) is repelled — but less, because it is farther away.",["I_1"],""],[2,5,37,"formula","A wire and a loop","The two short sides (perpendicular to the wire) carry opposite currents through identical fields: their forces cancel. The near side (current parallel to I_1) is attracted; the far side (antiparallel) is repelled — but less, because it is farther away.",["F_net = (μ_0I_1I_2L)/2π (1/a − 1/b) towards the wire"],"<i>F</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub><i>I</i><sub>1</sub><i>I</i><sub>2</sub><i>L</i></span><span>2<i>π</i></span></span> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>a</i></span></span> − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>b</i></span></span>) <span class=\"fx-t\">towards the wire</span>"],[2,5,38,"text","A wire and a loop","A loop in a non-uniform field can feel a net force, even though a loop in a uniform field never does.",[],""],[2,5,39,"q","The loop beside the wire · Integer answer","In the arrangement above, I_1 = 10 A, I_2 = 5.0 A, L = 20 cm, a = 5.0 cm and b = 10 cm. Find the net force on the loop in units of 10^(−5) N.",["I_1 = 10","I_2 = 5.0","L = 20","a = 5.0","b = 10","10^(−5)"],""],[2,5,39,"sol","The loop beside the wire · Integer answer","F = (μ_0I_1I_2L)/2π (1/a − 1/b) = 2 × 10^(−7) × 10 × 5.0 × 0.20 × (20 − 10) = 2.0 × 10^(−5) N Answer: 2, towards the wire. Reverse I_2 and the loop is pushed away with the same force.",["F = (μ_0I_1I_2L)/2π (1/a − 1/b) = 2 × 10^(−7) × 10 × 5.0 × 0.20 × (20 − 10) = 2.0 × 10^(−5) N","I_2"],""],[2,5,40,"pyq","JEE Main 2015 · Single correct","Two coaxial solenoids of different radii carry current I in the same direction. Let F_1 be the magnetic force on the inner solenoid due to the outer one, and F_2 the magnetic force on the outer solenoid due to the inner one. Then F_1 = F_2 = 0 · F_1 is radially inwards and F_2 is radially outwards · F_1 is radially inwards and F_2 = 0 · F_1 is radially outwards and F_2 = 0",["I","F_1","F_2","F_1 = F_2 = 0","F_1","F_2","F_1","F_2 = 0","F_1","F_2 = 0"],""],[2,5,40,"sol","JEE Main 2015 · Single correct","F_1 . The outer solenoid’s field inside it is uniform and along the axis. The inner solenoid is a set of closed current loops sitting in that uniform field — and the net force on a closed loop in a uniform field is zero. (Each piece is pushed radially, and opposite pieces cancel.) F_2 . The inner solenoid’s field is zero outside itself, so the outer solenoid’s wires sit in no field at all from it. Answer (A). (Newton’s third law agrees: F_1 = −F_2 , and both are zero.)",["F_1","F_2","F_1 = −F_2"],""],[2,5,41,"recap","Recap","F = IL × B , size BIL sin θ — the sum of qv_d × B over all the carriers. In a uniform field a bent wire feels the same force as the straight line joining its ends; a closed loop feels no net force (but may feel a torque). Parallel wires: F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd . Same direction attract, opposite repel. Equal and opposite forces. The ampere was defined (1948–2019) by 2 × 10^(−7) N/m between wires 1 m apart; it is now defined by the fixed electron charge. A loop beside a long wire: net force (μ_0I_1I_2L)/2π (1/a − 1/b) , towards the wire if the near side’s current is parallel to it.",["F = IL × B","BIL sin θ","qv_d × B","F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd","2 × 10^(−7)","(μ_0I_1I_2L)/2π (1/a − 1/b)"],""],[2,6,0,"lesson","Torque on a Loop & the Magnetic Dipole","A current loop in a magnetic field feels no net force — yet it twists. That twist drives every electric motor, and it reveals a surprise: a current loop *is* a magnet, with a north end and a south end, and so is every spinning electron.",[],""],[2,6,1,"sec","Zero force, plenty of twist","Lesson 5 showed that a closed loop in a uniform field feels no net force. But look at where the forces act. Take a rectangular loop, sides a and b , that can turn about an axis through its centre. Seen edge-on:",[],""],[2,6,2,"text","Zero force, plenty of twist","Lesson 5 showed that a closed loop in a uniform field feels no net force. But look at where the forces act. Take a rectangular loop, sides a and b , that can turn about an axis through its centre. Seen edge-on:",["a","b"],""],[2,6,3,"text","Zero force, plenty of twist","The two sides of length a that cut across the field each feel a force F = IaB — equal, opposite, and not in line. A pair like that is a couple: it cannot move the loop, but it turns it. Each force has a lever arm b/2 sin θ about the axis, where θ is the angle between the loop’s normal and B :",["a","F = IaB","b/2 sin θ","θ","B"],""],[2,6,4,"formula","Zero force, plenty of twist","t across the field each feel a force F = IaB — equal, opposite, and not in line. A pair like that is a couple: it cannot move the loop, but it turns it. Each force has a lever arm b/2 sin θ about the axis, where θ is the angle between the loop’s normal and B :",["τ = 2 × IaB × b/2 sin θ = I(ab)B sin θ = IAB sin θ"],"<i>τ</i> = 2 × <i>I</i><i>a</i><i>B</i> × <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>b</i></span><span>2</span></span> sin <i>θ</i> = <i>I</i>(<i>ab</i>)<i>B</i> sin <i>θ</i> = <i>I</i><i>A</i><i>B</i> sin <i>θ</i>"],[2,6,5,"text","Zero force, plenty of twist","With N turns, τ = NIAB sin θ . The torque is largest when the plane of the loop is parallel to the field (θ = 90^∘), and zero when the loop faces the field squarely.",["N","τ = NIAB sin θ","θ = 90^∘"],""],[2,6,6,"sec","The magnetic moment","Everything about the loop that matters for the torque is packed into one vector, its magnetic dipole moment:",[],""],[2,6,7,"text","The magnetic moment","Everything about the loop that matters for the torque is packed into one vector, its magnetic dipole moment:",[],""],[2,6,8,"formula","The magnetic moment","Everything about the loop that matters for the torque is packed into one vector, its magnetic dipole moment:",["m = NIA, ∣m∣ = NIA"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>m</i></span> = <i>N</i><i>I</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span>, ∣<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>m</i></span>∣ = <i>N</i><i>I</i><i>A</i>"],[2,6,9,"text","The magnetic moment","It points along the normal to the loop, given by the right hand: curl your fingers along the current, and your thumb points along m . Then, for a flat loop of any shape,",["m"],""],[2,6,10,"formula","The magnetic moment","It points along the normal to the loop, given by the right hand: curl your fingers along the current, and your thumb points along m . Then, for a flat loop of any shape,",["τ = m × B"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>τ</i></span> = <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>m</i></span> × <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span>"],[2,6,11,"text","The magnetic moment","The unit is A m² (the same as J/T). The torque always tries to swing m round to line up with B — exactly like a compass needle turning to point north.",["m","B"],""],[2,6,12,"text","The magnetic moment","Energy. Turning the loop against the torque takes work, so the loop has a potential energy that depends on its angle:",[],""],[2,6,13,"formula","The magnetic moment","Energy. Turning the loop against the torque takes work, so the loop has a potential energy that depends on its angle:",["U = −m ⋅ B = −mB cos θ"],"<i>U</i> = −<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>m</i></span> ⋅ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = −<i>m</i><i>B</i> cos <i>θ</i>"],[2,6,14,"table","The magnetic moment","θ = 0^∘ (m along B) θ = 90^∘ θ = 180^∘ (m against B) Torque 0 mB (largest) 0 Energy U −mB (lowest) 0 +mB (highest) Equilibrium stable — unstable",["θ = 0^∘","m","B","θ = 90^∘","θ = 180^∘","m","B","mB","U","−mB","+mB"],""],[2,6,15,"text","The magnetic moment","The work needed to turn the loop from θ_1 to θ_2 is W = U_2 − U_1 = mB(cos θ_1 − cos θ_2) . Flipping it from stable to unstable costs 2mB .",["θ_1","θ_2","W = U_2 − U_1 = mB(cos θ_1 − cos θ_2)","2mB"],""],[2,6,16,"intuition","You have met this before","Replace m by p and B by E : τ = p × E , U = −p ⋅ E . A current loop in a magnetic field behaves exactly like an electric dipole in an electric field — the same torque, the same energy, the same stable and unstable positions. That is why m is called a dipole moment.",["m","p","B","E","τ = p × E","U = −p ⋅ E","m"],""],[2,6,17,"q","A coil in a motor · Integer answer","A coil of 100 turns, each of area 2.0 × 10^(−3) m², carries 0.50 A in a uniform field of 0.40 T. The normal to the coil makes 30^∘ with the field. Find the torque on the coil, in units of 10^(−3) N m.",["2.0 × 10^(−3)","30^∘","10^(−3)"],""],[2,6,17,"sol","A coil in a motor · Integer answer","m = NIA = 100 × 0.50 × 2.0 × 10^(−3) = 0.10 A m ^2 τ = mB sin θ = 0.10 × 0.40 × 0.5 = 0.020 N m Answer: 20 (2.0 × 10^(−2) N m). Turn the coil until its plane is parallel to the field and the torque doubles to 0.040 N m.",["m = NIA = 100 × 0.50 × 2.0 × 10^(−3) = 0.10 A m ^2","τ = mB sin θ = 0.10 × 0.40 × 0.5 = 0.020 N m","2.0 × 10^(−2)"],""],[2,6,18,"lab","Twist a current loop","A current loop seen edge-on, in a uniform field pointing right. The arrow is its magnetic moment m ; the two small arrows are the forces on the edges that cut the field. Try these, in order: Drag the tip of m round in a full circle. Watch the torque readout and the two graphs. Where is the torque biggest? Where is the energy lowest? Park m pointing exactly against B . The torque is zero — but nudge it slightly and see which way it wants to go. That is an unstable equilibrium. Set an angle of about 30^∘ and press Release. The loop swings back and forth about the field direction, like a compass needle settling. Compare the measured period with T = 2π√(I/mB) — it comes out a little longer for big swings, because the formula assumes small angles. Double the current or the field. What happens to the period?",["m","m","m","B","30^∘","T = 2π√(I/mB)"],""],[2,6,19,"sec","Oscillating dipoles","Twist a dipole slightly away from the field and let go. For small angles sin θ ≈ θ , so the restoring torque is −mB θ , and with moment of inertia I :",[],""],[2,6,20,"text","Oscillating dipoles","Twist a dipole slightly away from the field and let go. For small angles sin θ ≈ θ , so the restoring torque is −mB θ , and with moment of inertia I :",["sin θ ≈ θ","−mB θ","I"],""],[2,6,21,"formula","Oscillating dipoles","Twist a dipole slightly away from the field and let go. For small angles sin θ ≈ θ , so the restoring torque is −mB θ , and with moment of inertia I :",["Iθ = −mB θ ⟹ T = 2π√(I/mB)"],"<i>I</i><span class=\"fx-acc\"><i>θ</i>̈</span> = −<i>m</i><i>B</i> <i>θ</i> ⟹ <i>T</i> = 2<i>π</i>√<span class=\"fx-sq\"><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span><i>m</i><i>B</i></span></span></span>"],[2,6,22,"text","Oscillating dipoles","Simple harmonic motion, just like a pendulum. A compass needle is a magnetic dipole too (Lesson 8 shows why), so it obeys the same formula.",[],""],[2,6,23,"pyq","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","A magnetic needle of magnetic moment 6.7 × 10^(−2) A m² and moment of inertia 7.5 × 10^(−6) kg m² performs simple harmonic oscillations in a magnetic field of 0.01 T. The time taken for 10 complete oscillations is 6.98 s · 8.76 s · 6.65 s · 8.89 s",["6.7 × 10^(−2)","7.5 × 10^(−6)"],""],[2,6,23,"sol","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","T = 2π√(I/mB) = 2π√((7.5 × 10^(−6))/(6.7 × 10^(−2) × 0.01)) = 2π√(0.01119) = 2π × 0.1058 ≈ 0.665 s Ten oscillations take 6.65 s. Answer (C).",["T = 2π√(I/mB) = 2π√((7.5 × 10^(−6))/(6.7 × 10^(−2) × 0.01)) = 2π√(0.01119) = 2π × 0.1058 ≈ 0.665 s","6.65"],""],[2,6,24,"sec","Loops of every shape","Since m = I × (area) , finding a magnetic moment is mostly a geometry problem: find the area enclosed by the current, and the direction from the right hand.",[],""],[2,6,25,"text","Loops of every shape","Since m = I × (area) , finding a magnetic moment is mostly a geometry problem: find the area enclosed by the current, and the direction from the right hand.",["m = I × (area)"],""],[2,6,26,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","A loop carrying current I lies in the x – y plane as shown: a square of side a with a semicircle of diameter a on each of its four sides. The unit vector k points out of the page. The magnetic moment of the loop is a^2I k · (π/2 + 1) a^2I k · − (π/2 + 1) a^2I k · (2π + 1) a^2I k",["I","x","y","a","a","k","a^2I k","(π/2 + 1) a^2I k","− (π/2 + 1) a^2I k","(2π + 1) a^2I k"],""],[2,6,26,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","Area. The square plus four semicircles of radius a/2 : A = a^2 + 4 × 1/2π (a/2)^2 = a^2 + (πa^2)/2 = (π/2 + 1) a^2 Direction. The current runs anticlockwise as seen from the front, so by the right hand m points out of the page, along +k . m = (π/2 + 1) a^2I k Answer (B).",["a/2","A = a^2 + 4 × 1/2π (a/2)^2 = a^2 + (πa^2)/2 = (π/2 + 1) a^2","m","+k","m = (π/2 + 1) a^2I k"],""],[2,6,27,"pyq","JEE Main 2018 · Single correct","The dipole moment of a circular loop carrying a current I is m , and the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is B_1 . When the dipole moment is doubled by keeping the current constant, the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is B_2 . The ratio B_1/B_2 is √2 · 1/(√2) · 2 · 1/2",["I","m","B_1","B_2","B_1/B_2","√2","1/(√2)","2","1/2"],""],[2,6,27,"sol","JEE Main 2018 · Single correct","With I fixed, doubling m = IπR^2 means doubling the area, so R_2 = √2 R_1 . The field at the centre is μ_0I/2R ∝ 1/R : B_1/B_2 = R_2/R_1 = √2 Answer (A). A bigger loop has a bigger moment but a weaker field at its centre — the wire is farther away.",["I","m = IπR^2","R_2 = √2 R_1","μ_0I/2R ∝ 1/R","B_1/B_2 = R_2/R_1 = √2"],""],[2,6,28,"q","Circle or square? · Single correct","A fixed length of wire carrying a fixed current is bent into a single loop. Compared with bending it into a square, bending it into a circle multiplies the magnetic moment by 1 · π/4 · π/2 · 4/π",["1","π/4","π/2","4/π"],""],[2,6,28,"sol","Circle or square? · Single correct","With wire length L : a circle has R = L/2π and area (L^2)/4π ; a square has side L/4 and area (L^2)/16 . (m_(circle))/(m_(square)) = (L^2/4π)/(L^2/16) = 4/π ≈ 1.27 Answer (D). Of all shapes with a given perimeter, the circle encloses the most area — so it makes the strongest magnet from a given wire.",["L","R = L/2π","(L^2)/4π","L/4","(L^2)/16","(m_(circle))/(m_(square)) = (L^2/4π)/(L^2/16) = 4/π ≈ 1.27"],""],[2,6,29,"sec","Spinning charges are magnets too","A charge q going round a circle of radius r at speed v is a tiny current loop. It passes any point once per lap, so the current is",[],""],[2,6,30,"text","Spinning charges are magnets too","A charge q going round a circle of radius r at speed v is a tiny current loop. It passes any point once per lap, so the current is",["q","r","v"],""],[2,6,31,"formula","Spinning charges are magnets too","A charge q going round a circle of radius r at speed v is a tiny current loop. It passes any point once per lap, so the current is",["I = q/T = qv/2πr ⟹ m = I πr^2 = qvr/2"],"<i>I</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i></span><span><i>T</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>v</i></span><span>2<i>π</i><i>r</i></span></span> ⟹ <i>m</i> = <i>I</i> <i>π</i><i>r</i><sup>2</sup> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>v</i><i>r</i></span><span>2</span></span>"],[2,6,32,"text","Spinning charges are magnets too","Its angular momentum is L = m_qvr (with m_q the particle’s mass). Divide, and the speed and radius vanish:",["L = m_qvr","m_q"],""],[2,6,33,"formula","Spinning charges are magnets too","Its angular momentum is L = m_qvr (with m_q the particle’s mass). Divide, and the speed and radius vanish:",["m/L = q/(2m_q)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i></span><span>2<i>m</i><sub><i>q</i></sub></span></span>"],[2,6,34,"text","Spinning charges are magnets too","This gyromagnetic ratio holds for any orbiting charge, and for any spinning body whose charge and mass are spread out in the same way. For an electron in the first Bohr orbit, L = h/2π , so",["L = h/2π"],""],[2,6,35,"formula","Spinning charges are magnets too","This gyromagnetic ratio holds for any orbiting charge, and for any spinning body whose charge and mass are spread out in the same way. For an electron in the first Bohr orbit, L = h/2π , so",["m = eh/(4πm_e) ≈ 9.27 × 10^(−24) A m ^2"],"<i>m</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>e</i><i>h</i></span><span>4<i>π</i><i>m</i><sub><i>e</i></sub></span></span> ≈ 9.27 × 10<sup>−24</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">A m</span><sup>2</sup>"],[2,6,36,"text","Spinning charges are magnets too","— the Bohr magneton, the natural unit of atomic magnetism. Every atom with unpaired orbiting or spinning electrons is a tiny magnet. Lesson 8 is about what happens when you put billions of billions of them together.",[],""],[2,6,37,"text","Spinning charges are magnets too","For spinning charged bodies, cut them into rings. A ring of charge q spinning at ω is a current qω/2π , so",["q","ω","qω/2π"],""],[2,6,38,"formula","Spinning charges are magnets too","For spinning charged bodies, cut them into rings. A ring of charge q spinning at ω is a current qω/2π , so",["m_(ring) = qω/2π πR^2 = (qωR^2)/2"],"<i>m</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">ring</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>ω</i></span><span>2<i>π</i></span></span> <i>π</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>q</i><i>ω</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>2</span></span>"],[2,6,39,"q","A spinning disc · Single correct","A thin disc of radius R carries charge Q spread uniformly over its surface. It spins about its axis at angular speed ω . Its magnetic moment is (QωR^2)/2 · (QωR^2)/4 · (QωR^2)/8 · QωR^2",["R","Q","ω","(QωR^2)/2","(QωR^2)/4","(QωR^2)/8","QωR^2"],""],[2,6,39,"sol","A spinning disc · Single correct","A ring of radius r and width dr carries dq = Q/(πR^2) 2πr dr = (2Qr dr)/(R^2) . Its moment is dm = (dq ωr^2)/2 = (Qωr^3 dr)/(R^2) m = Qω/(R^2) ∫_0^R r^3 dr = (QωR^2)/4 Answer (B). Shortcut: the disc’s mass would be spread the same way as its charge, so m/L = Q/2M . With L = 1/2MR^2ω , m = Q/(2M) ⋅ (MR^2ω)/2 = (QωR^2)/4 . Cut the disc into rings of radius r and width dr . Each is a spinning ring with dm = (dq ωr^2)/2 .",["r","dr","dq = Q/(πR^2) 2πr dr = (2Qr dr)/(R^2)","dm = (dq ωr^2)/2 = (Qωr^3 dr)/(R^2)","m = Qω/(R^2) ∫_0^R r^3 dr = (QωR^2)/4","m/L = Q/2M","L = 1/2MR^2ω","m = Q/(2M) ⋅ (MR^2ω)/2 = (QωR^2)/4","r","dr","dm = (dq ωr^2)/2"],""],[2,6,40,"sec","Far from a loop: the dipole field","Lesson 3 gave the field on a loop’s axis far away: μ_0/4π(2m)/(x^3) . In the loop’s own plane, far away, the field is half as big and points the opposite way to m :",[],""],[2,6,41,"text","Far from a loop: the dipole field","Lesson 3 gave the field on a loop’s axis far away: μ_0/4π(2m)/(x^3) . In the loop’s own plane, far away, the field is half as big and points the opposite way to m :",["μ_0/4π(2m)/(x^3)","m"],""],[2,6,42,"formula","Far from a loop: the dipole field","Lesson 3 gave the field on a loop’s axis far away: μ_0/4π(2m)/(x^3) . In the loop’s own plane, far away, the field is half as big and points the opposite way to m :",["B_(axis) = μ_0/4π (2m)/(r^3), B_(equator) = μ_0/4π m/(r^3)"],"<i>B</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">axis</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>2<i>m</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>, <i>B</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">equator</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>4<i>π</i></span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i></span><span><i>r</i><sup>3</sup></span></span>"],[2,6,43,"text","Far from a loop: the dipole field","The electric dipole had exactly these formulas with 1/(4πε_0) and p . Far away, a current loop’s field lines look just like a bar magnet’s — leaving from one face and curving round into the other. One face of the loop behaves as a north pole, the other as a south pole: m points from south to north. Lesson 8 takes that idea seriously.",["1/(4πε_0)","p","m"],""],[2,6,44,"q","A square frame’s first twitch · Think, then reveal","A rigid square wire frame PQRS, with sides of length L and each side of mass M , carries a steady current I_0 . It lies in the x – y plane with its centre at the origin and its sides parallel to the axes, the current flowing anticlockwise as seen from +z . A uniform field B lies in the x – y plane at 45^∘ to the x -axis. The frame is released from rest at t = 0 . Find (i) the torque on the frame about its centre, and (ii) the angle through which it turns in a short time Δt , and about which axis. Decide before you reveal.",["L","M","I_0","x","y","+z","B","x","y","45^∘","x","t = 0"],""],[2,6,44,"sol","A square frame’s first twitch · Think, then reveal","(i) Torque. m = I_0L^2 k and B = B/(√2)(i + j) : τ = m × B = (I_0L^2B)/(√2) (k × i + k × j) = (I_0L^2B)/(√2) (j − i) , ∣τ∣ = I_0L^2B It acts along (−i + j)/√2 — the direction of the frame’s diagonal that is perpendicular to B . (ii) Rotation. About the axis perpendicular to the frame through its centre, each side contributes 1/12ML^2 + M (L/2)^2 = 1/3ML^2 , so I_z = 4/3ML^2 . By the perpendicular-axis theorem and the square’s symmetry, every axis in its plane through the centre — the diagonal included — has I = 1/2I_z = 2/3ML^2 . So α = τ/I = 2/3ML^2I_0L^2B = (3I_0B)/(2M), θ = 1/2α Δt^2 = (3I_0B Δt^2)/(4M) The frame starts to turn about the diagonal perpendicular to B (for short times the torque, and so the axis, barely changes).",["m = I_0L^2 k","B = B/(√2)(i + j)","τ = m × B = (I_0L^2B)/(√2) (k × i + k × j) = (I_0L^2B)/(√2) (j − i) , ∣τ∣ = I_0L^2B","(−i + j)/√2","B","1/12ML^2 + M (L/2)^2 = 1/3ML^2","I_z = 4/3ML^2","I = 1/2I_z = 2/3ML^2","α = τ/I = 2/3ML^2I_0L^2B = (3I_0B)/(2M), θ = 1/2α Δt^2 = (3I_0B Δt^2)/(4M)","B"],""],[2,6,45,"recap","Recap","A loop in a uniform field: no net force, but a torque τ = m × B , size NIAB sin θ . Magnetic moment m = NIA , by the right-hand curl; unit A m². Any plane shape: m = I× area. Energy U = −m ⋅ B : stable with m along B , unstable against it; flipping costs 2mB . Small oscillations: T = 2π√(I/mB) . Orbiting or spinning charge: m/L = q/2m . Ring qωR^2/2 , disc QωR^2/4 . Bohr magneton eh/4πm_e ≈ 9.27 × 10^(−24) A m². Far field of a loop = dipole field: μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3) on the axis, μ_0/4πm/(r^3) on the equator.",["τ = m × B","NIAB sin θ","m = NIA","m = I×","U = −m ⋅ B","m","B","2mB","T = 2π√(I/mB)","m/L = q/2m","qωR^2/2","QωR^2/4"],""],[2,7,0,"lesson","The Moving-Coil Galvanometer","Put the torque on a coil to work and you can see a current of a few microamperes. One clever trick — a radial magnetic field — makes the needle’s swing exactly proportional to the current, and two sensitivities tell you how good the meter is.",[],""],[2,7,1,"sec","A torque you can read","Lesson 6 gave the torque on a coil: τ = NIAB sin θ . A moving-coil galvanometer turns that torque into a pointer reading. A light rectangular coil of N turns is wound on a frame and hung between the poles of a permanent magnet, with a fixed soft-iron cylinder inside it. A fine spring (or a thin phos",[],""],[2,7,2,"text","A torque you can read","Lesson 6 gave the torque on a coil: τ = NIAB sin θ . A moving-coil galvanometer turns that torque into a pointer reading. A light rectangular coil of N turns is wound on a frame and hung between the poles of a permanent magnet, with a fixed soft-iron cylinder inside it. A fine spring (or a thin phosphor-bronze suspension wire) resists its turning.",["τ = NIAB sin θ","N"],""],[2,7,3,"text","A torque you can read","The concave pole pieces and the iron core make the field in the gap radial: wherever the coil turns, its sides move through field lines that point straight at (or away from) the axis. So the plane of the coil is always parallel to the field lines, sin θ = 1 at every deflection, and the magnetic torque is simply NIAB .",["sin θ = 1","NIAB"],""],[2,7,4,"text","A torque you can read","The spring pushes back with a torque proportional to the twist, kϕ . The coil settles where the two balance:",["kϕ"],""],[2,7,5,"formula","A torque you can read","The spring pushes back with a torque proportional to the twist, kϕ . The coil settles where the two balance:",["NIAB = kϕ ⟹ ϕ = (NAB/k) I"],"<i>N</i><i>I</i><i>A</i><i>B</i> = <i>k</i><i>ϕ</i> ⟹ <i>ϕ</i> = (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>N</i><i>A</i><i>B</i></span><span><i>k</i></span></span>) <i>I</i>"],[2,7,6,"text","A torque you can read","The deflection is directly proportional to the current, so the scale can be marked in equal divisions.",[],""],[2,7,7,"text","A torque you can read","Pause & predictWhat would go wrong with an ordinary uniform field between flat poles? As the coil turned, the angle between its normal and the field would change, so the torque would fall off as cos ϕ (for a coil starting with its plane along the field). Balance would give NIAB cos ϕ = kϕ — the deflection would no longer be proportional to I , the scale would bunch up, and large currents would be almost impossible to read. Try both in the lab below.",["cos ϕ","NIAB cos ϕ = kϕ","I"],""],[2,7,8,"lab","Inside the galvanometer","A galvanometer dial. Slide the current and watch the needle settle. With the radial field on, step the current up in equal amounts. The needle moves in equal steps — a linear scale. Switch to a uniform field. Same currents, but now the steps get smaller and smaller as the needle swings: the scale is cramped at the top. Turn damping off and change the current suddenly. The needle overshoots and wobbles. Real meters wind the coil on an aluminium frame so the needle settles at once — you will see why in Electromagnetic Induction.",[],""],[2,7,9,"q","Why radial? · Single correct","The pole pieces of a moving-coil galvanometer are made concave, with a soft-iron cylinder between them. The main purpose is to increase the resistance of the coil · make the field uniform throughout the gap · make the deflection proportional to the current · reduce the moment of inertia of the coil",[],""],[2,7,9,"sol","Why radial? · Single correct","The concave poles and the core make the field radial, so the coil’s plane stays parallel to the field lines at every angle. The torque is then NIAB at all deflections, and balancing it against kϕ gives ϕ ∝ I . Answer (C). (B) is the trap: the field is not uniform in direction — it is radial — though its strength is the same where the coil’s sides move. The iron core also concentrates the field, which raises the sensitivity.",["NIAB","kϕ","ϕ ∝ I"],""],[2,7,10,"sec","How sensitive is it?","Two numbers grade a galvanometer:",[],""],[2,7,11,"text","How sensitive is it?","Two numbers grade a galvanometer:",[],""],[2,7,12,"formula","How sensitive is it?","Two numbers grade a galvanometer:",["current sensitivity ϕ/I = NAB/k, voltage sensitivity ϕ/V = ϕ/IG = NAB/kG"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">current sensitivity </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ϕ</i></span><span><i>I</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>N</i><i>A</i><i>B</i></span><span><i>k</i></span></span>, <span class=\"fx-t\">voltage sensitivity </span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ϕ</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ϕ</i></span><span><i>I</i><i>G</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>N</i><i>A</i><i>B</i></span><span><i>k</i><i>G</i></span></span>"],[2,7,13,"text","How sensitive is it?","where G is the resistance of the coil. Both improve with a stronger magnet, a bigger coil, and a weaker spring (phosphor-bronze suspensions have a very small k).",["G","k"],""],[2,7,14,"trap","More turns do not always help","Add more turns of the same wire and N goes up — but the wire gets longer, so G goes up in the same proportion. The current sensitivity rises; the voltage sensitivity NAB/kG does not change. JEE has asked this many times.",["N","G","NAB/kG"],""],[2,7,15,"q","Double the turns · Single correct","The number of turns of a galvanometer coil is doubled, using more of the same wire (so the coil’s resistance doubles too). Everything else is unchanged. Then the current sensitivity doubles and the voltage sensitivity is unchanged · both sensitivities double · the current sensitivity is unchanged and the voltage sensitivity halves · both sensitivities are unchanged",[],""],[2,7,15,"sol","Double the turns · Single correct","Current sensitivity NAB/k : N doubles, so it doubles. Voltage sensitivity NAB/kG : N and G both double, so it is unchanged. Answer (A).",["NAB/k","N","NAB/kG","N","G"],""],[2,7,16,"q","A tiny current · Integer answer","A galvanometer coil has 50 turns of area 4.0 × 10^(−4) m² in a radial field of 0.20 T. The suspension has torsion constant 2.0 × 10^(−7) N m per radian. What current, in microamperes, deflects the coil by 0.20 rad?",["4.0 × 10^(−4)","2.0 × 10^(−7)"],""],[2,7,16,"sol","A tiny current · Integer answer","I = kϕ/NAB = (2.0 × 10^(−7) × 0.20)/(50 × 4.0 × 10^(−4) × 0.20) = (4.0 × 10^(−8))/(4.0 × 10^(−3)) = 1.0 × 10^(−5) A Answer: 10 μ A — for a visible swing of more than eleven degrees.",["I = kϕ/NAB = (2.0 × 10^(−7) × 0.20)/(50 × 4.0 × 10^(−4) × 0.20) = (4.0 × 10^(−8))/(4.0 × 10^(−3)) = 1.0 × 10^(−5) A","μ"],""],[2,7,17,"sec","Turning it into an ammeter or a voltmeter","A galvanometer reaches full scale at a small current I_g . Current Electricity, Lesson 7, showed how to stretch its range:",[],""],[2,7,18,"text","Turning it into an ammeter or a voltmeter","A galvanometer reaches full scale at a small current I_g . Current Electricity, Lesson 7, showed how to stretch its range:",["I_g"],""],[2,7,19,"text","Turning it into an ammeter or a voltmeter","Ammeter (range I): a small shunt in parallel, S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) , carries the extra current. Voltmeter (range V): a large resistance in series, R = V/I_g − G .",["I","S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g)","V","R = V/I_g − G"],""],[2,7,20,"text","Turning it into an ammeter or a voltmeter","The galvanometer’s sensitivities are often quoted in divisions per milliampere or per millivolt — and then the coil resistance is hiding in their ratio, G = (ϕ/I)/(ϕ/V) .",["G = (ϕ/I)/(ϕ/V)"],""],[2,7,21,"pyq","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","A moving-coil galvanometer has 150 equal divisions. Its current sensitivity is 10 divisions per milliampere and its voltage sensitivity is 2 divisions per millivolt. In order that each division reads 1 volt, the resistance in ohms that must be connected in series with the coil is 10^5 · 10^3 · 9995 · 99995",["10^5","10^3","9995","99995"],""],[2,7,21,"sol","AIEEE 2005 · Single correct","Full-scale current: 150 ÷ 10 per mA = 15 mA. Coil resistance: full scale is also 150 ÷ 2 per mV = 75 mV, so G = (75 mV)/(15 mA) = 5 Ω . Voltmeter: 1 V per division over 150 divisions means a 150 V range: R = V/I_g − G = 150/(0.015) − 5 = 10000 − 5 = 9995 Ω Answer (C). From the two sensitivities, find the full-scale current and the coil’s resistance first.",["150 ÷ 10","= 15","150 ÷ 2","= 75","G = (75 mV)/(15 mA) = 5 Ω","R = V/I_g − G = 150/(0.015) − 5 = 10000 − 5 = 9995 Ω"],""],[2,7,22,"recap","Recap","Radial field (concave poles + soft-iron core) keeps the coil’s plane along the field: torque NIAB at every angle. Balance with the spring: ϕ = NAB/k I — a linear scale. Current sensitivity NAB/k ; voltage sensitivity NAB/kG . More turns of the same wire raise the first, not the second. Shunt for an ammeter, S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g) ; series resistance for a voltmeter, R = V/I_g − G .",["NIAB","ϕ = NAB/k I","NAB/k","NAB/kG","S = (I_gG)/(I − I_g)","R = V/I_g − G"],""],[2,8,0,"lesson","Bar Magnets & Magnetic Materials","A fridge magnet has no wires and no battery — so where is its current? Inside every atom. Meet the magnet as a solenoid in disguise, the pole you can never cut off, and the three ways matter answers a magnetic field: shy away, lean in, or line up completely.",[],""],[2,8,1,"sec","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","Sprinkle iron filings round a bar magnet, then round a solenoid carrying a current. The two patterns are practically identical. Field lines leave one end, sweep round, and return into the other.",[],""],[2,8,2,"text","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","Sprinkle iron filings round a bar magnet, then round a solenoid carrying a current. The two patterns are practically identical. Field lines leave one end, sweep round, and return into the other.",[],""],[2,8,3,"figure","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","Outside the magnet the lines run from N to S; inside they carry on from S to N. Every line is a closed loop.",[],""],[2,8,4,"text","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","Ampère guessed the reason in the 1820s: a magnet must be full of tiny circulating currents. He was right. Electrons orbiting and spinning in atoms are the current loops of Lesson 6, each with a moment of the order of a Bohr magneton. In a magnet, enough of them point the same way to add up to one big m .",["m"],""],[2,8,5,"text","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","So everything you know about loops carries over:",[],""],[2,8,6,"text","A bar magnet is a solenoid in disguise","the north pole is the end from which the field lines come out; m points from S to N; far away, the field is the dipole field: μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3) on the axis, μ_0/4πm/(r^3) on the equator, pointing opposite to m there; in a uniform field it feels τ = m × B and has energy U = −m ⋅ B — which is exactly why a compass needle swings round to point along the field.",["m","μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3)","μ_0/4πm/(r^3)","m","τ = m × B","U = −m ⋅ B"],""],[2,8,7,"sec","The pole you can never cut off","Break a bar magnet in half, hoping to separate the north pole from the south. You get two complete magnets, each with its own N and S. Break those, and again. Down to single atoms, you never find a lone pole — a magnetic monopole — and no experiment has ever found one anywhere.",[],""],[2,8,8,"text","The pole you can never cut off","Break a bar magnet in half, hoping to separate the north pole from the south. You get two complete magnets, each with its own N and S. Break those, and again. Down to single atoms, you never find a lone pole — a magnetic monopole — and no experiment has ever found one anywhere.",[],""],[2,8,9,"text","The pole you can never cut off","This is a law of nature. Magnetic field lines have no starting points and no end points: they always close on themselves. So any closed surface has as many lines going in as coming out:",[],""],[2,8,10,"formula","The pole you can never cut off","This is a law of nature. Magnetic field lines have no starting points and no end points: they always close on themselves. So any closed surface has as many lines going in as coming out:",["∮ B ⋅ dA = 0"],"∮ <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> ⋅ <i>d</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>A</i></span> = 0"],[2,8,11,"text","The pole you can never cut off","This is Gauss’s law for magnetism. Compare it with ∮ E ⋅ dA = q/ε_0 : there is no “magnetic charge” to put on the right.",["∮ E ⋅ dA = q/ε_0"],""],[2,8,12,"q","Snap a magnet · Single correct","A uniform bar magnet of magnetic moment m is cut in half straight across its length. Which statement is correct? One half is a north pole and the other half is a south pole · Each half is a complete magnet with magnetic moment m/2 · Each half is a complete magnet with magnetic moment m · Neither half is magnetised any more",["m","m/2","m"],""],[2,8,12,"sol","Snap a magnet · Single correct","There are no isolated poles, so each half is a complete magnet with its own N and S. The atomic moments inside are lined up as before; each half simply contains half of them. The magnetic moment is moment per volume times volume, so each half has m/2 . Answer (B).",["m/2"],""],[2,8,13,"q","Axis versus equator · Integer answer","A short bar magnet is placed on a table. Find the ratio of the field strength at a point on its axis to that at a point on its equator, both at the same distance r (much larger than the magnet).",["r"],""],[2,8,13,"sol","Axis versus equator · Integer answer","(B_(axis))/(B_(equator)) = μ_0/4πm/(r^3)μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3) = 2 Answer: 2. The same ratio as for an electric dipole. (Directions differ too: along m on the axis, opposite to m on the equator.)",["(B_(axis))/(B_(equator)) = μ_0/4πm/(r^3)μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3) = 2","m","m"],""],[2,8,14,"story","Earth is a magnet — upside down","The north end of a compass needle points (roughly) north. Since unlike poles attract, the magnetic pole up in the Arctic must be a magnetic south pole. Earth’s field is close to that of a giant bar magnet tilted a little from the spin axis — and its poles wander, by tens of kilometres a year.",[],""],[2,8,15,"sec","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","Fill a long solenoid with some material. The coil’s current alone would give μ_0nI . The material’s atoms add their own contribution, measured by the magnetisation M — the magnetic moment per unit volume (unit A/m).",[],""],[2,8,16,"text","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","Fill a long solenoid with some material. The coil’s current alone would give μ_0nI . The material’s atoms add their own contribution, measured by the magnetisation M — the magnetic moment per unit volume (unit A/m).",["μ_0nI","M"],""],[2,8,17,"text","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","It helps to separate the part we control from the part the material adds. The magnetic intensity H is set by the free currents in the wires: in a solenoid, H = nI . Then",["H","H = nI"],""],[2,8,18,"formula","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","It helps to separate the part we control from the part the material adds. The magnetic intensity H is set by the free currents in the wires: in a solenoid, H = nI . Then",["B = μ_0 (H + M)"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub> (<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>H</i></span> + <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>M</i></span>)"],[2,8,19,"text","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","For most materials, M is proportional to H . The constant is the magnetic susceptibility χ :",["M","H","χ"],""],[2,8,20,"formula","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","For most materials, M is proportional to H . The constant is the magnetic susceptibility χ :",["M = χH, B = μ_0(1 + χ)H = μH, μ_r = μ/μ_0 = 1 + χ"],"<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>M</i></span> = <i>χ</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>H</i></span>, <span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>B</i></span> = <i>μ</i><sub>0</sub>(1 + <i>χ</i>)<span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>H</i></span> = <i>μ</i><span class=\"fx-vec\"><i>H</i></span>, <i>μ</i><sub><i>r</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>μ</i></span><span><i>μ</i><sub>0</sub></span></span> = 1 + <i>χ</i>"],[2,8,21,"text","Magnetisation, H and susceptibility","χ is a pure number. Its sign and size sort every material into one of three families.",["χ"],""],[2,8,22,"pyq","JEE Main 2014 · Single correct","The coercivity of a small magnet, where the ferromagnet gets demagnetised, is 3 × 10^3 A m⁻¹. The current that must be passed through a solenoid of length 10 cm and 100 turns, so that the magnet is demagnetised when placed inside it, is 30 mA · 60 mA · 3 A · 6 A",["3 × 10^3"],""],[2,8,22,"sol","JEE Main 2014 · Single correct","The solenoid must supply H = 3 × 10^3 A/m. With n = 100/0.10 = 1000 turns per metre: I = H/n = (3 × 10^3)/(1000) = 3 A Answer (C). No μ_0 anywhere: H is measured in A/m, straight from the turns and the current. Coercivity is a value of H — the magnetic intensity that must be applied to wipe out the magnetisation. In a solenoid, H = nI .",["H = 3 × 10^3","n = 100/0.10 = 1000","I = H/n = (3 × 10^3)/(1000) = 3 A","μ_0","H","H","H = nI"],""],[2,8,23,"sec","Three ways matter responds","Diamagnetism happens in every material: a field slightly changes the orbits of the electrons so that they oppose it (a preview of Lenz’s law). It is tiny, and in most materials something stronger hides it. Superconductors are the extreme case: they expel the field from their interior completely.",[],""],[2,8,24,"table","Three ways matter responds","Diamagnetic Paramagnetic Ferromagnetic χ small, negative (about −10^(−5)) small, positive (10^(−5) – 10^(−3)) huge, positive (10^3 and more) In a non-uniform field weakly pushed towards weaker field weakly pulled towards stronger field strongly pulled in Atoms no permanent moment; the field induces a small opposing one permanent moments, jostled into disorder by heat permanent moments locked together in domains Temperature almost no effect Curie’s law, χ = C/T becomes paramagnetic above the Curie temperature Examples bismuth, copper, water, gold; superconductors (χ = −1) aluminium, sodium, oxygen iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium (below about 20 °C), many alloys",["χ","−10^(−5)","10^(−5)","10^(−3)","10^3","χ = C/T","χ = −1"],""],[2,8,25,"text","Three ways matter responds","Diamagnetism happens in every material: a field slightly changes the orbits of the electrons so that they oppose it (a preview of Lenz’s law). It is tiny, and in most materials something stronger hides it. Superconductors are the extreme case: they expel the field from their interior completely.",[],""],[2,8,26,"text","Three ways matter responds","Paramagnetism needs atoms that are magnets already. A field tries to line them up; thermal jiggling tries to scramble them. The colder it is, the better the field wins: χ ∝ 1/T (Curie’s law).",["χ ∝ 1/T"],""],[2,8,27,"text","Three ways matter responds","Ferromagnetism is cooperative. Neighbouring atomic moments lock parallel to each other over regions called domains, each fully magnetised. In unmagnetised iron the domains point every which way. A field makes the domains that point its way grow, and the iron becomes a strong magnet — far stronger than the field that magnetised it. Heat it past its Curie temperature (about 770 °C for iron) and the locking breaks: it becomes merely paramagnetic.",[],""],[2,8,28,"story","The flying frog","In 1997 Andre Geim’s team at Nijmegen in the Netherlands put a live frog inside a 16-tesla magnet — and it floated. Water and the other molecules in a frog are diamagnetic. The effect is feeble, but in such an intense, non-uniform field the gentle push towards weaker field was enough to balance gravity. The frog was unharmed; Geim won the 2000 Ig Nobel Prize for it, and in 2010 the real Nobel Prize, for graphene.",[],""],[2,8,29,"q","Spot the diamagnets · One or more correct","Which of the following are diamagnetic? Copper · Aluminium · Bismuth · Liquid oxygen",[],""],[2,8,29,"sol","Spot the diamagnets · One or more correct","Copper: diamagnetic (a small negative χ). ✔ Aluminium: paramagnetic. ✘ Bismuth: diamagnetic — one of the most strongly diamagnetic elements. ✔ Liquid oxygen: paramagnetic — poured between the poles of a strong magnet, it clings there. ✘ Answer (A), (C). Being a metal says nothing about magnetism: copper and aluminium are both excellent conductors, but they fall in different families.",["χ"],""],[2,8,30,"pyq","JEE Main 2019 · Numerical answer","A paramagnetic material has 10^28 atoms per m³. Its magnetic susceptibility at 350 K is 2.8 × 10^(−4) . What is its susceptibility at 300 K? Give your answer in units of 10^(−4) .",["10^28","2.8 × 10^(−4)","10^(−4)"],""],[2,8,30,"sol","JEE Main 2019 · Numerical answer","Curie’s law: χ ∝ 1/T . χ_300 = χ_350 × 350/300 = 2.8 × 10^(−4) × 7/6 ≈ 3.267 × 10^(−4) Answer: 3.267. The number of atoms per cubic metre is a red herring — it is the same at both temperatures.",["χ ∝ 1/T","χ_300 = χ_350 × 350/300 = 2.8 × 10^(−4) × 7/6 ≈ 3.267 × 10^(−4)"],""],[2,8,31,"q","Too hot to be a magnet · Single correct","A piece of iron is heated well above 800 °C and placed between the poles of a strong magnet. It is now strongly attracted, as before · weakly repelled, because it has become diamagnetic · not affected at all · only weakly attracted, because it has become paramagnetic",[],""],[2,8,31,"sol","Too hot to be a magnet · Single correct","Above its Curie temperature (about 770 °C), the domains in iron break up: thermal agitation wins over the forces that lock neighbouring atomic moments together. The atoms still carry their own moments, so iron becomes paramagnetic — weakly attracted, with χ following Curie’s law. Answer (D). Cool it down again and it becomes ferromagnetic once more.",["χ"],""],[2,8,32,"recap","Recap","A bar magnet behaves like a solenoid: m from S to N; dipole field, torque m × B , energy −m ⋅ B . No monopoles: field lines are closed loops, and ∮ B ⋅ dA = 0 . A cut magnet gives two magnets. B = μ_0(H + M) ; H = nI in a solenoid; χ = M/H ; μ_r = 1 + χ . Diamagnetic: χ < 0 , tiny, temperature-independent. Paramagnetic: χ > 0 , small, χ ∝ 1/T . Ferromagnetic: huge, domains, paramagnetic above the Curie temperature. Coercivity is the reverse H needed to wipe out a ferromagnet’s magnetisation.",["m","m × B","−m ⋅ B","∮ B ⋅ dA = 0","B = μ_0(H + M)","H = nI","χ = M/H","μ_r = 1 + χ","χ < 0","χ > 0","χ ∝ 1/T","H"],""],[2,9,0,"lesson","Revision & Mixed PYQs","The whole chapter on one screen — which tool to reach for, the formulas worth memorising, the traps examiners reuse — and four JEE Advanced problems that stitch it all together.",[],""],[2,9,1,"sec","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Most lost marks in this chapter come from the first few seconds: writing qvB = mv^2/r before checking that the velocity is perpendicular, or integrating Biot–Savart when Ampère would take one line. Run down this list first.",[],""],[2,9,2,"text","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","Most lost marks in this chapter come from the first few seconds: writing qvB = mv^2/r before checking that the velocity is perpendicular, or integrating Biot–Savart when Ampère would take one line. Run down this list first.",["qvB = mv^2/r"],""],[2,9,3,"table","Which tool? Decide before you calculate","If you see… Reach for… “which way is the force?” q v × B with the right hand; flip for negative charges speed or kinetic energy in a magnetic field unchanged — the magnetic force does no work both E and B E changes the speed, B only steers; released from rest, only E starts it crossed fields, “undeflected” v = E/B , whatever the charge or mass a charge entering a region of field draw the circle: r = mv/qB , centre on the perpendicular to v ; it leaves an edge at the angle it entered “how long in the field?” angle turned ÷ ω , with ω = qB/m velocity at an angle to B helix: v_∥ glides, v_⊥ circles; pitch 2πmv cos θ/qB straight pieces, arcs, polygons Biot–Savart pieces: (μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β) and (μ_0Iθ)/4πR long wires, cylinders, solenoids, toroids Ampère with a loop on which B is constant several sources find each field separately, then add as vectors a bent wire in a uniform field replace it by the straight line joining its ends; closed loop → no net force two currents side by side μ_0I_1I_2/2πd per metre: like currents attract a loop in a uniform field τ = m × B , U = −m ⋅ B a spinning charged body m/L = q/2M , or slice it into rings",["q v × B","E","B","E","B","E","v = E/B","r = mv/qB","v","÷ ω","ω = qB/m","B"],""],[2,9,4,"sec","The formulas worth memorising","",[],""],[2,9,5,"table","The formulas worth memorising","Idea Formula Lorentz force F = q(E + v × B) Circular motion r = mv/qB = (√(2mK))/qB , T = 2πm/qB Helix r = (mv sin θ)/qB , p = (2πmv cos θ)/qB Cyclotron f = qB/2πm , K_max = (q^2B^2R^2)/(2m) Biot–Savart dB = μ_0/4π(I dl × r)/(r^2) Straight wire (μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β) ; infinite (μ_0I)/2πd Circular arc, loop (μ_0Iθ)/4πR ; (μ_0NI)/(2R) ; axis (μ_0IR^2)/(2(R^2 + x^2)^(3/2)) Ampère ∮ B ⋅ dl = μ_0I_enc Solid wire (μ_0Ir)/(2πR^2) inside, (μ_0I)/2πr outside Solenoid, toroid μ_0nI (end: μ_0nI/2); (μ_0NI)/2πr Force on a wire F = IL × B Parallel wires F/L = (μ_0I_1I_2)/2πd Magnetic moment m = NIA ; τ = m × B ; U = −m ⋅ B Dipole field axis μ_0/4π(2m)/(r^3) , equator μ_0/4πm/(r^3) Oscillation T = 2π√(I/mB) Galvanometer NIAB = kϕ ; ϕ/I = NAB/k , ϕ/V = NAB/kG Materials B = μ_0(H + M) , χ = M/H , μ_r = 1 + χ ; Curie χ ∝ 1/T",["F = q(E + v × B)","r = mv/qB = (√(2mK))/qB","T = 2πm/qB","r = (mv sin θ)/qB","p = (2πmv cos θ)/qB","f = qB/2πm","K_max = (q^2B^2R^2)/(2m)","dB = μ_0/4π(I dl × r)/(r^2)","(μ_0I)/4πd(sin α + sin β)","(μ_0I)/2πd","(μ_0Iθ)/4πR","(μ_0NI)/(2R)"],""],[2,9,6,"sec","Traps examiners reuse","",[],""],[2,9,7,"text","Traps examiners reuse","The electron’s direction. Do the right-hand rule for +q , then flip. Never switch hands. Same kinetic energy ≠ same momentum. r ∝ √mK/q . A proton and an alpha with equal K have equal radii. The period has no speed in it. Faster particles run bigger circles in the same time. Fields add as vectors. Before adding magnitudes, check with your hand that each piece points the same way. Pieces that contribute nothing: a wire at a point on its own line; straight leads aimed at the centre of an arc; outside currents in Ampère’s integral (not in the field!). Zero force ≠ zero field. A charge moving along B , or a loop in a uniform field, feels no force in a non-zero field. Opposite rule to charges: like currents attract. More turns, same voltage sensitivity — the coil’s resistance grows in step.",["+q","r ∝ √mK/q","K","B"],""],[2,9,8,"sec","Four problems that mix it all","",[],""],[2,9,9,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2017 · One or more correct","A uniform magnetic field B exists in the region between x = 0 and x = (3R)/2 (region 2 in the figure), pointing normally into the plane of the paper. A particle with charge +Q and momentum p directed along the x -axis enters region 2 from region 1 at the point P_1 (y = −R). Which of the following option(s) is/are correct? For B > 2/3p/QR , the particle will re-enter region 1 · For B = 8/13p/QR , the particle will enter region 3 through the point P_2 on the x -axis · When the particle re-enters region 1 through the longest possible path in region 2, the magnitude of the change in its linear momentum between P_1 and the point farthest from the y -axis is p/√2 · For a fixed B , for particles of the same charge Q and the same velocity v , the distance between P_1 and the point of re-entry into region 1 is inversely proportional to the mass of the particle",["B","x = 0","x = (3R)/2","+Q","p","x","P_1","y = −R","B > 2/3p/QR","B = 8/13p/QR","P_2","x"],""],[2,9,9,"sol","JEE Advanced 2017 · One or more correct","Inside region 2 the path is a circle of radius r = p/QB . For a positive charge moving along +x with B into the page, x × (−z) = +y : it curves upward, with its centre at (0, −R + r) . (A) It turns back into region 1 if its circle never reaches x = 3R/2 : r < (3R)/2 , i.e. B > (2p)/(3QR) . Correct. (B) With B = (8p)/(13QR) , r = (13R)/8 and the centre is at y = −R + (13R)/8 = (5R)/8 . Where the circle meets x = (3R)/2 : y = (5R)/8 − √(((13R)/8)^2 − ((3R)/2)^2) = (5R)/8 − √((169 − 144)/64) R = (5R)/8 − (5R)/8 = 0 It crosses into region 3 exactly on the x -axis, at P_2 . Correct. (C) The longest path that still returns has r = (3R)/2 : the circle just touches the far boundary, where the velocity is along +y . The momentum has turned from p x to p y , so ∣Δp∣ = p√2 , not p/√2 . Wrong. (D) It re-enters region 1 a distance 2r = (2mv)/QB above P_1 — proportional to the mass. Wrong. Answer (A), (B). The particle circles with r = p/QB , bending towards +y , with its centre directly above P_1 . Place the centre and use Pythagoras.",["r = p/QB","+x","B","x × (−z) = +y","(0, −R + r)","x = 3R/2","r < (3R)/2","B > (2p)/(3QR)","B = (8p)/(13QR)","r = (13R)/8","y = −R + (13R)/8 = (5R)/8","x = (3R)/2"],""],[2,9,10,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2018 · One or more correct","Two infinitely long straight wires lie in the xy -plane along the lines x = ±R . The wire at x = +R carries a constant current I_1 and the wire at x = −R carries a constant current I_2 (a current is positive if it flows along +j). A circular loop of radius R is suspended with its centre at (0, 0, √3R) , in a plane parallel to the xy -plane. It carries a constant current I clockwise as seen from above. Which statement(s) about the magnetic field B is/are true? If I_1 = I_2 , then B cannot be equal to zero at the origin (0, 0, 0) · If I_1 > 0 and I_2 < 0 , then B can be equal to zero at the origin · If I_1 < 0 and I_2 > 0 , then B can be equal to zero at the origin · If I_1 = I_2 , then the z -component of the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is (−(μ_0I)/(2R))",["xy","x = ±R","x = +R","I_1","x = −R","I_2","+j","R","(0, 0, √3R)","xy","I","B"],""],[2,9,10,"sol","JEE Advanced 2018 · One or more correct","Fields at the origin. The origin is a distance R from each wire. Wire at x = +R (current I_1j): the origin lies in the −x direction from it, and j × (−i) = +k . So it gives (μ_0I_1)/2πR k . Wire at x = −R (current I_2j): the origin lies in the +x direction, j × i = −k . So it gives −(μ_0I_2)/2πR k . The loop: clockwise seen from above means its field on its axis points down, along −k (non-zero). (A) I_1 = I_2 : the wires cancel, leaving the loop’s field. Not zero. Correct. (B) I_1 > 0 , I_2 < 0 : both wire terms point along +k , opposite to the loop’s −k . They can cancel. Correct. (C) I_1 < 0 , I_2 > 0 : both wire terms point along −k , the same way as the loop’s. They add. Wrong. (D) At the loop’s centre (0, 0, √3R) , each wire is 2R away. Working out j × r for each, their z -components are equal and opposite when I_1 = I_2 , while their x -components add. So the z -component comes from the loop alone: −(μ_0I)/(2R) . Correct. Answer (A), (B), (D). At the origin, find the direction of each wire’s field (± k ?) and the loop’s field (clockwise from above: which way along z ?).",["R","x = +R","I_1j","−x","j × (−i) = +k","(μ_0I_1)/2πR k","x = −R","I_2j","+x","j × i = −k","−(μ_0I_2)/2πR k","−k"],""],[2,9,11,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2017 · Single correct","A symmetric star-shaped conducting wire loop carries a steady current I as shown. The distance between diametrically opposite vertices of the star is 4a . The magnitude of the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is (μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 − 1] · (μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 + 1] · (μ_0I)/4πa 3 [√3 − 1] · (μ_0I)/4πa 3 [2 − √3]",["I","4a","(μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 − 1]","(μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 + 1]","(μ_0I)/4πa 3 [√3 − 1]","(μ_0I)/4πa 3 [2 − √3]"],""],[2,9,11,"sol","JEE Advanced 2017 · Single correct","The outer points are 2a from the centre. The star’s outline lies along the sides of two equilateral triangles with circumradius 2a , and each such side is at distance (inradius) 1/2 × 2a = a from the centre. Each of the 12 short sides runs from an outer point to an inner corner. Seen from the centre, measured from the perpendicular to its line, it stretches from 30^∘ (inner corner) to 60^∘ (outer point) — both on the same side of the foot. So each contributes (μ_0I)/4πa (sin 60^∘ − sin 30^∘) = (μ_0I)/4πa ⋅ (√3 − 1)/2 The current circulates the same way round the centre along all 12 sides, so they add: B = 12 × (μ_0I)/4πa ⋅ (√3 − 1)/2 = (μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 − 1] Answer (A). The star is two overlapping equilateral triangles. How far is each triangle’s side from the centre? Then each of the 12 sides of the star is a piece of such a line, with both ends on the same side of the foot of the perpendicular.",["2a","2a","1/2 × 2a = a","30^∘","60^∘","(μ_0I)/4πa (sin 60^∘ − sin 30^∘) = (μ_0I)/4πa ⋅ (√3 − 1)/2","B = 12 × (μ_0I)/4πa ⋅ (√3 − 1)/2 = (μ_0I)/4πa 6 [√3 − 1]"],""],[2,9,12,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2018 · Numerical answer","In the xy -plane, the region y > 0 has a uniform magnetic field B_1k and the region y < 0 has another uniform magnetic field B_2k . A positively charged particle is projected from the origin along the positive y -axis with speed v_0 = π m/s at t = 0 . Neglect gravity. Let t = T be the time when the particle crosses the x -axis from below for the first time. If B_2 = 4B_1 , find the average speed of the particle, in m/s, along the x -axis in the time interval T .",["xy","y > 0","B_1k","y < 0","B_2k","y","v_0 = π","t = 0","t = T","x","B_2 = 4B_1","x"],""],[2,9,12,"sol","JEE Advanced 2018 · Numerical answer","Above the axis. q (v_0j) × (B_1k) = qv_0B_1 i : the particle curves towards +x , completing a semicircle of radius r_1 = (mv_0)/(qB_1) in time πm/(qB_1) , and comes down through the axis at x = 2r_1 , moving along −j . Below the axis. Now q (−v_0j) × (B_2k) = −qv_0B_2 i : it curves back towards −x , a semicircle of radius r_2 = r_1/4 , taking πm/(qB_2) = 1/4 ⋅ πm/(qB_1) , and crosses the axis upward at x = 2r_1 − 2r_2 . Totals. Distance covered along x : 2r_1 + 2r_2 = 5/2r_1 . Time: T = 5/4 ⋅ πm/(qB_1) . v_x = 5/4 πm/qB_15/2 r_1 = 2/π ⋅ (qB_1r_1)/m = (2v_0)/π = 2 m/s Answer: 2. Speed is distance over time. (If you divide the displacement along x , 2r_1 − 2r_2 , by T you get the average velocity, 1.2 m/s — a different quantity.) Each half of the motion is a semicircle. Which way does each one curve? Then “speed along x ” means the distance covered along x , divided by the time.",["q (v_0j) × (B_1k) = qv_0B_1 i","+x","r_1 = (mv_0)/(qB_1)","πm/(qB_1)","x = 2r_1","−j","q (−v_0j) × (B_2k) = −qv_0B_2 i","−x","r_2 = r_1/4","πm/(qB_2) = 1/4 ⋅ πm/(qB_1)","x = 2r_1 − 2r_2","x"],""],[2,9,13,"story","Back to the compass","Ørsted’s needle swung because the current in his wire made a magnetic field (Biot–Savart), and the needle — a magnetic dipole made of countless atomic current loops — felt a torque m × B that turned it to line up (Lesson 6). Two moving-charge effects, one on each side. Every motor, loudspeaker, meter and MRI scanner in this chapter is the same pair of ideas. Next comes the twist that makes generators possible: a changing magnetic field can push charges too. That is Electromagnetic Induction.",["m × B"],""],[3,1,0,"lesson","Temperature & the Zeroth Law","Your fingers are terrible thermometers. Here is what temperature really measures, why a “zeroth” law had to be squeezed in before the first, and why every temperature scale is just a straight line.",[],""],[3,1,1,"sec","Your skin is lying to you","On a winter morning, touch a steel almirah and then a wooden door. The steel feels far colder. Now the awkward fact: both have been sitting in the same room all night, so they are at exactly the same temperature.",[],""],[3,1,2,"text","Your skin is lying to you","On a winter morning, touch a steel almirah and then a wooden door. The steel feels far colder. Now the awkward fact: both have been sitting in the same room all night, so they are at exactly the same temperature.",[],""],[3,1,3,"text","Your skin is lying to you","Your skin does not sense temperature. It senses how fast heat is leaving your hand. Steel conducts heat a few hundred times better than wood, so it drains your fingertips faster and your brain files that under “cold”. (We will put numbers on this in Lesson 7.)",[],""],[3,1,4,"text","Your skin is lying to you","So before a single formula, we need ideas that do not depend on anybody’s fingers.",[],""],[3,1,5,"story","The plan for this chapter","Four questions, in order. What is temperature? (Lessons 1–2.) What is heat, and how do we keep its accounts? (Lesson 3.) What do the laws of thermodynamics allow — and forbid? (Lessons 4–6.) How fast does heat actually travel? (Lessons 7–8.) Every JEE problem in this chapter is one of these four questions wearing a costume.",[],""],[3,1,6,"sec","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Pour hot tea into a cold steel tumbler. The tumbler warms, the tea cools, and after a while nothing changes any more. The two have reached thermal equilibrium.",[],""],[3,1,7,"text","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Pour hot tea into a cold steel tumbler. The tumbler warms, the tea cools, and after a while nothing changes any more. The two have reached thermal equilibrium.",[],""],[3,1,8,"text","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Now the law that sounds too obvious to be a law:",[],""],[3,1,9,"text","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Zeroth law. If bodies A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with a third body C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other.",[],""],[3,1,10,"text","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Why does it matter? Because C can be a thermometer. The zeroth law guarantees that there is one single property which decides whether two bodies will exchange heat when they touch. We call that property temperature:",[],""],[3,1,11,"text","Thermal equilibrium and the zeroth law","Two systems are in thermal equilibrium if, and only if, their temperatures are equal.",[],""],[3,1,12,"intuition","Why “zeroth”?","By the time physicists realised this statement had to be a law in its own right, the first and second laws were already famous and numbered. Since it logically comes before both, Ralph Fowler gave it the number zero in the 1930s. It is the only law of physics named like the ground floor of a building.",[],""],[3,1,13,"trap","Equal temperature does not mean equal energy","A bucket of water at 30^∘ C holds far more internal energy than a teaspoon of water at 30^∘ C — yet put them in contact and nothing flows. Heat flow is decided by temperature alone, never by “who has more energy”.",["30^∘ C","30^∘ C"],""],[3,1,14,"q","Which way does heat flow? · Single correct","A cup of tea at 60^∘ C is lowered into a swimming pool at 30^∘ C (the cup is sealed, so nothing mixes). The pool contains vastly more internal energy than the tea. Which statement is correct? Heat flows from the pool to the tea, because the pool has more internal energy. · No heat flows, because the two bodies are made of the same substance. · Heat flows from the tea to the pool, because the tea is at the higher temperature. · Heat flows both ways equally, so neither temperature changes.",["60^∘ C","30^∘ C"],""],[3,1,14,"sol","Which way does heat flow? · Single correct","The direction of heat flow is set by temperature, not by energy content. The tea is hotter, so heat flows from the tea to the pool until both are at the same temperature — which will be a hair above 30^∘ C , because the pool is enormous. Answer (C). Internal energy is like the amount of water in a tank; temperature is like the water level. Connect a tall thin tank to a wide shallow lake and water runs from the higher level to the lower, no matter which holds more.",["30^∘ C"],""],[3,1,15,"sec","Every temperature scale is a straight line","To build a thermometer you need three things:",[],""],[3,1,16,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","To build a thermometer you need three things:",[],""],[3,1,17,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","A thermometric property X — anything that changes steadily with temperature: the length of a mercury thread, the pressure of a gas held at fixed volume, the resistance of a platinum wire, the voltage of a thermocouple. Two fixed points that anyone can reproduce — traditionally melting ice and boiling water at 1 atm. Numbers assigned to those two points, with equal divisions in between.",["X"],""],[3,1,18,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","That is all a scale is: a straight-line map from X to a number t . So for any linear scale,",["X","t"],""],[3,1,19,"formula","Every temperature scale is a straight line","That is all a scale is: a straight-line map from X to a number t . So for any linear scale,",["(t − t_ice)/(t_(steam) − t_ice) = (X − X_ice)/(X_(steam) − X_ice)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>t</i> − <i>t</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">ice</span></sub></span><span><i>t</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">steam</span></sub> − <i>t</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">ice</span></sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>X</i> − <i>X</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">ice</span></sub></span><span><i>X</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">steam</span></sub> − <i>X</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">ice</span></sub></span></span>"],[3,1,20,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","Read it as “what fraction of the way from ice to steam are we?” — that fraction is the same on every scale. This one line solves every conversion, every “faulty thermometer” and every “new scale” problem JEE has ever set.",[],""],[3,1,21,"table","Every temperature scale is a straight line","Scale Ice point Steam point Divisions between Celsius 0^∘ C 100^∘ C 100 Fahrenheit 32^∘ F 212^∘ F 180 Kelvin 273.15 K 373.15 K 100",["0^∘ C","100^∘ C","32^∘ F","212^∘ F","273.15 K","373.15 K"],""],[3,1,22,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","Apply the fraction rule to all three at once:",[],""],[3,1,23,"formula","Every temperature scale is a straight line","Apply the fraction rule to all three at once:",["C/100 = (F − 32)/180 = (K − 273.15)/100 ⟹ F = 9/5C + 32, K = C + 273.15"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>C</i></span><span>100</span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>F</i> − 32</span><span>180</span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>K</i> − 273.15</span><span>100</span></span> ⟹ <i>F</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>9</span><span>5</span></span><i>C</i> + 32, <i>K</i> = <i>C</i> + 273.15"],[3,1,24,"trap","Never add 273 to a temperature difference","A change of 1^∘ C is a change of 1 K and of 1.8^∘ F . So ΔT = 20^∘ C = 20 K , not 293 K . The +273 shifts the zero; it does not stretch the scale.",["1^∘ C","1 K","1.8^∘ F","ΔT = 20^∘ C = 20 K","293 K","+273"],""],[3,1,25,"text","Every temperature scale is a straight line","A fun consequence: set F = C in the formula and you get C = −40 . At −40^∘ the two scales agree, so nobody has to ask “Celsius or Fahrenheit?”.",["F = C","C = −40","−40^∘"],""],[3,1,26,"q","Double trouble · Numerical answer","At what temperature, in ^∘ C , is the reading on the Fahrenheit scale exactly twice the reading on the Celsius scale?",["^∘ C"],""],[3,1,26,"sol","Double trouble · Numerical answer","2C = 9/5C + 32 ⟹ 1/5C = 32 ⟹ C = 160^∘ C . Check: 160^∘ C = 9/5(160) + 32 = 320^∘ F . Exactly double. Put F = 2C into F = 9/5C + 32 .",["2C = 9/5C + 32 ⟹ 1/5C = 32 ⟹ C = 160^∘ C .","160^∘ C = 9/5(160) + 32 = 320^∘ F","F = 2C","F = 9/5C + 32"],""],[3,1,27,"pyq","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","A thermometer graduated according to a linear scale reads a value x_0 when in contact with boiling water, and x_0/3 when in contact with ice. What is the temperature of an object in ^∘ C , if this thermometer in contact with the object reads x_0/2 ? 25 · 60 · 40 · 35",["x_0","x_0/3","^∘ C","x_0/2","25","60","40","35"],""],[3,1,27,"sol","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","Use the fraction rule with X_ice = x_0/3 and X_(steam) = x_0 : (t − 0)/(100 − 0) = x_0 − x_0/3x_0/2 − x_0/3 = (1/6)/(2/3) = 1/4 ⟹ t = 25^∘ C . The reading is a quarter of the way from ice to steam, so the temperature is a quarter of the way from 0 to 100 . Answer (A). What fraction of the way from the ice reading to the steam reading is x_0/2 ?",["X_ice = x_0/3","X_(steam) = x_0","(t − 0)/(100 − 0) = x_0 − x_0/3x_0/2 − x_0/3 = (1/6)/(2/3) = 1/4 ⟹ t = 25^∘ C .","0","100","x_0/2"],""],[3,1,28,"sec","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","Here is a problem. A mercury thermometer and an alcohol thermometer agree at the two fixed points (they were made to), but disagree slightly in between, because mercury and alcohol do not expand in exactly the same way. Whose “ 50^∘ ” is the real one?",[],""],[3,1,29,"text","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","Here is a problem. A mercury thermometer and an alcohol thermometer agree at the two fixed points (they were made to), but disagree slightly in between, because mercury and alcohol do not expand in exactly the same way. Whose “ 50^∘ ” is the real one?",["50^∘"],""],[3,1,30,"text","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","Nature offers a referee. Trap a small amount of any gas at constant volume and measure its pressure. As long as the gas is dilute, the pressure rises in a perfect straight line with temperature — and every gas gives the same answer. Extend those straight lines backwards and something remarkable happens:",[],""],[3,1,31,"figure","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","Constant-volume gas thermometers. Different gases, different slopes — but every line points at the same spot.",[],""],[3,1,32,"text","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","All of them hit zero pressure at −273.15^∘ C . That temperature is absolute zero, and the Kelvin scale simply starts counting from there:",["−273.15^∘ C"],""],[3,1,33,"formula","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","All of them hit zero pressure at −273.15^∘ C . That temperature is absolute zero, and the Kelvin scale simply starts counting from there:",["T (K) = t (^∘ C) + 273.15"],"<i>T</i> (<span class=\"fx-t\">K</span>) = <i>t</i> (<sup> ∘ </sup><span class=\"fx-t\">C</span>) + 273.15"],[3,1,34,"text","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","On this scale the gas thermometer becomes beautifully simple — pressure is directly proportional to temperature:",[],""],[3,1,35,"formula","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","On this scale the gas thermometer becomes beautifully simple — pressure is directly proportional to temperature:",["T_1/T_2 = P_1/P_2 (constant volume) ⟹ T = 273.16 K × P/(P_tr)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i><sub>1</sub></span><span><i>P</i><sub>2</sub></span></span> (<span class=\"fx-t\">constant volume</span>) ⟹ <i>T</i> = 273.16 <span class=\"fx-t\">K</span> × <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">tr</span></sub></span></span>"],[3,1,36,"text","The gas thermometer and absolute zero","Here P_tr is the pressure the thermometer shows at the triple point of water — the one unique state (273.16 K , i.e. 0.01^∘ C) where ice, liquid water and water vapour coexist. For decades that single point defined the kelvin. Since 2019 the kelvin has been defined by fixing the value of the Boltzmann constant instead, but the triple point still sits at 273.16 K to within a few parts in ten million.",["P_tr","273.16 K","0.01^∘ C","273.16 K"],""],[3,1,37,"intuition","Why nothing is colder than 0 K","A gas cannot have negative pressure, so the straight line cannot continue below the axis. Absolute zero is the floor: the state from which no more thermal energy can be taken out. That is why kelvin temperatures are never negative — and why every gas law and every radiation law in this chapter demands kelvin, not Celsius.",[],""],[3,1,38,"q","Gas thermometer · Single correct","A constant-volume gas thermometer shows a pressure of 40 kPa at the triple point of water. When its bulb is placed in a hot oil bath the pressure reads 60 kPa . The temperature of the oil is closest to 137^∘ C · 410^∘ C · 150^∘ C · 182^∘ C",["40 kPa","60 kPa","137^∘ C","410^∘ C","150^∘ C","182^∘ C"],""],[3,1,38,"sol","Gas thermometer · Single correct","T = 273.16 K × 60/40 = 409.7 K . In Celsius: 409.7 − 273.15 ≈ 137^∘ C . Answer (A). Option (B) is the classic slip — reading the kelvin value as Celsius. Option (C) comes from scaling the Celsius temperature by 1.5, which is meaningless: ratios only make sense on the absolute scale. The gas scale gives kelvin. Convert at the very end.",["T = 273.16 K × 60/40 = 409.7 K .","409.7 − 273.15 ≈ 137^∘ C"],""],[3,1,39,"recap","Recap","Temperature is the property that is equal for bodies in thermal equilibrium (zeroth law). Heat flows from higher temperature to lower — never “from more energy to less”. Every linear scale obeys the fraction rule: (t − t_ice)/(t_(steam) − t_ice) = (X − X_ice)/(X_(steam) − X_ice) . F = 9/5C + 32 , K = C + 273.15 . A difference of 1^∘ C equals 1 K equals 1.8^∘ F . Constant-volume gas thermometer: T ∝ P , with T in kelvin. All dilute gases extrapolate to absolute zero, −273.15^∘ C .",["(t − t_ice)/(t_(steam) − t_ice) = (X − X_ice)/(X_(steam) − X_ice)","F = 9/5C + 32","K = C + 273.15","1^∘ C","1 K","1.8^∘ F","T ∝ P","T","−273.15^∘ C"],""],[3,2,0,"lesson","Thermal Expansion","Heat something and it grows — rails, bridges, mercury threads, even the holes in things. One tiny coefficient explains clocks that run slow, jam-jar lids that won’t budge, and why fish survive the winter.",[],""],[3,2,1,"sec","Why things grow when they are heated","Picture the atoms of a solid as balls joined by springs, all jiggling. Heat the solid and they jiggle harder. If the springs were perfectly symmetric, the atoms would just swing wider about the same average positions and nothing would expand.",[],""],[3,2,2,"text","Why things grow when they are heated","Picture the atoms of a solid as balls joined by springs, all jiggling. Heat the solid and they jiggle harder. If the springs were perfectly symmetric, the atoms would just swing wider about the same average positions and nothing would expand.",[],""],[3,2,3,"text","Why things grow when they are heated","But real bonds are lopsided: it is much harder to squash two atoms together than to pull them apart. So when the jiggling grows, each atom spends more time on the “far” side, the average spacing increases, and the whole solid swells.",[],""],[3,2,4,"text","Why things grow when they are heated","For everyday temperature changes the growth is proportional to the original length and to the temperature rise:",[],""],[3,2,5,"formula","Why things grow when they are heated","For everyday temperature changes the growth is proportional to the original length and to the temperature rise:",["ΔL = α L_0 ΔT ⟺ L = L_0 (1 + α ΔT)"],"Δ<i>L</i> = <i>α</i> <i>L</i><sub>0</sub> Δ<i>T</i> ⟺ <i>L</i> = <i>L</i><sub>0</sub> (1 + <i>α</i> Δ<i>T</i>)"],[3,2,6,"text","Why things grow when they are heated","α is the coefficient of linear expansion. Its unit is K ^(−1) — or ^∘ C ^(−1) , the same thing, because only a temperature difference appears.",["α","K ^(−1)","^∘ C ^(−1)"],""],[3,2,7,"table","Why things grow when they are heated","Material α (in 10^(−5) K ^(−1)) Aluminium 2.3 Brass 1.9 Copper 1.7 Steel / iron 1.2 Ordinary glass 0.9 Pyrex glass 0.32 Invar (iron + 36% nickel) 0.12",["α","10^(−5) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,2,8,"intuition","Tiny number, big structure","A 1 m steel rod heated through 100^∘ C grows by just 1.2 mm . But scale it up: the Eiffel Tower is about 300 m of iron. Between a freezing winter night and a hot summer afternoon — say a 35^∘ C swing — the formula gives ΔL ≈ (1.2 × 10^(−5))(300 m)(35) ≈ 13 cm . An estimate, not a measurement (the real tower never heats evenly) — but it tells you why engineers take a “negligible” coefficient very seriously.",["1 m","100^∘ C","1.2 mm","300 m","35^∘ C","ΔL ≈ (1.2 × 10^(−5))(300 m)(35) ≈ 13 cm ."],""],[3,2,8,"formula","Tiny number, big structure","ht and a hot summer afternoon — say a 35^∘ C swing — the formula gives ΔL ≈ (1.2 × 10^(−5))(300 m)(35) ≈ 13 cm . An estimate, not a measurement (the real tower never heats evenly) — but it tells you why engineers take a “negligible” coefficient very seriously.",["ΔL ≈ (1.2 × 10^(−5))(300 m)(35) ≈ 13 cm ."],"Δ<i>L</i> ≈ (1.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup>)(300 <span class=\"fx-t\">m</span>)(35) ≈ 13 <span class=\"fx-t\">cm</span>."],[3,2,10,"story","The alloy that won a Nobel Prize by doing nothing","In 1896 Charles Édouard Guillaume found that iron alloyed with about 36% nickel hardly expands at all — roughly a tenth as much as ordinary steel. He named it Invar (for invariable). It made accurate pendulum clocks and surveying tapes possible, and earned him the 1920 Nobel Prize in Physics.",[],""],[3,2,11,"sec","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","Heat a square plate of side L . Each side becomes L(1 + αΔT) , so the area becomes",[],""],[3,2,12,"text","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","Heat a square plate of side L . Each side becomes L(1 + αΔT) , so the area becomes",["L","L(1 + αΔT)"],""],[3,2,13,"formula","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","Heat a square plate of side L . Each side becomes L(1 + αΔT) , so the area becomes",["A = L^2(1 + αΔT)^2 = L^2 (1 + 2αΔT + α^2ΔT^2)."],"<i>A</i> = <i>L</i><sup>2</sup>(1 + <i>α</i>Δ<i>T</i>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>L</i><sup>2</sup> (1 + 2<i>α</i>Δ<i>T</i> + <i>α</i><sup>2</sup>Δ<i>T</i><sup>2</sup>)."],[3,2,14,"text","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","With αΔT ∼ 10^(−3) , the last term is a thousand times smaller than the middle one. Drop it. The same argument with a cube gives a factor of 3. So",["αΔT ∼ 10^(−3)"],""],[3,2,15,"formula","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","With αΔT ∼ 10^(−3) , the last term is a thousand times smaller than the middle one. Drop it. The same argument with a cube gives a factor of 3. So",["ΔA = βA_0 ΔT, ΔV = γV_0 ΔT, α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3"],"Δ<i>A</i> = <i>β</i><i>A</i><sub>0</sub> Δ<i>T</i>, Δ<i>V</i> = <i>γ</i><i>V</i><sub>0</sub> Δ<i>T</i>, <i>α</i> : <i>β</i> : <i>γ</i> = 1 : 2 : 3"],[3,2,16,"text","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","Since mass does not change while volume grows, density falls:",[],""],[3,2,17,"formula","Area and volume: the 1 : 2 : 3 rule","Since mass does not change while volume grows, density falls:",["ρ = ρ_0/(1 + γ ΔT) ≈ ρ_0 (1 − γ ΔT)."],"<i>ρ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><sub>0</sub></span><span>1 + <i>γ</i> Δ<i>T</i></span></span> ≈ <i>ρ</i><sub>0</sub> (1 − <i>γ</i> Δ<i>T</i>)."],[3,2,18,"trap","Holes grow too","Thermal expansion is a photographic enlargement: every length in the object is multiplied by the same factor (1 + αΔT) — the diameter of a hole, the gap in a broken ring, the distance between two scratches. A hole expands exactly as if it were filled with the same material. That is why running hot water over a stuck metal lid loosens it, and how a steel rim is fitted on a wheel: heat the rim, slip it on, let it cool and grip.",["(1 + αΔT)"],""],[3,2,19,"q","The hole story · Single correct","A thin copper plate has a circular hole drilled through its centre. The plate is heated uniformly. The diameter of the hole decreases, because the metal expands inwards into the hole · increases, in the same proportion as every other length in the plate · stays the same, because there is no material in the hole to expand · increases or decreases depending on how big the hole is",[],""],[3,2,19,"sol","The hole story · Single correct","Imagine the plate before the hole was cut, and draw a circle where the hole will be. On heating, that disc of copper expands — and it must still fit exactly into the space around it. So the boundary of the hole grows by exactly the same factor (1 + αΔT) as the disc that used to fill it. Answer (B).",["(1 + αΔT)"],""],[3,2,20,"q","Mind the gap · Numerical answer","Steel rails, each 12 m long, are laid on a morning when the temperature is 15^∘ C . On the hottest afternoon of the year the rails reach 55^∘ C . What is the smallest gap, in mm, that should be left between neighbouring rails so that they just touch on that afternoon? Take α_(steel) = 1.2 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1) .",["12 m","15^∘ C","55^∘ C","α_(steel) = 1.2 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,2,20,"sol","Mind the gap · Numerical answer","Each rail lengthens by ΔL = αL ΔT = (1.2 × 10^(−5))(12 m)(40) = 5.76 × 10^(−3) m = 5.76 mm . Each rail pushes half of that into the gap at each end, and each gap receives a half from the rail on either side — so every gap must be 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 whole ΔL : 5.76 mm. Each rail grows by αL ΔT in total — half at each end. How much does each gap have to swallow?",["ΔL = αL ΔT = (1.2 × 10^(−5))(12 m)(40) = 5.76 × 10^(−3) m = 5.76 mm .","1/2 + 1/2 = 1","ΔL","αL ΔT"],""],[3,2,21,"sec","When expansion is not allowed: thermal stress","Clamp a rod between two rigid walls and heat it by ΔT . It wants to grow by αLΔT , but the walls refuse. In effect the walls are compressing it by exactly that amount, so",[],""],[3,2,22,"text","When expansion is not allowed: thermal stress","Clamp a rod between two rigid walls and heat it by ΔT . It wants to grow by αLΔT , but the walls refuse. In effect the walls are compressing it by exactly that amount, so",["ΔT","αLΔT"],""],[3,2,23,"formula","When expansion is not allowed: thermal stress","Clamp a rod between two rigid walls and heat it by ΔT . It wants to grow by αLΔT , but the walls refuse. In effect the walls are compressing it by exactly that amount, so",["strain = ΔL/L = α ΔT, stress = Yα ΔT, F = YA α ΔT."],"<span class=\"fx-t\">strain</span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>L</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> = <i>α</i> Δ<i>T</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">stress</span> = <i>Y</i><i>α</i> Δ<i>T</i>, <i>F</i> = <i>Y</i><i>A</i> <i>α</i> Δ<i>T</i>."],[3,2,24,"text","When expansion is not allowed: thermal stress","Notice what is missing: the length. A long rod and a short rod of the same material feel the same stress.",[],""],[3,2,25,"intuition","Why bridges have teeth","For steel, Y = 2 × 10^11 Pa . A 40^∘ C rise gives a stress of (2 × 10^11)(1.2 × 10^(−5))(40) ≈ 10^8 Pa — about a thousand atmospheres. Rails without gaps buckle sideways in a heatwave; bridges are built with comb-like expansion joints so they can breathe.",["Y = 2 × 10^11 Pa","40^∘ C","(2 × 10^11)(1.2 × 10^(−5))(40) ≈ 10^8 Pa"],""],[3,2,26,"text","When expansion is not allowed: thermal stress","The bimetallic strip. Rivet a strip of brass to a strip of steel and heat the pair. Brass (α = 1.9) wants to grow more than steel (α = 1.2), and the only way both can be satisfied is for the strip to curl, with the brass on the outside of the curve. Cool it and it curls the other way. That bending switch is the thermostat inside an electric iron.",["α = 1.9","α = 1.2"],""],[3,2,27,"pyq","JEE Main 2014 · Single correct","The pressure that has to be applied to the ends of a steel wire of length 10 cm to keep its length constant when its temperature is raised by 100^∘ C is (for steel, Young’s modulus is 2 × 10^11 N m ^(−2) and the coefficient of thermal expansion is 1.1 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)) 2.2 × 10^8 Pa · 2.2 × 10^9 Pa · 2.2 × 10^7 Pa · 2.2 × 10^6 Pa",["10 cm","100^∘ C","2 × 10^11 N m ^(−2)","1.1 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)","2.2 × 10^8 Pa","2.2 × 10^9 Pa","2.2 × 10^7 Pa","2.2 × 10^6 Pa"],""],[3,2,27,"sol","JEE Main 2014 · Single correct","Keeping the length constant means cancelling the thermal strain αΔT with an equal compressive strain: stress = Yα ΔT = (2 × 10^11)(1.1 × 10^(−5))(100) = 2.2 × 10^8 Pa . The 10 cm never enters — thermal stress does not depend on length. Answer (A). One number in the question is a decoy.",["αΔT","stress = Yα ΔT = (2 × 10^11)(1.1 × 10^(−5))(100) = 2.2 × 10^8 Pa .","10 cm"],""],[3,2,28,"pyq","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","An external pressure P is applied on a cube at 0^∘ C so that it is equally compressed from all sides. K is the bulk modulus of the material of the cube and α is its coefficient of linear expansion. Suppose we want to bring the cube back to its original size by heating. The temperature should be raised by P/3αK · P/αK · 3α/PK · 3PKα",["P","0^∘ C","K","α","P/3αK","P/αK","3α/PK","3PKα"],""],[3,2,28,"sol","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","Squeezing reduces the volume by a fraction ΔV/V = P/K (definition of bulk modulus). Heating increases it by a fraction γ ΔT = 3α ΔT . Set them equal: 3α ΔT = P/K ⟹ ΔT = P/3αK. Answer (A). The whole question is a test of γ = 3α . Pressure shrinks the volume. Which expansion coefficient undoes a volume change?",["ΔV/V = P/K","γ ΔT = 3α ΔT","3α ΔT = P/K ⟹ ΔT = P/3αK.","γ = 3α"],""],[3,2,29,"sec","Clocks that lose time","A pendulum clock ticks with period τ = 2π√(L/g) . Warm the room and the pendulum rod lengthens, so each swing takes longer and the clock runs slow. Since τ ∝ L^(1/2) ,",[],""],[3,2,30,"text","Clocks that lose time","A pendulum clock ticks with period τ = 2π√(L/g) . Warm the room and the pendulum rod lengthens, so each swing takes longer and the clock runs slow. Since τ ∝ L^(1/2) ,",["τ = 2π√(L/g)","τ ∝ L^(1/2)"],""],[3,2,31,"formula","Clocks that lose time","A pendulum clock ticks with period τ = 2π√(L/g) . Warm the room and the pendulum rod lengthens, so each swing takes longer and the clock runs slow. Since τ ∝ L^(1/2) ,",["Δτ/τ = 1/2 ΔL/L = 1/2 α Δθ ⟹ time lost per day = 1/2 α Δθ × 86 400 s ."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>τ</i></span><span><i>τ</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>Δ<i>L</i></span><span><i>L</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> <i>α</i> Δ<i>θ</i> ⟹ <span class=\"fx-t\">time lost per day</span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> <i>α</i> Δ<i>θ</i> × 86 400 <span class=\"fx-t\">s</span>."],[3,2,32,"text","Clocks that lose time","Hotter than the “correct” temperature → loses time. Colder → gains time. (Now you see why clockmakers loved Invar.)",[],""],[3,2,33,"pyq","JEE Main 2016 · Single correct","A pendulum clock loses 12 s a day if the temperature is 40^∘ C and gains 4 s a day if the temperature is 20^∘ C . The temperature at which the clock will show correct time, and the coefficient of linear expansion α of the metal of the pendulum shaft, are respectively 60^∘ C ; α = 1.85 × 10^(−4) /^∘ C · 30^∘ C ; α = 1.85 × 10^(−3) /^∘ C · 25^∘ C ; α = 1.85 × 10^(−5) /^∘ C · 55^∘ C ; α = 1.85 × 10^(−2) /^∘ C",["12 s","40^∘ C","4 s","20^∘ C","α","60^∘ C","α = 1.85 × 10^(−4) /^∘ C","30^∘ C","α = 1.85 × 10^(−3) /^∘ C","25^∘ C","α = 1.85 × 10^(−5) /^∘ C","55^∘ C"],""],[3,2,33,"sol","JEE Main 2016 · Single correct","Let the clock be correct at θ_0 . 12 = 1/2 α (40 − θ_0) × 86400, 4 = 1/2 α (θ_0 − 20) × 86400. Divide: (40 − θ_0)/(θ_0 − 20) = 3 ⟹ θ_0 = 25^∘ C . Put it back into the second equation: α = (2 × 4)/((5)(86400)) = 1.85 × 10^(−5) /^∘ C . Answer (C). You could also have spotted it without algebra — only one option has a believable α for a metal. Write “seconds lost per day = 1/2α Δθ × 86400 ” twice and divide.",["θ_0","12 = 1/2 α (40 − θ_0) × 86400, 4 = 1/2 α (θ_0 − 20) × 86400.","(40 − θ_0)/(θ_0 − 20) = 3 ⟹ θ_0 = 25^∘ C","α = (2 × 4)/((5)(86400)) = 1.85 × 10^(−5) /^∘ C .","α","= 1/2α Δθ × 86400"],""],[3,2,34,"sec","Liquids — and the strange case of water","A liquid has no shape of its own, so only volume expansion makes sense. Liquids expand much more than solids: for mercury γ ≈ 1.8 × 10^(−4) K ^(−1) , against about 2.7 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1) for ordinary glass.",[],""],[3,2,35,"text","Liquids — and the strange case of water","A liquid has no shape of its own, so only volume expansion makes sense. Liquids expand much more than solids: for mercury γ ≈ 1.8 × 10^(−4) K ^(−1) , against about 2.7 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1) for ordinary glass.",["γ ≈ 1.8 × 10^(−4) K ^(−1)","2.7 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,2,36,"text","Liquids — and the strange case of water","But a liquid always sits in a container, and the container expands too. What you see is only the difference:",[],""],[3,2,37,"formula","Liquids — and the strange case of water","But a liquid always sits in a container, and the container expands too. What you see is only the difference:",["γ_(apparent) = γ_(liquid) − γ_(vessel)"],"<i>γ</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">apparent</span></sub> = <i>γ</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">liquid</span></sub> − <i>γ</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">vessel</span></sub>"],[3,2,38,"text","Liquids — and the strange case of water","The thread in a thermometer climbs only because mercury out-expands glass. If the two had equal γ , the level would not move at all.",["γ"],""],[3,2,39,"text","Liquids — and the strange case of water","Water breaks the rule. Between 0^∘ C and about 4^∘ C , water contracts when heated. Its density is greatest near 4^∘ C :",["0^∘ C","4^∘ C","4^∘ C"],""],[3,2,40,"intuition","Why lakes freeze from the top — and fish survive","As winter sets in, surface water cools, becomes denser and sinks, stirring the whole lake down towards 4^∘ C . Cool the surface further and it now gets lighter, so it stays on top and eventually freezes there. Ice is lighter still, so it floats — and acts as a blanket. The deep water stays liquid at about 4^∘ C all winter. If water behaved like a normal liquid, lakes would freeze from the bottom up, solid.",["4^∘ C","4^∘ C"],""],[3,2,41,"q","Overflow · Numerical answer","A glass flask of volume 1000 cm ^3 is filled to the brim with mercury at 20^∘ C . Flask and mercury are then heated to 100^∘ C . How many cm ^3 of mercury overflow? Take γ_(mercury) = 1.8 × 10^(−4) K ^(−1) and α_(glass) = 9 × 10^(−6) K ^(−1) .",["1000 cm ^3","20^∘ C","100^∘ C","cm ^3","γ_(mercury) = 1.8 × 10^(−4) K ^(−1)","α_(glass) = 9 × 10^(−6) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,2,41,"sol","Overflow · Numerical answer","The cavity of the flask expands like solid glass: γ_(glass) = 3α = 2.7 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1) . V_(overflow) = V_0 (γ_Hg − γ_(glass)) ΔT = 1000 (1.8 − 0.27) × 10^(−4) × 80 = 12.2 cm ^3. Ignore the flask and you would get 14.4 cm ^3 — the real expansion of the mercury. What spills is only the apparent expansion. The flask’s capacity grows as well — with γ_(glass) = 3α_(glass) .",["γ_(glass) = 3α = 2.7 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)","V_(overflow) = V_0 (γ_Hg − γ_(glass)) ΔT = 1000 (1.8 − 0.27) × 10^(−4) × 80 = 12.2 cm ^3.","14.4 cm ^3","γ_(glass) = 3α_(glass)"],""],[3,2,42,"recap","Recap","ΔL = αL_0ΔT , ΔA = βA_0ΔT , ΔV = γV_0ΔT , with α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3 . Expansion is a photographic enlargement — holes and gaps grow by the same factor as the material. Expansion prevented → thermal stress = YαΔT (independent of length). Pendulum clock: fractional change in period = 1/2αΔθ . Hot → loses time. Liquids: γ_(apparent) = γ_(liquid) − γ_(vessel) . Water is densest near 4^∘ C .",["ΔL = αL_0ΔT","ΔA = βA_0ΔT","ΔV = γV_0ΔT","α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3","= YαΔT","= 1/2αΔθ","γ_(apparent) = γ_(liquid) − γ_(vessel)","4^∘ C"],""],[3,3,0,"lesson","Calorimetry","Heat is not something a body has — it is energy on the move. Learn to keep its accounts: specific heat, latent heat, and the single bookkeeping rule that cracks every mixing problem.",[],""],[3,3,1,"sec","So what is heat, really?","For most of the 1700s, scientists believed heat was an invisible, weightless fluid called caloric that soaked into hot bodies and trickled into cold ones. It was a good theory. It was also wrong, and the proof came from a cannon factory.",[],""],[3,3,2,"text","So what is heat, really?","For most of the 1700s, scientists believed heat was an invisible, weightless fluid called caloric that soaked into hot bodies and trickled into cold ones. It was a good theory. It was also wrong, and the proof came from a cannon factory.",[],""],[3,3,3,"text","So what is heat, really?","In 1798 Count Rumford was supervising the boring of brass cannon at the arsenal in Munich. A blunt borer, turned by horses, made the metal fiercely hot — hot enough, with the barrel sunk in a tank of water, to bring the water to the boil in about two and a half hours. And it kept coming, for as long as the horses kept walking. If caloric were a substance stored in the brass, it should eventually run out. It never did. The only thing being fed in was motion.",[],""],[3,3,4,"text","So what is heat, really?","Half a century later James Joule made it quantitative. He let falling weights spin a paddle wheel inside an insulated tub of water and measured the tiny temperature rise. The warming was always in strict proportion to the work done. His 1850 result corresponds to about 4.15 joules per calorie; today’s value is",["4.15"],""],[3,3,5,"formula","So what is heat, really?","ve. He let falling weights spin a paddle wheel inside an insulated tub of water and measured the tiny temperature rise. The warming was always in strict proportion to the work done. His 1850 result corresponds to about 4.15 joules per calorie; today’s value is",["1 cal = 4.186 J (JEE usually rounds this to 4.2 J)."],"1 <span class=\"fx-t\">cal</span> = 4.186 <span class=\"fx-t\">J</span> (<span class=\"fx-t\">JEE usually rounds this to </span>4.2 <span class=\"fx-t\">J</span>)."],[3,3,6,"text","So what is heat, really?","So here is the modern definition, and it is worth reading twice:",[],""],[3,3,7,"text","So what is heat, really?","Heat is energy in transit — energy that flows from one body to another because of a temperature difference.",[],""],[3,3,8,"trap","A body never “contains heat”","What a body contains is internal energy. Heat is the name for energy only while it is crossing a boundary — like rain, which stops being “rain” once it is in the lake. Saying “this water has a lot of heat” is as wrong as saying “this lake has a lot of rain”.",[],""],[3,3,9,"story","The honeymoon experiment","If falling water turns its energy of motion into warmth, the pool at the foot of a waterfall should be slightly warmer than the top. In 1847 Joule — on his honeymoon in the Alps — and William Thomson (later Lord Kelvin) planned to test exactly this at a waterfall near Chamonix. It proved impractical. You, however, can do it on paper in the next question.",[],""],[3,3,10,"sec","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Give the same heat to 1 kg of water and 1 kg of copper. The copper’s temperature shoots up more than ten times as far. Materials differ in how much energy they demand per degree:",[],""],[3,3,11,"text","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Give the same heat to 1 kg of water and 1 kg of copper. The copper’s temperature shoots up more than ten times as far. Materials differ in how much energy they demand per degree:",["1 kg","1 kg"],""],[3,3,12,"formula","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Give the same heat to 1 kg of water and 1 kg of copper. The copper’s temperature shoots up more than ten times as far. Materials differ in how much energy they demand per degree:",["Q = m c ΔT"],"<i>Q</i> = <i>m</i> <i>c</i> Δ<i>T</i>"],[3,3,13,"text","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","c is the specific heat capacity — the heat needed to warm unit mass by one degree.",["c"],""],[3,3,14,"table","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Substance c in J kg ^(−1) K ^(−1) in cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1) Water 4186 1.00 Ice 2100 0.50 Aluminium 900 0.215 Iron 450 0.108 Copper 386 0.092 Mercury 140 0.033 Lead 128 0.031",["c","J kg ^(−1) K ^(−1)","cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)"],""],[3,3,15,"intuition","Water, the heavyweight","Water’s specific heat is enormous — about eleven times copper’s. That is why it is the coolant in car radiators and the filling in hot-water bottles, and why coastal cities have milder summers and winters than inland ones: the sea soaks up and gives back heat while barely changing its own temperature.",[],""],[3,3,16,"text","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Three close relatives of c that JEE uses freely:",["c"],""],[3,3,17,"text","Specific heat: how stubborn is a material?","Heat capacity of a whole body: C = mc (in J K ^(−1)). Water equivalent: the mass of water with the same heat capacity as the body, w = mc/(c_(water)) . A 100 g copper calorimeter with c = 0.1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1) is “worth” 10 g of water — just add 10 g to the water and forget the copper. Molar heat capacity: heat per mole per degree, C = Mc . For gases this becomes a story of its own (Lesson 5).",["C = mc","J K ^(−1)","w = mc/(c_(water))","100 g","c = 0.1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)","10 g","10 g","C = Mc"],""],[3,3,18,"q","Joule’s waterfall · Numerical answer","Water goes over the Victoria Falls and drops 108 m . Suppose all of its lost potential energy ends up warming the water itself. By how many kelvin does its temperature rise? Take g = 9.8 m s ^(−2) and c_(water) = 4200 J kg ^(−1) K ^(−1) .",["108 m","g = 9.8 m s ^(−2)","c_(water) = 4200 J kg ^(−1) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,3,18,"sol","Joule’s waterfall · Numerical answer","mgh = mc ΔT ⟹ ΔT = gh/c = ((9.8)(108))/(4200) ≈ 0.25 K . A quarter of a degree, from one of the tallest curtains of water on Earth — and the mass cancelled, so a bucketful and a whole river warm by the same amount. Now you can see how small an effect Joule was hunting for — and why a real Alpine waterfall was never going to settle it. mgh = mc ΔT . What happens to the mass?",["mgh = mc ΔT ⟹ ΔT = gh/c = ((9.8)(108))/(4200) ≈ 0.25 K .","mgh = mc ΔT"],""],[3,3,19,"sec","Latent heat: heating without warming","Keep heating a block of ice at 0^∘ C and something odd happens: the thermometer stops moving. Energy is pouring in, but the temperature stays at 0^∘ C until the last crystal has melted. The same thing happens at 100^∘ C while water boils.",[],""],[3,3,20,"text","Latent heat: heating without warming","Keep heating a block of ice at 0^∘ C and something odd happens: the thermometer stops moving. Energy is pouring in, but the temperature stays at 0^∘ C until the last crystal has melted. The same thing happens at 100^∘ C while water boils.",["0^∘ C","0^∘ C","100^∘ C"],""],[3,3,21,"text","Latent heat: heating without warming","During a change of phase, the energy goes into loosening bonds, not into making molecules move faster. The heat needed per unit mass is the latent heat L (latent = hidden):",["L"],""],[3,3,22,"formula","Latent heat: heating without warming","During a change of phase, the energy goes into loosening bonds, not into making molecules move faster. The heat needed per unit mass is the latent heat L (latent = hidden):",["Q = mL"],"<i>Q</i> = <i>m</i><i>L</i>"],[3,3,23,"table","Latent heat: heating without warming","Change (water, 1 atm) L in J kg ^(−1) in cal g ^(−1) Fusion: ice ⇌ water at 0^∘ C 3.34 × 10^5 ≈ 80 Vaporisation: water ⇌ steam at 100^∘ C 2.26 × 10^6 ≈ 540",["L","J kg ^(−1)","cal g ^(−1)","0^∘ C","3.34 × 10^5","≈ 80","100^∘ C","2.26 × 10^6","≈ 540"],""],[3,3,24,"text","Latent heat: heating without warming","Here is the life story of 1 g of ice taken from −20^∘ C all the way to steam:",["1 g","−20^∘ C"],""],[3,3,25,"figure","Latent heat: heating without warming","Flat stretches are phase changes. The horizontal axis is to scale — look how long boiling takes.",[],""],[3,3,26,"intuition","Why a steam burn is so much worse","Vaporising water takes nearly seven times the energy of melting ice: melting only loosens the molecules, boiling tears them completely apart. Run it backwards and you see the danger. A gram of steam at 100^∘ C landing on your skin hands over 540 cal just by condensing — before it starts cooling like ordinary hot water. A gram of boiling water has only about 63 cal to give on its way down to skin temperature.",["100^∘ C","540 cal","63 cal"],""],[3,3,27,"sec","The one rule: heat lost = heat gained","Put several bodies inside an insulated container — a calorimeter — and let them settle. No energy escapes, so",[],""],[3,3,28,"text","The one rule: heat lost = heat gained","Put several bodies inside an insulated container — a calorimeter — and let them settle. No energy escapes, so",[],""],[3,3,29,"formula","The one rule: heat lost = heat gained","Put several bodies inside an insulated container — a calorimeter — and let them settle. No energy escapes, so",["∑(heat lost by the hot bodies) = ∑(heat gained by the cold bodies)"],"∑(<span class=\"fx-t\">heat lost by the hot bodies</span>) = ∑(<span class=\"fx-t\">heat gained by the cold bodies</span>)"],[3,3,30,"text","The one rule: heat lost = heat gained","A recipe that never fails:",[],""],[3,3,31,"text","The one rule: heat lost = heat gained","List every body with its starting temperature and state. If ice or steam is involved, do a budget check first: is there enough heat on offer to finish the phase change? (See the trap below.) Write “lost = gained” with every term positive — always (higher temperature − lower temperature). Solve, then sanity-check: the final temperature must lie between the extremes.",[],""],[3,3,32,"key","Mixing with no phase change","The final temperature is a weighted average, with heat capacities mc as the weights: T = (m_1c_1T_1 + m_2c_2T_2)/(m_1c_1 + m_2c_2) Equal amounts of the same liquid simply meet in the middle.",["mc","T = (m_1c_1T_1 + m_2c_2T_2)/(m_1c_1 + m_2c_2)"],""],[3,3,32,"formula","Mixing with no phase change","The final temperature is a weighted average, with heat capacities mc as the weights: T = (m_1c_1T_1 + m_2c_2T_2)/(m_1c_1 + m_2c_2) Equal amounts of the same liquid simply meet in the middle.",["T = (m_1c_1T_1 + m_2c_2T_2)/(m_1c_1 + m_2c_2)"],"<i>T</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>m</i><sub>1</sub><i>c</i><sub>1</sub><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>m</i><sub>2</sub><i>c</i><sub>2</sub><i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>m</i><sub>1</sub><i>c</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>m</i><sub>2</sub><i>c</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[3,3,34,"trap","The budget check","Mix 100 g of ice at 0^∘ C with 100 g of water at 40^∘ C . Assume “all the ice melts” and solve blindly, and you get a final temperature of −20^∘ C — colder than both ingredients. Nonsense. Check the budget instead. The water can supply at most 100 × 1 × 40 = 4000 cal by cooling to 0^∘ C . Melting all the ice costs 100 × 80 = 8000 cal . There isn’t enough. So only 50 g melts, and the mixture ends at 0^∘ C with 50 g of ice still floating.",["100 g","0^∘ C","100 g","40^∘ C","−20^∘ C","100 × 1 × 40 = 4000 cal","0^∘ C","100 × 80 = 8000 cal","50 g","0^∘ C","50 g"],""],[3,3,35,"q","Do the budget check · Single correct","150 g of ice at 0^∘ C is dropped into 200 g of water at 50^∘ C in an insulated flask of negligible heat capacity. (Latent heat of fusion = 80 cal g ^(−1) , specific heat of water = 1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1) .) When everything settles, the flask contains water at 0^∘ C with 25 g of ice still unmelted · water at 0^∘ C with the ice only just completely melted · only water, at about 5.7^∘ C · only water, at about 28.6^∘ C",["150 g","0^∘ C","200 g","50^∘ C","= 80 cal g ^(−1)","= 1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)","0^∘ C","25 g","0^∘ C","5.7^∘ C","28.6^∘ C"],""],[3,3,35,"sol","Do the budget check · Single correct","Budget. The water can give 200 × 1 × 50 = 10 000 cal in cooling to 0^∘ C . Cost. Melting all the ice needs 150 × 80 = 12 000 cal . Not enough — so the temperature ends at 0^∘ C and only (10 000)/80 = 125 g of ice melts. That leaves 150 − 125 = 25 g of ice. Answer (A). Option (D) is what you get by averaging temperatures as if the ice were just cold water. Latent heat is never optional. How much heat can the warm water give before it reaches 0^∘ C ? How much does the ice need?",["200 × 1 × 50 = 10 000 cal","0^∘ C","150 × 80 = 12 000 cal","0^∘ C","(10 000)/80 = 125 g","150 − 125 = 25 g","0^∘ C"],""],[3,3,36,"pyq","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","A copper ball of mass 100 g is at a temperature T . It is dropped in a copper calorimeter of mass 100 g , filled with 170 g of water at room temperature. Subsequently, the temperature of the system is found to be 75^∘ C . T is given by (room temperature = 30^∘ C , specific heat of copper = 0.1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)) 800^∘ C · 885^∘ C · 1250^∘ C · 825^∘ C",["100 g","T","100 g","170 g","75^∘ C","T","= 30^∘ C","= 0.1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)","800^∘ C","885^∘ C","1250^∘ C","825^∘ C"],""],[3,3,36,"sol","JEE Main 2017 · Single correct","Heat lost by the ball = heat gained by the calorimeter + heat gained by the water. 100(0.1)(T − 75) = 100(0.1)(75 − 30) + 170(1)(75 − 30) 10 (T − 75) = 450 + 7650 = 8100 ⟹ T = 885^∘ C . Answer (B). Shortcut: the calorimeter is worth 10 g of water, so “ 180 g of water” warmed by 45^∘ C → 8100 cal , in one line. The calorimeter warms up along with the water. Its water equivalent is 10 g .",["=","+","100(0.1)(T − 75) = 100(0.1)(75 − 30) + 170(1)(75 − 30)","10 (T − 75) = 450 + 7650 = 8100 ⟹ T = 885^∘ C .","10 g","180 g","45^∘ C","8100 cal","10 g"],""],[3,3,37,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","A piece of ice (heat capacity = 2100 J kg ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1) and latent heat = 3.36 × 10^5 J kg ^(−1)) of mass m grams is at −5^∘ C at atmospheric pressure. It is given 420 J of heat so that the ice starts melting. Finally, when the ice–water mixture is in equilibrium, it is found that 1 g of ice has melted. Assuming there is no other heat exchange in the process, find the value of m .",["= 2100 J kg ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)","= 3.36 × 10^5 J kg ^(−1)","m","−5^∘ C","420 J","1 g","m"],""],[3,3,37,"sol","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","The mixture is in equilibrium with ice and water together, so it is at 0^∘ C . Melting 1 g : (10^(−3))(3.36 × 10^5) = 336 J . That leaves 420 − 336 = 84 J for warming m grams of ice through 5^∘ C : (m × 10^(−3))(2100)(5) = 84 ⟹ m = 8. Answer: 8. The 420 J is spent on two jobs: warming all the ice to 0^∘ C , then melting one gram of it.",["0^∘ C","1 g","(10^(−3))(3.36 × 10^5) = 336 J","420 − 336 = 84 J","m","5^∘ C","(m × 10^(−3))(2100)(5) = 84 ⟹ m = 8.","420 J","0^∘ C"],""],[3,3,38,"pyq","IIT-JEE 1986 · Single correct","Steam at 100^∘ C is passed into 1.1 kg of water contained in a calorimeter of water equivalent 0.02 kg at 15^∘ C , till the temperature of the calorimeter and its contents rises to 80^∘ C . The mass of the steam condensed, in kilograms, is 0.130 · 0.065 · 0.260 · 0.135",["100^∘ C","1.1 kg","0.02 kg","15^∘ C","80^∘ C","0.130","0.065","0.260","0.135"],""],[3,3,38,"sol","IIT-JEE 1986 · Single correct","Work in grams and calories (L_v = 540 cal g ^(−1)). Gained by water + calorimeter (together worth 1120 g of water): 1120 × 1 × (80 − 15) = 72 800 cal . Lost by m grams of steam: condensing gives 540m ; the resulting water then cools by 20^∘ C and gives 20m . 560 m = 72 800 ⟹ m = 130 g = 0.130 kg . Answer (A). Forget the second term and you land on option (D), 0.135 — it is sitting there waiting for you. Each gram of steam gives heat twice: once on condensing, and again as the condensed water cools from 100^∘ C to 80^∘ C .",["L_v = 540 cal g ^(−1)","1120 g","1120 × 1 × (80 − 15) = 72 800 cal","m","540m","20^∘ C","20m","560 m = 72 800 ⟹ m = 130 g = 0.130 kg .","0.135","100^∘ C","80^∘ C"],""],[3,3,39,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · Numerical answer","A liquid at 30^∘ C is poured very slowly into a calorimeter that is at a temperature of 110^∘ C . The boiling temperature of the liquid is 80^∘ C . It is found that the first 5 g of the liquid completely evaporates. After pouring another 80 g of the liquid the equilibrium temperature is found to be 50^∘ C . The ratio of the latent heat of the liquid to its specific heat will be ______ ^∘ C . (Neglect the heat exchange with the surroundings.)",["30^∘ C","110^∘ C","80^∘ C","5 g","80 g","50^∘ C","^∘ C"],""],[3,3,39,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · Numerical answer","Let the liquid have specific heat s and latent heat L , and the calorimeter heat capacity C . Stage 1. The first 5 g is heated from 30 to 80^∘ C and then just evaporates completely. That is only possible if the calorimeter has come down exactly to the boiling point, 80^∘ C : C (110 − 80) = 5s (80 − 30) + 5L ⟹ 30C = 250s + 5L. Stage 2. The vapour has left. The calorimeter (now at 80^∘ C) and the next 80 g (at 30^∘ C) settle at 50^∘ C , with no boiling: C (80 − 50) = 80s (50 − 30) ⟹ 30C = 1600s. Combine: 1600s = 250s + 5L ⟹ L/s = 270^∘ C . Answer: 270. Two stages, two equations. The unknown that links them is the heat capacity of the calorimeter. After the first 5 g has just boiled away, what is the calorimeter’s temperature?",["s","L","C","5 g","30","80^∘ C","80^∘ C","C (110 − 80) = 5s (80 − 30) + 5L ⟹ 30C = 250s + 5L.","80^∘ C","80 g","30^∘ C","50^∘ C"],""],[3,3,40,"recap","Recap","Heat is energy in transit due to a temperature difference; 1 cal = 4.186 J . Warming: Q = mc ΔT . Phase change (no temperature change): Q = mL . Water: c = 1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1) , L_f ≈ 80 , L_v ≈ 540 cal g ^(−1) ; ice: c = 0.5 . Calorimetry: heat lost = heat gained. Replace a container by its water equivalent. With ice or steam, do the budget check before assuming the phase change completes.",["1 cal = 4.186 J","Q = mc ΔT","Q = mL","c = 1 cal g ^(−1) ^∘ C ^(−1)","L_f ≈ 80","L_v ≈ 540 cal g ^(−1)","c = 0.5"],""],[3,4,0,"lesson","The First Law: Heat, Work & Internal Energy","Energy conservation, upgraded to include heat. One equation, three quantities — and a diagram on which work is nothing more than an area.",[],""],[3,4,1,"sec","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","Almost every thermodynamics problem stars the same actor: a gas trapped in a cylinder under a piston.",[],""],[3,4,2,"text","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","Almost every thermodynamics problem stars the same actor: a gas trapped in a cylinder under a piston.",[],""],[3,4,3,"text","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","The system is the gas. Everything else — piston, flame, atmosphere — is the surroundings. The state of the gas is fixed by its pressure, volume and temperature. For n moles of an ideal gas, PV = nRT ties them together, so any two fix the third. A state is therefore a single point on a P – V diagram. A process is a journey from one state to another — a path on the diagram. We assume it happens slowly enough that the gas is in equilibrium at every instant (a quasi-static process); otherwise there would be no well-defined P to plot.",["n","PV = nRT","P","V","P"],""],[3,4,4,"text","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","Three kinds of energy appear, and keeping them apart is the whole game:",[],""],[3,4,5,"table","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","Symbol Name What it is U Internal energy Energy stored inside the gas — the random motion of its molecules. Q Heat Energy crossing the boundary because of a temperature difference. W Work Energy crossing the boundary because the boundary moves (the piston).",["U","Q","W"],""],[3,4,6,"text","A gas in a cylinder: meet the cast","U is something the gas has. Q and W are things that happen to it.",["U","Q","W"],""],[3,4,7,"key","For an ideal gas, U depends on temperature alone","Same temperature → same internal energy, no matter what the pressure or volume. So isothermal means ΔU = 0 , and any two processes between the same pair of temperatures have the same ΔU .",["ΔU = 0","ΔU"],""],[3,4,8,"sec","The first law","Give a gas some heat. Where can the energy go? Only two places: it can stay in the gas (raising U), or it can leave again as work done on the piston.",[],""],[3,4,9,"text","The first law","Give a gas some heat. Where can the energy go? Only two places: it can stay in the gas (raising U), or it can leave again as work done on the piston.",["U"],""],[3,4,10,"formula","The first law","Give a gas some heat. Where can the energy go? Only two places: it can stay in the gas (raising U), or it can leave again as work done on the piston.",["Q = ΔU + W"],"<i>Q</i> = Δ<i>U</i> + <i>W</i>"],[3,4,11,"text","The first law","Think of a bank account. Q is your salary coming in, W is what you spend, and ΔU is the change in your balance. Nothing is ever lost — it is just energy conservation with heat invited to the party.",["Q","W","ΔU"],""],[3,4,12,"text","The first law","Sign convention (JEE Physics):",[],""],[3,4,13,"table","The first law","Positive when… Negative when… Q heat is absorbed by the gas heat is given out W work is done by the gas (expansion) work is done on the gas (compression) ΔU temperature rises temperature falls",["Q","W","ΔU"],""],[3,4,14,"trap","Physics and Chemistry disagree about a sign","Chemistry writes the same law as ΔU = q + w , where w is the work done on the system. Identical physics, opposite sign for work. In a Physics paper, W is work done by the gas unless the question says otherwise.",["ΔU = q + w","w","W"],""],[3,4,15,"q","Sign discipline · Single correct","A gas absorbs 500 J of heat while 200 J of work is done on it by pushing the piston in. The change in its internal energy is +300 J · +700 J · −300 J · +500 J",["500 J","200 J","+300 J","+700 J","−300 J","+500 J"],""],[3,4,15,"sol","Sign discipline · Single correct","Work done on the gas is 200 J , so work done by the gas is W = −200 J . ΔU = Q − W = 500 − (−200) = +700 J . Both the heat and the push put energy into the gas, so they add. Answer (B). Option (A) is what you get by forgetting who did the work.",["200 J","W = −200 J","ΔU = Q − W = 500 − (−200) = +700 J ."],""],[3,4,16,"sec","Work is the area under the curve","Let the gas push a piston of area A outward by a small distance dx . The force is PA , so",[],""],[3,4,17,"text","Work is the area under the curve","Let the gas push a piston of area A outward by a small distance dx . The force is PA , so",["A","dx","PA"],""],[3,4,18,"formula","Work is the area under the curve","Let the gas push a piston of area A outward by a small distance dx . The force is PA , so",["dW = PA dx = P dV ⟹ W = ∫_V_i^V_f P dV"],"<i>d</i><i>W</i> = <i>P</i><i>A</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i> = <i>P</i> <i>d</i><i>V</i> ⟹ <i>W</i> = ∫<sub><i>V</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></sub><sup><i>V</i><sub><i>f</i></sub></sup> <i>P</i> <i>d</i><i>V</i>"],[3,4,19,"text","Work is the area under the curve","On a P – V diagram that integral is simply the area under the path. Moving right (expansion) counts positive; moving left (compression) counts negative.",["P","V"],""],[3,4,20,"table","Work is the area under the curve","Process What is constant Work done by the gas Isochoric volume 0 — the piston never moves Isobaric pressure P ΔV = nR ΔT Isothermal temperature nRT ln V_f/V_i = nRT ln P_i/P_f",["0","P ΔV = nR ΔT","nRT ln V_f/V_i = nRT ln P_i/P_f"],""],[3,4,21,"text","Work is the area under the curve","DerivationWork in an isothermal process With T fixed, P = nRT/V , so W = ∫_V_i^V_f nRT/V dV = nRT ln V_f/V_i. And because P_iV_i = P_fV_f on an isotherm, V_f/V_i = P_i/P_f . Since ΔU = 0 , this is also the heat absorbed: Q = W .",["T","P = nRT/V","W = ∫_V_i^V_f nRT/V dV = nRT ln V_f/V_i.","P_iV_i = P_fV_f","V_f/V_i = P_i/P_f","ΔU = 0","Q = W"],""],[3,4,21,"formula","Work is the area under the curve","DerivationWork in an isothermal process With T fixed, P = nRT/V , so W = ∫_V_i^V_f nRT/V dV = nRT ln V_f/V_i. And because P_iV_i = P_fV_f on an isotherm, V_f/V_i = P_i/P_f . Since ΔU = 0 , this is also the heat absorbed: Q = W .",["W = ∫_V_i^V_f nRT/V dV = nRT ln V_f/V_i."],"<i>W</i> = ∫<sub><i>V</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></sub><sup><i>V</i><sub><i>f</i></sub></sup> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>R</i><i>T</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span> <i>d</i><i>V</i> = <i>n</i><i>R</i><i>T</i> ln <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sub><i>f</i></sub></span><span><i>V</i><sub><i>i</i></sub></span></span>."],[3,4,23,"text","Work is the area under the curve","Now the idea that JEE tests more than any other in this lesson. Go from state A to state B by two different routes. The areas under the two paths are different, so W depends on the path. But ΔU depends only on the end points. So Q = ΔU + W must depend on the path as well.",["W","ΔU","Q = ΔU + W"],""],[3,4,24,"text","Work is the area under the curve","U is a state function. Q and W are path functions. There is no such thing as “the heat in state B”.",["U","Q","W"],""],[3,4,25,"text","Work is the area under the curve","Cyclic process. If the gas returns to where it started, ΔU = 0 , so",["ΔU = 0"],""],[3,4,26,"formula","Work is the area under the curve","Cyclic process. If the gas returns to where it started, ΔU = 0 , so",["Q_net = W_net = area enclosed by the loop"],"<i>Q</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> = <i>W</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-t\">area enclosed by the loop</span>"],[3,4,27,"text","Work is the area under the curve","clockwise loop → positive (the gas is working as an engine); anticlockwise → negative (you are running a refrigerator).",[],""],[3,4,28,"lab","The P–V playground","One mole of an ideal gas. Drag state A and state B anywhere, then choose how to travel between them. Try these, in order: With Straight line selected, note W , ΔU and Q . Now switch to Isobaric → isochoric, then Isochoric → isobaric. Two of the three numbers change every time. Which one refuses to? Drag B to sit directly below A (same volume). The shaded area vanishes: W = 0 , and every joule of heat goes into ΔU . Turn on Isotherms. Slide B along A’s isotherm until T_B = T_A . Now ΔU = 0 and Q = W — heat in, work out, nothing kept. Select Round trip. The gas goes A → B along the top and comes back along the bottom. ΔU is zero and the net work is the enclosed area. Drag B to the left of A: the loop now runs anticlockwise and the work changes sign. Turn on Adiabat through A — the curve the gas would follow with no heat exchange. Notice it is steeper than the isotherm. Lesson 5 explains why. The tinted region is the work: green when the gas does work, red when work is done on it. Switch on Diatomic gas and watch ΔU and Q jump while W stays put.",["W","ΔU","Q","W = 0","ΔU","T_B = T_A","ΔU = 0","Q = W","ΔU","ΔU","Q","W"],""],[3,4,29,"q","Area under a line · Numerical answer","An ideal gas is taken from state A (P = 300 kPa , V = 2 L) to state B (P = 100 kPa , V = 6 L) along a straight line on the P – V diagram. How much work, in joules, does the gas do?",["P = 300 kPa","V = 2 L","P = 100 kPa","V = 6 L","P","V"],""],[3,4,29,"sol","Area under a line · Numerical answer","W = 1/2 (P_A + P_B)(V_B − V_A) = 1/2 (300 + 100) kPa × 4 L = 800 J . Bonus. Compare PV at the two ends: 300 × 2 = 600 and 100 × 6 = 600 . Same PV means same temperature, so ΔU = 0 and the gas must have absorbed exactly Q = 800 J of heat. (It was not an isothermal process, though — in between, the temperature rose and came back down.) The area under a straight line is a trapezium. And 1 kPa × 1 L = 1 J .",["W = 1/2 (P_A + P_B)(V_B − V_A) = 1/2 (300 + 100) kPa × 4 L = 800 J .","PV","300 × 2 = 600","100 × 6 = 600","PV","ΔU = 0","Q = 800 J","1 kPa × 1 L = 1 J"],""],[3,4,30,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · Integer answer","A thermodynamic system is taken from an initial state i with internal energy U_i = 100 J to the final state f along two different paths iaf and ibf , as schematically shown in the figure (the step i → a is at constant volume). The work done by the system along the paths af , ib and bf are W_af = 200 J , W_ib = 50 J and W_bf = 100 J respectively. The heat supplied to the system along the paths iaf , ib and bf are Q_iaf , Q_ib and Q_bf respectively. If the internal energy of the system in the state b is U_b = 200 J and Q_iaf = 500 J , find the ratio Q_bf/Q_ib .",["i","U_i = 100 J","f","iaf","ibf","i → a","af","ib","bf","W_af = 200 J","W_ib = 50 J","W_bf = 100 J"],""],[3,4,30,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · Integer answer","Find U_f from path iaf . The step i → a is at constant volume, so no work is done there and W_iaf = W_af = 200 J . Q_iaf = (U_f − U_i) + W_iaf ⟹ 500 = (U_f − 100) + 200 ⟹ U_f = 400 J . Step ib : Q_ib = (U_b − U_i) + W_ib = (200 − 100) + 50 = 150 J . Step bf : Q_bf = (U_f − U_b) + W_bf = (400 − 200) + 100 = 300 J . (Q_bf)/(Q_ib) = 300/150 = 2. Answer: 2. Notice the totals: Q_ibf = 450 J but Q_iaf = 500 J — same end points, different heat. Path functions in action. U is a state function. Use the path iaf only to find U_f ; after that you never need it again.",["U_f","iaf","i → a","W_iaf = W_af = 200 J","Q_iaf = (U_f − U_i) + W_iaf ⟹ 500 = (U_f − 100) + 200 ⟹ U_f = 400 J .","ib","Q_ib = (U_b − U_i) + W_ib = (200 − 100) + 50 = 150 J","bf","Q_bf = (U_f − U_b) + W_bf = (400 − 200) + 100 = 300 J","(Q_bf)/(Q_ib) = 300/150 = 2.","Q_ibf = 450 J","Q_iaf = 500 J"],""],[3,4,31,"pyq","JEE Main 2016 · Single correct","n moles of an ideal gas undergoes a process A → B as shown in the figure. The maximum temperature of the gas during the process will be (9P_0V_0)/(4nR) · (3P_0V_0)/(2nR) · (9P_0V_0)/(2nR) · (9P_0V_0)/nR",["n","A → B","(9P_0V_0)/(4nR)","(3P_0V_0)/(2nR)","(9P_0V_0)/(2nR)","(9P_0V_0)/nR"],""],[3,4,31,"sol","JEE Main 2016 · Single correct","The line through A(V_0, 2P_0) and B(2V_0, P_0) is P = 3P_0 − P_0/V_0 V. Then T = PV/nR = 1/nR (3P_0V − P_0/V_0V^2) , a downward parabola in V . Setting dT/dV = 0 gives V = 3/2V_0 , where P = 3/2P_0 : T_max = nR(3/2P_0) (3/2V_0) = (9P_0V_0)/(4nR). Answer (A). The end points both have PV = 2P_0V_0 — equal temperatures — yet the gas gets hotter in the middle. A straight line on a P – V diagram is never an isotherm. Temperature is proportional to the product PV . Write P as a function of V along the line and maximise PV .",["A(V_0, 2P_0)","B(2V_0, P_0)","P = 3P_0 − P_0/V_0 V.","T = PV/nR = 1/nR (3P_0V − P_0/V_0V^2) ,","V","dT/dV = 0","V = 3/2V_0","P = 3/2P_0","T_max = nR(3/2P_0) (3/2V_0) = (9P_0V_0)/(4nR).","PV = 2P_0V_0","P","V"],""],[3,4,32,"sec","Free expansion: the process with no path","A rigid, insulated box is divided in two. One half holds a gas; the other is vacuum. Puncture the partition and the gas rushes in to fill everything.",[],""],[3,4,33,"text","Free expansion: the process with no path","A rigid, insulated box is divided in two. One half holds a gas; the other is vacuum. Puncture the partition and the gas rushes in to fill everything.",[],""],[3,4,34,"text","Free expansion: the process with no path","Insulated → Q = 0 . The gas pushes against nothing → W = 0 . Therefore ΔU = 0 , and for an ideal gas that means the temperature does not change.",["Q = 0","W = 0","ΔU = 0"],""],[3,4,35,"text","Free expansion: the process with no path","Joule tried exactly this experiment in the 1840s and could detect no temperature change. It is the cleanest evidence that an ideal gas’s internal energy does not care about its volume.",[],""],[3,4,36,"trap","“No heat exchange” does not always mean PV^γ = constant","Free expansion is sudden and violent — not quasi-static — so it has no path on the P – V diagram, only a start point and an end point. The adiabatic formulas of the next lesson assume a slow, controlled expansion against a piston, in which the gas does work and cools. In free expansion it does no work and (if ideal) does not cool at all.",["P","V"],""],[3,4,37,"q","Into the vacuum · One or more correct","A thermally insulated rigid container is divided into two equal halves by a partition. One half contains an ideal gas at pressure P and temperature T ; the other half is evacuated. The partition is suddenly removed and the gas is allowed to settle. Which of the following is/are correct? The final temperature of the gas is T . · The work done by the gas is zero. · The internal energy of the gas decreases. · The final pressure of the gas is P/2 .",["P","T","T","P/2"],""],[3,4,37,"sol","Into the vacuum · One or more correct","Q = 0 (insulated) and W = 0 (expansion into vacuum; the rigid walls do not move). So ΔU = 0 — (B) is correct and (C) is wrong. For an ideal gas, ΔU = 0 means the temperature is unchanged: (A) is correct. Same T , double the volume: from PV = nRT the pressure halves. (D) is correct. Answer: A, B, D. What is there for the gas to push against?",["Q = 0","W = 0","ΔU = 0","ΔU = 0","T","PV = nRT"],""],[3,4,38,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","An ideal monoatomic gas is confined in a horizontal cylinder by a spring-loaded piston (as shown in the figure). Initially the gas is at temperature T_1 , pressure P_1 and volume V_1 , and the spring is in its relaxed state. The gas is then heated very slowly to temperature T_2 , pressure P_2 and volume V_2 . During this process the piston moves out by a distance x . Ignoring the friction between the piston and the cylinder, the correct statement(s) is (are) If V_2 = 2V_1 and T_2 = 3T_1 , then the energy stored in the spring is 1/4P_1V_1 . · If V_2 = 2V_1 and T_2 = 3T_1 , then the change in internal energy is 3P_1V_1 . · If V_2 = 3V_1 and T_2 = 4T_1 , then the work done by the gas is 7/3P_1V_1 . · If V_2 = 3V_1 and T_2 = 4T_1 , then the heat supplied to the gas is 17/6P_1V_1 .",["T_1","P_1","V_1","T_2","P_2","V_2","x","V_2 = 2V_1","T_2 = 3T_1","1/4P_1V_1","V_2 = 2V_1","T_2 = 3T_1"],""],[3,4,38,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · One or more correct","Setting up. Initially the spring exerts no force, so the outside (atmospheric) pressure on the piston must equal P_1 . Later, with piston area A , the balance of forces gives P_2 = P_1 + kx/A , and x = (V_2 − V_1)/A . The gas’s work goes two ways: pushing back the atmosphere, P_1(V_2 − V_1) , and stretching the spring, 1/2kx^2 . Case 1: V_2 = 2V_1 , T_2 = 3T_1 . From PV ∝ T : P_2 = 3/2P_1 . So kx = 1/2P_1A and x = V_1/A . Spring energy = 1/2(kx) x = 1/2 ⋅ 1/2P_1A ⋅ V_1/A = 1/4P_1V_1 . (A) ✓ ΔU = 3/2(P_2V_2 − P_1V_1) = 3/2(3P_1V_1 − P_1V_1) = 3P_1V_1 . (B) ✓ Case 2: V_2 = 3V_1 , T_2 = 4T_1 . Now P_2 = 4/3P_1 , so kx = 1/3P_1A and x = 2V_1/A . W = P_1(2V_1) + 1/2 ⋅ 1/3P_1A ⋅ (2V_1)/A = 2P_1V_1 + 1/3P_1V_1 = 7/3P_1V_1 . (C) ✓ ΔU = 3/2(4P_1V_1 − P_1V_1) = 9/2P_1V_1 , so Q = 9/2P_1V_1 + 7/3P_1V_1 = 41/6P_1V_1 . (D) ✗ Answer: A, B, C. Cross-check with the area method: P rises linearly with V (spring force ∝ x), so W is a trapezium: 1/2(P_1 + P_2)(V_2 − V_1) = 1/2 ⋅ 7/3P_1 ⋅ 2V_1 = 7/3P_1V_1 . Same answer, two lines. The spring starts relaxed while the gas is at P_1 — so what is the pressure outside? Then: the gas does work against that outside pressure and against the spring.",["P_1","A","P_2 = P_1 + kx/A","x = (V_2 − V_1)/A","P_1(V_2 − V_1)","1/2kx^2","V_2 = 2V_1","T_2 = 3T_1","PV ∝ T","P_2 = 3/2P_1","kx = 1/2P_1A","x = V_1/A"],""],[3,4,39,"recap","Recap","First law: Q = ΔU + W , with Q = heat absorbed by the gas and W = work done by the gas. W = ∫ P dV = area under the path. Isochoric: 0 . Isobaric: PΔV . Isothermal: nRT ln(V_f/V_i) . U is a state function (ideal gas: depends only on T). Q and W are path functions. Cycle: ΔU = 0 , Q_net = W_net = enclosed area; clockwise is positive. Free expansion (ideal gas): Q = 0 , W = 0 , ΔU = 0 , temperature unchanged.",["Q = ΔU + W","Q","W","W = ∫ P dV","0","PΔV","nRT ln(V_f/V_i)","U","T","Q","W","ΔU = 0"],""],[3,5,0,"lesson","Specific Heats of a Gas & the Adiabatic Process","A gas does not have one specific heat — it has infinitely many. Two of them, at constant volume and at constant pressure, unlock every process — including the adiabatic one that fires diesel engines and builds clouds.",[],""],[3,5,1,"sec","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","For a block of copper, “heat needed per degree” is a single number. For a gas, the question has no answer until you say what the gas is allowed to do while you heat it.",[],""],[3,5,2,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","For a block of copper, “heat needed per degree” is a single number. For a gas, the question has no answer until you say what the gas is allowed to do while you heat it.",[],""],[3,5,3,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Lock the piston. All the heat goes into raising the temperature. Let the piston rise freely. Now the gas also does work, so you must supply more heat for the same one-degree rise. Push the piston in while heating, and the gas may warm up with no heat at all.",[],""],[3,5,4,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","So the molar heat capacity C = Q/nΔT depends on the process — it can be anything from −∞ to +∞ . Two values matter above all.",["C = Q/nΔT","−∞","+∞"],""],[3,5,5,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","At constant volume, W = 0 , so every joule goes into internal energy:",["W = 0"],""],[3,5,6,"formula","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","At constant volume, W = 0 , so every joule goes into internal energy:",["Q_V = nC_V ΔT = ΔU."],"<i>Q</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> = <i>n</i><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> Δ<i>T</i> = Δ<i>U</i>."],[3,5,7,"key","The most useful sentence in this chapter","For an ideal gas, U depends only on T . So the change in internal energy between two temperatures is the same for every process — and we already know it for one of them: ΔU = nC_V ΔT — always, even when the volume is not constant.",["U","T","ΔU = nC_V ΔT — always, even when the volume is not constant."],""],[3,5,7,"formula","The most useful sentence in this chapter","For an ideal gas, U depends only on T . So the change in internal energy between two temperatures is the same for every process — and we already know it for one of them: ΔU = nC_V ΔT — always, even when the volume is not constant.",["ΔU = nC_V ΔT — always, even when the volume is not constant."],"Δ<i>U</i> = <i>n</i><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> Δ<i>T</i> <span class=\"fx-t\">— always, even when the volume is not constant.</span>"],[3,5,9,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","At constant pressure, the gas also does work P ΔV = nR ΔT :",["P ΔV = nR ΔT"],""],[3,5,10,"formula","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","At constant pressure, the gas also does work P ΔV = nR ΔT :",["nC_P ΔT = ΔUnC_V ΔT + WnR ΔT ⟹ C_P − C_V = R"],"<i>n</i><i>C</i><sub><i>P</i></sub> Δ<i>T</i> = Δ<i>U</i><i>n</i><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> Δ<i>T</i> + <i>W</i><i>n</i><i>R</i> Δ<i>T</i> ⟹ <i>C</i><sub><i>P</i></sub> − <i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> = <i>R</i>"],[3,5,11,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","This is Mayer’s relation. C_P is bigger than C_V by exactly the work one mole does in expanding through one kelvin. Their ratio gets its own symbol:",["C_P","C_V"],""],[3,5,12,"formula","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","This is Mayer’s relation. C_P is bigger than C_V by exactly the work one mole does in expanding through one kelvin. Their ratio gets its own symbol:",["γ = C_P/C_V > 1, C_V = R/(γ − 1), C_P = γR/(γ − 1)."],"<i>γ</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>C</i><sub><i>P</i></sub></span><span><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub></span></span> &gt; 1, <i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i></span><span><i>γ</i> − 1</span></span>, <i>C</i><sub><i>P</i></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>γ</i><i>R</i></span><span><i>γ</i> − 1</span></span>."],[3,5,13,"table","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Gas C_V C_P γ Monatomic (He, Ne, Ar) 3/2R 5/2R 5/3 ≈ 1.67 Diatomic, near room temperature (H₂, N₂, O₂, air) 5/2R 7/2R 7/5 = 1.40 Non-linear polyatomic, rigid (e.g. H₂O vapour) 3R 4R 4/3 ≈ 1.33",["C_V","C_P","γ","3/2R","5/2R","5/3 ≈ 1.67","5/2R","7/2R","7/5 = 1.40","3R","4R","4/3 ≈ 1.33"],""],[3,5,14,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Why these particular fractions? That is a story about how molecules store energy, and it belongs to the Kinetic Theory chapter. Here, treat them as measured facts — with R = 8.314 J mol ^(−1) K ^(−1) .",["R = 8.314 J mol ^(−1) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,5,15,"intuition","Where does the heat go at constant pressure?","Of every joule supplied at constant pressure, a fraction C_V/C_P = 1/γ raises the internal energy and the remaining 1 − 1/γ leaves as work. For a monatomic gas that is 60% kept, 40% spent; for a diatomic gas, about 71% and 29%.",["C_V/C_P = 1/γ","1 − 1/γ"],""],[3,5,16,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Mixtures. Internal energies simply add, so heat capacities are mole-weighted averages:",[],""],[3,5,17,"formula","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Mixtures. Internal energies simply add, so heat capacities are mole-weighted averages:",["C_(V, mix) = (n_1C_(V1) + n_2C_(V2))/(n_1 + n_2), C_(P, mix) = C_(V, mix) + R, γ_mix = (C_(P, mix))/(C_(V, mix))."],"<i>C</i><sub><i>V</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><sub>1</sub><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i>1</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>2</sub><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i>2</sub></span><span><i>n</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>, <i>C</i><sub><i>P</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub> = <i>C</i><sub><i>V</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub> + <i>R</i>, <i>γ</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>C</i><sub><i>P</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub></span><span><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i>, <span class=\"fx-t\">mix</span></sub></span></span>."],[3,5,18,"text","Why a gas refuses to have one specific heat","Never average the γ ’s themselves.",["γ"],""],[3,5,19,"q","Where did the heat go? · Single correct","Heat is supplied to a diatomic ideal gas (γ = 7/5) at constant pressure. What fraction of the heat is used by the gas to do work? 5/7 · 2/7 · 2/5 · 3/5",["γ = 7/5","5/7","2/7","2/5","3/5"],""],[3,5,19,"sol","Where did the heat go? · Single correct","At constant pressure Q = nC_PΔT and W = PΔV = nRΔT , so W/Q = R/C_P = 7/2RR = 2/7. The other 5/7 stays in the gas as internal energy. Answer (B). (For a monatomic gas the same question gives 2/5 — option (C) is the answer to a different gas.)",["Q = nC_PΔT","W = PΔV = nRΔT","W/Q = R/C_P = 7/2RR = 2/7.","5/7","2/5"],""],[3,5,20,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2009 · One or more correct","C_V and C_P denote the molar specific heat capacities of a gas at constant volume and constant pressure, respectively. Then C_P − C_V is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas · C_P + C_V is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas · C_P/C_V is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas · C_P ⋅ C_V is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas",["C_V","C_P","C_P − C_V","C_P + C_V","C_P/C_V","C_P ⋅ C_V"],""],[3,5,20,"sol","IIT-JEE 2009 · One or more correct","Just fill in the table. Monatomic Diatomic C_P − C_V R R C_P + C_V 4R 6R C_P/C_V 1.67 1.40 C_P ⋅ C_V 15/4R^2 35/4R^2 The difference is R for every ideal gas (Mayer), and the ratio is smaller for the diatomic gas. Only the sum and the product are larger. Answer: B, D.",["C_P − C_V","R","R","C_P + C_V","4R","6R","C_P/C_V","1.67","1.40","C_P ⋅ C_V","15/4R^2","35/4R^2"],""],[3,5,21,"sec","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","In an adiabatic process no heat enters or leaves: Q = 0 . That happens when the gas is insulated — or when things happen so fast that heat has no time to flow (a bicycle-pump stroke, a sound wave, the piston of an engine).",[],""],[3,5,22,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","In an adiabatic process no heat enters or leaves: Q = 0 . That happens when the gas is insulated — or when things happen so fast that heat has no time to flow (a bicycle-pump stroke, a sound wave, the piston of an engine).",["Q = 0"],""],[3,5,23,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","The first law then reads ΔU = −W . The gas has to pay for its work out of its own pocket:",["ΔU = −W"],""],[3,5,24,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","adiabatic expansion → the gas does work → it cools; adiabatic compression → work is done on it → it heats up.",[],""],[3,5,25,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","For a slow (quasi-static) adiabatic process of an ideal gas, three equivalent equations hold:",[],""],[3,5,26,"formula","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","For a slow (quasi-static) adiabatic process of an ideal gas, three equivalent equations hold:",["PV^γ = constant , TV^(γ − 1) = constant , T^γP^(1 − γ) = constant ."],"<i>P</i><i>V</i><sup><i>γ</i></sup> = <span class=\"fx-t\">constant</span>, <i>T</i><i>V</i><sup><i>γ</i> − 1</sup> = <span class=\"fx-t\">constant</span>, <i>T</i><sup><i>γ</i></sup><i>P</i><sup> 1 − <i>γ</i></sup> = <span class=\"fx-t\">constant</span>."],[3,5,27,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","DerivationWhere PV^γ comes from For a small step with dQ = 0 : nC_V dT = −P dV . Substitute P = nRT/V and divide by nC_VT : dT/T = −R/C_VdV/V = −(γ − 1)dV/V, because R/C_V = (C_P − C_V)/C_V = γ − 1 . Integrating: ln T + (γ − 1) ln V = constant , i.e. TV^(γ − 1) = constant . Replace T by PV/nR to get PV^γ = constant ; replace V by nRT/P to get the third form.",["PV^γ","dQ = 0","nC_V dT = −P dV","P = nRT/V","nC_VT","dT/T = −R/C_VdV/V = −(γ − 1)dV/V,","R/C_V = (C_P − C_V)/C_V = γ − 1","ln T + (γ − 1) ln V = constant","TV^(γ − 1) = constant","T","PV/nR","PV^γ = constant"],""],[3,5,27,"formula","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","V and divide by nC_VT : dT/T = −R/C_VdV/V = −(γ − 1)dV/V, because R/C_V = (C_P − C_V)/C_V = γ − 1 . Integrating: ln T + (γ − 1) ln V = constant , i.e. TV^(γ − 1) = constant . Replace T by PV/nR to get PV^γ = constant ; replace V by nRT/P to get the third form.",["dT/T = −R/C_VdV/V = −(γ − 1)dV/V,"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>T</i></span><span><i>T</i></span></span> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i></span><span><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub></span></span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>V</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span> = −(<i>γ</i> − 1)<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>V</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span>,"],[3,5,29,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","Work done. No calculus needed — just the first law:",[],""],[3,5,30,"formula","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","Work done. No calculus needed — just the first law:",["W = −ΔU = nC_V (T_1 − T_2) = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(γ − 1)."],"<i>W</i> = −Δ<i>U</i> = <i>n</i><i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> (<i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub>) = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i><sub>1</sub><i>V</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>P</i><sub>2</sub><i>V</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>γ</i> − 1</span></span>."],[3,5,31,"text","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","An adiabat is steeper than an isotherm. Through the same point, the slopes are",[],""],[3,5,32,"formula","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","An adiabat is steeper than an isotherm. Through the same point, the slopes are",["dP/dV_(isothermal) = −P/V, dP/dV_(adiabatic) = −γ P/V."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>P</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>V</i></span></span><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">isothermal</span></sub> = −<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span>, <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>P</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>V</i></span></span><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">adiabatic</span></sub> = −<i>γ</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>V</i></span></span>."],[3,5,33,"figure","The adiabatic process: no heat, all work","Expanding adiabatically, the gas cools as well as thins out — so its pressure falls faster than on the isotherm.",[],""],[3,5,34,"intuition","Adiabatic processes you have already met","Diesel engines have no spark plugs. They squeeze air to roughly one-eighteenth of its volume so quickly that it has no time to lose heat. With γ = 1.4 , the ideal-gas estimate is T_2 = 300 K × 18^0.4 ≈ 950 K — real engines fall somewhat short of that, but the air is still hot enough to ignite the fuel the instant it is sprayed in. Clouds form the opposite way: moist air rises, the pressure around it drops, it expands adiabatically and cools until its water vapour condenses. The wisp of fog in the neck of a freshly opened cold-drink bottle is the same thing in miniature.",["γ = 1.4","T_2 = 300 K × 18^0.4 ≈ 950 K"],""],[3,5,35,"trap","Slow or sudden? Read the adverb","“Slowly, in a conducting vessel” → isothermal (heat has time to flow, temperature stays put). “Suddenly”, “quickly”, or “insulated” → adiabatic. The words in the question are choosing the process for you.",[],""],[3,5,36,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","5.6 litre of helium gas at STP is adiabatically compressed to 0.7 litre. Taking the initial temperature to be T_1 , the work done in the process is 9/8RT_1 · 3/2RT_1 · 15/8RT_1 · 9/2RT_1",["5.6","0.7","T_1","9/8RT_1","3/2RT_1","15/8RT_1","9/2RT_1"],""],[3,5,36,"sol","IIT-JEE 2011 · Single correct","Moles: one mole occupies 22.4 L at STP, so n = 5.6/(22.4) = 1/4 . Final temperature (helium is monatomic, γ − 1 = 2/3): T_2 = T_1 (V_1/V_2)^(γ − 1) = T_1 (8)^(2/3) = 4T_1. Work done on the gas = increase in internal energy: ∣W∣ = nC_V (T_2 − T_1) = 1/4 ⋅ 3/2R ⋅ 3T_1 = 9/8RT_1. Answer (A). (The work done by the gas is −9/8RT_1 ; the question asks for the magnitude.) How many moles is 5.6 L at STP? Then use TV^(γ − 1) = constant with γ = 5/3 .",["22.4 L","n = 5.6/(22.4) = 1/4","γ − 1 = 2/3","T_2 = T_1 (V_1/V_2)^(γ − 1) = T_1 (8)^(2/3) = 4T_1.","=","∣W∣ = nC_V (T_2 − T_1) = 1/4 ⋅ 3/2R ⋅ 3T_1 = 9/8RT_1.","−9/8RT_1","5.6 L","TV^(γ − 1) = constant","γ = 5/3"],""],[3,5,37,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2018 · Numerical answer","One mole of a monatomic ideal gas undergoes an adiabatic expansion in which its volume becomes eight times its initial value. If the initial temperature of the gas is 100 K and the universal gas constant R = 8.0 J mol ^(−1) K ^(−1) , the decrease in its internal energy, in joule, is ______.",["100 K","R = 8.0 J mol ^(−1) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,5,37,"sol","JEE Advanced 2018 · Numerical answer","T_2 = T_1 (V_1/V_2)^(2/3) = 100/(8^(2/3)) = 100/4 = 25 K . Decrease in U = nC_V (T_1 − T_2) = (1) (3/2 × 8.0) (75) = 900 J . Answer: 900. That is also exactly the work the gas did — it had no other source of energy. TV^(γ − 1) = constant , and 8^(2/3) = 4 .",["T_2 = T_1 (V_1/V_2)^(2/3) = 100/(8^(2/3)) = 100/4 = 25 K .","Decrease in U = nC_V (T_1 − T_2) = (1) (3/2 × 8.0) (75) = 900 J .","TV^(γ − 1) = constant","8^(2/3) = 4"],""],[3,5,38,"sec","All the processes in one table","Everything so far, for n moles of an ideal gas going from temperature T_1 to T_2 (with ΔT = T_2 − T_1):",[],""],[3,5,39,"text","All the processes in one table","Everything so far, for n moles of an ideal gas going from temperature T_1 to T_2 (with ΔT = T_2 − T_1):",["n","T_1","T_2","ΔT = T_2 − T_1"],""],[3,5,40,"table","All the processes in one table","Process Constant Q W ΔU Isochoric V nC_VΔT 0 nC_VΔT Isobaric P nC_PΔT nR ΔT nC_VΔT Isothermal T nRT ln V_2/V_1 nRT ln V_2/V_1 0 Adiabatic PV^γ 0 −nC_VΔT nC_VΔT",["Q","W","ΔU","V","nC_VΔT","0","nC_VΔT","P","nC_PΔT","nR ΔT","nC_VΔT","T"],""],[3,5,41,"text","All the processes in one table","The last column is the same formula every time. Learn the W column, get ΔU for free, and Q follows from the first law.",["W","ΔU","Q"],""],[3,5,42,"key","One formula for every “strange” process","JEE loves to invent processes such as P ∝ V or PV^2 = constant . They are all polytropic: PV^x = constant . For any of them, W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(x − 1) = (nR ΔT)/(1 − x), C = C_V + R/(1 − x). Check it against what you know: x = 0 (isobaric) gives C_V + R = C_P ✓. x = γ gives C = 0 (adiabatic) ✓. x → 1 gives C → ∞ (isothermal: heat flows in but the temperature will not rise) ✓.",["P ∝ V","PV^2 = constant","PV^x = constant","W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(x − 1) = (nR ΔT)/(1 − x), C = C_V + R/(1 − x).","x = 0","C_V + R = C_P","x = γ","C = 0","x → 1","C → ∞"],""],[3,5,42,"formula","One formula for every “strange” process","m, W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(x − 1) = (nR ΔT)/(1 − x), C = C_V + R/(1 − x). Check it against what you know: x = 0 (isobaric) gives C_V + R = C_P ✓. x = γ gives C = 0 (adiabatic) ✓. x → 1 gives C → ∞ (isothermal: heat flows in but the temperature will not rise) ✓.",["W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(x − 1) = (nR ΔT)/(1 − x), C = C_V + R/(1 − x)."],"<i>W</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>P</i><sub>1</sub><i>V</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>P</i><sub>2</sub><i>V</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>x</i> − 1</span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>n</i><i>R</i> Δ<i>T</i></span><span>1 − <i>x</i></span></span>, <i>C</i> = <i>C</i><sub><i>V</i></sub> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>R</i></span><span>1 − <i>x</i></span></span>."],[3,5,44,"q","A made-up process · Single correct","One mole of a monatomic ideal gas is taken through a process in which its pressure is directly proportional to its volume (P ∝ V). The molar heat capacity of the gas for this process is 2R · 3/2R · 5/2R · 1/2R",["P ∝ V","2R","3/2R","5/2R","1/2R"],""],[3,5,44,"sol","A made-up process · Single correct","This is polytropic with x = −1 : C = C_V + R/(1 − x) = 3/2R + R/2 = 2R. Answer (A). From scratch, if you distrust formulas: P = kV and PV = RT give kV^2 = RT , so 2kV dV = R dT , i.e. P dV = 1/2R dT . Then dQ = C_V dT + P dV = (3/2R + 1/2R) dT . Same answer. P ∝ V means PV^(−1) = constant .",["x = −1","C = C_V + R/(1 − x) = 3/2R + R/2 = 2R.","P = kV","PV = RT","kV^2 = RT","2kV dV = R dT","P dV = 1/2R dT","dQ = C_V dT + P dV = (3/2R + 1/2R) dT","P ∝ V","PV^(−1) = constant"],""],[3,5,45,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","Consider one mole of helium gas enclosed in a container at initial pressure P_1 and volume V_1 . It expands isothermally to volume 4V_1 . After this, the gas expands adiabatically and its volume becomes 32V_1 . The work done by the gas during the isothermal and adiabatic expansion processes are W_iso and W_(adia) , respectively. If the ratio (W_iso)/(W_(adia)) = f ln 2 , then f is ______.",["P_1","V_1","4V_1","32V_1","W_iso","W_(adia)","(W_iso)/(W_(adia)) = f ln 2","f"],""],[3,5,45,"sol","JEE Advanced 2020 · Numerical answer","Isothermal step (V_1 → 4V_1): W_iso = P_1V_1 ln 4 = 2P_1V_1 ln 2 . Adiabatic step (4V_1 → 32V_1 , a factor of 8). It starts on the same isotherm, so PV is still P_1V_1 at the start. With γ = 5/3 : (T_(final))/(T_(start)) = (1/8)^(2/3) = 1/4 ⟹ (PV)_(final) = 1/4P_1V_1. W_(adia) = ((PV)_(start) − (PV)_(final))/(γ − 1) = 2/3P_1V_1 (1 − 1/4) = 9/8P_1V_1. Ratio: (W_iso)/(W_(adia)) = (2 ln 2)/(9/8) = 16/9 ln 2 ⟹ f = 16/9 ≈ 1.78 . Answer: 1.78 (16/9 = 1.777 … , so 1.77 is accepted here too). Express both works in units of P_1V_1 . For the adiabatic step you only need the temperature ratio.",["V_1 → 4V_1","W_iso = P_1V_1 ln 4 = 2P_1V_1 ln 2","4V_1 → 32V_1","PV","P_1V_1","γ = 5/3","(T_(final))/(T_(start)) = (1/8)^(2/3) = 1/4 ⟹ (PV)_(final) = 1/4P_1V_1.","W_(adia) = ((PV)_(start) − (PV)_(final))/(γ − 1) = 2/3P_1V_1 (1 − 1/4) = 9/8P_1V_1.","(W_iso)/(W_(adia)) = (2 ln 2)/(9/8) = 16/9 ln 2 ⟹ f = 16/9 ≈ 1.78","16/9 = 1.777 …","P_1V_1"],""],[3,5,46,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","A mixture of ideal gas containing 5 moles of monatomic gas and 1 mole of rigid diatomic gas is initially at pressure P_0 , volume V_0 and temperature T_0 . If the gas mixture is adiabatically compressed to a volume V_0/4 , then the correct statement(s) is/are (given 2^1.2 = 2.3 ; 2^3.2 = 9.2 ; R is the gas constant) The final pressure of the gas mixture after compression is in between 9P_0 and 10P_0 . · The average kinetic energy of the gas mixture after compression is in between 18RT_0 and 19RT_0 . · The adiabatic constant of the gas mixture is 1.6 . · The work ∣W∣ done during the process is 13RT_0 .",["P_0","V_0","T_0","V_0/4","2^1.2 = 2.3","2^3.2 = 9.2","R","9P_0","10P_0","18RT_0","19RT_0","1.6"],""],[3,5,46,"sol","JEE Advanced 2019 · One or more correct","Mixture. C_V = 65 (3/2R) + 1 (5/2R) = 5/3R , so C_P = 8/3R and γ = 8/5 = 1.6 . (C) ✓ Final pressure. P = P_0 (V_0/(V_0/4))^1.6 = 4^1.6P_0 = 2^3.2P_0 = 9.2P_0 . (A) ✓ Final temperature. T = T_0 ⋅ 4^0.6 = 2^1.2 T_0 = 2.3T_0 . Work. ∣W∣ = nC_V ΔT = 6 ⋅ 5/3R ⋅ (1.3T_0) = 13RT_0 . (D) ✓ Kinetic energy. For these rigid molecules all the internal energy is kinetic, so the total is U = nC_VT = 6 ⋅ 5/3R ⋅ 2.3T_0 = 23RT_0 — outside the stated range. (B) ✗ Answer: A, C, D. Find C_V of the mixture first — a mole-weighted average — and then γ . Everything else follows.",["C_V = 65 (3/2R) + 1 (5/2R) = 5/3R","C_P = 8/3R","γ = 8/5 = 1.6","P = P_0 (V_0/(V_0/4))^1.6 = 4^1.6P_0 = 2^3.2P_0 = 9.2P_0","T = T_0 ⋅ 4^0.6 = 2^1.2 T_0 = 2.3T_0","∣W∣ = nC_V ΔT = 6 ⋅ 5/3R ⋅ (1.3T_0) = 13RT_0","U = nC_VT = 6 ⋅ 5/3R ⋅ 2.3T_0 = 23RT_0","C_V","γ"],""],[3,5,47,"recap","Recap","C_V : heat capacity at constant volume; C_P = C_V + R (Mayer); γ = C_P/C_V . ΔU = nC_VΔT for every process of an ideal gas. Monatomic: 3/2R, 5/2R, 5/3 . Diatomic: 5/2R, 7/2R, 7/5 . Adiabatic: Q = 0 ; PV^γ , TV^(γ − 1) , T^γP^(1 − γ) constant; W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(γ − 1) . Steeper than an isotherm by the factor γ . Polytropic PV^x = const : C = C_V + R/(1 − x) . Mixtures: average C_V and C_P by moles — never average γ .",["C_V","C_P = C_V + R","γ = C_P/C_V","ΔU = nC_VΔT","3/2R, 5/2R, 5/3","5/2R, 7/2R, 7/5","Q = 0","PV^γ","TV^(γ − 1)","T^γP^(1 − γ)","W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(γ − 1)","γ"],""],[3,6,0,"lesson","The Second Law: Engines, Fridges & Carnot","The first law says you can’t get more energy out than you put in. The second says you can’t even get that. Meet the law that gives time its direction — and puts a hard ceiling on every engine ever built.",[],""],[3,6,1,"sec","The things that never happen","A cup of hot tea left on the table cools down. It has never, in the history of the universe, spontaneously grown hotter by cooling the room — even though that would conserve energy perfectly well.",[],""],[3,6,2,"text","The things that never happen","A cup of hot tea left on the table cools down. It has never, in the history of the universe, spontaneously grown hotter by cooling the room — even though that would conserve energy perfectly well.",[],""],[3,6,3,"text","The things that never happen","A bouncing ball loses height and comes to rest, warming the floor a little. No ball has ever cooled the floor and leapt into the air.",[],""],[3,6,4,"text","The things that never happen","The first law has no objection to either reversed film. Something else forbids them, and that something is the second law of thermodynamics. The first law is about the amount of energy. The second is about its direction.",[],""],[3,6,5,"text","The things that never happen","Reversible and irreversible. A process is reversible if it can be run backwards so that both the system and the surroundings return exactly to where they started. That needs two things: the process must be quasi-static, and there must be no friction or other dissipation. It is an ideal that real processes can only approach. Friction, heat flowing across a finite temperature difference, free expansion, mixing — all are irreversible, and all are one-way streets.",[],""],[3,6,6,"story","The laws, as students have summarised them for decades","First law: you can’t win — you never get out more energy than you put in. Second law: you can’t even break even — you can’t turn all your heat into work. Keep that in mind and the rest of this lesson is just the fine print.",[],""],[3,6,7,"sec","Heat engines","A heat engine is any device that runs in a cycle and turns heat into work. Strip away the pistons and turbines and every engine does the same three things:",[],""],[3,6,8,"text","Heat engines","A heat engine is any device that runs in a cycle and turns heat into work. Strip away the pistons and turbines and every engine does the same three things:",[],""],[3,6,9,"text","Heat engines","takes in heat Q_1 from a hot source at temperature T_1 ; converts part of it into work W ; dumps the rest, Q_2 , into a cold sink at temperature T_2 .",["Q_1","T_1","W","Q_2","T_2"],""],[3,6,10,"figure","Heat engines","An engine (left) and the same machine run backwards — a refrigerator (right).",[],""],[3,6,11,"text","Heat engines","After a full cycle the working substance is back where it began, so ΔU = 0 and the first law gives W = Q_1 − Q_2 . The efficiency is “what you get ÷ what you pay for”:",["ΔU = 0","W = Q_1 − Q_2"],""],[3,6,12,"formula","Heat engines","After a full cycle the working substance is back where it began, so ΔU = 0 and the first law gives W = Q_1 − Q_2 . The efficiency is “what you get ÷ what you pay for”:",["η = W/Q_1 = 1 − Q_2/Q_1"],"<i>η</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>W</i></span><span><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> = 1 − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub></span></span>"],[3,6,13,"text","Heat engines","Could a clever engineer make Q_2 = 0 and reach 100%? The second law says no:",["Q_2 = 0"],""],[3,6,14,"text","Heat engines","Kelvin–Planck statement. It is impossible for an engine working in a cycle to take heat from a single reservoir and convert it entirely into work.",[],""],[3,6,15,"text","Heat engines","Every engine needs an exhaust.",[],""],[3,6,16,"intuition","This is not bad engineering","A petrol car engine turns only about a quarter of its fuel’s heat into work, and even the best combined-cycle power stations manage roughly 60%. The rest is not “lost” through carelessness — it is the Q_2 that the second law requires to be thrown away.",["Q_2"],""],[3,6,17,"sec","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Run an engine backwards. Now work W goes in, heat Q_2 is pulled out of a cold space, and Q_1 = Q_2 + W is dumped into the warm room — that is the warm grille at the back of your fridge.",[],""],[3,6,18,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Run an engine backwards. Now work W goes in, heat Q_2 is pulled out of a cold space, and Q_1 = Q_2 + W is dumped into the warm room — that is the warm grille at the back of your fridge.",["W","Q_2","Q_1 = Q_2 + W"],""],[3,6,19,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","For a refrigerator, “what you get” is the heat removed from the cold space, so its figure of merit is the coefficient of performance:",[],""],[3,6,20,"formula","Refrigerators and heat pumps","For a refrigerator, “what you get” is the heat removed from the cold space, so its figure of merit is the coefficient of performance:",["β = Q_2/W = Q_2/(Q_1 − Q_2)"],"<i>β</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>W</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[3,6,21,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Unlike η , the COP is usually greater than 1. A heat pump is the same machine used for its warm end; its COP is Q_1/W = β + 1 .",["η","Q_1/W = β + 1"],""],[3,6,22,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Can a refrigerator run without being plugged in? Again the second law says no:",[],""],[3,6,23,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Clausius statement. It is impossible for a device working in a cycle to produce no other effect than the transfer of heat from a colder body to a hotter one.",[],""],[3,6,24,"text","Refrigerators and heat pumps","Heat does not flow uphill for free. The two statements look different but stand or fall together: with a machine that breaks one, you can build a machine that breaks the other.",[],""],[3,6,25,"q","The open fridge · Single correct","On a hot day someone leaves the refrigerator door wide open, hoping to cool a closed, thermally insulated kitchen. After a few hours the temperature of the kitchen will have fallen, because the refrigerator keeps removing heat from the air · stayed the same, because the heat removed from the air in front equals the heat released at the back · risen, because the electrical work fed to the refrigerator ends up as heat in the room · fallen at first and then returned exactly to its starting value",[],""],[3,6,25,"sol","The open fridge · Single correct","The fridge removes Q_2 from the air at the front and releases Q_1 = Q_2 + W at the back — into the same room. The net heat delivered to the room per cycle is Q_1 − Q_2 = W > 0 . Or think of the kitchen as one insulated box: the only energy crossing its walls is electrical energy coming in. Its internal energy can only go up. Answer (C).",["Q_2","Q_1 = Q_2 + W","Q_1 − Q_2 = W > 0"],""],[3,6,26,"sec","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","In 1824 a 28-year-old French engineer, Sadi Carnot, asked a question nobody had thought to ask: forget the details of boilers and pistons — what is the maximum efficiency any heat engine could have? He published the answer in a slim book, Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire. Hardly anyone read i",[],""],[3,6,27,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","In 1824 a 28-year-old French engineer, Sadi Carnot, asked a question nobody had thought to ask: forget the details of boilers and pistons — what is the maximum efficiency any heat engine could have? He published the answer in a slim book, Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire. Hardly anyone read it, and he died at only 36. It turned out to be the foundation of the second law.",[],""],[3,6,28,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","Carnot’s ideal engine uses only reversible steps — two isotherms joined by two adiabats:",[],""],[3,6,29,"figure","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","The Carnot cycle. The shaded area is the work done per cycle; the two unlabelled sides are adiabats.",[],""],[3,6,30,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","1 → 2 Isothermal expansion at T_1 : absorbs Q_1 from the source. 2 → 3 Adiabatic expansion: the gas cools from T_1 to T_2 . 3 → 4 Isothermal compression at T_2 : rejects Q_2 to the sink. 4 → 1 Adiabatic compression: the gas warms back up to T_1 .",["T_1","Q_1","T_1","T_2","T_2","Q_2","T_1"],""],[3,6,31,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","DerivationEfficiency of the Carnot cycle On the isotherms, ΔU = 0 , so heat equals work: Q_1 = nRT_1 ln V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4. On the two adiabats, TV^(γ − 1) is constant: T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1). Divide one by the other: V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4 . The two logarithms are therefore equal and cancel: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1.",["ΔU = 0","Q_1 = nRT_1 ln V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4.","TV^(γ − 1)","T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1).","V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4","Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1."],""],[3,6,31,"formula","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","n V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4. On the two adiabats, TV^(γ − 1) is constant: T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1). Divide one by the other: V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4 . The two logarithms are therefore equal and cancel: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1.",["Q_1 = nRT_1 ln V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4."],"<i>Q</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>n</i><i>R</i><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> ln <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>V</i><sub>1</sub></span></span>, <i>Q</i><sub>2</sub> = <i>n</i><i>R</i><i>T</i><sub>2</sub> ln <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>V</i><sub>3</sub></span><span><i>V</i><sub>4</sub></span></span>."],[3,6,31,"formula","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","n V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4. On the two adiabats, TV^(γ − 1) is constant: T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1). Divide one by the other: V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4 . The two logarithms are therefore equal and cancel: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1.",["T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1)."],"<i>T</i><sub>1</sub><i>V</i><sub>2</sub><sup><i>γ</i> − 1</sup> = <i>T</i><sub>2</sub><i>V</i><sub>3</sub><sup><i>γ</i> − 1</sup>, <i>T</i><sub>1</sub><i>V</i><sub>1</sub><sup><i>γ</i> − 1</sup> = <i>T</i><sub>2</sub><i>V</i><sub>4</sub><sup><i>γ</i> − 1</sup>."],[3,6,31,"formula","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","n V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4. On the two adiabats, TV^(γ − 1) is constant: T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1). Divide one by the other: V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4 . The two logarithms are therefore equal and cancel: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1.",["Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>Q</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>Q</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub></span></span>."],[3,6,35,"formula","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","n V_2/V_1, Q_2 = nRT_2 ln V_3/V_4. On the two adiabats, TV^(γ − 1) is constant: T_1V_2^(γ − 1) = T_2V_3^(γ − 1), T_1V_1^(γ − 1) = T_2V_4^(γ − 1). Divide one by the other: V_2/V_1 = V_3/V_4 . The two logarithms are therefore equal and cancel: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1.",["η_(Carnot) = 1 − T_2/T_1 (T in kelvin)"],"<i>η</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">Carnot</span></sub> = 1 − <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> (<i>T</i><span class=\"fx-t\"> in kelvin</span>)"],[3,6,36,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","Not a word about the gas, the size of the cylinder or the pressure. Only the two temperatures matter.",[],""],[3,6,37,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","Carnot’s theorem — the real punchline:",[],""],[3,6,38,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","No engine working between two given temperatures can be more efficient than a reversible engine working between the same two temperatures. All reversible engines working between the same two temperatures have the same efficiency, whatever they are made of.",[],""],[3,6,39,"text","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","Run the Carnot cycle backwards and you get the best possible refrigerator:",[],""],[3,6,40,"formula","Carnot’s engine: the best that physics allows","Run the Carnot cycle backwards and you get the best possible refrigerator:",["β_(Carnot) = T_2/(T_1 − T_2), and for the same pair of reservoirs β = (1 − η)/η."],"<i>β</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">Carnot</span></sub> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>, <span class=\"fx-t\">and for the same pair of reservoirs</span> <i>β</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1 − <i>η</i></span><span><i>η</i></span></span>."],[3,6,41,"trap","Three ways to lose marks here","Celsius. η = 1 − T_2/T_1 works only in kelvin. Between 27^∘ C and 127^∘ C the limit is 1 − 300/400 = 25% , not 1 − 27/127 . 100% efficiency would need a sink at T_2 = 0 K , which cannot be reached. Which helps more — heating the source or cooling the sink by the same amount? Cooling the sink. Lowering T_2 by x raises η by x/T_1 ; raising T_1 by x raises it by only about xT_2/T_1^2 , which is smaller because T_2 < T_1 .",["η = 1 − T_2/T_1","27^∘ C","127^∘ C","1 − 300/400 = 25%","1 − 27/127","T_2 = 0 K","T_2","x","η","x/T_1","T_1","x"],""],[3,6,42,"intuition","Einstein’s verdict","Looking back on his career, Einstein wrote that classical thermodynamics is “the only physical theory of universal content” which he was convinced “will never be overthrown”. Coming from the person who overthrew Newton, that is high praise.",[],""],[3,6,43,"q","The inventor’s pitch · Single correct","An inventor claims to have built an engine that absorbs 1000 J of heat from a source at 500 K , rejects heat to a sink at 300 K , and delivers 450 J of work per cycle. The claim is acceptable, since less work comes out than heat goes in · is impossible, because it violates the first law · is impossible, because it violates the second law · is acceptable only if the working substance is an ideal gas",["1000 J","500 K","300 K","450 J"],""],[3,6,43,"sol","The inventor’s pitch · Single correct","First law: 450 J of work from 1000 J of heat, with 550 J rejected. Energy balances — no problem there. Second law: no engine between 500 K and 300 K can beat η_(Carnot) = 1 − 300/500 = 40%. The claim is 45% . Impossible — and Carnot’s theorem makes no exceptions for the working substance. Answer (C).",["450 J","1000 J","550 J","500 K","300 K","η_(Carnot) = 1 − 300/500 = 40%.","45%"],""],[3,6,44,"pyq","AIEEE 2007 · Single correct","A Carnot engine, having an efficiency of η = 1/10 as a heat engine, is used as a refrigerator. If the work done on the system is 10 J , the amount of energy absorbed from the reservoir at lower temperature is 99 J · 90 J · 1 J · 100 J",["η = 1/10","10 J","99 J","90 J","1 J","100 J"],""],[3,6,44,"sol","AIEEE 2007 · Single correct","As an engine: η = W/Q_1 = 1/10 , so W = 10 J goes with Q_1 = 100 J and Q_2 = Q_1 − W = 90 J . A Carnot engine is reversible: run backwards, the same numbers apply with the arrows flipped. Put in 10 J of work and it lifts 90 J out of the cold reservoir, delivering 100 J to the hot one. Or in one line: β = (1 − η)/η = 9 , so Q_2 = βW = 90 J . Answer (B). As an engine, what Q_1 goes with W = 10 J ? The reversed machine handles the same three quantities.",["η = W/Q_1 = 1/10","W = 10 J","Q_1 = 100 J","Q_2 = Q_1 − W = 90 J","10 J","90 J","100 J","β = (1 − η)/η = 9","Q_2 = βW = 90 J","Q_1","W = 10 J"],""],[3,6,45,"pyq","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","Two Carnot engines A and B are operated in series. The first one, A, receives heat at T_1 (= 600 K) and rejects to a reservoir at temperature T_2 . The second engine B receives the heat rejected by the first engine and, in turn, rejects to a heat reservoir at T_3 (= 400 K) . Calculate the temperature T_2 if the work outputs of the two engines are equal. 600 K · 400 K · 300 K · 500 K",["T_1 (= 600 K)","T_2","T_3 (= 400 K)","T_2","600 K","400 K","300 K","500 K"],""],[3,6,45,"sol","JEE Main 2019 · Single correct","For reversible engines Q/T is the same at every reservoir, so we can write Q_1 = cT_1 , Q_2 = cT_2 , Q_3 = cT_3 with the same constant c . W_A = Q_1 − Q_2 = c (T_1 − T_2), W_B = Q_2 − Q_3 = c (T_2 − T_3). Equal work: T_1 − T_2 = T_2 − T_3 ⟹ T_2 = (T_1 + T_3)/2 = 500 K . Answer (D). Worth remembering: equal work → T_2 is the arithmetic mean. If the question had asked for equal efficiency, you would need T_2/T_1 = T_3/T_2 — the geometric mean, √(T_1T_3) . For a Carnot engine the heats are in the ratio of the temperatures: Q_1 : Q_2 : Q_3 = T_1 : T_2 : T_3 .",["Q/T","Q_1 = cT_1","Q_2 = cT_2","Q_3 = cT_3","c","W_A = Q_1 − Q_2 = c (T_1 − T_2), W_B = Q_2 − Q_3 = c (T_2 − T_3).","T_1 − T_2 = T_2 − T_3 ⟹ T_2 = (T_1 + T_3)/2 = 500 K","T_2","T_2/T_1 = T_3/T_2","√(T_1T_3)","Q_1 : Q_2 : Q_3 = T_1 : T_2 : T_3"],""],[3,6,46,"pyq","AIEEE 2012 · Single correct","Helium gas goes through a cycle ABCDA (consisting of two isochoric and two isobaric lines) as shown in the figure. The efficiency of this cycle is nearly (assume the gas to be close to an ideal gas) 15.4% · 9.1% · 10.5% · 12.5%",["15.4%","9.1%","10.5%","12.5%"],""],[3,6,46,"sol","AIEEE 2012 · Single correct","Work per cycle = area of the rectangle = (2P_0 − P_0)(2V_0 − V_0) = P_0V_0 . Heat absorbed. T ∝ PV , so the temperature rises along A → B and B → C, and falls along the other two legs. For helium, C_V = 3/2R and C_P = 5/2R : A → B (isochoric): Q = nC_VΔT = 3/2 Δ(PV) = 3/2 (2P_0V_0 − P_0V_0) = 3/2P_0V_0 B → C (isobaric): Q = nC_PΔT = 5/2 Δ(PV) = 5/2 (4P_0V_0 − 2P_0V_0) = 5P_0V_0 η = W/(Q_(absorbed)) = 13/2P_0V_0P_0V_0 = 2/13 ≈ 15.4%. Answer (A). Compare with Carnot: the hottest point (C) has PV = 4P_0V_0 and the coldest (A) has P_0V_0 , so a Carnot engine between those extremes would manage 1 − 1/4 = 75% . This cycle is nowhere near — it keeps exchanging heat across large temperature differences, and that is irreversible. Efficiency = (area of the loop) ÷ (heat absorbed). Heat is absorbed only on the legs where the temperature rises.",["= (2P_0 − P_0)(2V_0 − V_0) = P_0V_0","T ∝ PV","C_V = 3/2R","C_P = 5/2R","Q = nC_VΔT = 3/2 Δ(PV) = 3/2 (2P_0V_0 − P_0V_0) = 3/2P_0V_0","Q = nC_PΔT = 5/2 Δ(PV) = 5/2 (4P_0V_0 − 2P_0V_0) = 5P_0V_0","η = W/(Q_(absorbed)) = 13/2P_0V_0P_0V_0 = 2/13 ≈ 15.4%.","PV = 4P_0V_0","P_0V_0","1 − 1/4 = 75%"],""],[3,6,47,"recap","Recap","Engine: W = Q_1 − Q_2 , η = 1 − Q_2/Q_1 . Refrigerator: β = Q_2/W . Second law. Kelvin–Planck: no engine converts heat from one reservoir entirely into work. Clausius: heat will not flow from cold to hot with no other effect. Carnot (two isotherms + two adiabats, all reversible): Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1 , so η = 1 − T_2/T_1 and β = T_2/(T_1 − T_2) — kelvin only. No engine between the same two temperatures beats Carnot; all reversible engines tie. Efficiency of any cycle on a P – V diagram: enclosed area ÷ heat absorbed (positive Q ’s only).",["W = Q_1 − Q_2","η = 1 − Q_2/Q_1","β = Q_2/W","Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1","η = 1 − T_2/T_1","β = T_2/(T_1 − T_2)","P","V","Q"],""],[3,7,0,"lesson","Heat Transfer I: Conduction","Heat creeping through a solid behaves exactly like current through a resistor. Learn that one analogy and every rod, slab and frozen-lake problem turns into a circuit you already know how to solve.",[],""],[3,7,1,"sec","Three ways for heat to travel","Sit beside a campfire and you meet all three at once.",[],""],[3,7,2,"text","Three ways for heat to travel","Sit beside a campfire and you meet all three at once.",[],""],[3,7,3,"text","Three ways for heat to travel","Hold a metal poker with one end in the flames: the handle slowly gets hot. Energy is passed from atom to atom while the material itself stays put. That is conduction. Hold your hand above the fire: hot air rises and carries energy up with it. The material itself moves. That is convection. Hold your hand to the side: you still feel the glow, though no hot air reaches you. Energy is arriving as electromagnetic waves, which need no medium at all. That is radiation.",[],""],[3,7,4,"text","Three ways for heat to travel","This lesson is about the first. The other two are next.",[],""],[3,7,5,"sec","Fourier’s law","Take a slab of area A and thickness L whose faces are held at temperatures T_1 and T_2 (T_1 > T_2). Once things have settled, heat flows through it at a steady rate — the heat current:",[],""],[3,7,6,"text","Fourier’s law","Take a slab of area A and thickness L whose faces are held at temperatures T_1 and T_2 (T_1 > T_2). Once things have settled, heat flows through it at a steady rate — the heat current:",["A","L","T_1","T_2","T_1 > T_2"],""],[3,7,7,"formula","Fourier’s law","Take a slab of area A and thickness L whose faces are held at temperatures T_1 and T_2 (T_1 > T_2). Once things have settled, heat flows through it at a steady rate — the heat current:",["H = dQ/dt = kA (T_1 − T_2)/L"],"<i>H</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>Q</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>t</i></span></span> = <i>k</i><i>A</i> <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>L</i></span></span>"],[3,7,8,"text","Fourier’s law","k is the thermal conductivity of the material (unit: W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)), and (T_1 − T_2)/L is the temperature gradient. In calculus form, H = −kA dT/dx — the minus sign says heat flows down the temperature slope.",["k","W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)","(T_1 − T_2)/L","H = −kA dT/dx"],""],[3,7,9,"table","Fourier’s law","Material k in W m ^(−1) K ^(−1) Copper 401 Aluminium 237 Steel ≈ 50 Ice 2.2 Glass ≈ 1 Water 0.6 Wood ≈ 0.15 Air (still) 0.026",["k","W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)"],""],[3,7,10,"intuition","What this table explains","Metals conduct heat superbly because their free electrons carry energy as well as charge — which is why good electrical conductors are good thermal conductors too. Still air is about the worst conductor around. Woollens, quilts, double-glazed windows and a bird’s fluffed-up feathers all work the same way: they trap pockets of still air. And remember the cold steel almirah from Lesson 1? Steel’s k is a few hundred times wood’s. Same temperature — but the steel pulls heat out of your fingers far faster.",["k"],""],[3,7,11,"trap","Steady state is not thermal equilibrium","In steady state the temperature at each point has stopped changing with time — but it still varies from place to place, and heat keeps flowing. In equilibrium the temperature is the same everywhere and nothing flows. For a uniform, well-lagged rod in steady state, the same H crosses every cross-section (heat is not piling up anywhere), so the temperature falls linearly from the hot end to the cold end.",["H"],""],[3,7,12,"q","Steady state · Single correct","A uniform metal rod, lagged so that no heat escapes from its sides, has one end kept in steam at 100^∘ C and the other in melting ice at 0^∘ C . After a long time, every point of the rod is at 50^∘ C · the rate of heat flow is greatest near the hot end and falls to zero at the cold end · the midpoint of the rod is at 50^∘ C and its temperature no longer changes · the rod is in thermal equilibrium, so no heat flows through it",["100^∘ C","0^∘ C","50^∘ C","50^∘ C"],""],[3,7,12,"sol","Steady state · Single correct","The rod reaches a steady state, not equilibrium. Heat keeps flowing, at the same rate through every cross-section — otherwise some slice would be gaining or losing energy and its temperature would still be changing. That rules out (B) and (D). Same H , same k , same A everywhere means the same gradient everywhere: the temperature drops in a straight line from 100^∘ C to 0^∘ C . The midpoint sits at 50^∘ C and stays there. Answer (C).",["H","k","A","100^∘ C","0^∘ C","50^∘ C"],""],[3,7,13,"sec","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Rewrite Fourier’s law like this:",[],""],[3,7,14,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Rewrite Fourier’s law like this:",[],""],[3,7,15,"formula","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Rewrite Fourier’s law like this:",["H = (T_1 − T_2)/R, R = L/kA"],"<i>H</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>R</i></span></span>, <i>R</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>L</i></span><span><i>k</i><i>A</i></span></span>"],[3,7,16,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","It is Ohm’s law with new names.",[],""],[3,7,17,"table","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Electricity Heat current I heat current H potential difference ΔV temperature difference ΔT resistance L/σA thermal resistance L/kA conductivity σ conductivity k",["I","H","ΔV","ΔT","L/σA","L/kA","σ","k"],""],[3,7,18,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","So everything you know about circuits carries over.",[],""],[3,7,19,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Series (slabs one behind the other — the same heat current passes through each):",[],""],[3,7,20,"formula","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Series (slabs one behind the other — the same heat current passes through each):",["R = R_1 + R_2"],"<i>R</i> = <i>R</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>2</sub>"],[3,7,21,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","The temperature of the junction follows from “heat in = heat out”. For equal cross-sections,",[],""],[3,7,22,"formula","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","The temperature of the junction follows from “heat in = heat out”. For equal cross-sections,",["(k_1(T_1 − T_j))/L_1 = (k_2(T_j − T_2))/L_2."],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><sub>1</sub>(<i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub><i>j</i></sub>)</span><span><i>L</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><sub>2</sub>(<i>T</i><sub><i>j</i></sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub>)</span><span><i>L</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>."],[3,7,23,"text","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Parallel (slabs side by side — each sees the full temperature difference, and the currents add):",[],""],[3,7,24,"formula","Thermal resistance: Ohm’s law for heat","Parallel (slabs side by side — each sees the full temperature difference, and the currents add):",["1/R = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i></span></span> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>1</sub></span></span> + <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span>1</span><span><i>R</i><sub>2</sub></span></span>"],[3,7,25,"key","Equivalent conductivity of two slabs","Two slabs of equal thickness in series: k_eq = (2k_1k_2)/(k_1 + k_2) (dominated by the poorer conductor). Two slabs of equal area in parallel: k_eq = (k_1 + k_2)/2 . Exactly like resistors — because they are resistors.",["k_eq = (2k_1k_2)/(k_1 + k_2)","k_eq = (k_1 + k_2)/2"],""],[3,7,26,"q","The junction · Numerical answer","A copper rod (k = 400 W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)) and a steel rod (k = 50 W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)) of the same length and cross-section are welded end to end. The free end of the copper is kept at 100^∘ C and the free end of the steel at 0^∘ C ; the sides are lagged. What is the temperature of the junction in steady state, in ^∘ C ?",["k = 400 W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)","k = 50 W m ^(−1) K ^(−1)","100^∘ C","0^∘ C","^∘ C"],""],[3,7,26,"sol","The junction · Numerical answer","Equal lengths and areas, so equate the heat currents: 400 (100 − T) = 50 (T − 0) ⟹ 40 000 = 450 T ⟹ T ≈ 88.9^∘ C . The junction sits very close to the copper’s end temperature. Copper is the better conductor (smaller thermal resistance), so it needs only a small temperature drop; the steel takes almost all of it — just as the bigger resistor in a series circuit takes most of the voltage. Same heat current through both rods. Which rod needs the bigger temperature drop to push it through?",["400 (100 − T) = 50 (T − 0) ⟹ 40 000 = 450 T ⟹ T ≈ 88.9^∘ C ."],""],[3,7,27,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2013 · Single correct","Two rectangular blocks, having identical dimensions, can be arranged either in configuration I or in configuration II as shown in the figure. One of the blocks has thermal conductivity k and the other 2k . The temperature difference between the ends along the x -axis is the same in both configurations. It takes 9 s to transport a certain amount of heat from the hot end to the cold end in configuration I. The time to transport the same amount of heat in configuration II is 2.0 s · 3.0 s · 4.5 s · 6.0 s",["k","2k","x","9 s","2.0 s","3.0 s","4.5 s","6.0 s"],""],[3,7,27,"sol","JEE Advanced 2013 · Single correct","Let each block have length L along x and cross-section A . Then R_k = L/kA and R_(2k) = L/(2kA) . Configuration I (series): R_I = L/kA + L/(2kA) = (3L)/(2kA) . Configuration II (parallel): 1/(R_II) = kA/L + (2kA)/L ⟹ R_II = L/(3kA) . The heat transported is Q = ΔT/R t . Same Q and ΔT means t ∝ R : t_II = t_I (R_II)/R_I = 9 s × (1/3)/(3/2) = 2.0 s . Answer (A). Series in I, parallel in II. For the same ΔT and the same heat, time is proportional to thermal resistance.",["L","x","A","R_k = L/kA","R_(2k) = L/(2kA)","R_I = L/kA + L/(2kA) = (3L)/(2kA)","1/(R_II) = kA/L + (2kA)/L ⟹ R_II = L/(3kA)","Q = ΔT/R t","Q","ΔT","t ∝ R","t_II = t_I (R_II)/R_I = 9 s × (1/3)/(3/2) = 2.0 s ."],""],[3,7,28,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2009 · Integer answer","A metal rod AB of length 10x has its one end A in ice at 0^∘ C and the other end B in water at 100^∘ C . If a point P on the rod is maintained at 400^∘ C , then it is found that equal amounts of water and ice evaporate and melt per unit time. The latent heat of evaporation of water is 540 cal g ^(−1) and the latent heat of melting of ice is 80 cal g ^(−1) . If the point P is at a distance of λx from the ice end A, find the value of λ . (Neglect any heat loss to the surroundings.)",["10x","0^∘ C","100^∘ C","400^∘ C","540 cal g ^(−1)","80 cal g ^(−1)","λx","λ"],""],[3,7,28,"sol","IIT-JEE 2009 · Integer answer","Heat flows from P both ways. Towards the ice (length λx , temperature drop 400^∘ C): H_A = (kA (400))/λx . Towards the water (length (10 − λ)x , temperature drop 300^∘ C): H_B = (kA (300))/((10 − λ)x) . In unit time, mass melted = H_A/80 and mass evaporated = H_B/540 . These are equal: 400/80λ = 300/(540 (10 − λ)) ⟹ 5/λ = 5/(9 (10 − λ)) ⟹ 9 (10 − λ) = λ ⟹ λ = 9. Answer: 9. It makes sense that P is close to the water end: boiling a gram needs nearly seven times the heat of melting a gram, so the water side needs a much steeper gradient. P feeds two heat currents, one towards each end. “Equal masses per unit time” links them through the two latent heats.",["λx","400^∘ C","H_A = (kA (400))/λx","(10 − λ)x","300^∘ C","H_B = (kA (300))/((10 − λ)x)","= H_A/80","= H_B/540","400/80λ = 300/(540 (10 − λ)) ⟹ 5/λ = 5/(9 (10 − λ)) ⟹ 9 (10 − λ) = λ ⟹ λ = 9."],""],[3,7,29,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2016 · Single correct","The ends Q and R of two thin wires, PQ and RS, are soldered (joined) together. Initially each of the wires has a length of 1 m at 10^∘ C . Now the end P is maintained at 10^∘ C , while the end S is heated and maintained at 400^∘ C . The system is thermally insulated from its surroundings. If the thermal conductivity of wire PQ is twice that of the wire RS and the coefficient of linear thermal expansion of PQ is 1.2 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1) , the change in length of the wire PQ is 0.78 mm · 0.90 mm · 1.56 mm · 2.34 mm",["1 m","10^∘ C","10^∘ C","400^∘ C","1.2 × 10^(−5) K ^(−1)","0.78 mm","0.90 mm","1.56 mm","2.34 mm"],""],[3,7,29,"sol","JEE Advanced 2016 · Single correct","Junction temperature. Same heat current through both wires (equal lengths and cross-sections), with k_PQ = 2k and k_RS = k : 2k (T − 10) = k (400 − T) ⟹ 3T = 420 ⟹ T = 140^∘ C . Expansion of PQ. In steady state the temperature along PQ rises linearly from 10^∘ C at P to 140^∘ C at Q. Each little piece expands according to its own temperature rise, so the whole wire expands as if it had the average rise: ΔT = (0 + 130)/2 = 65 K , ΔL = αL ΔT = (1.2 × 10^(−5))(1 m)(65) = 0.78 mm . Answer (A). Using the full 130 K gives option (C) — the trap for anyone who forgets the gradient. First find the junction temperature. Then remember that PQ is not at one temperature — it varies linearly along the wire.",["k_PQ = 2k","k_RS = k","2k (T − 10) = k (400 − T) ⟹ 3T = 420 ⟹ T = 140^∘ C .","10^∘ C","140^∘ C","ΔT = (0 + 130)/2 = 65 K , ΔL = αL ΔT = (1.2 × 10^(−5))(1 m)(65) = 0.78 mm .","130 K"],""],[3,7,30,"sec","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","A lake is at 0^∘ C and the air above it is at −θ ^∘ C . A layer of ice has already grown to thickness x . How fast does it thicken?",[],""],[3,7,31,"text","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","A lake is at 0^∘ C and the air above it is at −θ ^∘ C . A layer of ice has already grown to thickness x . How fast does it thicken?",["0^∘ C","−θ ^∘ C","x"],""],[3,7,32,"text","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","For more ice to form at the bottom of the layer, the water there must give up its latent heat — and that heat has to be conducted up through the ice already present. In a time dt a new layer dx forms over area A :",["dt","dx","A"],""],[3,7,33,"formula","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","For more ice to form at the bottom of the layer, the water there must give up its latent heat — and that heat has to be conducted up through the ice already present. In a time dt a new layer dx forms over area A :",["conducted through the ice kAθ/x dt = released by freezing (ρA dx) L_f ⟹ x dx = kθ/(ρL_f) dt"],"<span class=\"fx-t\">conducted through the ice</span><span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>A</i> <i>θ</i></span><span><i>x</i></span></span> <i>d</i><i>t</i> = <span class=\"fx-t\">released by freezing</span>(<i>ρ</i><i>A</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i>) <i>L</i><sub><i>f</i></sub> ⟹ <i>x</i> <i>d</i><i>x</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>k</i><i>θ</i></span><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i><sub><i>f</i></sub></span></span> <i>d</i><i>t</i>"],[3,7,34,"text","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","Integrate from thickness x_1 to x_2 :",["x_1","x_2"],""],[3,7,35,"formula","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","Integrate from thickness x_1 to x_2 :",["t = (ρL_f)/(2kθ) (x_2^2 − x_1^2)"],"<i>t</i> = <span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>ρ</i><i>L</i><sub><i>f</i></sub></span><span>2<i>k</i><i>θ</i></span></span> (<i>x</i><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> − <i>x</i><sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>)"],[3,7,36,"text","Ice on a lake: a problem that integrates itself","Starting from nothing, x ∝ √t . The ice is its own blanket: the thicker it gets, the more slowly it grows. (This, together with water’s density maximum at 4^∘ C , is why deep lakes almost never freeze solid.)",["x ∝ √t","4^∘ C"],""],[3,7,37,"q","Slower and slower · Single correct","On a still, freezing night the ice on a pond takes 2 hours to grow from nothing to a thickness of 1 cm . If the air temperature stays the same, how much more time will it take for the ice to grow from 1 cm to 3 cm ? 4 hours · 6 hours · 16 hours · 18 hours",["2","1 cm","1 cm","3 cm","4","6","16","18"],""],[3,7,37,"sol","Slower and slower · Single correct","t ∝ x_2^2 − x_1^2 with everything else fixed. 0 → 1 cm : 1^2 − 0^2 = 1 unit, which takes 2 hours. 1 → 3 cm : 3^2 − 1^2 = 8 units, which takes 8 × 2 = 16 hours. Answer (C). Option (D), 18 hours, is the total time from zero to 3 cm ; option (A) assumes the ice grows at a steady rate. Time is proportional to x_2^2 − x_1^2 , not to x_2 − x_1 .",["t ∝ x_2^2 − x_1^2","0 → 1 cm","1^2 − 0^2 = 1","2","1 → 3 cm","3^2 − 1^2 = 8","8 × 2 = 16","18","3 cm","x_2^2 − x_1^2","x_2 − x_1"],""],[3,7,38,"recap","Recap","Fourier: H = kA (T_1 − T_2)/L . In steady state the same H crosses every section of a lagged rod. Thermal resistance R = L/kA , with H = ΔT/R . Series: resistances add. Parallel: reciprocals add. Junction temperature: equate the heat current on either side. A rod with a temperature gradient expands as if at its average temperature. Ice layer: t = (ρL_f)/(2kθ)(x_2^2 − x_1^2) , so thickness grows like √t .",["H = kA (T_1 − T_2)/L","H","R = L/kA","H = ΔT/R","t = (ρL_f)/(2kθ)(x_2^2 − x_1^2)","√t"],""],[3,8,0,"lesson","Heat Transfer II: Convection, Radiation & Cooling","How the Sun’s warmth crosses 150 million kilometres of nothing, why a star’s colour is a thermometer, and the law that predicts when your tea will be cool enough to drink.",[],""],[3,8,1,"sec","Convection: heat that hitch-hikes","Warm a fluid from below. The heated part expands, becomes lighter than its surroundings and rises; cooler, denser fluid slides in underneath to take its place. A circulating convection current is born, and it carries energy along with the moving fluid.",[],""],[3,8,2,"text","Convection: heat that hitch-hikes","Warm a fluid from below. The heated part expands, becomes lighter than its surroundings and rises; cooler, denser fluid slides in underneath to take its place. A circulating convection current is born, and it carries energy along with the moving fluid.",[],""],[3,8,3,"text","Convection: heat that hitch-hikes","Convection needs two things: a fluid (liquid or gas) and — for the natural kind — gravity to sort light from heavy.",[],""],[3,8,4,"text","Convection: heat that hitch-hikes","The heating coil of a kettle or geyser sits at the bottom; the cooling unit of a fridge or the outlet of an air-conditioner sits near the top. Both let convection do the stirring. Sea breeze. By day the land warms faster than the sea (water’s giant specific heat again). Air over the land rises and cooler sea air flows in to replace it. At night the land cools faster and the breeze reverses. Blow on your soup, switch on a fan, or let your heart pump blood, and you have forced convection.",[],""],[3,8,5,"text","Convection: heat that hitch-hikes","JEE asks no formulas here — only whether you can recognise it.",[],""],[3,8,6,"sec","Radiation: everything glows","Every object above absolute zero sends out electromagnetic waves, simply because its charges are jiggling thermally. This thermal radiation needs no medium: sunlight crosses about 150 million km of vacuum and reaches us in roughly 8 minutes 20 seconds.",[],""],[3,8,7,"text","Radiation: everything glows","Every object above absolute zero sends out electromagnetic waves, simply because its charges are jiggling thermally. This thermal radiation needs no medium: sunlight crosses about 150 million km of vacuum and reaches us in roughly 8 minutes 20 seconds.",[],""],[3,8,8,"text","Radiation: everything glows","When radiation lands on a body, three things can happen to it — it is absorbed, reflected or transmitted — and the fractions must add up:",[],""],[3,8,9,"formula","Radiation: everything glows","When radiation lands on a body, three things can happen to it — it is absorbed, reflected or transmitted — and the fractions must add up:",["a + r + t = 1"],"<i>a</i> + <i>r</i> + <i>t</i> = 1"],[3,8,10,"text","Radiation: everything glows","The black body. An ideal black body absorbs everything that falls on it: a = 1 . The best practical one is not a lump of soot but a small hole in a hollow box. Any ray that wanders in bounces around inside, losing a little at every bounce, and has almost no chance of finding the hole again. The hole is the black body.",["a = 1"],""],[3,8,11,"text","Radiation: everything glows","Emissivity. No real surface radiates as strongly as a black body at the same temperature. The ratio is the emissivity e , between 0 (a perfect mirror) and 1 (a black body).",["e"],""],[3,8,12,"text","Radiation: everything glows","Kirchhoff’s law. At any given temperature, a body’s emissivity equals its absorptivity: e = a . Good absorbers are good emitters; poor absorbers are poor emitters.",["e = a"],""],[3,8,13,"intuition","Seeing Kirchhoff’s law","Heat a white china plate with a dark pattern on it until it glows, and look at it in a dark room. The pattern reverses: the parts that looked dark in daylight (good absorbers) now glow brighter than the white parts. It is also why a vacuum flask is silvered. The vacuum blocks conduction and convection; the shiny walls are poor emitters and poor absorbers, so radiation gets little chance either way.",[],""],[3,8,14,"trap","A “black body” need not look black","“Black” describes how it absorbs, not how it looks. The Sun is very nearly a black body. So is the glowing mouth of a furnace.",[],""],[3,8,15,"sec","The Stefan–Boltzmann law","How much power does a hot surface radiate? Josef Stefan spotted the pattern in experimental data in 1879, and his former student Ludwig Boltzmann derived it from thermodynamics five years later:",[],""],[3,8,16,"text","The Stefan–Boltzmann law","How much power does a hot surface radiate? Josef Stefan spotted the pattern in experimental data in 1879, and his former student Ludwig Boltzmann derived it from thermodynamics five years later:",[],""],[3,8,17,"formula","The Stefan–Boltzmann law","How much power does a hot surface radiate? Josef Stefan spotted the pattern in experimental data in 1879, and his former student Ludwig Boltzmann derived it from thermodynamics five years later:",["P = e σA T^4, σ = 5.67 × 10^(−8) W m ^(−2) K ^(−4)"],"<i>P</i> = <i>e</i> <i>σ</i><i>A</i> <i>T</i><sup>4</sup>, <i>σ</i> = 5.67 × 10<sup>−8</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">W m</span><sup>−2</sup><span class=\"fx-t\">K</span><sup>−4</sup>"],[3,8,18,"text","The Stefan–Boltzmann law","A is the surface area and T the absolute temperature. A body at T sitting in surroundings at T_0 also absorbs, so its net loss is",["A","T","T","T_0"],""],[3,8,19,"formula","The Stefan–Boltzmann law","A is the surface area and T the absolute temperature. A body at T sitting in surroundings at T_0 also absorbs, so its net loss is",["P_net = e σA (T^4 − T_0^4)."],"<i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> = <i>e</i> <i>σ</i><i>A</i> (<i>T</i><sup>4</sup> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub><sup>4</sup>)."],[3,8,20,"intuition","The fourth power is brutal","Double the absolute temperature and the radiated power goes up sixteen times. A lamp filament at 3000 K radiates ten thousand times more per square metre than your skin at about 300 K .",["3000 K","300 K"],""],[3,8,21,"key","Weighing the Sun’s surface temperature from your desk","Just above Earth’s atmosphere, sunlight delivers S = 1361 W m ^(−2) (the solar constant). The Sun’s total output is spread over a sphere of radius d , the Earth–Sun distance, so P_⊙ = S ⋅ 4πd^2 . Treat the Sun (radius R) as a black body, P_⊙ = σ ⋅ 4πR^2 T^4 , and T = ((S d^2)/(σR^2))^(1/4) . With d/R ≈ 215 this gives T ≈ 5770 K — the accepted value of the Sun’s effective temperature, from nothing but a power meter and some geometry.",["S = 1361 W m ^(−2)","d","P_⊙ = S ⋅ 4πd^2","R","P_⊙ = σ ⋅ 4πR^2 T^4","T = ((S d^2)/(σR^2))^(1/4) .","d/R ≈ 215","T ≈ 5770 K"],""],[3,8,21,"formula","Weighing the Sun’s surface temperature from your desk","o P_⊙ = S ⋅ 4πd^2 . Treat the Sun (radius R) as a black body, P_⊙ = σ ⋅ 4πR^2 T^4 , and T = ((S d^2)/(σR^2))^(1/4) . With d/R ≈ 215 this gives T ≈ 5770 K — the accepted value of the Sun’s effective temperature, from nothing but a power meter and some geometry.",["T = ((S d^2)/(σR^2))^(1/4) ."],"<i>T</i> = (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>S</i> <i>d</i><sup>2</sup></span><span><i>σ</i><i>R</i><sup>2</sup></span></span>)<sup>1/4</sup> ."],[3,8,23,"q","Kelvin, always · Single correct","A black body at 27^∘ C radiates energy at a rate P . If its temperature is raised to 327^∘ C , the rate becomes 16P · 2P · 4P · about 21 500 P",["27^∘ C","P","327^∘ C","16P","2P","4P","21 500 P"],""],[3,8,23,"sol","Kelvin, always · Single correct","Convert first: 27^∘ C = 300 K and 327^∘ C = 600 K . The absolute temperature has doubled: (P^′)/P = (600/300)^4 = 16. Answer (A). Option (D) is (327/27)^4 — what you get by forgetting kelvin. The numbers 27 , 127 , 327 , 627 are JEE favourites precisely because they become 300 , 400 , 600 , 900 .",["27^∘ C = 300 K","327^∘ C = 600 K","(P^′)/P = (600/300)^4 = 16.","(327/27)^4","27","127","327","627","300","400","600","900"],""],[3,8,24,"sec","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","A black body radiates at all wavelengths, but not equally. Plot the intensity against wavelength and you get a lopsided hump whose shape depends only on temperature:",[],""],[3,8,25,"text","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","A black body radiates at all wavelengths, but not equally. Plot the intensity against wavelength and you get a lopsided hump whose shape depends only on temperature:",[],""],[3,8,26,"figure","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","Real Planck curves. Hotter means higher everywhere — and a peak that slides towards shorter wavelengths (dashed line).",[],""],[3,8,27,"text","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","Two things happen as the body gets hotter. The area under the curve grows as T^4 — that is Stefan’s law. And the peak shifts to shorter wavelengths:",["T^4"],""],[3,8,28,"formula","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","Two things happen as the body gets hotter. The area under the curve grows as T^4 — that is Stefan’s law. And the peak shifts to shorter wavelengths:",["λ_m T = b, b = 2.9 × 10^(−3) m K (Wien’s displacement law)"],"<i>λ</i><sub><i>m</i></sub> <i>T</i> = <i>b</i>, <i>b</i> = 2.9 × 10<sup>−3</sup> <span class=\"fx-t\">m K</span> (<span class=\"fx-t\">Wien’s displacement law</span>)"],[3,8,29,"table","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","Object Temperature λ_m Where that is The Sun 5772 K ≈ 500 nm middle of the visible band A lamp filament ≈ 3000 K ≈ 1 μ m infrared — most of its output is heat, not light You ≈ 310 K ≈ 9.4 μ m far in the infrared",["λ_m","5772 K","≈ 500 nm","≈ 3000 K","≈ 1 μ m","≈ 310 K","≈ 9.4 μ m"],""],[3,8,30,"text","Wien’s law: colour is a thermometer","That last row is how thermal cameras see people in the dark. And the first is why the sky is full of coloured thermometers: reddish Betelgeuse has a surface near 3600 K , while blue-white Rigel is more than three times hotter.",["3600 K"],""],[3,8,31,"trap","Hotter is higher everywhere","The curves never cross. A hotter black body emits more than a cooler one at every wavelength — not just near its own peak.",[],""],[3,8,32,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","Two spherical bodies A (radius 6 cm) and B (radius 18 cm) are at temperatures T_1 and T_2 , respectively. The maximum intensity in the emission spectrum of A is at 500 nm and in that of B is at 1500 nm . Considering them to be black bodies, what will be the ratio of the rate of total energy radiated by A to that of B?",["6 cm","18 cm","T_1","T_2","500 nm","1500 nm"],""],[3,8,32,"sol","IIT-JEE 2010 · Integer answer","Wien: T_1/T_2 = λ_B/λ_A = (1500)/500 = 3 . Stefan: P = σ (4πr^2) T^4 , so P_A/P_B = (r_A/r_B)^2 (T_1/T_2)^4 = (6/18)^2 (3)^4 = 81/9 = 9. Answer: 9. B has nine times the area, but A is three times hotter — and 3^4 beats 3^2 . Wien’s law for the temperature ratio; Stefan’s law — with the area — for the power ratio.",["T_1/T_2 = λ_B/λ_A = (1500)/500 = 3","P = σ (4πr^2) T^4","P_A/P_B = (r_A/r_B)^2 (T_1/T_2)^4 = (6/18)^2 (3)^4 = 81/9 = 9.","3^4","3^2"],""],[3,8,33,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2015 · Integer answer","Two spherical stars A and B emit blackbody radiation. The radius of A is 400 times that of B and A emits 10^4 times the power emitted from B. The ratio (λ_A/λ_B) of their wavelengths λ_A and λ_B at which the peaks occur in their respective radiation curves is ______.",["400","10^4","(λ_A/λ_B)","λ_A","λ_B"],""],[3,8,33,"sol","JEE Advanced 2015 · Integer answer","Stefan: P_A/P_B = (R_A/R_B)^2 (T_A/T_B)^4 , so 10^4 = (400)^2 (T_A/T_B)^4 ⟹ (T_A/T_B)^4 = (10^4)/(1.6 × 10^5) = 1/16 ⟹ T_A/T_B = 1/2. Wien: λ_A/λ_B = T_B/T_A = 2 . Answer: 2. Star A is a cool giant: huge, so it out-shines B, but its peak sits at twice the wavelength. Run the previous question backwards: power and radius first, then temperature, then wavelength.",["P_A/P_B = (R_A/R_B)^2 (T_A/T_B)^4","10^4 = (400)^2 (T_A/T_B)^4 ⟹ (T_A/T_B)^4 = (10^4)/(1.6 × 10^5) = 1/16 ⟹ T_A/T_B = 1/2.","λ_A/λ_B = T_B/T_A = 2"],""],[3,8,34,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2016 · Integer answer","A metal is heated in a furnace where a sensor is kept above the metal surface to read the power radiated (P) by the metal. The sensor has a scale that displays log_2 (P/P_0) , where P_0 is a constant. When the metal surface is at a temperature of 487^∘ C , the sensor shows a value 1 . Assume that the emissivity of the metallic surface remains constant. What is the value displayed by the sensor when the temperature of the metal surface is raised to 2767^∘ C ?",["P","log_2 (P/P_0)","P_0","487^∘ C","1","2767^∘ C"],""],[3,8,34,"sol","JEE Advanced 2016 · Integer answer","In kelvin: 487 + 273 = 760 K and 2767 + 273 = 3040 K . The ratio is exactly 4 . So the radiated power goes up by 4^4 = 256 = 2^8 . A reading of 1 means P_1 = 2P_0 . Then P_2 = 2^8 ⋅ 2P_0 = 2^9P_0 , and the sensor shows log_2 (P_2/P_0) = 9. Answer: 9. On a log scale, multiplying the power by 2^8 simply adds 8 to the reading. Convert both temperatures to kelvin and look at their ratio before doing anything else.",["487 + 273 = 760 K","2767 + 273 = 3040 K","4","4^4 = 256 = 2^8","1","P_1 = 2P_0","P_2 = 2^8 ⋅ 2P_0 = 2^9P_0","log_2 (P_2/P_0) = 9.","2^8"],""],[3,8,35,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","Three very large plates of same area are kept parallel and close to each other. They are considered as ideal black surfaces and have very high thermal conductivity. The first and third plates are maintained at temperatures 2T and 3T respectively. The temperature of the middle (i.e. second) plate under steady state condition is (65/2)^(1/4) T · (97/4)^(1/4) T · (97/2)^(1/4) T · (97)^(1/4) T",["2T","3T","(65/2)^(1/4) T","(97/4)^(1/4) T","(97/2)^(1/4) T","(97)^(1/4) T"],""],[3,8,35,"sol","IIT-JEE 2012 · Single correct","Let the middle plate be at T^′ and each face have area A . All surfaces are black, so each absorbs everything that reaches it. Absorbed by the middle plate: σA(2T)^4 on one face, σA(3T)^4 on the other. Emitted by the middle plate: σAT^(′4) from each of its two faces. σA (2T)^4 + σA (3T)^4 = 2 σA T^(′4) ⟹ T^(′4) = (16 + 81)/2 T^4 ⟹ T^′ = (97/2)^(1/4) T. Answer (C). That is about 2.64 T — between 2T and 3T , but closer to the hot plate, because the fourth power makes the hot side shout louder. Steady state for the middle plate: power absorbed = power emitted. How many faces does it emit from?",["T^′","A","σA(2T)^4","σA(3T)^4","σAT^(′4)","σA (2T)^4 + σA (3T)^4 = 2 σA T^(′4) ⟹ T^(′4) = (16 + 81)/2 T^4 ⟹ T^′ = (97/2)^(1/4) T.","2.64 T","2T","3T"],""],[3,8,36,"sec","Newton’s law of cooling","Stefan’s law is exact but awkward. When a body is only a little warmer than its surroundings, it simplifies beautifully. Write T = T_0 + ΔT with ΔT ≪ T_0 :",[],""],[3,8,37,"text","Newton’s law of cooling","Stefan’s law is exact but awkward. When a body is only a little warmer than its surroundings, it simplifies beautifully. Write T = T_0 + ΔT with ΔT ≪ T_0 :",["T = T_0 + ΔT","ΔT ≪ T_0"],""],[3,8,38,"formula","Newton’s law of cooling","Stefan’s law is exact but awkward. When a body is only a little warmer than its surroundings, it simplifies beautifully. Write T = T_0 + ΔT with ΔT ≪ T_0 :",["T^4 − T_0^4 ≈ 4T_0^3 ΔT ⟹ P_net ≈ (4eσAT_0^3) (T − T_0)."],"<i>T</i><sup>4</sup> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub><sup>4</sup> ≈ 4<i>T</i><sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> Δ<i>T</i> ⟹ <i>P</i><sub><span class=\"fx-t\">net</span></sub> ≈ (4<i>e</i><i>σ</i><i>A</i><i>T</i><sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup>) (<i>T</i> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>)."],[3,8,39,"text","Newton’s law of cooling","The rate of heat loss is simply proportional to the excess temperature. (Convection losses are roughly proportional to it as well, so the rule works well for ordinary warm objects in air.) Since losing heat dQ lowers the temperature by dQ/mc ,",["dQ","dQ/mc"],""],[3,8,40,"formula","Newton’s law of cooling","The rate of heat loss is simply proportional to the excess temperature. (Convection losses are roughly proportional to it as well, so the rule works well for ordinary warm objects in air.) Since losing heat dQ lowers the temperature by dQ/mc ,",["dT/dt = −k (T − T_0) ⟹ T − T_0 = (T_i − T_0) e^(−kt)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>d</i><i>T</i></span><span><i>d</i><i>t</i></span></span> = −<i>k</i> (<i>T</i> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>) ⟹ <i>T</i> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub> = (<i>T</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>) <i>e</i><sup>−<i>k</i><i>t</i></sup>"],[3,8,41,"text","Newton’s law of cooling","The excess temperature decays exponentially. Newton published this rule in 1701 — anonymously.",[],""],[3,8,42,"key","The JEE shortcut","For a fall from T_1 to T_2 in time t , use the average excess temperature: (T_1 − T_2)/t = k ((T_1 + T_2)/2 − T_0) It is an approximation, but an excellent one when the interval is short — and it is what most answer keys expect.",["T_1","T_2","t","(T_1 − T_2)/t = k ((T_1 + T_2)/2 − T_0)"],""],[3,8,42,"formula","The JEE shortcut","For a fall from T_1 to T_2 in time t , use the average excess temperature: (T_1 − T_2)/t = k ((T_1 + T_2)/2 − T_0) It is an approximation, but an excellent one when the interval is short — and it is what most answer keys expect.",["(T_1 − T_2)/t = k ((T_1 + T_2)/2 − T_0)"],"<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> − <i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span><i>t</i></span></span> = <i>k</i> (<span class=\"fx-fr\"><span><i>T</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>T</i><sub>2</sub></span><span>2</span></span> − <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>)"],[3,8,44,"trap","Equal drops do not take equal times","A body that cools from 60^∘ C to 50^∘ C in 5 minutes will take longer than 5 minutes to go from 50^∘ C to 40^∘ C : the closer it gets to room temperature, the more slowly it cools.",["60^∘ C","50^∘ C","50^∘ C","40^∘ C"],""],[3,8,45,"pyq","JEE Main 2020 · Single correct","A metallic sphere cools from 50^∘ C to 40^∘ C in 300 s . If the atmospheric temperature around is 20^∘ C , then the sphere’s temperature after the next 5 minutes will be close to 31^∘ C · 33^∘ C · 28^∘ C · 35^∘ C",["50^∘ C","40^∘ C","300 s","20^∘ C","31^∘ C","33^∘ C","28^∘ C","35^∘ C"],""],[3,8,45,"sol","JEE Main 2020 · Single correct","Exact method. The excess temperature falls by the same factor in equal times. In the first 300 s it goes from 50 − 20 = 30^∘ C to 40 − 20 = 20^∘ C — a factor of 2/3 . In the next 300 s it falls by 2/3 again: 20 × 2/3 = 13.3^∘ C , so the sphere is at 20 + 13.3 = 33.3^∘ C . Shortcut method. First interval: (50 − 40)/5 = k (45 − 20) ⇒ k = 2/25 min ^(−1) . Second interval: (40 − T)/5 = 2/25 ((40 + T)/2 − 20) = T/25 ⟹ 200 − 5T = T ⟹ T = 33.3^∘ C . Both give the same number here. Answer (B). Note that it dropped 10^∘ C in the first five minutes but only 6.7^∘ C in the next five. Work with the excess temperature over the surroundings. What fraction of it survives each 5-minute interval?",["300 s","50 − 20 = 30^∘ C","40 − 20 = 20^∘ C","2/3","300 s","2/3","20 × 2/3 = 13.3^∘ C","20 + 13.3 = 33.3^∘ C","(50 − 40)/5 = k (45 − 20) ⇒ k = 2/25 min ^(−1)","(40 − T)/5 = 2/25 ((40 + T)/2 − 20) = T/25 ⟹ 200 − 5T = T ⟹ T = 33.3^∘ C .","10^∘ C","6.7^∘ C"],""],[3,8,46,"q","The tea problem · Single correct","Your tea has just been poured at 80^∘ C in a room at 20^∘ C , and you want it to be as hot as possible when you drink it five minutes from now. You are going to add a splash of cold milk. When should you add it? Right away · Just before drinking · It makes no difference · Halfway through, at two and a half minutes",["80^∘ C","20^∘ C"],""],[3,8,46,"sol","The tea problem · Single correct","The tea loses heat to the room at a rate proportional to its excess temperature. Add the milk now and the mixture starts cooler — so for the whole five minutes it sits closer to room temperature and leaks heat more slowly. Add it at the end and the black tea spends five minutes at a high excess temperature, pouring heat into the room at the maximum rate. Then the milk cools it further. The milk brings the same “coldness” either way; what differs is how much heat the room steals in the meantime. Less is stolen if you add the milk early. Answer (A). The rate at which the cup loses heat to the room depends on how far above room temperature it is.",[],""],[3,8,47,"recap","Recap","Convection needs a moving fluid; radiation needs nothing at all. Black body: a = 1 . Kirchhoff: e = a — good absorbers are good emitters. Stefan–Boltzmann: P = eσAT^4 ; net loss eσA(T^4 − T_0^4) . Kelvin only. Wien: λ_mT = 2.9 × 10^(−3) m K . Hotter → peak at shorter wavelength, and brighter at every wavelength. Newton’s cooling (small excess): dT/dt = −k(T − T_0) ; excess temperature decays exponentially.",["a = 1","e = a","P = eσAT^4","eσA(T^4 − T_0^4)","λ_mT = 2.9 × 10^(−3) m K","dT/dt = −k(T − T_0)"],""],[3,9,0,"lesson","Revision & Mixed PYQs","The whole chapter on one screen — which idea to reach for, the formulas worth memorising, the traps examiners reuse — and three problems that stitch it all together.",[],""],[3,9,1,"sec","Which idea? Decide before you calculate","Read the question, spot the trigger words, and choose:",[],""],[3,9,2,"text","Which idea? Decide before you calculate","Read the question, spot the trigger words, and choose:",[],""],[3,9,3,"table","Which idea? Decide before you calculate","The question mentions… Reach for… a faulty thermometer, a new scale, “reads x in ice” The fraction rule: (t − t_ice)/(t_(steam) − t_ice) is the same on every linear scale. a rod, rail, plate or hole being heated ΔL = αLΔT with α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3 . Holes grow too. a rod that is clamped or kept at constant length Thermal stress = YαΔT . Length is a decoy. things mixed or dropped into a calorimeter Heat lost = heat gained. With ice or steam, budget check first. a gas with heat supplied / work done Q = ΔU + W , with ΔU = nC_VΔT always and W = area under the path. “slowly, conducting walls” / “suddenly, insulated” Isothermal (PV = const) / adiabatic (PV^γ = const). a strange process such as P ∝ V or PT^2 = const Turn it into PV^x = const, then C = C_V + R/(1 − x) . a cycle and its efficiency W = enclosed area; η = W/Q_(absorbed) (positive Q ’s only). two reservoirs, Carnot, a refrigerator η = 1 − T_2/T_1 , β = T_2/(T_1 − T_2) , kelvin only. rods and slabs in steady state Thermal resistance R = L/kA ; solve it as a circuit. “radiates”, black body, peak wavelength P = eσAT^4 and λ_mT = b . “cools from … to … in … minutes” Newton’s law of cooling; excess temperature decays exponentially.",["x","(t − t_ice)/(t_(steam) − t_ice)","ΔL = αLΔT","α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3","= YαΔT","Q = ΔU + W","ΔU = nC_VΔT","W","PV","PV^γ","P ∝ V","PT^2"],""],[3,9,4,"sec","The formulas worth memorising","",[],""],[3,9,5,"table","The formulas worth memorising","Topic Formula Scales F = 9/5C + 32 , K = C + 273.15 Expansion ΔL = αLΔT , ρ = ρ_0(1 − γΔT) , γ_app = γ_liq − γ_(vessel) Thermal stress Yα ΔT Pendulum clock time lost per day = 1/2α Δθ × 86400 s Calorimetry Q = mcΔT , Q = mL ; water: c = 1 , L_f = 80 , L_v = 540 (cal, g, °C) First law Q = ΔU + W , W = ∫ P dV Ideal gas ΔU = nC_VΔT , C_P − C_V = R , γ = C_P/C_V Isothermal W = Q = nRT ln V_2/V_1 Adiabatic PV^γ , TV^(γ − 1) constant; W = (P_1V_1 − P_2V_2)/(γ − 1) Engine / fridge η = 1 − Q_2/Q_1 , β = Q_2/W ; Carnot: Q_2/Q_1 = T_2/T_1 Conduction H = (kA ΔT)/L ; ice layer: t = (ρL_f)/(2kθ)(x_2^2 − x_1^2) Radiation P = eσAT^4 , λ_mT = 2.9 × 10^(−3) m K Cooling dT/dt = −k(T − T_0)",["F = 9/5C + 32","K = C + 273.15","ΔL = αLΔT","ρ = ρ_0(1 − γΔT)","γ_app = γ_liq − γ_(vessel)","Yα ΔT","= 1/2α Δθ × 86400 s","Q = mcΔT","Q = mL","c = 1","L_f = 80","L_v = 540"],""],[3,9,6,"sec","Ten traps that keep coming back","",[],""],[3,9,7,"text","Ten traps that keep coming back","Celsius where kelvin is needed. Gas laws, Carnot, Stefan and Wien all demand kelvin. Only temperature differences can stay in Celsius. “The hole shrinks.” It doesn’t. Everything scales up together. Skipping the budget check. If ice or steam is present, make sure there is enough heat to finish the phase change before you assume it. Forgetting the condensed steam. After giving up 540 cal g ^(−1) , it is still water at 100^∘ C with more heat to give. The sign of W . Physics: work done by the gas. Chemistry: work done on it. Thinking ΔU = nC_VΔT needs constant volume. It holds for every process of an ideal gas. Using PV^γ for free expansion. No work, no cooling, no path. Dividing by the wrong heat. Efficiency uses the heat absorbed, not the net heat (which is just W again). Calling steady state “equilibrium”. In steady state heat still flows and the temperature still varies along the rod. Forgetting the area in Stefan’s law. Power depends on AT^4 — radius matters as much as temperature.",["540 cal g ^(−1)","100^∘ C","W","ΔU = nC_VΔT","PV^γ","W","AT^4"],""],[3,9,8,"sec","Mixed practice: three questions that span the chapter","Each of these borrows from at least two lessons. Try them on paper, properly, before you look at the solution.",[],""],[3,9,9,"text","Mixed practice: three questions that span the chapter","Each of these borrows from at least two lessons. Try them on paper, properly, before you look at the solution.",[],""],[3,9,10,"pyq","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","An ideal gas is expanding such that PT^2 = constant . The coefficient of volume expansion of the gas is 1/T · 2/T · 3/T · 4/T",["PT^2 = constant","1/T","2/T","3/T","4/T"],""],[3,9,10,"sol","IIT-JEE 2008 · Single correct","From PV = nRT , P = nRT/V . Put this into the process equation: nRT/V T^2 = constant ⟹ V ∝ T^3. Take logs and differentiate: dV/V = 3 dT/T , so γ = 1/VdV/dT = 3/T. Answer (C). For comparison, a gas heated at constant pressure has V ∝ T and so γ = 1/T — the definition of “coefficient of expansion” from Lesson 2 works for gases too, it just isn’t constant. The coefficient of volume expansion is γ = 1/VdV/dT . Use the gas equation to turn the process into a relation between V and T .",["PV = nRT","P = nRT/V","nRT/V T^2 = constant ⟹ V ∝ T^3.","dV/V = 3 dT/T","γ = 1/VdV/dT = 3/T.","V ∝ T","γ = 1/T","γ = 1/VdV/dT","V","T"],""],[3,9,11,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2016 · Single correct","A gas is enclosed in a cylinder with a movable frictionless piston. Its initial thermodynamic state at pressure P_i = 10^5 Pa and volume V_i = 10^(−3) m ^3 changes to a final state at P_f = 1/32 × 10^5 Pa and V_f = 8 × 10^(−3) m ^3 in an adiabatic quasi-static process, such that P^3V^5 = constant . Consider another thermodynamic process that brings the system from the same initial state to the same final state in two steps: an isobaric expansion at P_i followed by an isochoric (isovolumetric) process at volume V_f . The amount of heat supplied to the system in the two-step process is approximately 112 J · 294 J · 588 J · 813 J",["P_i = 10^5 Pa","V_i = 10^(−3) m ^3","P_f = 1/32 × 10^5 Pa","V_f = 8 × 10^(−3) m ^3","P^3V^5 = constant","P_i","V_f","112 J","294 J","588 J","813 J"],""],[3,9,11,"sol","JEE Advanced 2016 · Single correct","Read γ off the adiabat. P^3V^5 = const is PV^(5/3) = const , so γ = 5/3 . ΔU — the same for any route. With P_iV_i = 100 J and P_fV_f = 1/32 × 10^5 × 8 × 10^(−3) = 25 J : ΔU = nC_V ΔT = (P_fV_f − P_iV_i)/(γ − 1) = (25 − 100)/(2/3) = −112.5 J . Work on the two-step route. Only the isobaric step does work: W = P_i (V_f − V_i) = 10^5 × 7 × 10^(−3) = 700 J . First law: Q = ΔU + W = −112.5 + 700 = 587.5 ≈ 588 J . Answer (C). This is the lesson of the P – V playground in one question: same end points, same ΔU — but the adiabatic route needs no heat at all, and this one needs 588 J . Option (A) is ∣ΔU∣ , waiting for anyone who stops one step early. ΔU is the same for both routes. Get it from the adiabatic route (where Q = 0), then add the work of the two-step route.",["γ","P^3V^5 = const","PV^(5/3) = const","γ = 5/3","ΔU","P_iV_i = 100 J","P_fV_f = 1/32 × 10^5 × 8 × 10^(−3) = 25 J","ΔU = nC_V ΔT = (P_fV_f − P_iV_i)/(γ − 1) = (25 − 100)/(2/3) = −112.5 J .","W = P_i (V_f − V_i) = 10^5 × 7 × 10^(−3) = 700 J .","Q = ΔU + W = −112.5 + 700 = 587.5 ≈ 588 J","P","V"],""],[3,9,12,"pyq","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","In the figure a container is shown to have a movable (without friction) piston on top. The container and the piston are all made of perfectly insulating material, allowing no heat transfer between outside and inside the container. The container is divided into two compartments by a rigid partition made of a thermally conducting material that allows slow transfer of heat. The lower compartment of the container is filled with 2 moles of an ideal monatomic gas at 700 K and the upper compartment is filled with 2 moles of an ideal diatomic gas at 400 K . The heat capacities per mole of an ideal monatomic gas are C_V = 3/2R , C_P = 5/2R , and those for an ideal diatomic gas are C_V = 5/2R , C_P = 7/2R . Consider the partition to be rigidly fixed so that it does not move. When equilibrium is achieved, the final temperature of the gases will be 550 K · 525 K · 513 K · 490 K",["700 K","400 K","C_V = 3/2R","C_P = 5/2R","C_V = 5/2R","C_P = 7/2R","550 K","525 K","513 K","490 K"],""],[3,9,12,"sol","JEE Advanced 2014 · Single correct","This is a calorimetry problem — heat lost = heat gained — but the “specific heats” must be chosen with care. Lower gas (monatomic). Trapped between a rigid floor and a fixed partition: constant volume. It gives out heat nC_VΔT . Upper gas (diatomic). Above it is a free piston, so as it warms it expands at constant pressure. It takes in heat nC_PΔT . 2 (3/2R) (700 − T) = 2 (7/2R) (T − 400) ⟹ 3 (700 − T) = 7 (T − 400) ⟹ T = 490 K . Answer (D). The paper’s follow-up lets the partition slide freely too. Then both gases are at constant pressure: 5(700 − T) = 7(T − 400) gives T = 525 K , and the total work done by the gases is nR ΔT for each: 2R(525 − 700) + 2R(525 − 400) = −100R . Same container, one bolt removed, completely different answer — because a gas’s specific heat depends on what it is allowed to do. The two gases exchange heat under different conditions. Which one is at constant volume, and which is at constant pressure?",["nC_VΔT","nC_PΔT","2 (3/2R) (700 − T) = 2 (7/2R) (T − 400) ⟹ 3 (700 − T) = 7 (T − 400) ⟹ T = 490 K .","5(700 − T) = 7(T − 400)","T = 525 K","nR ΔT","2R(525 − 700) + 2R(525 − 400) = −100R"],""],[3,9,13,"recap","Recap","Trigger words choose the tool; decide before calculating. Kelvin for anything involving gases, Carnot or radiation. ΔU depends only on the end points; Q and W depend on the route. A gas’s heat capacity is set by the process: C_V , C_P , 0 , ∞ — or C_V + R/(1 − x) . When in doubt, write the energy balance. Every problem in this chapter is energy conservation in disguise.",["ΔU","Q","W","C_V","C_P","0","∞","C_V + R/(1 − x)"],""]]});
